2 CONC soon after taken, suffered decapitation in London anno 1483. he m. Elizabeth''', daughter of Sir William '''Paston''', and widow of Richard Lord Poynings ('''NOTE BY CP., THIS SHOULD BE ROBERT POYNINGS'''), '''and was s. by his so
2 CONC n,
2 CONT *   SIR MATTHEW BROWNE, knt., of Beechworth Castle, sheriff of Surrey in 1496, living in 1530, who m. Fridiswide, daughter of Sir Richard Guilford, K.G. of Hempsted, in Kent, and had issue,
2 CONT ** .... etc. ______________
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Brockham
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.brockhamvillage.co.uk/about/about_history/about_history.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Dame Elizabeth (1429–1488), widow of Sir George Browne was a member of the famous Norfolk family of Paston. Sir George Browne, 1st son of Betchworth Castle and Sheriff of Kent, was executed in 1483 in London for supporting Henry V
2 CONC II. His father Sir Thomas Browne, of Betchworth Castle and Sheriff of Kent also suffered a similar fate, being executed on 20 Jul 1460 at the age of 46, after a battle in the War of the Roses. The link with the FitzAlan family is 
2 CONC here with Sir Thomas who married Alainor FitzAlan, daughter of Sir Thomas FitzAlan (d 1431). 
2 CONT
2 CONT During the time that the Brownes lived at Betchworth Castle, until about 1690, the house underwent several alterations and redevelopment, including a major remodelling.
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT THE NATIONAL ARCHIVES PROB 11/43, ff. 457-8
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.oxford-shakespeare.com/Probate/PROB_11-43_ff_457-8.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT SUMMARY: The document below is the Prerogative Court of Canterbury copy of the
2 CONT
2 CONT will dated 24 August 1558 and proved 11 December 1560 of Eleanor (nee Browne)
2 CONT
2 CONT Kempe, the widow of Sir William Kempe (1487-1539) of Olantigh in Kent, and the
2 CONT
2 CONT grandmother of the comedian Will Kempe and of Alice (nee Kempe) Hales (d.1592), the
2 CONT
2 CONT dedicatee of Robert Greene’s Menaphon (1589), to which Thomas Nashe contributed a
2 CONT
2 CONT preface. For the will of Sir Wiliam Kempe, see PROB 11/27, f. 228.
2 CONT
2 CONT The testatrix was the only daughter and heir of Robert Browne of the manor of West
2 CONT
2 CONT Betchworth in Surrey and his wife Mary Mallet, the daughter of Sir William Mallet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Browne’s brother, Sir Anthony Browne (d.1506), by his second wife, Lucy
2 CONT
2 CONT Neville (d.1534), was the great-grandfather of Mary Browne (c.1552-1607), the mother of
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Wriothesley, 3rd Earl of Southampton, the dedicatee of Shakespeare’s Venus and
2 CONT
2 CONT Adonis (1593), and The Rape of Lucrece (1594), and of Thomas Nashe’s The Unfortunate
2 CONT
2 CONT Traveller (1594). By his first wife, Sir Anthony Browne (d.1506) had a daughter, Anne
2 CONT
2 CONT Browne, who was the first wife of Charles Brandon (c.1484-1545), Duke of Suffolk, who
2 CONT
2 CONT married as his fourth wife Katherine Willoughby (1519-1580), later the mother-in-law of
2 CONT
2 CONT Oxford’s sister, Mary de Vere (d.1624). Another brother, Sir George Browne (d.1483),
2 CONT
2 CONT married Elizabeth Paston, and had a son, Sir Matthew Browne (d.1557) of West
2 CONT
2 CONT Betchworth, who married Frideswide Guildford, the daughter of Sir Richard Guildford
2 CONT
2 CONT (c.1450-1506) and his first wife, Anne Pympe. Frideswide’s sister, Mary Guildford,
2 CONT
2 CONT married Christopher Kempe, the elder brother of the testatrix’ husband, Sir William
2 CONT
2 CONT Kempe (1487-1539). Sir Edward Guildford (c.1479-1534), the brother of Mary and
2 CONT
2 CONT Frideswide, married his daughter Jane Guildford to his ward, John Dudley (1504-1553),
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke of Northumberland. They were the parents of Queen Elizabeth’s favourite, Robert
2 CONT
2 CONT Dudley, Earl of Leicester. For the Browne family of Betchworth, who were descended
2 CONT
2 CONT from King John of England, see Richardson, Douglas, Plantagenet Ancestry (Baltimore:
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Publishing, 2004), pp. 62-3, 160. For Browne and Guildford pedigrees, see
2 CONT
2 CONT Burke, John and Bernard Burke, A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Extinct and
2 CONT
2 CONT Dormant Baronetcies of England, Ireland and Scotland, 2nd ed. (London: Scott, 1841),
2 CONT
2 CONT pp. 87, 231.
2 CONT
2 CONT _____________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir George Browne, born in 1434, resided at Betchworth Castle, in Surrey, England. Sir George Browne was Knighted by King Edward IV after the Battle of Wekesbury in 1471. During the first year of the reign of King Richard III, Sir
2 CONC  George Browne fell victim to the intrigues of Royal power. The New Duke of Norfolk had taken over and using his influence, Sir George was convicted of being involved in the revolt of the Duke of Buckingham against King Richard II
2 CONC I, and was beheaded on Tower Hill on 4 Dec 1483. This plot was an excuse for the elimination of any descendents of the Plantagenets of which the Betchworth Brownes stood in line to progress towards the crown of England. Sir George
2 CONC  Browne's fifth great-grandfather was King Henry III of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT *	'Paston01'
2 CONT *William Paston of Paston (b c1378, d 14.08.1444, judge) - continued above
2 CONT *m. Agnes Berry (dau of Sir Edmund Berry or Barrey by Alice, dau of Sir John Gerbridge by Elizabeth)
2 CONT **1. John Paston of Paston (b c1421, d 1466)
2 CONT **m. Margaret Mawtby (dau of John Mawtby by Margaret, dau of John Barney of Redham)
2 CONT ***A. .... etc.
2 CONT **The following are shown by BEB1841 as children of William but by Visitation as children of William's son John. Other than to report that he was son of Judge William, BE1883 starts with John, showing him as married to Margaret Ma
2 CONC ntby (having John and "several other children") then Anne Beaufort (having Sir William "inter alios"). Following cross-references from elsewhere and noting the dates, we suggest the following allocation but stress that it is not c
2 CONC ertain.
2 CONT ***C. Sir William Paston
2 CONT ***m. Anne Beaufort (dau of Edmund Beaufort, Duke of Somerset)
2 CONT ****i. William Paston (dsp)
2 CONT ****ii. Anne Paston
2 CONT ****m. Sir Gilbert Talbot (d 22.10.1542)
2 CONT ****iii. Elizabeth Paston
2 CONT ****m. Sir John Savill of Thornhill 
2 CONT ****iv. Mary Paston (b 19.01.1469/70, d 1489) apparently of this generation
2 CONT ****m. Ralph Nevill (dvp 1498, son of Earl of Westmorland)
2 CONT ***D. Anne Paston
2 CONT ***m. William Yelverton (a 1474)
2 CONT **'''2. Elizabeth Paston
2 CONT **m1. Robert Poynings (d 17.02.1460/1, son of Lord Robert)
2 CONT **We presume that this was the Elizabeth, described by BEB1841 (Browne of Beechworth) as widow of Richard, Lord Poynings, who also married ...
2 CONT **'''m2. Sir George Browne of Beechworth Castle (d 1483)
2 CONT **3. Margery Paston uncertain on what generation she belonged to
2 CONT **m. Robert Colle or Call
2 CONT **4.+ other issue - Edmund, Walter
2 CONT *Main source(s):
2 CONT * (1) For upper section : Visitation (Norfolk, 1563+1589+1613, Paston) 
2 CONT * (2) For lower section : BEB1841 (Paston of Oxnead), BE1883 (Paston of Yarmouth), Visitation (Norfolk, 1563+1589+1613, Paston) 
2 CONT *From: Stirnet.com
