2 CONT *'''Anthony BROWNE (Sir)
2 CONT *'''Born: 1443
2 CONT *'''Died: 19 Nov 1506
2 CONT *'''Buried: Calais
2 CONT *'''Notes: Standard Bearer of England 1485, Esquire for the Body, Governor of Queenborough Castle in Kent, and Knighted 1486. Constable of yea Castle of Calais, 1503. Will dated 25 Sep 1505.
2 CONT *Father: Thomas BROWNE (Sir Sheriff)
2 CONT *Mother: Eleanor FITZALAN
2 CONT *'''Married 1: Eleanor OUGHTRED Jan 1488, St Wilfred's, York, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Anne BROWNE
2 CONT *'''Married 2: Lucy NEVILLE
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **2. Anthony BROWNE (Sir Knight)
2 CONT **3. Elizabeth BROWNE (C. Worcester)
2 CONT **4. Lucy BROWNE
2 CONT **5. William BROWNE
2 CONT **6. Henry BROWNE
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/BROWNE1.htm#Anthony BROWNE (Sir)2 ______________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Anthony Browne
2 CONT *'''Birth: 	Jun. 29, 1443, England
2 CONT *'''Death: 	Nov., 1506  Calais, Departement du Pas-de-Calais, Nord-Pas-de-Calais, France
2 CONT *'''Knight of Calais, Standard Bearer to King Henry VII. Governor of Queensborough, Lieutenant of Calais. 
2 CONT *Fourth son of Sir Thomas Browne and Eleanor Arundel. Grandson of Sir Richard Browne, Sir Thomas Arundel and Joan Myone. 
2 CONT *'''Husband of Eleanor Ughtred''', the daughter of Sir Robert Ughtred and Katherine Eure. '''They married by licence dated 20 Jan 1488 at St Wilfrid's in Yorkshire, and had one daughter''', Anne, who married Sir Charles Brandon, D
2 CONC uke of Suffolk. 
2 CONT *'''Secondly, he married Lucy Neville''', daughter of Sir John Neville and Isabel Ingaldesthorpe and widow of Sir Thomas FitzWilliam who died in 1498. '''They had two sons and two daughters'''; Sir Anthony, Henry, Lucy, wife of Jo
2 CONC hn Cutts and Thomas Clifford, and Elizabeth, wife of Sir Henry Somerset. 
2 CONT *'''Sir Anthony left a will dated 25 Sept 1505 requesting burial at St Nicholas Church, Calais, next to his first wife. 
2 CONT *Family links: 
2 CONT * Spouses:
2 CONT *'''  Eleanor Ughtred Browne
2 CONT *'''  Lucy Neville Browne (____ - 1534)
2 CONT * Children:
2 CONT **  Anthony Browne (1500 - 1548)*
2 CONT *'''Burial:  St Nicholas Church, Calais, Departement du Pas-de-Calais, Nord-Pas-de-Calais, France
2 CONT *Find A Grave Memorial# 110997289
2 CONT *From: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=110997289 _________________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical and heraldic history of the extinct and dormant baronetcies ...    By John Burke, Sir Bernard Burke
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=LKIKAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA87&lpg=PA87&dq=George+Browne+1483&source=bl&ots=4TOUz1a9fJ&sig=hQxyWw8O05RE7JqgevDemme1ruY&hl=en&ei=rM_lS47ZC4L6sgP0n_XQCw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=2&ved=0CBkQ6AEwATg
2 CONC K#v=onepage&q=George%20Browne%201483&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 87
2 CONT *   SIR ANTHONY BROWNE, created a knight of the Bath at the coronation of RICHARD II. married, and had two sons, Sir Richard, his heir, and Stephen, lord mayor of London in 1430. The elder
2 CONT *   SIR ROBERT BROWNE, living temp. HENRY V. was father of
2 CONT *   SIR THOMAS BROWNE, treasurer of the household to HENRY VI. and sheriff of Kent in 1444 and 1460. He m. Eleanor, daughter and sole heir of Sir Thomas Fitz-Alan, of Beechworth Castle, brother of John, Earl of Arundel, and had is
2 CONC sue,
2 CONT **	I. GEORGE (Sir), his heir.
2 CONT **	II. William, whose son removed to Tavistock. This line is extinct.
2 CONT **	III. Robert (Sir), knt. m. Mary, daughter of Sir William Mallet, knt., and had an only daughter and heiress, Eleanor, wife, first, of Sir Thomas Fogge, and secondly, of Sir William Kempe.
2 CONT **'''	IV. Anthony (Sir), standard-bearer of England, esquire of the body, governor of Queenboro' Castle, and constable fo the castle of Calais. From this eminent person derive the LORDS MONTAGU, the heiress of which distinguished 
2 CONC family, the Hon. ELIZABETH MARY BROWN, wedded WILLIAM STEPHEN POYNTZ, esq. M.P. (See BURKE'S Extinct Peerage and Commoners.)
2 CONT **	I. Catherine, m. to Humphry Sackville of Buckhurst.
2 CONT *Sir Thomas was s. by his eldest son,
2 CONT *   SIR GEORGE BROWNE, knt. of Beechworth Castle, sheriff of Kent in 1481, who espousing the cause of the Earl of Richmond, was included in the proclamation for apprehending the Duke of Buckingham and his associates, and being soo
2 CONC n after taken, suffered decapitation in London anno 1483. he m. Elizabeth, daughter of Sir William Pason, and widow of Richard Lord Poynings, and was s. by his son,  .... etc.
2 CONT *Pg. 88
2 CONT *'''   SIR ANTHONY BROWNE, was appointed in the first year of King HENRY VII. standard-bearer for the whole realm of England and elsewhere; and the next year, being then one of the esquires of the king's body, was constituted gove
2 CONC rnor of Queenborough Castle, in Kent, in which year, being in the battle of Neward upon Trent, 16th June, when John de la Pole, Earl of Lincoln, and the pretender, Labert Simnell, sustained a signal defeat, he was knighted for his
2 CONC  valiant behaviour. In the eighteenth of the same reign, being constable of the castle of Calais, he and Sir Richard Nasan, deputy-lieutenant of Calais, were commissioned, in consideration of "their loyalty, industry, foresight, a
2 CONC nd car,"  to receive the sum of 25,000 franks in gold, due 1st November, 1502, bieng an annual payment from the French king, according to agreement concluded 3rd November, 1492; and again, in two years afterwards he was commission
2 CONC ed to receive the same payment. His last will and testament is dated at Calais, 25th September, 1505, ans was proved 19th November, 1506, wherein, being written, Sir Anthony Browne, knt. Lieutenant of the castle of Calais, he orde
2 CONC rs his body to be buried in the resurrection church, in St. Nicholas's Chapel, by his wife, and bequeathes to every brotherhood within the said church ten shillings, and to the lord prior of Christ Church, Canterbury, a standing c
2 CONC up of silver gilt; as also two others, to Sir Edward Poynings and Sir Hugh Conway, whom he sonsitutes overseers of his will, and Lucy, his wife, executrix, which Lucy was one of the daughters and co-heirs of John Nevil, MARQUESS O
2 CONC F MONTAGU, and widow of Sir Thomas FitzWilliams, of Aldwarke, in the county of York. By her he left, with two daughters, Elizabeth, the wife of Henry Somerset, Earl of Worcester, and Lucy, of Sir Thomas Clifford, knt. on only son,
2 CONC  his successor,
2 CONT *   SIR ANTHONY BROWNE, who was with the Earl of Surry, lord high admiral, at Southampton, in the fourteenth HENRY VIII. when he conveyed the Emperor from that port to Biscay; and after landing at Morleis, in Brittany, was knighte
2 CONC d for is valour in the assault and winning of that town. He m. Alice, daughter of Sir John Gage, K.G. and had issue, ______________
2 CONT *BROWNE, Sir Anthony (c.1500-48), of Battle Abbey and Cowdray Park, Suss.
2 CONT *b. c.1500, 1st s. of '''Sir Anthony Browne by Lucy, da. and coh. of John Neville, Marquess of Montagu'''; half-bro. of William Fitzwilliam, Earl of Southampton. m. (1) by 1528, Alice, da. of Sir John Gage of Firle, Suss., 7s. inc
2 CONC . Anthony I 3da.; (2) 1542, Elizabeth, da. of Gerald Fitzgerald, 9th Earl of Kildare, 2s.; 1s. 1da. illegit. suc. fa. 1506. Kntd. 1 July 1522; KG nom. 23 Apr., inst. 19 May 1540.3
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/browne-sir-anthony-1500-48 ______________________
2 CONT *BROWNE, Sir ANTHONY (d. 1548), politician, only son of '''Sir Anthony Browne, standard-bearer of England and constable of Calais, and of his wile Lady Lucy Nevill, daughter and coheiress of John Nevill, marquis Montacute, and nie
2 CONC ce of Richard, earl of Warwick''', was knighted in 1623 after the successful siege of Morlaix. In 1624 he was made esquire of the body to King Henry VIII, and from that time until the death of Henry he became more and more the fri
2 CONC end of his sovereign. In 1626 he was created lieutenant of the Isle of Man during the minority of Edward, earl of Derby. In 1628, and again in 1533, Browne was sent into France; on the first occasion to invest Francis I with the o
2 CONC rder of the Garter, and on the second to attend that king to Nice for the conference with the pope respecting the divorce of Henry VIII and Catherine of Arragon. In 1539 Browne was made master of the horse, and in 1540 he was crea
2 CONC ted a knight of the Garter. .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Browne,_Anthony_(d.1548)_(DNB00) _____________________
