2 CONT
2 CONT NICHOLAS BROWNE, son of EDWARD BROWNE and ?
2 CONT
2 CONT From the History of New Hampshire, p. 1560: (I) Nicholas Browne, son of Edward Browne, of Inkburrow, Worcestershire, England, settled first at Lynn, Massachusetts, and early removed from there to Reading, where he appears to have 
2 CONC owned two places. He was a man of comfortables means as appears from the fact of his sending his son John, in 1660, to England to look after certain property, to which he had become heir. He died in 1673. His wife's name was Eliza
2 CONC beth, and their children were, John, Edward, Joseph, Cornelius, Joshua and perhaps Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From A Genealogical Dictionary or The first Settlers of New England showing Three Generations or Those who came Before May, 1692 on the Basis of Farmer's Register by James Savage, Vol 1, p 272: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas, Lynn 1630, as Lewis Conjectured, but I think 7 years too early, son of Edward, of Inkberrow, 8 miles from Droitwich, Worcestershire; freeman 7 Sept. 1638, rep. 1641, removed to Reading 1644, and died April 5, 1673. His e
2 CONC ldest son John went in 1660 to England, but came back and was excor. of the will of 29 March 1673, made by his father pro. 17 June following in which with Elizabeth and other children Josiah; Edward born August 15, 1640; Elizabeth
2 CONC  and Joseph, 10 December 1647;possibly another Sarah, 26 June 1650 are mentioned
2 CONT
2 CONT "There is a lot of speculation on Thomas' father but this information was the best documented. He married Elizabeth Lyde in England and is supposed to have arrived in America in the spring of 1630 on one of the eleven ships in the
2 CONC  Winthrop fleet.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the problems in listing Thomas as Nicholas' son comes from the fact that some sources list him as being born in Lynn. Other sources, however, say that he was born in England and accompanied his father, Nicholas, to America 
2 CONC from England. He should not be confused with the Thomas Browne from Malford, weaver, who came over about 1635. Our Thomas would have been only 7 then. Thus Thomas, born in England, would not have been listed in the Essex Co, Mass.
2 CONC  records and could easily have been overlooked as a son of Nicholas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another problem is that when Nicholas inherited his maternal grandfather's property in England, "he gave power of attorney...to his eldest son John Browne bound for England to call William Rand to account for the shops, houses and
2 CONC  lands belonging to him as next heir of the Lides." Which Rand recovered by a former power of attorney from Nicholas Browne. Somehow John was named the eldest son. Maybe, an assumption on the clerk's part or to give him more lever
2 CONC age. Perhaps there was no elder son, Thomas, but then there is this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas and family were among 1700 people who sailed in the Winthrop fleet from Southampton in spring 1630. Nicholas and three other men from the fleet moved to the Lynn area, one of whom was Thomas Newhall, our Thomas Browne's f
2 CONC ather-in-law." - www.sound.net/~beckins/mason
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Acres/5626/dat3.htm#13 says b. 1601, Of,Inkburrow Parish,Worcestershire,England, d. 16 Nov 1694, Reading,Middx,Massachusetts,United States
2 CONT
2 CONT BALLARD, William, came in the Mary and John, March 26, 1634. William, husbandman, ae. 32, with [wife] Mary, ae. 26, [ch.] Hester, ae. 2, and John, ae. 1, came in the James in July, 1635. Mr. William appointed by the Gen. Court mag
2 CONC istrate at Salem in 1638. Propr. at Sal. and also at Lynn. His children had lands assigned them at Reading In 1644. Mr. William, of Lynn, failed to make a will, but told Nicholas Browne and Gerard Spencer, Jr., that he wished half
2 CONC  his est. to go to his wife, and the rest to be divided amongst his children. Test 1 (1) [1639]. [Suf. Prob.] John deposed 29 (4) 1655, ae. about 21 years, regarding his bro. Jenckes, in whose service he was. - Pioneers in Massach
2 CONC usetts, p.30;
2 CONT
2 CONT BROWN, BROWNE, Nicholas, mariner, sent power of attorney 24 (10) 1649 for collection of
2 CONT
2 CONT rents etc. at Moreton Underhill [Birtsmor-ton?] co. Worcester. [A.] - Pioneers in Massachusetts, p.72b [not sure this is my Nicholas - NPH]
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a Nicholas Browne who died in Dover in 1648. Perhaps this is the Nicholas Brown, son of the Pilgrim who some people confuse with my Nicholas Browne - NPH
