1 NAME John /Sackville/ Sheriff of Surrey & Sussex
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Sackville
2 NSFX Sheriff of Surrey & Sussex
2 _MARNM Sackville
1 NAME Sir John Sackville, Sheriff of Surrey & Sussex
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF 17 MAR 1484
2 PLAC Chiddingleigh, Sussex, England
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 27 SEP 1557
2 PLAC Withyham Chiddingley, Sussex, England
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE 5 OCT 1557
2 PLAC St.Michael church, Withyham, Sussex, England 
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Esquire, Sherriff of Surrey and Sussex
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.theweald.org/N10a.asp?NId=473 (source)
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sackville, Esq. MP (born before 17 March 1484 – 27 September 1557) was an English nobleman and Member of Parliament for East Grinstead. He was born in Sussex, England to Richard Sackville and Isabel Digges. He was married twi
2 CONC ce, first by the year 1507 to Margaret Boleyn, the daughter of Sir William Boleyn of Blickling and Anne Hoo. They had six children. His second marriage was to Anne Torrell, daughter of Humphrey Torrell of Willingale Doe and Alice 
2 CONC Leventhorpe. No children were born from his second marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children by Margaret Boleyn
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher Sackville (born about 1514) married Constance Colepepper 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard de Sackville (1516 – 21 April 1566) married Winifred Brydges 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Sackville (born about 1518) married Richard Ashburnham 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Sackville (born about 1521) married Nicholas Pelham 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Sackville (born about 1524 – 30 June 1571) married John Lunsford 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT John Sackville’s father held lands in both Sussex and Essex, but Sackville’s early domicile and public service in Essex probably arose from his marriage into the Boleyn family. From 1524, when he came into his inheritance, he live
2 CONC d in Sussex and his career was thereafter confined to that county and Surrey. The John Sackville of Calais, ‘late soldier, late of Withyham, Sussex’, pardoned in 1509, was his uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sackville’s return for a Sussex borough to the Parliament of 1542 followed immediately on the end of his second term as sheriff. The suggestion made in the Official Return that the borough was East Grinstead is borne out by the ap
2 CONC pearance among the electors’ names on the indenture concerned (which is fragmentary and lacks the name of the other Member) of one which is found on several later indentures for East Grinstead. It was there, too, that Sackville, a
2 CONC s the leading gentleman of the district, could have been expected to find a seat. The loss of returns leaves it in doubt whether Sackville had sat for the borough in 1539 or would do so again in 1545, but the shrievalty which kept
2 CONC  him out of the first Edwardian Parliament he doubtless used on behalf of his sons John II, who came in for East Grinstead, and Richard II, elected at Chichester, as well as for his son-in-law Nicholas Pelham, who sat for Arundel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apart from sharing with his brother-in-law Sir Thomas Boleyn in the presentation to a prebend of St. Stephen’s chapel, Westminster, Sackville seems to have derived no benefit from his first marriage, and he did little on his own a
2 CONC ccount to augment his inheritance. It is thus tempting to see behind some transactions of the 1540s the acquisitiveness for which his son Richard was to become notorious. In 1541 the pair bound themselves to perform an award made 
2 CONC by Thomas Bromley I and William Whorwood governing the manumission of one John Selwyn of Friston, Sussex, gentleman, whom they claimed as their bondman; a year later they sold to Selwyn for £6 13s.4d. the wardship and marriage of 
2 CONC the heir of John Bray of Westdean, Sussex, and the custody of the manor of Westdean which was held of the elder Sackville by knight’s service. Their acquisition in 1544 for over £900 of ex-monastic property in Surrey, Sussex, Lond
2 CONC on and elsewhere, and the profitable disposal of parts of it within the next two years, certainly foreshadowed Richard Sackville’s own operations in the land market.
2 CONT
2 CONT After making settlements of his property Sackville passed his closing years at Chiddingly, and it was there that he made his will on 1 July 1556. He bequeathed sums to the poor of five Sussex villages, and of Mount Bures in Essex,
2 CONC  and asked for candles at his funeral, a requiem mass for himself and prayers for his parents. His wife was to have the household goods at Chiddingly, with remainder to the three daughters, all his livestock, including sheep, whic
2 CONC h she could give ‘to those that she findeth the most friendship in’, and the contents of Buckhurst, the house at Withyham of which only the gatehouse survives. The executors were the widow and Sir Nicholas Pelham. Sackville died o
2 CONC n 27 Sept.1557 and was buried at Withyham on 5 Oct. The execution of the will was to be successfully challenged by Sir Richard Sackville who, for what reason is unknown, had not been mentioned in it; he was granted the administrat
2 CONC ion in October 1559.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1509-1558/member/sackville-john-i-1484-1557
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/194105754/john-sackville
