1 NAME Robert /Mansell/ Kt. MP
2 GIVN Robert
2 SURN Mansell
2 NSFX Kt. MP
2 _MARNM Mansell
2 NICK Sir Robert Mansell of Pentney and Norwich
2 NICK Norf. and Penrice
2 NICK Glam.
1 NAME Vice-Adm. Sir Robert Mansell, MP
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BET 1569 AND 1573
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1656
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Mansell_(Royal_Navy_officer)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert Mansel (1573–1656) was an admiral of the English Royal Navy and a Member of Parliament (MP), mostly for Welsh constituencies. His name was sometimes given as Sir Robert Mansfield and Sir Robert Maunsell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mansel was a Welshman, the son of Sir Edward Mansel of Penrice and Margam (died 1585), although he later established himself among the gentry of Norfolk. His early naval career is not recorded, but he served in the 1596 raid on Ca
2 CONC diz under the Earl of Essex, commanding HMS Vanguard, and was knighted for his part in it. He subsequently took part in Essex's Islands Voyage to the Azores (1597), then held commands off the Irish coast during Essex's campaign in
2 CONC  Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1600, Sir Robert's quarrel with a Norfolk neighbour, Sir John Heydon, ended in a notorious duel. Heydon's brother, Sir Christopher Heydon, was already noted for such affairs and in fact was at that moment detained in Lo
2 CONC ndon by the Privy Council to prevent him duelling with Sir John Townshend. The Council seem to have got wind of the dispute between Mansell and Heydon, and the Lord Chief Justice wrote to Sir Robert Cecil, urging him to forestall 
2 CONC it since the county was "already too much wrought into faction". But he was too late, and the fight took place outside Norwich: Heydon was badly wounded, and lost a hand (which is now, in a mummified state, on display in Norwich C
2 CONC astle Museum). Both Heydons were followers of the Earl of Essex, and took part in his rebellion in the following year. Mansell remained loyal to the Queen, and took an active part in arresting those implicated as accomplices.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1601, Mansel stood for Parliament as a candidate for Norfolk, the election having been delayed by the Essex Rebellion. He was defeated, perhaps because of disapproval of his duel, but was however elected as MP for King's Lynn a
2 CONC t the same election. He later served as member for Carmarthenshire (1604–14), Glamorgan (1624–1625 and 1628) and Lostwithiel (1626).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mansel was named Vice Admiral of the Narrow Seas in 1603 and became Treasurer of the Navy in 1604. He accompanied the Earl of Nottingham on his mission to Spain in 1605.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1609, his name appears on the Second Charter of Virginia dated May 23, 1609. As investor (London Company) and on the council.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1613, he was accused of political disaffection and imprisoned in the Marshalsea Prison, but was soon released and seems not to have remained in disfavour for long since he not only retained his offices but in 1615 obtained a mo
2 CONC nopoly on the manufacture of glass. He established glass factories in various places, including the first at Newcastle, pioneering the use of sea coal rather than wood in the manufacturing process, and there are records of his lat
2 CONC er defence of his patents in parliamentary debates.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1618, Mansel was appointed Vice-Admiral of England in 1618, and ceased to be Treasurer of the Navy. Contemporary papers suggest was not in fact intended as a promotion, and that he was deliberately moved to a less influential p
2 CONC osition because of suspicions of his dishonest administration as treasurer. Nevertheless, he apparently retained royal favour, even after the failure of the expedition he led against the pirates of Algiers in 1621.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1620, his name appears on the Charter of New England Nov 3, 1620. As investor (Plymouth Company) and on the council.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert married twice: first to Elizabeth Bacon, daughter of Sir Nicholas Bacon, the Lord Keeper of the Great Seal; and secondly, in 1617, to Anne Roper, daughter of Sir John Roper. He had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1656.
2 CONT
2 CONT Honors
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1613, Mansel Island in Nunavut, Canada was named in his honor by Sir Thomas Button.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/mansell-sir-robert-1569-1656<br/>
2 CONT >"b. c.1569, 4th s. of (Sir) Edward Mansell† of Margam, Glam. by Lady Jane Somerset, da. of Henry, 2nd Earl of Worcester; bro. of Sir Thomas. educ. Brasenose, Oxf. 1587. m. (1) c.1593, Elizabeth, da. of (Sir) Nicholas Bacon†, wid.
2 CONC  of Francis Wyndham, s.p.; (2) 1617, Anne, da. of Sir John Roper, s.p. Kntd. on Cadiz voyage 1596."<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
