1 NAME Alice Kempe
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC St. Michael's Chapel, Canterbury Cathedral
2 ADDR
3 CITY Canterbury
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1592
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The 'Lady Hales’ to whom Robert Greene dedicated 'Menaphon' in 1589. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Hales
2 CONT
2 CONT This striking memorial on the north wall of the nave relates to four members of the influential Hales family in Kent.
2 CONT
2 CONT What to see:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir James Hales (grandfather of Sir James Hales mentioned below) drowned himself in the River Stour in 1555 in old age following imprisonment and torture in the Fleet prison - the painting backdrop to the central panel of the memo
2 CONC rial provides an overview of the Stour at Thannington where this took place (writers have disputed  whether the square towered church is St Mildred's or Thannington) 
2 CONT
2 CONT the grandson, another Sir James Hales, died at sea following the attack on Cadiz in 1589, a reprisal for the Spanish Armada attack the year before - he is shown here as an armed figure being lowered into the water (Image 2 - the o
2 CONC nly example of such a scene in English cathedrals) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice (Image 3) the wife of the grandson kneels at a priedieu or prayer desk in front of (unfortunately obscuring the painted overview of the Stour) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cheyney Hales, son of the above, survived his father by only 7 years - he appears in the lowest panel of the memorial as a young Elizabethan gallant 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that Alice and Cheyney face west (those at prayer in church memorials normally face east) but there is evidence that the entire memorial was once located in St Michael's chapel 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.canterbury-archaeology.org.uk/hales/4590809667
