1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ST. GERMANS1593Family and Education b. 1542, 2nd or 3rd s. of John Glanville, merchant of Tavistock, by Thomasine, da. of John Brown. educ. L. Inn 1567, called 1574. m. Alice, da. of John Skerret of Tavistock, 7ch.
2 CONC  inc. John, Speaker of House of Commons in Apr. 1640.
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT J.p.q. Cornw., Devon from c.1583; Lent and Autumn reader, L. Inn 1589; serjeant-at-law 1589; recorder, Launceston 1590; justice of the common pleas from 1598.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography According to tradition Glanville began his legal career as an attorney and was the first of that branch of the profession to reach the bench. He was a servant of the Russell family, and the inference is that it was the 2
2 CONC nd Earl of Bedford who obtained him his first parliamentary seat. As the son of a local merchant, known to the Russells, Glanville would have had his own claim at Tavistock in 1586. During this Parliament he was appointed to a com
2 CONC mittee on the continuation of certain statutes (17 Mar. 1587), the only mention of him in the surviving records of the House. By 1592 he was being employed by Sir Walter Ralegh in stannary business. He was interested in his own ri
2 CONC ght in the St. Margaret’s tin works in Cornwall, and probably owned property near St. Germans, for which he was returned in the 1593 Parliament, possibly assisted by Ralegh. In 1599 the 3rd Earl of Bedford addressed him as ‘my ver
2 CONC y good trusty and loving friend’. While on circuit 27 July 1600 Glanville fell from his horse and with ‘the unwieldy weight of his own body toppled down’ head first, breaking his neck. Administration of his large estate was grante
2 CONC d to his widow on 8 Aug. of that year, and an inquisition post mortem was taken at Exeter 12 Aug. 1602. His heir was his son Francis, aged 14 in 1602. His widow married Sir Francis Godolphin.
2 CONT
2 CONT DNB; Foss, Judges, v. 494; Devon RO, q. sess. order bk. 1592-1600, p. 216; Trans. Dev. Assoc. viii. 381; C. S. Gilbert, Hist. Surv. Cornw. ii. 412; Bodl. Tanner 115, f. 195; PCC admon. act bk. 1600, f. 57; C142/271/158; D’Ewes, 41
2 CONC 6; N. Pevsner, South Devon, 277. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1558-1603 Author: P. W. Hasler Notes
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir John Glanville of Tavistock.   He was one of the Justices of the Common Pleas in 1598, during the reign of Queen Anne.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father's will proved Feb. 1, 1580.
2 CONT
2 CONT Photo above at Tavistock Church with wife Alice
2 CONT
2 CONT Devon, Tavistock  1542-1600 Sir John Glanville in judge's robes, with wife Alice 1632 daughter of John Skerret / Skirret of Tavistock with headless 3 sons and 4 daughters; John was the 3rd son of John Glanville, merchant of Tavist
2 CONC ock, by Thomasine daughter of John Brown. John began his legal career as an attorney but joined Lincoln's Inn in 1567 and was called to the bar in 1574: his practice proved lucrative and he amassed a considerable fortune, building
2 CONC  KIlworth House. He became a serjeant-at-law in 1589, He was a servant of the Russell family and the 2nd Earl of Bedford could have obtained him his first parliamentary seat. . By 1592 he was employed by Sir Walter Ralegh in stann
2 CONC ary business. He also had interests in his own right in the St. Margaret’s tin works in Cornwall, and probably owned property near St. Germans for which he was returned in the 1593 Parliament, possibly assisted by Ralegh. In 1599 
2 CONC the 3rd Earl of Bedford addressed him as ‘my very good trusty and loving friend’. The family were formerly from Hallwell House Whitchurch and moved here to Kilworthy near here. While on circuit 27 July 1600 Glanville fell from his
2 CONC  horse and with ‘the unwieldy weight of his own body toppled down’ head first, breaking his neck. Administration of his large estate being granted to his widow. . Children 1. Francis b1588 m Elizabeth Crymes of Buckland Monachorum
2 CONC  2. Sir John Glanville the younger 1661 thecheapsidestandard.wordpress.com/tag/sir-john-glanvill/ Speaker of the House of Commons in 1640 in the Short Parliament afterwards imprisoned in the Tower and heavily fined for treason aga
2 CONC inst the parliamentarians. which might account for the "beheadings" He m Winifred daughter of William Bourchier of Barnsley Glos (son Francis killed in Civil War is at Broad Hinton www.flickr.com/photos/52219527@N00/11869233543/ )
2 CONC  3. Thomas 1. Mary m Sir Edward Escourt of Salisbury 2. Dionilla / Dionilia m Thomas Polewheele 3. Alice d1608 died unmarried 4. Joan m Sampson Hele, MP of Gatton High Sheriff of Devon 1621
2 CONT
2 CONT Alice m2 Sir Francis Godolphin on Christmas Day 1600
2 CONT
2 CONT *' Honorats sacrum memoriv Johannis Glanvil unius quondam JusticSaromm de Communi Banco. Qui merito fkctaa judex summo cum labore adflsinistrafit Justiciam; Justicia conservavit Paoem} Pace expectavit Mortem ; et Morta inveoit Req
2 CONC uiem, 27 Julii, Ann. Dom.1600 '' Statum erat hoc monumentum, Ann. Dom. 1615. Tempensis Dominae Aucmb Godolphin viduse, prihs uxoris ejusdem Johannis Glanvil, renuptse vero Franciso Godolphin militi jam etiam defimeco. Quae peperit
2 CONC  fidem Johanni viro mo et septem liberos.* "I. Maria defuncta nupta Edwardo Estcourt Armigero postea militi. 2. FrancisGus qui duxit in uxorem Elizabetham filiam Wiilelmi Grymes Armigeri. 3 Dionisia nupta Thomas Polewheele Armiger
2 CONC o. 4. Johannes qui duxit in uxorem Winifredam fiiliam Wiilelmi Burchier Armigeri. 5. Alicia defuncta innupta. 6. Johanna nupta Samson Hele. 7. Thomas.'*
2 CONT
