1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00058201&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry Wriothesley, 3rd Earl of Southampton''' (6 October 1573 – 10 November 1624), was the second son of Henry Wriothesley, 2nd Earl of Southampton, and his wife Mary Browne, Countess of Southampton, daughter of the 1st Viscoun
2 CONC t Montagu. Shakespeare's first two narrative poems, Venus and Adonis and The Rape of Lucrece, were dedicated to Southampton, who is one of the major candidates to be the "Fair Youth" in Shakespeare's Sonnets.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a patron of William Shakespeare
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Wriothesley was born in Cowdray House, Sussex, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father died he moved to the nearby town of Midhurst, England, and inherited the Earldom in 1581 when he became a royal ward under the immediate care of Lord Burghley. He entered St John's College, Cambridge, in 1585, grad
2 CONC uating M.A. in 1589, and his name was entered at Gray's Inn before he left the university. At the age of 17 he was presented at court where he was soon counted among the friends of the Earl of Essex, and was distinguished by extra
2 CONC ordinary marks of the Queen's favour. He became a munificent patron of poets: Nashe dedicated his romance of Jack Willon to him and Gervase Markham his poem on Sir Richard Grenville's last fight. His name is also associated with B
2 CONC arnabe Barnes's Parthenophil and Parthenope, and with the Worlde of Wordes of John Florio, who was for some years in his personal service as teacher of Italian.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1624 Southampton was one of four Englishmen appointed to command English troops fighting in the Low Countries against the Spanish. Shortly after their arrival, James, the earl's eldest son, succumbed to a fever at Rosendael; fi
2 CONC ve days later on 10 November 1624, Southampton died of the same cause at Bergen-op-Zoom. Both were buried in the parish church of Titchfield, Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Wriothesley,_3rd_Earl_of_Southampton
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8J56-6Q ===
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8J56-6Q
2 CONT ===  A favourite of Elizabeth I and patron o ===
2 CONT A favourite of Elizabeth I and patron of Shakespeare, who dedicated Venus and Adonis to him (1593), Southampton's tempestuous relationship with the Queen culminated in his involvement in Essex's rebellion in 1601. Condemned to dea
2 CONC th when the rebellion failed, his punishment was commuted to life imprisonment and he was released by James I. National Portrait Gallery
2 CONT === Ancestral  File L.D.S.  Pedigree  Resour ===
2 CONT Ancestral  File L.D.S.  Pedigree  Resource  Disc 6 IGI  v5.0 .
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Wriothesley,_3rd_Earl_of_Southampton
2 CONT === Probably the "lovely boy" immortalized i ===
2 CONT Shakespeare's sonnets and some characters were written to/about Henry, 3rd Earl, not his father.  Apparently Shakespeare was in love with 3rd Earl.  For an interesting and collaborated account, see https://theshakespearecode.wordp
2 CONC ress.com/2012/05/15/just-how-gay-was-henry-wriothesley-the-third-earl-of-southampton/ confirms this.
2 CONT === !#21-v12pt1-p128-130*,676; #218-v1-p199; ===
