1 NAME Henry O'Brien, 5th Earl of Thomond
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1588
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 22 APR 1639
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Enumeration Note:''' ''The Complete Peerage'', Vol. 12, Pt. 2 (p. 862) makes reference to this Earl for the 2nd marriage of Robert Dormer, 1st Earl of Carnarvon, stating RE marriage in—
2 CONT >"Aug. 1639, Margaret (dowry £20,000),(3) 2nd da. and coh. of Henry (O’BRIEN), 4th EARL OF THOMOND [L]," 
2 CONT TCP's enumeration must be an error. When one searches the ''Dictionary of Irish Biography'' website, it tags [https://www.libraryireland.com/biography/DonoughOBrien4thEarlofThomond.php O'Brien, Donough] as 4th Earl of Thomond (the
2 CONC  "Great Earl").
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''From [https://www.dib.ie/biography/obrien-barnabas-bryan-barnaby-a6452#co-subject-A Dictionary of Irish Biography] contributor Bernadette Cunningham:—'''
2 CONT >"'''Henry O'Brien (d. 1639), 5th earl of Thomond''', nobleman, was educated at Eton College and TCD, as well as Brasenose College, Oxford. He was appointed governor of Clare on 19 March 1616, when his father, Donough, resigned th
2 CONC e post in his favour. This office was a device to exempt the earldom of Thomond from the jurisdiction of the provincial presidency. He became commissioner for Munster in the absence of his father in 1618 and again from 19 Septembe
2 CONC r 1624 to May 1625 following the death of his father. In contrast to his brother, Barnabas, he probably enjoyed some of the best of times at Bunratty castle, where he resided as earl of Thomond from 1624 to his death in 1639: thes
2 CONC e were years of relative peace and prosperity. The Dublin administration approved of Henry's ‘honesty and ability and soundness in religion’ (CSPI, 1611–14, 119). His loyalty was rewarded in the 1630s when Sir Francis Aungier (qv)
2 CONC  advised Lord Deputy Wentworth to proceed with a plantation of Thomond, while ensuring ‘that nothing should be done therein to the prejudice of the noble earl of Thomond, nor his brother Sir Barnaby Brien, being both good and firm
2 CONC  protestants, and all their children’ (letter book 1, June 1632, Strafford papers, Sheffield Archives). The 5th earl enjoyed Wentworth's favour, and in return left him a gift to the value of £100 in his will. Thomond was appointed
2 CONC  to the Irish privy council in September 1633, though his public career was largely ineffective.
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >"Henry married Mary, daughter of Sir William Brereton, on 13 July 1608. They had five daughters, Mary, Elizabeth, Margaret, Ann, and Honora, for whom the 5th earl strove to arrange suitable marriages, financed by mortgaging exten
2 CONC sive portions of the Thomond estate for immediate cash gain. Having no male heir, Henry provided generously for his daughters in his will, drawn up three weeks before his death. Thus, when his brother, Barnabas, succeeded to the e
2 CONC arldom in 1639 he was left with a heavily indebted estate, and the outbreak of rebellion in 1641 meant that it could not meet all of the financial commitments that had been entered into. Henry O'Brien, 5th earl of Thomond, was bur
2 CONC ied in late April 1639 in the elaborate tomb he had erected for his father in Limerick cathedral, after a funeral that he had stipulated should be ‘without any vain ostentation’.
2 CONT > 
2 CONT > '''Webpage Sources:''' Letter book 1, Strafford papers, Sheffield Archives; Thomas Westropp, ‘Notes on Clare’, RIA, MS 3 A 40, f. 161; CSPI, 1611–14; John O'Donoghue, Historical memoir of the O'Briens (1860); G.E.C., Peerage; Do
2 CONC nough O'Brien, History of the O'Briens from Brian Boroimhe, AD 1000 to AD 1945 (1949); Hugh F. Kearney, Strafford in Ireland, 1633–1641: a study in absolutism (1959); Bernadette Cunningham, ‘Political and social change in the lord
2 CONC ships of Clanricard and Thomond’ (MA thesis, NUI, 1977); Brian Ó Dálaigh, ‘A comparative study of the wills of the first and fourth earls of Thomond’, North Munster Antiquarian Journal, xxxiv (1992), 48–63; Brian Ó Dálaigh, ‘An in
2 CONC ventory of the contents of Bunratty castle and the will of Henry, fifth earl of Thomond, 1639’, North Munster Antiquarian Journal, xxxvi (1995), 139–65; Clodagh Tait, ‘The earl and the bishop: further light on the Thomond and O'De
2 CONC a monuments in St Mary's cathedral, Limerick’, North Munster Antiquarian Journal, xlii (2002), 7–9; ODNB
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>————————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ===='Henry O'Brien, 5th Earl of Thomond
2 CONT '''Henry O'Brien, 5th Earl of Thomond (1588 – 26 July 1639) was an Irish peer, styled Lord Ibrickane until 1624.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''O'Brien was the son''' of Donogh O'Brien, 4th Earl of Thomond and Elisabeth FitzGerald. '''He married Mary Brereton''', daughter of William Brereton, 1st Baron Brereton and Margaret Savage, and '''together they had 5 daughters:
2 CONC '''
2 CONT * Lady Margaret O'Brien
2 CONT * Lady Mary O'Brien
2 CONT * Lady Elisabeth O'Brien
2 CONT * Lady Anne O'Brien
2 CONT * Lady Honora O'Brien
2 CONT
2 CONT '''O'Brien died in 1639, and was succeeded in his peerages by his brother Barnabas O'Brien, 6th Earl of Thomond.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_O%27Brien,_5th_Earl_of_Thomond
2 CONT _______________________________________
2 CONT *'''Henry O'Brien, 4th Earl of Thomond1
2 CONT *'''M, #24656, b. circa 1588, d. before 22 April 1639
2 CONT *Last Edited=22 Oct 2012
2 CONT *'''     Henry O'Brien, 4th Earl of Thomond was born circa 1588.2 He was the son of Donough O'Brien, 3rd Earl of Thomond and Lady Elizabeth FitzGerald.3 He married Mary Brereton, daughter of William Brereton, 1st Baron Brereton of
2 CONC  Leighlin and Margaret Savage, on 13 July 1608 at Brereton, Cheshire, England.2 He died before 22 April 1639.2
2 CONT *'''     He was educated at Eton College, Windsor, Berkshire, England.2 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) [Ireland].2 He was educated at Brasenose College, Oxford University, Oxford, Oxfordshire, England.2 He succeeded 
2 CONC to the title of 4th Baron of Ibracken [I., 1543] on 31 March 1613, in his father's lifetime.2 He succeeded to the title of 4th Earl of Thomond [I., 1552] on 5 September 1624.2
2 CONT *'''Children of Henry O'Brien, 4th Earl of Thomond and Mary Brereton
2 CONT ** 1.Lady Margaret O'Brien1 d. c 26 Jul 1681
2 CONT ** 2.Lady Mary O'Brien+2 d. 1686
2 CONT ** 3.Lady Elizabeth O'Brien2 d. bt 9 Apr 1656 - 30 Jun 1659
2 CONT ** 4.Lady Anne O'Brien+3 d. c 13 Sep 1644
2 CONT ** 5.Lady Honora O'Brien4
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume XII/2, page 862. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT * 2.[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 2033. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT * 3.[S37] BP2003. [S37]
2 CONT * 4.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume XII/2, page 492.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p2466.htm#i24656 ___________________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical history of the dormant, abeyant, forfeited, and extinct peerages of the British empire (1978)
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/agenealogicalhi00burkgoog
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/agenealogicalhi00burkgoog#page/n95/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.75
2 CONT *   SIR WILLIAM BRERETON, of Brereton, Knt., bapt. 6 February, 1550, who built the Hall of Brereton in 1586, and was created Baron Brereton, of Leighlin, co. Carlow, 11 May, 1624. His lordship m. Margaret, dau. of Sir John Savage,
2 CONC  Knt., by Elizabeth his wife, dau. of the Earl of Rutland, and by her (who d. 7 April, 1597,) had issue, besides other children, who d. young,
2 CONT **	I. John (Sir), of Brereton, b. 25 March, 1591, m. Anne. Dau. of Sir Edward Fitton, of awsworth, and dying in the lifetime of his father, left by her (who m. 2ndly, Sir Gilbert Gerard, Knt.) three sons and two daus., viz.,
2 CONT ***		1 .... etc.
2 CONT **'''	I. Mary, m. 13 July, 1608, to Henry, Lord Inchiquin.
2 CONT *Lord Brereton d. in 1631, and was s. by his grandson, .... etc. ______________________________
2 CONT *	Brereton, Baron (I, 1624 - 1722)
2 CONT *William [Brereton], 1st Baron Brereton
2 CONT *son and heir of Sir William Brereton, of Brereton Hall, co. Chester, by his wife Jane Warburton, dau. of Sir Peter Warburton, of Warburton, co. Chester (by his wife Elizabeth Winnington, dau. of Richard Winnington, of Winnington,
2 CONC  co. Chester), son and heir of Sir John Warburton, of Warburton, co. Chester, by his wife Jane Stanley, dau. of Sir William Stanley KG, of Holt, Wrexham, Chamberlain to King Henry VII (by his second wife Elizabeth Hopton, dau. of 
2 CONC Thomas Hopton, of Hopton), brother of Thomas [Stanley], 1st Earl of Derby, and 2nd son of Thomas [Stanley], 1st Baron Stanley
2 CONT *bapt.   6 Feb 1550
2 CONT *mar.     Margaret Savage (b. 1549; d. 7 Apr 1597; bur. at Brereton, co. Chester), dau. of Sir John Savage, of Rocksavage, co. Chester, by his wife Lady Elizabeth Manners, 5th dau. by his second wife of Thomas [Manners], 1st Earl 
2 CONC of Rutland
2 CONT *children
2 CONT **	1. .... etc.
2 CONT **'''	2. Hon Mary Brereton (bapt. 28 Dec 1580; dspm. betw. 21 Apr and 2 May 1640), mar. 13 Jul 1608 Henry [O'Brien], 4th Earl of Thomond, and had issue
2 CONT *died   1 Oct 1631 (bur. at Brereton, co. Chester)
2 CONT *created   11 May 1624 Baron Brereton, of Laghlin in the County of Carlow
2 CONT *suc. by   grandson
2 CONT *note     built "the Hall" at Brereton about 1586; knighted at Flushing by the Earl of Leicester 1588
2 CONT *From: http://www.cracroftspeerage.co.uk/online/	- SEARCH BRERETON __________________________
2 CONT *	Thomond, Earl of (I, 1543 - 1552 & 1552 - 1741)
2 CONT *Donough [O'Brien], 3rd Earl of Thomond, PC
2 CONT *born
2 CONT *mar. (1)     aft. Sep 1577 Hon Helen Roche (dspm. 1583; bur. in Ennis Abbey), 1st dau. of Maurice [Roche], 1st Viscount Fermoy, by his first wife Eleanor FitzGerald, 1st dau. of Maurice FitzJohn FitzGerald, Lord of Kerricurrihy (
2 CONC by his wife Sile O'Mulrian, dau. of Diarmid O'Mulrian, of Sulloghode, co. Tipperary), brother of James FitzJohn [FitzGerald], 13th Earl of Desmond
2 CONT *only child by first wife
2 CONT **	1. Lady Margaret O'Brien (d. bef. c. 1599), mar. c. 1590 as his first wife Charles/Cormac Oge [McCarthy], 1st Viscount Muskerry, and had issue
2 CONT *mar. (2)     aft. 6 Nov 1585 Lady Elizabeth FitzGerald (d. 12 Jan 1617), dau. of Gerald [FitzGerald], 11th Earl of Kildare, by his wife Mabel Browne, sister of Anthony [Browne], 1st Viscount Montagu, and 2nd dau. of Sir Anthony B
2 CONC rowne KG, Master of the Horse
2 CONT *children by second wife
2 CONT **'''	1. Hon Henry O'Brien, later 4th Baron Ibrackan later 4th Earl of Thomond
2 CONT **	2. Hon Barnabas O'Brien, later 5th Earl of Thomond
2 CONT *died    5 Sep 1624 (bur. in Limerick Cathedral)
2 CONT *suc. by   son by second wife
2 CONT *note     called "The Great Earl";  like his father continued to support successive Lords Deputy and the Earl of Ormond, and took a prominent part in the campaign against the Irish "rebels" and in the pacification of Ireland; Memb
2 CONC er of the Council of Connaught and Thomond 1588, and of Munster 1599; granted a pension of £200 for life by Queen Elizabeth I 1589; Commander under Sir Thomas Norris of the forces in Munster 1598; Col of a Regiment of Foot 1599; G
2 CONC overnor of co. Clare and of Thomond 1599 and re-appointed by King James I 1603; Privy Councillor [I] 1603; Constable (with his second son, Barnabas) of Carlow Castle 1604; Commissioner for the President of Munster 1607 and for the
2 CONC  Plantation of Ulster 1610; President of Munster 1615-24
2 CONT *
2 CONT *'''Henry [O'Brien], 4th Baron Ibrackan later 4th Earl of Thomond, PC
2 CONT *'''born   c. 1588
