1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The magnificient alabaster tomb is that of Sir John Shurly and his two wives, Jane and Dorothy. The nine childrens effeigies arranged in the front of the tomb are the echildren born to Jane and John Shurley. All of
2 CONC  the figures of this tomb are delicately and attractively carved. Sir John Shurley is arranged in the seme-armour of the period. Lady Jane is on his right hand and has a close fitting cap, Lady Dorothy on his left and has a hood w
2 CONC ith long veil flowing down to hre feet. Both have farthingales and ruff. The daughters have stiff lace collars, puffed and slashed sleeves, ruffles, no neck ruff and hair in ringlets with a band. Of the children, Thomas, John, Cac
2 CONC elie and Hannah died young. 
2 CONT
