1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =='''Thomas Scott (died 1610)
2 CONT '''Thomas Scott (c.1563-1610), of Scot's Hall, Smeeth, Kent, was an English politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He married Elizabeth Honywood.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He was a Member (MP) of the Parliament of England for Aylesbury in 1586.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Scott_(died_1610)
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Thomas Scot1
2 CONT *M, #148294
2 CONT *Last Edited=6 Jun 2005
2 CONT *'''     Sir Thomas Scot lived at Scot's Hall, Kent, England.1
2 CONT *'''Children of Sir Thomas Scot
2 CONT ** 1.Elizabeth Scot1
2 CONT ** 2.Anne Scot+1 b. b 1569
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S37] BP2003 volume 1, page 476. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p14830.htm#i148294 __________________
2 CONT *'''SCOTT, Thomas (c.1563-1610), of Scot's Hall, Smeeth, Kent.
2 CONT *'''b. c.1563, 1st s.''' of Sir Thomas Scott of Scot’s Hall by his 1st w. Elizabeth, da. of Sir John Baker†, of Sissinghurst, Kent; '''bro. of''' Edward Scott and Sir John Scott. '''educ. Hart Hall, Oxf. 1580. m. (1) Mary, da. of 
2 CONC John Knatchbull of Mersham, 1s. d.v.p.; (2) bef. 1587, Elizabeth, da. and h. of Thomas Honywood of Sene, Newington, s.p. suc. fa. 30 Dec. 1594.1
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **'''Capt. of lancers, Northbourne camp, 1588, 1589; j.p.q. Kent from c.1596, sheriff 1601-2; commr. survey crown lands in Kent 1608.2
2 CONT *'''Scott was brought into Parliament for Aylesbury by his father, who was knight of the shire for Kent in 1586. Sir Thomas had already tried to secure him a parliamentary seat at Hythe in 1581. That it was the son who sat for Ayl
2 CONC esbury in 1586 is clear from D’Ewes, 8 Mar. 1587: ‘Sir Thomas Scott and his son have leave to depart’. The Scotts were related by marriage to Thomas Smythe I, the customer of London, who himself represented Aylesbury, as did two o
2 CONC f his sons. Thomas Sackville, the 1563 Aylesbury MP, was also a relative.3
2 CONT *For the eldest son of a leading Kent landowner, surprisingly little is known about Scott, the absence of whose name from national and local records is all the more surprising when the active lives of his younger brothers are comp
2 CONC aratively well documented. Several of them married into important families; two were knighted and sat for Kent in Parliament. That Sir John Scott, the second son, was knight of the shire and deputy lieutenant while his elder broth
2 CONC er was still alive and head of the family, testifies to the importance of the Scotts in Kent and, at the same time, accentuates Thomas’s minor role. He had no military experience, was not knighted, performed few services for the c
2 CONC ounty (though holding the shrievalty once), and neither of his two wives belonged to families which were his social equal or the equal of those into which his brothers married. The reason for this undistinguished obscurity is matt
2 CONC er for speculation. Perhaps, as a letter from a Kent neighbour, Thomas Wotton, suggests, efforts had been made to estrange Sir Thomas Scott from his eldest son, though if so they were reconciled by the time of Sir Thomas’s death. 
2 CONC Thomas, his principal executor and residuary legatee, inherited seven manors, his seat at Scot’s Hall and all his goods. In 1599 Thomas leased three manors to Anthony St. Leger and Richard Smythe, and three years later made a simi
2 CONC lar grant to Thomas Honywood and John Gibbon. The lands were to revert to Thomas’s younger brothers, John and Edward, and their families. Scott was one of those appointed, in September 1608, to help the lord lieutenant, Edward, Lo
2 CONC rd Wotton, to survey the Crown’s lands in Kent. Earlier, he had acted as trustee for the lands of his brother, Sir John Scott, when he was abroad in the army.4
2 CONT *'''Scott’s own military service was limited to Kent. In 1588 and in the following year he served as a captain of lancers in the army assembled at Northbourne, near Dover, under his father’s command, to resist any Spanish landing.
2 CONC  Later, he appears from time to time among the officers for the county musters, and he helped to supply men to watch the coast at Romney Marsh. In 1604 he attended upon the lord lieutenant when he welcomed the constable of Spain.5
2 CONT *'''Scott died on 24 Sept. 1610,6 leaving his brother''' John as heir: '''the last known reference to him appears to be in September 1608. No will has been found, but a reference in an indenture reveals that he made one. This ment
2 CONC ions that he left £2,000 to his niece''', Elizabeth Scott, and '''his widow was to be looked after by''' Sir John; on the latter’s death, which occurred in 1616, she was to receive a life annuity of £240. She died in May 1627 and 
2 CONC was buried in Brabourne church. '''By that time the lord of Scot’s Hall was Scott’s younger brother''' Sir Edward.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/scott-thomas-1563-1610 _____________________________
2 CONT *SCOTT, Sir Thomas (c.1535-94), of Scot's Hall, Smeeth, Kent.
2 CONT *b. c.1535,1 1st s. of Sir Reginald Scott of Scot’s Hall by Emmeline, da. of Sir William Kempe of Olantigh, Wye. educ. I. Temple Nov. 1554. m. (1) Elizabeth, da. of Sir John Baker of Sissinghurst, 17 ch. at least 11s. inc. '''Thom
2 CONC as'''; (2) Elizabeth, da. of Ralph Heyman of Somerfield House, Sellinge, s.p.; (3) Dorothy, da. of John Bere of Horsman’s Place, Dartford, s.p. suc. fa. 1554. Kntd. 1570.2
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **J.p. Kent from c.1561, q. by 1571, commr. piracy 1565, grain by 1573, sheriff 1576-7, dep. lt. by 1582; commr. piracy Cinque Ports in Suss. 1578; superintendent of works, Dover harbour 1580; col.-gen. of Kent forces 1588, 1589.3
2 CONT *Scott’s family had owned estates in Kent since the fourteenth century and had lived in style at Scot’s Hall since the reign of Henry VI. Contemporary sources contain many examples of his wealth and hospitality, and describe his l
2 CONC ife style in terms usually associated with the great medieval barons. He was related to Leicester and corresponded with many leading statesmen. With him his family, perhaps, reached the highest point of their history.4
2 CONT *Barely a month after his entry into the Inner Temple he heard of the death of his father, who left him the bulk of his property consisting of 30 manors centred on Brabourne and Smeeth near Ashford. Scott was still under 20, but a
2 CONC  marriage had already been arranged with the daughter of one of their wealthiest neighbours, and Sir John Baker, his future father-in-law, may have acquired his wardship as well. In May 1556 he came of age and entered into his inh
2 CONC eritance. From that date until his death 38 years later, he is said to have held sway over a part of Kent like a reigning monarch.5
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *Scott died 30 Dec. 1594, aged about 59. His will was proved on 7 Jan. 1595. If his mode of living was as luxurious as writers suggest, it was still within his financial resources. He left part of his household goods and several p
2 CONC ortions of lands, including the manor of Thevegate, to his wife, who had to surrender her jointure, Nettlestead, to the second son, John. '''The bulk of the estates went to the eldest son, Thomas, the new lord of Scot’s Hall'''. T
2 CONC he other surviving sons were remembered, either by grants of land or by annuities; even grandchildren find their place in the will. There were no charitable bequests, in contrast with his generosity while still alive, though his w
2 CONC ife’s maidservant was singled out and given £5 ‘for the pains she hath taken in the times of my sickness’. The executors were his sons '''Thomas''' and Sir John, and his brother Charles. Lord Buckhurst, a relative, acted as overse
2 CONC er, for which he was paid £40. The will ends with a list of the household items at Scot’s Hall which the widow could remove, and a request to the executors to complete the buildings at Thevegate, where she was to live.13
2 CONT *Scott was buried with his ancestors in Brabourne church, despite a plea by the citizens of Ashford that he might be laid to rest in the chancel of their church, free of all charges. His tomb, according to local tradition, was des
2 CONC ecrated by Parliamentarians in the civil war. No trace of it remains in the church, but in what was formerly the chapel of Scot’s Hall itself a mural slab was found in the nineteenth century, bearing the words: ‘Here lies all that
2 CONC  is mortal of Sir Thomas Scott’. Perhaps his body was transferred there after the civil war. Three of his sons succeeded him in the possession of Scot’s Hall, but its great days died with him.14
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/scott-sir-thomas-1535-94 _________________
