3 CITY Boughton Aluph
3 STAE England
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} No connection whatsoever with the Scotts of Rattlesden, Suffolk.
2 CONT
2 CONT No proof of marriage.  (He certainly did not marry a daugher of William Howard, 1st Baron Howard of Effingham, nor did he marry a daughter of Charles Howard, 1st Earl of Nottingham.)
2 CONT
2 CONT No proof of his alleged knighthood.
2 CONT
2 CONT No proof of his alleged ambassadorship.
2 CONT
2 CONT ***************
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Scott probably did have a son named William, but as no records have been found of his adult life, William may have died as an infant.
2 CONT
2 CONT What appears to be his baptismal record may actually belong to his cousin, the barrister and author William Scott  (c.1571–c.1617), son of Charles Scott and Jane Wyatt:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The baptism of a William Scott on 20 April 1571 is recorded in the parish register (and the Bishop’s transcript of the same) for Boughton Aluph parish, [and] described as a son of Sir Thomas Scott.  [8] There is no other record o
2 CONC f a son of Sir Thomas Scott named William,[9] and that this William should be baptised in Boughton Aluph is perplexing, since it is close to where [Thomas's half-brother] Charles Scott was living at this time (it is the parish bet
2 CONC ween Challock and Godmersham) and rather further from Scot’s Hall, where Sir Thomas lived. The William Scott born at Boughton Aluph is more likely to be the son of the as yet itinerant Charles Scott, living in that area, than of S
2 CONC ir Thomas Scott, firmly ensconced in Scot’s Hall several parishes away. But that would require a slip of the pen from the parson."
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 Parish register: KHLC-p36/1/5. Bishop’s transcript: CCA-dca/bt/22. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9 For pedigree see Robert Hovenden (ed.), The visitation of Kent, taken in the years 1619–1621, Harleian Society 42 (London, 1898), 128–9.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://assets.cambridge.org/97805211/96116/frontmatter/9780521196116_frontmatter.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Scott, Sheriff of Kent1,2,3,4,5,6
2 CONT *M, #52503, b. circa 1537, d. 30 December 1594
2 CONT *Father	Sir Reginald Scott, Sheriff of Kent, Captain of Calais & Sangatte7,8 b. c 1512, d. 16 Dec 1554
2 CONT *Mother	Emelyn Kempe1,7,8 b. c 1517, d. b 1542
2 CONT *     Sir Thomas Scott, Sheriff of Kent was born circa 1537 at of Scott's Castle, East Sutton, Kent, England; Age 39 in 1576.1,3,6 He married Elizabeth Baker, daughter of Sir John Baker, Chancellor of the Exchequer, Speaker of the
2 CONC  House of Commons, Ambassador to Denmark, Chancellor of the Exchequer, Under-Sheriff & Recorder of London and Elizabeth Dineley, circa 1556; They had 10 sons (Sir Edward; Thomas, Esq; Sir John; Richard; Robert; Reginald; '''Sir Wi
2 CONC lliam'''; Joseph; Anthony; & Benjamin) and 4 daughters (Elizabeth, wife of John Knatchbull, & of Sir Richard Smyth; Emeline, wife of Sir Robert Edolphe; Anne, wife of Richard Knatchbull, & Sir Henry Bromley; & Mary, wife of Richar
2 CONC d Argall, Esq.).9,2,3,4,5,6 Sir Thomas Scott, Sheriff of Kent left a will on 17 December 1594.3,6 He died on 30 December 1594 at Scott's Hall, East Sutton, Kent, England.3,6 His estate was probated on 7 January 1595.3,6
