1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Baptised at Wye 1581. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Headed petition to government (Sir Henry Martin) for the rights of the oyster dredgers 1631-32. (Other source indicates 1633). He and other Whitstable fishermen wished to protect their ancient rights of dredging, which Doctor Bedg
2 CONC rave, Dean of Canterbury had claimed as his prerogative. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From the times of Richard I I the fishermen of Whitstable had enjoyed the privilege of fishing oysters within the seaport near Whitstable. But that of late they had been molested by the fishermen of Faversham, and, on appealing to
2 CONC  the Dean of Canterbury, instead of obtaining protection, they had been told that the Dean had power to control the whole fishing rights, and would demand part of the future profits. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawsuits were instituted, but the Dean of Canterbury summoned the fishermen to his own court, and "pretended that the liberty belonged to him." Thus, feeling that a great injustice was done to the dredgers, Gregory Kempe headed th
2 CONC e petition and was successful in getting Sir Henry Martin to order an enquiry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Dean was called upon for an explanation and having failed to justify his right to the liberty, was obliged to retract his claim. Thus the ancient oyster fisheries flourished again without fear of molestation . . ." 
2 CONT
2 CONT A later Kempe was the actuary for the Incorporated Company of Dredgers. Possibly later of Townsford, Bedo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Probably named from the patron saint of Wye Church, St. Gregory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Margaret Homes by Licence -August 24, 1637. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1641 - petition parliament requesting assistance for a minister . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Churchwarden 1641 and 1642. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly married to another Katherine buried May 25 1618. 
