1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''William Browne''' (c. 1590 – c. 1645) was an English pastoral poet, born at Tavistock, Devon, and educated at Exeter College, Oxford; subsequently he entered the Inner Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT His chief works were the long poem Britannia's Pastorals (1613), and a contribution to The Shepheard's Pipe (1614). Britannia's Pastorals was never finished: in his lifetime Books I & II were published successively in 1613 and 161
2 CONC 6. The manuscript of Book III (unfinished) was not published until 1852. The poem is concerned with the loves and woes of Celia, Marina, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT To him is due the epitaph for the dowager Countess of Pembroke ("Sidney's sister, Pembroke's mother").[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT >"Browne had apparently an early disappointment; yet, if so, it did but serve to make him chivalrous to women, and he gained his reward in a happy but brief period of married life. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >"In a letter now first published from one of the .Ashmolean MSS., Mr. Hazlitt shows that " in 1640 Browne was resident, either permanently or temporarily, at Dorking, in Surrey, in what he terms ' his poore cell and sequestration
2 CONC  from all businesse.' " This is an interesting fact, not only because an ancestor of Browne had resided at Betchwortb. Castle, afterwards the residence of Abraham Tucker, but because Browne's name may now be added to the long list
2 CONC  of illustrious men who have lived or sojourned in that charming neighbourhood. He, too, in common with Spenser, Drayton, Milton, Pope, and Thomson, has sung of the Mole, a comparatively insignificant stream, except for the peculi
2 CONC arity of its swallows, but a stream upon which has been conferred poetical immortality.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * http://archive.spectator.co.uk/article/17th-september-1870/20/william-browne-of-tavistock
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Browne_(poet)_
2 CONT * http://www.bartleby.com/337/316.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT '''William Browne''' (c. 1590 – c. 1645) was an English pastoral poet, born at Tavistock, Devon, and educated at Exeter College, Oxford; subsequently he entered the Inner Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT His chief works were the long poem Britannia's Pastorals (1613), and a contribution to The Shepheard's Pipe (1614). Britannia's Pastorals was never finished: in his lifetime Books I & II were published successively in 1613 and 161
2 CONC 6. The manuscript of Book III (unfinished) was not published until 1852. The poem is concerned with the loves and woes of Celia, Marina, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT To him is due the epitaph for the dowager Countess of Pembroke ("Sidney's sister, Pembroke's mother"). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Browne_%28poet%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Said by some to have been married twice, with his first wife dying in 1614.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Obitum M.S., Xo Maij, 1614<br>
2 CONT May! be thou never grac'd with birds that sing,<br>
2 CONT Nor Flora's pride!<br>
2 CONT In thee all flowers and roses spring,<br>
2 CONT Mine only died.
2 CONT
2 CONT The heartbreak in these lines comes through clearly - if "M.S." was not his wife, she was someone he loved very dearly.
