2 PLAC Great Hallingbury, Essex, England
2 ADDR
1 OCCU 11th Baron Morley, 4th Baron Monteagle
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''William Parker''', ''4th Baron Monteagle'' was born in 1575 and died on the 1st of July 1622 at Great Hallingbury, Essex, where he was buried.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:  eldest son of Edward Parker, 12th Baron Morley (died 1618), and of Elizabeth Stanley, daughter and heiress of William Stanley, 3rd Baron Monteagle (died 1581). (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage with Elizabeth Tresham he had, besides daughters, three sons, the eldest of whom, Henry, (died 1655) succeeded him as 14th Baron Morley and 5th Baron Monteagle. These baronies fell into abeyance when Henry's son Th
2 CONC omas died in about 1686. His daughter Catherine Parker married John Savage, 2nd Earl Rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Gunpowder Plot of 1605==
2 CONT
2 CONT On 26 October 1605, while sitting at supper at Hoxton, he received a letter warning of the gunpowder plot, perhaps written by Sir Francis Tresham. After having caused it to be read aloud by Ward, a gentleman in his service and an 
2 CONC intimate friend of Robert Wintour, one of the chief conspirators, he took it to Whitehall and showed it to Robert Cecil, 1st Earl of Salisbury and other ministers. It is believed by some historians (such as Lady Antonia Fraser), h
2 CONC owever, that he authored the letter himself in order to win acclaim and favour with the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Married:
2 CONT # Elizabeth Tresham b: ABT 1598 in Rushton, Dorsetshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT # Henry Parker Sir Lord Morley b: ABT 1600 in Newby, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT # William Parker b: ABT 1604 in Newby, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT # Frances Parker b: ABT 1606 in Newby, Yorkshire, England [a nun]
2 CONT # Charles Parker b: ABT 1608 in Newby, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT # Catherine Parker b: ABT 1610 in Newby, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT # Elizabeth Parker b: ABT 1612 in Newby, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.oxforddnb.com/view/10.1093/ref:odnb/9780198614128.001.0001/odnb-9780198614128-e-21345
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Monteagle died at his Essex residence, Great Hallingbury, on 1 July 1622, where he was also buried. He was succeeded by his son Henry, and a further five children—William, Charles, Frances, Elizabeth, and Katherine—are mentioned i
2 CONC n his will, drawn up shortly before his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT *William Parker, 4th Baron of Monteagle had three sons; Henry, William and Charles. His titles were inherited by the eldest son Henry who died in 1655. When Henry's only son Thomas Parker died in 1686, the titles went into abeyanc
2 CONC e as there were no more male in his direct line descendants.
2 CONT *William Parker did not have a surviving son George Parker. He did not leave any surviving male descendants in his direct line.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Links==
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=john_d_newport&id=I22405
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Parker,_4th_Baron_Monteagle William Parker, 4th Baron Monteagle (1575 – 1 July 1622)]
2 CONT * http://thepeerage.com/p6612.htm#i66120
