2 CONT ==Citations==
2 CONT
2 CONT # [S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume IX, page 117. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT # [S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume III, page 128.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Children: Henry "14th Baron Morley" "5th Baron Monteagle" Parker William Parker Elizabeth Cranfeild (born Parker) Catherine Parker Nun: Frances Parker Charles Parker
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT 1641, June 2.—Draft order for Sir William Russell to answer the petition of Richard Man. Annexed, 1. Petition of Richard Man, gent.
2 CONT
2 CONT The late King James bestowed upon William Lord Morley and Monteagle a pension of 500lb. a year for his happy discovery of the Gunpowder Treason. At the time of Lord Morley's death there was an arrear of 1,750.7 due to him, which s
2 CONC um he left to petitioner as his executor for payment of his debts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the petition of Lady Morley, the King made two orders, one to Lord Treasurer Cranfield, and the other to Sir Richard Weston, Chancellor of the Exchequer, commanding satisfaction to be made to petitioner, both which orders wer
2 CONC e neglected, but after earnest solicitation the said Chancellor at last proposed that petitioner should abate 750lb and receive 1,000lb, giving a release for the whole amount due.
2 CONT
2 CONT This he eventually agreed to do, and a tally was thereupon levied upon Sir William Russell, collector of the impost of silk. Russell denied payment unless petitioner made a further abatement of 200lb for interest, 300lb for a debt
2 CONC  which he pretended due by Lord Morley to a tailor, a tenant of Russell's, and 20lb as a gratuity to Sir John Cooke for obtaining His Majesty's reference.
2 CONT
2 CONT Petitioner eventually only received 475lb, a sum far short of the Lord Morley's debts, and he has in consequence of these unjust proceedings consumed all his own estate and suffered long imprisonments. He is 77 years of age, and h
2 CONC as a wife and five children ready to starve. Prays for redress against Sir William Russell.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Reports/uV8pAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0 Reports Part 1, p.70]
