1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Jane Bamfeild
2 CONT *'''F, #171582, b. 1580, d. 26 February 1612/13
2 CONT *Last Edited=21 Jul 2012
2 CONT *'''     Jane Bamfeild was born in 1580. She was the daughter of John Bamfield and Elizabeth Drake. She married Sir Francis Drake, 1st Bt., son of Thomas Drake and Elizabeth (?), on 22 September 1602.1 She died on 26 February 1612
2 CONC /13 at Buckland Monachorum.
2 CONT *'''      From 22 September 1602, her married name became Drake.
2 CONT *'''Child of Jane Bamfeild and Sir Francis Drake, 1st Bt.
2 CONT ** 1.Dorothea Drake b. 1611, d. 1616
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S82] Ashworth P. Burke, editor, Burke's Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Peerage, Baronetage and Knightage, 64th edition (London, U.K.: Burke's Peerage Ltd, 1902), page 490. Hereinafter cited as Burkes Peerage and Bar
2 CONC oentage, 64th ed.
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p17159.htm#i171582 
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT *'''DRAKE, Sir Francis, 1st Bt. (1588-1637), of Buckland Abbey, Buckland Monachorum, Devon
2 CONT *b. 16 Sept. 1588,1 o.s. of Thomas Drake of Buckland Abbey and Elizabeth, da. of one Gregory of Plympton St. Mary, Devon and wid. of John Elford of Sheepstor, Devon.2 educ. Exeter Coll. Oxf. 1604; L. Inn 1606.3 '''m (1) 22 Sept. 1
2 CONC 602 (with £660), Jane (bur. 26 Feb. 1613), da. of Sir Amias Bampfield† of Poltimore, Devon, 1da. d.v.p.'''; (2) lic. 5 Oct. 1615, Joan, da. of Sir William Strode* of Newnham, Plympton St. Mary, 5s. 4da.4 suc. fa. 1606;5 cr. bt. 2 
2 CONC Aug. 1622.6 d. 11 Mar. 1637.7 sig. Fra[ncis] Drake.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1604-1629/member/drake-sir-francis-1588-1637 
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT *'''Sir Francis Drake, 1st Baronet''' (1588 – 11 March 1637) was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons in two parliaments between 1625 and 1629.
2 CONT '''Drake''' was the son of Thomas Drake of Buckland Abbey, Devon and his wife, Elizabeth Gregory, widow of John Elford. His father was the brother of Sir Francis Drake and accompanied him in his sea adventures.[1] He was baptised 
2 CONC at Buckland Monachorum on 16 September 1588. He matriculated at Exeter College, Oxford on 23 November 1604, aged 15, and was of Lincoln's Inn in 1606.[2]
2 CONT In 1622, King James sought to make up the money denied him by parliament, by seeking voluntary contributions from the gentry and the petty nobility. Following this, '''Drake''' was created baronet on 2 August 1622.[3]
2 CONT In 1624, '''Drake''' was elected Member of Parliament for Plympton Erle. He was elected MP for Devon in 1628 and sat until 1629 when King Charles decided to rule without parliament for eleven years.[4] He was High Sheriff of Devon
2 CONC  in 1633.[5]
2 CONT In 1628, he compiled the first detailed account of his uncle's circumnavigation, The World Encompassed by Sir Francis Drake, based on his uncle's journal, the notes of Francis Fletcher, and other sources.[6][7]
2 CONT '''Drake married, firstly, to Jane Bampfield, who died in 1613''', and secondly, to Joan Stroud, daughter of Sir William Stroud of Newnham. His son, Francis, succeeded to the baronetcy.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Francis_Drake,_1st_Baronet 
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT Sir Amyas Bampfylde (alias "Amias Bampfield" etc.[1]) (1560 – 9 February 1626) of Poltimore and North Molton in Devon, England, was a Member of Parliament for Devon in 1597.
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT Bampfylde was the son of Richard Bampfield (1526–1594),[2] of Poltimore and Bampfylde House in Exeter, Devon, Sheriff of Devon in 1576,[2] (whose monument survives in Poltimore Church) by his wife Elizabeth Sydenham (died 1599), d
2 CONC aughter of Sir John Sydenham of Brympton d'Evercy, Somerset.[3]
2 CONT Career
2 CONT He matriculated at Exeter College, Oxford on 3 December 1575, aged 15. He studied law at the Middle Temple in 1576.[4] He succeeded to the manor of Poltimore on the death of his father in 1594. He was JP for Devon from 1596. In 15
2 CONC 97, he was elected Member of Parliament for Devon. He was knighted at Windsor on 9 July 1603. He was Sheriff of Devon from 1603 to 1604. In 1616 he was Deputy Lieutenant.[3]
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT In 1576 Bampfylde married Elizabeth Clifton, a daughter of Sir John Clifton (died 1593) of Barrington Court, Somerset, by his wife Anne Stanley, daughter of Thomas Stanley, 2nd Baron Monteagle (1507–1560). Sir John Clifton's fathe
2 CONC r was a London merchant, Sir William Clifton (died 1564), who had purchased the manor of Barrington from Henry Grey, 1st Duke of Suffolk.[5] Elizabeth Clifton's brother was Gervase Clifton, 1st Baron Clifton (c. 1570 – 1618).[6]
2 CONT Her portrait circa 1640, in the style of Anthony van Dyck, survives in the collection of Antony House, Cornwall and her stone-sculpted effigy sits in mourning next to the recumbent one of her husband in North Molton Church, on whi
2 CONC ch monument are depicted the arms of Bampfylde impaling Clifton: Argent semée of cinquefoils pierced gules, a lion rampant of the last.
2 CONT By his wife he had six sons and four daughters as follows:
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT *John Bampfylde (c. 1586 – c. 1657), eldest son and heir, of Poltimore and North Molton, MP for Tiverton (1621) and Devon (1628–9). In 1602 Sir Amyas Bampfylde and Thomas Drake, brother and heir of Admiral Sir Francis Drake, made 
2 CONC a double marriage settlement for Bampfylde's eldest son John Bampfylde, then aged 14, and his daughter Jane Bampfield, then aged 16, who were to marry Drake's daughter and son, with each parent settling £660 on the other's daughte
2 CONC r.[3]
2 CONT *Richard Bampfield, second son, died without children.[2]
2 CONT *William Bampfield, third son.[2]
2 CONT *Amyas Bampfield, fourth son, of Weston Bampfield.[2]
2 CONT *Edward Bampfield, fifth son, of Stoke Canon.[2]
2 CONT *James Bampfield (died 1664), sixth son, Rector of Black Torrington.[2]
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT *Dorothy Bampfield (died 1614), wife successively of Edward Hancock (died 1603), MP, lord of the manor of Combe Martin, Devon, and later wife of Sir John Doddridge (1555–1628), of Bremridge, near North Molton, Justice of the King'
2 CONC s Bench.
2 CONT *'''Jane Bampfield, wife of Francis Drake''', son of Francis Drake of Buckland. In 1602 Sir Amyas Bampfylde and Thomas Drake, brother and heir of Admiral Sir Francis Drake, made a double marriage settlement for Bampfylde's eldest 
2 CONC son John Bampfylde, then aged 14, and a daughter, then aged 16, who were to marry Drake's daughter and son, with each parent settling £660 on the other's daughter.[3]
2 CONT *Anne Bampfield, died without children.[2]
2 CONT *Elizabeth Bampfield, died without children.[2]
2 CONT Monument to daughter
2 CONT A monument survives in the Lady Chapel of Exeter Cathedral to his daughter Dorothy Bampfield (died 1614), wife successively of Edward Hancock (died 1603), MP, lord of the manor of Combe Martin, Devon, and later wife of Sir John Do
2 CONC ddridge (1555–1628), of Bremridge, near North Molton, Justice of the King's Bench, whose effigy is situated adjacent. The Latin inscription on a tablet within a strapwork surround is as follows:
2 CONT *"Hic jacet d(omi)na Dorothea uxor Johannis Dioderidge militis unius justiciarioru(m) d(omi)ni regis as placita coram rege tenenda assignati et filia Amisii Bampfield militis quae obiit primo Martii Anno Dom(ini) 1614" ("Here lies
2 CONC  Dorothy the wife of John Doderidge, knight, one of the Justices of the Lord King assigned at the Pleas held before the King, and daughter of Amisus (Latinised form) Bampfield, knight, who died on the first of March in the Year of
2 CONC  Our Lord 1614").
2 CONT Death and burial
2 CONT Bampfield died at Cottonheath on 9 February 1625 at the age of 65[7] and stated in his will his wish to be "buried in North Molton church with his parents".[3][8] It might seem however that his parents were buried in Poltimore Chu
2 CONC rch, where a grand monument with their effigies survives, erected by Amias in 1604, with Latin inscription above: Hic tremibunda boni requiescunt membra Rich(ard)i Bampfildi... ("Here rest the trembling limbs of the worthy Richard
2 CONC  Bampfield..."). A grand monument to Amias with effigy survives in the Bampfield Chapel of North Molton Church.
2 CONT Monument at North Molton
2 CONT A fine stone monument to his memory exists against the south wall of the south aisle chapel ("Bampfylde Chapel") in North Molton Church. On the wall to its right is affixed a stone tablet inscribed with the following lines:
2 CONT "Stand passenger gaze such was he
2 CONT As thou tremble such shalt thou be
2 CONT He dy'd to live so live to dye
2 CONT Depart muse on eternity"
2 CONT Ameae Bamfylde equiti aurato patri suo charissimo ex antiqua Bamfydeorum (sic) de Poltimore familia patre Richardo Bamfyde (sic) armigero matre Elizabetha Clifton de Barrington per 45 annos conjugato ex qua 12 filios 5 filias susc
2 CONC epit prudenti sed pio animoso sed affabili provido sed hospitali et benigno de suis de patria bene merito ex hac miseriarum valle anno aetatis suae 67 ad immortalitatem commigranti et sui reliquias his in terris quas post diuturna
2 CONC  litigia et graves expensas sui posteritati foeliciter recuperavit in spem resurrectionis requiescere volenti Johannes Bamfylde filius et haeres qui etiam ex Elizabeth Drake de Buckland 8 filios et 7 filias ante patris obitum habu
2 CONC it anno Christi 1626 regis Caroli 2 hoc monumentum pietatis ergo posuit quas terras mea cura meis non-parva redemit hae mea jure sinu molliter ossa fovent.
2 CONT Translated literally into English thus:
2 CONT "To Amyas Bampfylde, knight bachelor, most dear to his father, from the ancient family of the Bampfyldes of Poltimore from Richard Bampfylde, esquire, his father, from Elizabeth Sydenham of Brympton his mother; with Elizabeth Clif
2 CONC ton of Barrington having been married during 45 years from whom he received 12 sons, 5 daughters. Prudent but with a pious spirit but easily spoken to; forward-looking but hospitable and benign, well-merited from his country. From
2 CONC  this vale of miseries he migrated towards immortality in the 67th year of his age and having wished the remains of him to rest in hope of resurrection in these lands which after long-lasting dispute and heavy expenses happily he 
2 CONC regained for his posterity. John Bampfylde his son and heir, who also from Elizabeth Drake of Buckland had before the decease of his father 8 sons and 7 daughters, in the year of Christ 1626, 2nd. of King Charles, therefore of pie
2 CONC ty placed this monument that these bones might gently warm the lands he redeemed not with small care".
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amyas_Bampfylde
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT BAMPFIELD, Amias (c.1560-1626), of Poltimore and South Molton, Devon.
2 CONT Family and Education
2 CONT b. c.1560, 2nd s. but h. of Richard Bampfield of Poltimore by Elizabeth, da. of Sir John Sydenham†. educ. Exeter Coll. Oxf. 1575, aged 15; M. Temple 1576. m. Elizabeth, da. of Sir John Clifton of Barrington, Som., 6s. 2da. suc. fa
2 CONC . 1594. Kntd. 1603.
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT J.p. Devon from 1596, sheriff 1603-4, dep. lt. 1616.
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT The Bampfields—since 1522 among the wealthiest Devon families, and third on the subsidy list in 1576—had close links with Somerset, where the branch of the family which had settled there in the fifteenth century was related by mar
2 CONC riage to the Trenchards. Returned for both Minehead and Devon in 1597, Bampfield chose to sit for the county. Nothing is known of his activities in the 1597 Parliament, except for a reference to a ‘Mr. Henry’ Bampfield sitting on 
2 CONC one committee dealing with armour and weapons (8 Nov.). However, as knight for Devon, Bampfield could have served on the following committees: enclosures (5 Nov.), the poor law (5 Nov., 22 Nov.), penal laws (8 Nov.), monopolies (1
2 CONC 0 Nov.), the subsidy (15 Nov.) and a cloth bill (8 Dec.). In the shire he served on various local commissions; viewing Thorverton Bridge with Richard Sparry on behalf of justices of the peace in 1600, and in 1608 investigating abu
2 CONC ses in the bridge trust at Bideford; in 1607 he and his friend John Acland were appointed arbitrators at the neighbouring town of Barnstaple.
2 CONT In 1602 Bampfield made a double marriage settlement for two of his children with Thomas Drake, the brother and heir of Francis Drake. '''His son and daughter, aged respectively 14 and 16, were to marry Drake’s daughter and son, ea
2 CONC ch parent settling £660 on the other’s daughter'''. The trustees included Edward Hancock, Richard Bowden (Bampfield’s brother-in-law) and Thomas Philipps of Barrington, a neighbour of Sir John Clifton. For some years before his of
2 CONC ficial appointment Bampfield deputised for the aged and infirm George Carey as deputy lieutenant of Devon.
2 CONT Bampfield, ‘not well of body’, made his will 4 June 1625, commending his soul to God in the belief that it would be received into His glory and placed in His heavenly kingdom among His angels and blessed saints. He left a living t
2 CONC o his son James, expressing the hope that he would become a ‘minister and preacher’. He named his son and heir John as executor, and left bequests to the poor, to whom he had, of late, been accustomed to give 2s. worth of white br
2 CONC ead each week. He wished to be buried in South Molton church, with his parents. Bampfield died in February 1626. His inquisition post mortem was taken 2 Mar. and his will was proved 31 Mar.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1558-1603/member/bampfield-amias-1560-1626
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT John Bampfield (c. 1586 – c. 1657)[1] of Poltimore House and North Molton, Devon, England, was a Member of Parliament for Tiverton in Devon (1621) and for the prestigious county seat of Devon (1628-9).
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT Bampfield was the eldest son and heir of Sir Amias Bampfield (c. 1560 – c. 1626), MP, of Poltimore and North Molton, by his wife Elizabeth Clifton, who was a daughter of Sir John Clifton of Barrington Court, Somerset.
2 CONT Career
2 CONT Bampfield matriculated at Exeter College, Oxford on 13 July 1604, aged 18. He was a law student at the Middle Temple in 1607. In 1621 he was elected a Member of Parliament for Tiverton, Devon. He was elected an MP for Devon in 162
2 CONC 8 and sat until 1629, when King Charles I decided to rule without parliament for eleven years.[2] In 1631 he founded almshouses in memory of his late wife. [3]
2 CONT Marriage and children
2 CONT In 1602 Bampfield married Elizabeth Drake, a daughter of Thomas Drake (d.1605) of Buckland Drake, Devon, and a niece of Admiral Sir Francis Drake (d.1596)[4] of Buckland Abbey, Devon. This was part of a double union in which '''hi
2 CONC s sister, Jane Bampfield, married Francis Drake''', who was a brother of Elizabeth Drake.[3] Drake and Bampfield then attended Oxford together two years later. [4] By his wife he had children, including:
2 CONT *Amias Bampfylde, eldest son, who died in Italy without children.[5]
2 CONT *Arthur Bampfield, second son, died without children.[5]
2 CONT *Sir John Bampfylde, 1st Baronet (1610–1650), third son and heir apparent, one of Devon's Parliamentarian leaders during the Civil War,[1] who predeceased his father.[1][5][6] The heir to his baronetcy and paternal inheritance was
2 CONC  his eldest son Sir Coplestone Bampfylde, 2nd Baronet (c. 1633 – 1692).
2 CONT *Francis Bampfield (died 1663/4), sixth son,[5] a Nonconformist minister[2] who died in Newgate Prison.
2 CONT *Thomas Bampfield (died 1693), eighth son,[5] MP, briefly Speaker of the House of Commons.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Bampfield
2 CONT ____________________________
2 CONT Devonshire wills: a collection of annotated testamentary abstracts, together with the family history and genealogy of many of the most ancient gentle houses of the west of England (1896)
2 CONT http://www.archive.org/details/devonshirewillsc00wortiala
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/devonshirewillsc00wortiala/page/478/mode/1up?view=theater
2 CONT Pg 478
2 CONT The name of Bampfylde, which was spelt "Bampfield" by the fifth Baronet, who died in 1823, and Bamfield at the creation of that title in 1641, has been also variously written Bamfeild, Bamfeld, Baunfelde, and Baunfeld.
2 CONT John de Baunfelde, the father of the first Baunfelde of Poltimore, by his wife Joan de Hastyngs, appears to have been settled in the parish of Weston Baunfell, or Baunville, now Bamfyld in Somersetshire, and was most probabaly a c
2 CONC adet of the house of Bonville, derived from Sir Nicholas Bonville, who was a landowner in that county as early as the first year of King John, and took his surname either from "Bonneville" in Savoy, or else from the town of the sa
2 CONC me name in the fertle province of Beauce, on the confines of the Isle of France. The "son" (or grandson ?) of Sir Nicholas, Sir William Bonville, 
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/devonshirewillsc00wortiala/page/479/mode/1up?view=theater
2 CONT Pg 479
2 CONT recovered his lands in Somerset upon the customary homage and service, in 1265, and was the grandfather of Nicholas Bonville, who married Avis, daughter and co-heir of Sir Thomas Pyne of Shute,* and died in 1294, leaving issue, Si
2 CONC r Nicholas, father of Sir William Bonville of Shute (an estate long subsequently, and after attainder, acquired from Petre, by Pole), the ancestor of William, Lord Bonville of Chewton, who was summoned to Parliament, as a baron, 2
2 CONC 8 Henry VI., and also a second son, John Bonville, who is said+ to have married Joan, daughter of Waryn Hampton, of Musbury, and to have died without issue.++
2 CONT "John Bamfielde of Poltimore," who commences the pedigree recorded by the Heralds at the Devonshire Visitation of 1620, married Ellinor, daughter of Sir Humphry Beauchamp, of Ryme, and is named as "Lord of Poltimore" in the year 1
2 CONC 316.S He had a son of the same name, who married Isabella, daughter of John Cobham, by his wife Anne Bollay, and who presented to Poltimore Rectory, 5th March, 1340-41. His son and heir, also called John, married Joan, daughter of
2 CONC  Geoffrey Gilbert and was dead, 13th November, 1360, when the said Joan, as "relict of John Bampfeld the elder," presented William Seger to the rectory of Poltimore. Consequently her son Thomas, who married a "daughter of Coplesto
2 CONC ne, must have been also dead at that date, but the latter's son and heir, "John Baunfeld, Esq.," was of full age, 7th November, 1361, when he gave the family living to a certain J. de Cobham, who was doubtless his kinsman. This "J
2 CONC ohn Baunfield, Esq." married Joane, daughter of Sir
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/devonshirewillsc00wortiala/page/480/mode/1up?view=theater
2 CONT Pg 480
2 CONT Richard Merton,* but died at a very early age, on which account his father-in-law, the said Sir Richard Merton, Kt., presented to Poltimore only sixteen months later, 24th March, 1362, as "Guardian of John Baunfeld, a minor son an
2 CONC d heir of John Baunfeld." This youthful heir also died young, but added much to the fortunes of the family by his marriage with Joan, daughter and heir of John de Hocesham, through which alliance his posterity acquired the adjacen
2 CONC t manor of Huxham,+ which is still the property of Lord Poltimore. His widow, Joan, presented to Poltimore Rectory, as "relict of John Baunfeld," and by right of her dowry, 4th January, 1372-73. Her eldest son, Thomas "Bampfeld," 
2 CONC resented to Poltimore 24 September, 1404, and to Huxham, as "true patron" 3rd February in the same year.++ He married Agnes, daughter and co-heir of John Faber of Bovey Tracy, and was the grandfather of John Bamfield of Poltimore,
2 CONC  who by his wife Agnes, daughter and heir of John Pederton, by Cecilia, daughter and heir of John Turney, was the father of Sir William Bamfield, son and heir of Poltimore. This John and his wife rebuilt the Parish Church of Polti
2 CONC more, as shown by an inscription on a gravestone which was, some years since, removed from the nave to the chancel, and which bears the following inscription: -
2 CONT *MC.C.XC."
2 CONT "Hic jacent Johes Baunfeld et Agnes uxor ejus, Pater et Mater Willi Baunfeld, qui hanc Ecclesiam et maximan Campanam fieri fecerunt."
2 CONT Their son, Sir William Bamfield,S was sheriff of Devon in 1426, and died in 1474. The Manor of Huxham appears to have been settled upon his second son William Bamfield,
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/devonshirewillsc00wortiala/page/481/mode/1up?view=theater
2 CONT Pg 481
2 CONT who may have acquired the Pinhoe property, mentioned in the mortgage above noted, by his second marriage with Margaret Kirkham, widow of John Cheyney, of Pinhoe; he succeeded his elder brother Walter "Bamfield," at Poltimore, 1st 
2 CONC Sept., 1478, and was the father, by his first wife Margaret St. Maur, of Sir Edward Bamfield of Poltimore, who married Elizabeth Wadham, and died in 1528. His son and heir, Richard Bamfeild, who was an only child and but two years
2 CONC  of age at this father's death, was presumably, the hero of a sensational story which has been handed down to us by John Prince, the author of the "Worthies of Devon," published in 1701, and which he tells us is "a most memorable 
2 CONC passage of undoubted credit," and to the effect that "one of the heir of the house, not many generations back," being ward to "some very great person in the east country," was taken away in his infancy, and brought up in ignorance
2 CONC  of his real position and prospects. He was trained to be a servant, and when discovered by one of his late father's tenantry, was employed as huntsman in his said guardian's establishment. The Poltimore farmer is then said to hav
2 CONC e abducted him, to have taken him before th proper authorities, and to have duly established the right of his young landlord to his inheritance.
2 CONT This Richard Bamfeild, at the age of fifty, became Sheriff of Devon in the eighteenth year of the reign of Queen Elizabeth. His mother, a widow, at the time of her second marriage, was a daughter of Nicholas Wadham, of Merrifield,
2 CONC  co. Somerset, and his wife was a daughter of Sir John Sydenham of the same county; by her he had a family of twelve children, viz., nine daughters and three sons. The eldest of the latter, Giles, predeceased him, having been drow
2 CONC ned during his passage to Ireland, so he was succeeded in 1594 by his second son Amias, then over thirty years of age, who was Sheriff of the county in 1603, and was knighted that same year at Windsor.* He married Elizabeth, daugh
2 CONC ter of Sir John Clifton, of Barrington, Somerset, and had ten children; '''one of'''
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/devonshirewillsc00wortiala/page/482/mode/1up?view=theater
2 CONT Pg 482
2 CONT '''his daughters married the nephew of the great Sir Francis Drake, who was created a baronet in 1622, and whose sister, Elizabeth, was the wife of his eldest son and successor''', John Bamfeild, of Poltimore, who was born about 1
2 CONC 590. The latter also had a large family, fifteen children; one of the daughters, Dorothy, was the wife of Henry Worth, of Worth. The sixth son, Francis was a Nonconformist minister, and died in Newgate Gaol in the spring of 1604; 
2 CONC the eighth son, Thomas Bampfield, was Recorder of Exeter during the Usurpation, and member of Exeter in 1656. The third son, John Bamfield, was created a baronet 14th July, 1641, and through the deaths of his two elder brothers, A
2 CONC mias and Arthur, succeeded to Poltimore at his father's death, and married Gertrude, sister and co-heir of John Coplestone, of Warleigh. During the great rebellion this first baronet was active on the side of the Parliament, and P
2 CONC oltimore House was garrisoned by Fairfax in 1645; its owner died in 1650, aged forty, when he was succeeded by his son Sir Coplestone Bampfield, the eldest of a family of nineteen, and who was zealous for the Restoration of monarc
2 CONC hy as his sire had been for its overthrow, and who was duly "pricked" Sheriff of Devon as soon as the king "came home again." etc.
2 CONT ____________________________
