1 NOTE WILL: 4 Oct. 1665; emigrated from Leeds, Yorkshire, Eng. prior 1636
2 CONT SERVICE: Member House of Burgesses from Henrico 1644, 1654-5; Sheriff in 1655;
2 CONT county commandant or Lieut.-Col.of Henrico.
2 CONT LAND: 3 plantations: Bremo, Malvern Hill and Curles; patented 3,000 acres of
2 CONT land on the James River in Henrico Co. due him for the transportation of 60
2 CONT immigrants (VA Mag. Hist. & Biog., VI, 186; III, 285, 405; V, 72). Again in
2 CONT 1639 "Richard Cocke gent" received a grant of 2,000 acres of land in the county
2 CONT of Henrico for the transportation of 40 persons; of which 300 acres was at
2 CONT Bremo, and the rest, called by the name of "Mauborne Hills" was at the head of
2 CONT Turkey Island Creek (Id, III,285; XIV,192); Gen.of VA Fam. Vol.2, Cl-Fi,Barnes.
2 CONT MILITARY: Lieutenant Colonel, Henrico Co. Militia,
2 CONT RESIDED: the original seats of the Cockes of Henrico were Bremo and Malvern
2 CONT Hills which were adacent to each other on the James river (Id., 3:283-4, 407,
2 CONT 410; 5:71-2, 37:158, 355, 357)
2 CONT !REF: The Col. VA Register by Wm. Glover Standard 1902, p. 64, 71;
2 CONT Register of Ancestors, Nat. Soc. of Col. Dames in VA, Rich., VA 1979 p. 24;
2 CONT Zelma Wells Price, Of Whom I Came--Wells-Wise, Rish-Wise, and Otherwise, vol.6
2 CONT (1963), part II, pp. 146-152, 161; The Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants to the
2 CONT Amer. Colonies, by Gary Boyd Roberts, 1993, p 361.
2 CONT Buried in his 'orchard' or garden (at Bremo) by the side of his early wife.
1 NOTE NOTE: Our Kin - Mary Aston was the second wife of Richard Cocke, and they were married about 1647. His first wife a widow Brown. Richard Cocke was born in England about 1600, came to Virginia in 1628, and died in 1665. He was Coun
2 CONC ty Commanedant or Lieutenant of Henrico Co., VA and patented 3,000 acres on James River in Henrico Co. He was a member of the House of Burgesses in 1632.
2 CONT
2 CONT TO-DO: The children of Richard Cocke are probably not listed with the correct mother. Very difficult to figure out.....too many Johns, too many Elizabeths, too many Williams and Richards......
2 CONT needs more investigation.....Shirley
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES FOR RICHARD COCKE: Came from England to Virginia, September 1628. Granted 3000 acres of land in Henrico Co. in 1636. Called his estate BREMO.
2 CONT Richard was a Lt. Col. in the Virginia Militia. He served as Representative of Henrico County in the Virginia House of Burgesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: ftp://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/va/henrico/bios/earlyfam2.txt
2 CONT Of the English ancestry of Richard Cocke (circa 1600-1665) and Francis Epped (died ante 1655) nothing definite is known(1), but from their first appearances in the colony, which was quite early, they both occupied a very solid pos
2 CONC ition. Richard Cocke was several times a member of the House of Burgesses and county lieutenant of Henrico. Richard Cocke amassed considerable means for the times in which he lived but just what were his other occupations, besides
2 CONC  planting and politics, is not now known. His sons and grandsons, at one time or another, occupied nearly every office of dignity and profit in Henrico County and as the years went by, other parts of the colony also.
2 CONT (1) A number of notes on various branches of the Cocke family in England may be found in Virginia Magazine, etc., Vol. III, p. 285 and Vol. V, p. 304, et seq. though they contain nothing definite as to the parentage of Richard Coc
2 CONC ke of Virginia. SOURCE: (1) Cocke Family: Virginia Magazine, etc., Vols. III, IV and V.
2 CONT
2 CONT TO-DO: Upper Bremo was built between 1817-1820. Find out who was living there at that time>>><http://www.iath.virginia.edu/schwartz/cville/Lay.html>
2 CONT "Charlottesville's Architectural Legacy", by K. Edward Lay - "The Northern Irish Builders"
2 CONT Referring on his seventy-fourth birthday to the building of the University, Jefferson wrote: "I suppose the superintendence of the buildings will rest chiefly on myself as most convenient. So far as it does I should wish to commit
2 CONC  it to yourself and Mr. Nelson...it will open a great field of future employment for you."(11) He was writing to forty-six year old James Dinsmore and referring to John Neilson, a team of builders who had lived and worked at "Mont
2 CONC icello" until 1808, been in President James Madison's employ at "Montpelier" until 1810, and worked on "Upper Bremo Plantation" between 1817 and 1820. These two accomplished Northern Irishmen became Jefferson's most prominent mast
2 CONC er builders at the University and overseers of his work. True to Jefferson's prediction, they subsequently remained in the forefront of American building activity until their deaths.
2 CONT James Dinsmore, born about 1771, became a naturalized citizen in Philadelphia on the fifth of June, 1798.(l2) His tools, purchased in Philadelphia at Jefferson's expense, were sent to "Monticello" eight days later, and the followi
2 CONC ng October Jefferson paid his travel expenses from Philadelphia to Charlottesville.(l3) Dinsmore worked as a master carpenter at Monticello," and at "Poplar Forest," Jefferson's Palladian retreat in Bedford County, until 1808.(14)
2 CONT On April 14, 1809, both he and Neilson left "Monticello" to work including the delightful Doric garden temple.(l5) In 1811, Dinsmore acquired a saw and merchant mill in Pen Park, north of Charlottesville, in partnership with John 
2 CONC H. Craven. Craven purchased Dinsmore's share in the enterprise four years later.(l6)
2 CONT Meanwhile, British troops had burned the nation's Capitol in 1814, and Jefferson recommended both Dinsmore and Neilson to Benjamin Henry Latrobe, America's first professionally trained architect, for restoration work there: "[I] o
2 CONC ffer you two house joiners of the very first order both in their knowledge in architecture, and their practical abilities. James Dinsmore... a more faithful, sober, honest and respectable man I have never known . . . John Nielson,
2 CONC  the other one . . I have found him also an honest, sober, and excellent man. They have done the whole [work] of the joiner; work of my house to which I can affirm I have never seen any superior in the U.S. After they had finished
2 CONC  with me they worked 2 or 3 years for the President, to whom, therefore they are well known. Mr. Mills also knows them personally and their works ...."(l7)
2 CONT Dinsmore's and Neilson`s whereabouts during this period are unknown, and it remains uncertain whether they actually worked on the Capitol. By 1817 Dinsmore was in Petersburg, where he prepared some drawings for James Monroe for a 
2 CONC house near Monroe's "Highlands" ("Ash Lawn").(18)
2 CONT In subsequent years Dinsmore resided in Charlottesville, living on Main Street and speculating in property along that thoroughfare. From 1818 to 1825 he subdivided thirteen contiguous lots between Tenth and Fourteenth streets (fig
2 CONC  2). Two people who purchased lots from him were also Jefferson builders: Malcolm F. Crawford, a University carpenter, and Irishman John Gorman, a stonemason. Lyman Peck, a carpenter, rented a dwelling there.(19) Surviving structu
2 CONC res from that period are the Vowles and Livers townhouses (Figs. 3,4).
2 CONT In 1819 Dinsmore purchased town lot number twenty-two, and in 1827 he deeded a portion of that plot to the Presbyterian Church.(20) He also owned over 500 acres just south of town called "Orange Dale."
2 CONT Studies suggest that Dinsmore built "Oak Lawn" (fig 5), the Jefferson paradigm patterned after plates in Robert Morris's Select Architecture (1757) and William Halfpenny's Useful Architecture (1752), for Nimrod Bramham in 1822.(22
2 CONC ) During his tenure at the University of Virginia Dinsmore was the principal master carpenter for Pavilions III (Fig. 6), V and VIII (fig. 7), fourteen dormitories, and together with John Neilson, the Rotunda and AnatomicalTheatre
2 CONC .(23)
2 CONT On May 13, 1830, at the age of fifty-nine, Dinsmore died by drowning in the Rivanna River at Ridgeway, northeast of town. A week later, his carpenter brother Andrew committed suicide.(24) Both brothers appear to have been unmarrie
2 CONC d, and James's will named his oldest brother, John, as his sole heir.(25) Two other surviving brothers received bequests of $500: Robert, in Ireland, and William (c. 1780-1836), who lived at "Rock Hill" in Charlottesville and late
2 CONC r at "Orange Dale."(26) Another brother, Samuel, of Havre de Grace, Maryland, received $300 and Dinsmore's chest of tools except for a set of bench tools and three saws "to my boy, John Boles."
2 CONT Dinsmore's will also reflects his compassionate and sentimental nature. He wished that his slave Stella and her children should live with his brother John, "but in case she should object to going with him she is at liberty to chus
2 CONC e her master." $500 went to Charles Stewart "in consideration of his fidelity and helpless situation . . . ," and James Dinsmore Pickands, the oldest son of his deceased Philadelphia friend Thomas, was named "in consideration of .
2 CONC  . . the affection I have for him."
2 CONT Jefferson's other principal builder, John Neilson, was born before 1775 and naturalized in Philadelphia on September 28, 1804.(27) From then until 1808 he lived and worked at "Monticello."(28) The following year he moved to "Montp
2 CONC elier," where he worked until 1810.(29)
2 CONT Neilson's most important work took place between 1817 and 1820 at "Upper Bremo Plantation."(30) The house contains many Jeffersonian features: dependencies recessed into the hillside and upper windows at floor level to reduce appa
2 CONC rent scale, bed alcoves, rotating food serving door, and small stairs tucked away. It also had the first jalousies in America in its portico in antis. Its classical temperance spring and barn are unique. "Upper Bremo" has been sai
2 CONC d to be the finest Jeffersonian building not designed by Jefferson.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Subject: Charles Coker of Virginia - 1713 (Found at http://members.tripod.com/~Coker_Forum/c00230.htm)
2 CONT From: Sscok <mailto:SSCOK@aol.com>Date: September 15, 1998For the Coker researchers of Burke Co., NC
2 CONT >From Cavaliers & Pioneers - Patent book 10, p 133
2 CONT 13 Nov, 1713. Joseph Pleasants. 1029 acres. Henrico Co., North side of James
2 CONT River. Adjacent Mr. John Pleasants. Importation of 21 persons.
2 CONT ...........CHARLES COAKER..............
2 CONT NOTE: Elizabeth Pleasants (do not know how related yet) married James Cocke, the great grandson of Richard Cocke
2 CONT 13 Nov, 1713. Mr John Pleasants. 1385 acres. Henrico Co., North side of James
2 CONT River adjacent John Woodson, below the mouth of Beaver Dam Creek & Land of
2 CONT AMOS LEAD (LADD). Importation of 28 persons..............
2 CONT In 1713 Virginia the Crown gave 50 acres of land to every new settler to the
2 CONT colony. This was known as a headright. Many settlers did not have the money
2 CONT to pay for their ship's passage and traded their headright for payment of
2 CONT passage.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Goochland Co. was formed from Henrico in 1727. Beaver Dam Creek is
2 CONT located in today's Goochland Co. and is about 20 miles due west of today's
2 CONT Richmond, VA. It is doubtful that anyone may have lived there in 1713 and the
2 CONT Pleasants are adding to their already substantial land holdings.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: John and Joseph Pleasants are brothers and son of the Quaker Merchant, John
2 CONT Pleasants who died in 1698, Henrico Co., VA. For more information on the
2 CONT Pleasants please refer to the 4 volume set, THE VALENTINE PAPERS by Edward
2 CONT Pleasants Valentine. It gives a complete genealogy of the Pleasants and many
2 CONT of the families listed below. Also note that the above Joseph Pleasants had
2 CONT married Martha Cocke.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Before stepping forward please step back in time to 1679.
2 CONT 1679 TYTHABLES FOR HENRICO COUNTY.
2 CONT At a Court holden at Varina for the county of Henrico the second day of June.
2 CONT Turkey Island
2 CONT Richard Cocke
2 CONT Wm. Randolph
2 CONT Thomas Cocke
2 CONT and a few others
2 CONT Curles
2 CONT Abraham Childress
2 CONT Robert Woodson
2 CONT John Woodson sr
2 CONT John Woodson
2 CONT Jno Huddlesee (Hulsey)
2 CONT Nich Perkins
2 CONT John Leadd (Ladd)
2 CONT Philemon Childress
2 CONT and others
2 CONT Please note that Henrico Co. is lightly populated at this time, about 130 families. Some of these families are significant in early American history. John Pleasants' descendants include a U. S. Senator and Governor of Virginia. Wi
2 CONC lliam Randolph's include John Marshall, first Chief Justice of the US, Gen. Robert E. Lee and Thomas Jefferson.
2 CONT The Curles and Turkey Island are on opposite sides of the James River where the James Estuary necks down to form the mouth of the James River. The Randolph's and Cockes would have large holdings also across the river at The Curles
2 CONC  where the Cockes would have their plantation, Bremo. If you move forward researching land records as settlement moved west you often find the names found on this 1679 list as central Virginia was settled.
2 CONT In 1724 Richard and Benjamin Cocke patent 6000 acres in what is today, Fluvanna Co., VA. It was bounded on the South by the James River, the West by New Bremo Creek and the East by Bremo Bluff. Sometime after 1800 the Cocke's woul
2 CONC d move their family home to their new Bremo Plantation at Bremo Bluff.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Let us now turn to the list of Heads of Families in Henrico in 1679.
2 CONT This list of heads of families in Henrico, or rather "As account of ye
2 CONT several fortye Tythables" as it was officially designated, was made in
2 CONT accordance with a provision of an act of the Assembly which convened 25
2 CONT April 1679. "An act for the defence of the country against the incursions
2 CONT of the Indian Enemy".(2)
2 CONT Mr. Epes is ordered to give notice to these. An account of ye several fortye Tythables ordered by this Wor'll Court
2 CONT to fitt out men horse armes &c. according to act, viz:
2 CONT
2 CONT In Bermuda Hundred: Curls
2 CONT Mr. Martin Elam 6 Abrah Childers 2
2 CONT Thomas Shippey 3 Richd Morish 6
2 CONT Edwd Stratton, Junr, 3 John Howard 2
2 CONT Samll Knibb 2 Wm Theobald 1
2 CONT Mr Fra Epes 9 Robert Woodson 5
2 CONT Jos Royall 3 John Woodson Sr 3
2 CONT Att Mrs Isham's 6 John Woodson Jr 2
2 CONT George Browninge 5 Mr John Pleasants 13
2 CONT Mr Kennon 3 Mr John Ball 1
2 CONT John Worsham 4 Edwd Goode 1
2 CONT ___________________ Edwd Lester 1
2 CONT Henry Brazeel 1
2 CONT John Greenhaugh 1
2 CONT Ben Hatcher
2 CONT
2 CONT Wm Hews
2 CONT Turkey Island Lewis Watkins 1
2 CONT Mr Richd Cocke 5 Tho Holmes 1
2 CONT Capt Wm Randolph 5 Sally Indian 1
2 CONT Giles Carter 6 ____________________
2 CONT John Aust 2
2 CONT Thomas Cocke 8
2 CONT The Cockes of the list were sons of Richard Cocke who
2 CONT had a patent for land in 1636.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Information on the will of Richard Cocke, d. 1776, can be found at:
2 CONT http://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/va/henrico/wills/wills1655-1800.txt
2 CONT HENRICO COUNTY - INDEX TO WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS, 1655-1800
2 CONT Contributed by: Joan Renfrow
2 CONT The Library of Virginia
2 CONT [http://eagle.vsla.edu/torrence/virtua-basic.html]
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: (INTERESTING. MAY BE ABLE TO HELP IN THE LOCATION OF COCKE LAND....Shirley)
2 CONT ftp://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/va/henrico/bios/earlyfam.txt
2 CONT (2) Robert Hollam, patented 1,000 acres in Henrico Co., adjoining Ed-
2 CONT ward Osborne and opposite to the fallan (Falling) Creek (due for trans-
2 CONT portation of 20 person), 2 June, 1636. Ann Hallam on 6 May, 1638, patented
2 CONT 1,000 acres in Henrico adjoining Richard Cocke and towards Bremo and to-
2 CONT wards Turkey Island Creek
