1 OCCU Married Joan Savage June 29 1631, Emigrant to New England and 2 more occupations
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1. * Ralph Earle , Jr (* Ralphe Earle1) was born 9 Feb 1605/06 in Exeter, England, and died 1678 in Portsmouth, Newport, RI. He married * Joan Savage 29 Jun 1631 in England, daughter of Richard Savage and Mary. She
2 CONC  was born 1609 in England, and died 1701 in RIchmond, Washington, RI.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph Earle and his wife Joan Savage had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Ralph Earle (Jr.), born about 1632 and died 1716.  He married Dorcas Sprague, daughter of Francis Sprague and Lydia on 26 Oct 1659.  
2 CONT # William Earle, 
2 CONT # Mary, born 1636 and married (1st) William Cory, (2nd) Joseph Timberlake
2 CONT # Martha, born 1638 and married William Wood, son of John Wood and Margaret Carter[6].
2 CONT # Sarah, born 1640 and married Thomas Cornell.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==brief biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://hylbom.com/family/paternal-lines/paternal-dy-to-gi/earle-5600/
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Ralph Earle (born about 1606) and his wife, Joan Savage[3] (born about 1595), came to the New World from Stortford, Hertfordshire, England.  There is little doubt that they were married in England and came together to America
2 CONC .  According to a tradition among his descendants, they came to Boston in 1634.  They may have come with a group of Puritans led by John Cotton[4] (a Puritan minister), which included William and Anne (Marbury) Hutchinson[5], who 
2 CONC arrived in 1633, with approximately 200 other passengers, on the ship Griffin.  The passenger list for this voyage is incomplete, so we do not know for certain whether they were on this ship or another.
2 CONT
2 CONT We also do not know for sure whether Ralph Earle was a Puritan or whether he came to the new world to flee religious persecution.  It is known that he joined himself with the Puritans of Massachusetts and later followed William an
2 CONC d Anne Hutchinson to Rhode Island, where he settled and lived for many years. The earliest trace of Ralph Earle hitherto discovered is in the records of Newport, Rhode Island.  His name is there found under date of first of eighth
2 CONC  month [October], 1638, in a list with fifty-eight others, arranged as,
2 CONT
2 CONT >"A catalogue of such persons who, by the General consent of the company, were admitted to the Inhabytants of the Island now called Aqueedneck, having submitted themselves to the Government that is, or shall be established accordi
2 CONC ng to the word of God therein."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph Earle appears to have been a person of some consequence in the colony. The records show that he was a large landholder, as many sales are found.  He was chosen to keep an inn in 1647.  In 1649, he was elected treasurer and a
2 CONC lso overseer of the poor. On 29 Apr 1650, Ralph Earle and five others were chosen for the committee for the General Assembly of Newport in May next.  In 1651 he was elected one of the committee to proportion every man’s farm, and 
2 CONC in the same year he was again chosen town treasurer.  He served later as juryman and grand juryman.  He was appointed in 1655 to keep a house of entertainment.  In 1667 joined the troope of horse of which subsequently he became ca
2 CONC ptain.   He fulfilled various other offices, served as grand juror and witnessed deeds and other instruments.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph Earle died 1678 at Dartmouth, Bristol County, Massachusetts.  Ralph’s will was dated 19 Nov 1673 in Portsmouth, Rhode Island.  This will, of which his friend John Tripp was the overseer, after providing for his widow, leaves
2 CONC  two-thirds of his real estate to his son Ralph, and one-third to his grandson Ralph, the son of his son William.  William received only one shilling, probably due to the fact that he had already provided for him.  Indeed, in Apri
2 CONC l 1655, he conveyed to him a homestead in Portsmouth near John Tripp’s property.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan died in 1679.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT # [http://hylbom.com/family/wp-content/uploads/2012/05/Earle-Family-Ralph-Earle-and-His-Descendants-by-Earle-1888.pdf The Earle Family:] Ralph Earle and his Descendants compiled by Pliny Earle of Northampton, Massachusetts (Worces
2 CONC ter, Massachusetts, privately printed for the family by Charles Hamilton) 1888.
2 CONT
2 CONT Links
2 CONT * http://hylbom.com/family/paternal-lines/paternal-dy-to-gi/earle-5600/
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==  
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://awt.ancestrylibrary.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=REG&db=fletcht2001&id=I04603&ti=5542
2 CONT
2 CONT Early records dated 09 Feb 1605/06 show that Ralph was babtized at Bishops Stortford, Hertz, England. His will is dated 19 Nov 1673. Source:Little Comptons Families Vol. 1 pg. 262.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Genealogical Dictionary of Rhode Island pg. 69
2 CONT
2 CONT Portsmouth, RI
2 CONT
2 CONT 1638. He and others, were admitted inhabitants of Aquidneck, having submitted themselves to the government, that is or shall be established.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1639, Apr 30. He and twenty-eight others signed declaration of allegiance. "We, whose names are underwritten, do acknowledge ourselves the legal subjects of his majesty, King Charles, and in his name, do hereby bind ourselves into
2 CONC  a civil body politicke, unto his laws according to matters of justice."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1640, Jan 07. It was ordered that he and his co-partner, Mr. Wilbur, shall serve the town with good sufficient stuff, viz: well sawn boards at 8s. the hundred, and half inch boards at 7s., to be delivered at the pit by the watersi
2 CONC de.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1640, Mar 21. He sold William Baulstone, certain land.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1647 He was chosen to keep an inn, to sell beer and wine, and to intertain strangers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1649 Treasurer
2 CONT
2 CONT 1649 Overseer of the poor
2 CONT
2 CONT 1651, Jan 16. He with three others, was chosen to apportion a tax. He was again elected treasurer, this year.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1651, May 21 He deeded Nicholas Hart, of Portsmouth, and Joan, his wife, 8 acres and messuage, for valuable consideration.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1652, Jun 24 He sold William Arnold, of Providence, land formely owned by Francis Weston's wife, Margaret, near Pawtuxet Falls.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1654, Apr 11 He and another, were chosen to oversee the work of the prison, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1655 Freeman
2 CONT
2 CONT 1655, May 05 Juryman
2 CONT
2 CONT 1655, May 25 He was appointed by the court of Commissioners, to keep a house of intertainment. A convenient sign was to be set out at the most perspicuous place, to give notice to strangers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1658, Dec 13 He sold William Cadman, 20 acres
2 CONT
2 CONT 1667, Aug 10 He joined a troop of horse(afterwards becoming Captain). At about this time he commenced suit against Richard Lord and James Richards, of Hartford, possessors of lnd there, claiming that he had purchased the land of U
2 CONC nderhill, in 1653, for 20 pounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1669, Apr 28 Grand Jury
2 CONT
2 CONT 1671, Jun 07 He was appointed with others, to sit as a Special Court, to try "two Indians, now imprisoned upon criminal charge."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1673, Nov 19 Will -- recorded 1678, Jan 14. Exx wife Jone. Overseer, John Tripp, Sr. To wife, all for life, and at her decease, as follows: To eldest son Ralph, and to Ralph, son of son William, all land and housing; said son Ralp
2 CONC h having two parts and grandson Ralph, one part. The movable estate to be divided into five parts, of which son Ralph to have two parts, he paying 1s. to my son William. The other three parts to three daughters, viz. Mary, the wif
2 CONC e of William Cory, Martha, wife of William Wood and Sarah, widow to late deceased, Thomas Cornell.
2 CONT
