2 CONT
2 CONT  Ralph Earle
2 CONT Photo added by C Walker
2 CONT
2 CONT Add Photos  Request Photo Ralph Earle BIRTH	9 Feb 1606 Hereford, Herefordshire Unitary Authority, Herefordshire, England DEATH	1678 (aged 71–72) Portsmouth, Newport County, Rhode Island, USA BURIAL	Unknown MEMORIAL ID	35514443 · V
2 CONC iew Source SHARE  SAVE TO SUGGEST EDITS MEMORIAL PHOTOS 1 FLOWERS 18 Ralph Earl Arrived in Boston in 1634 and in 1647 he was granted a License to Own and Operate a Tavern in Little Compton, Newport, RI. He is listed in the "Flagon
2 CONC  and Trenchers" as a Qualifying Member. ∼
2 CONT Date of baptism is used as date of birth.  St. Michael's Church: 1606; Raulphe, ye sonne of Raulphe Earles, baptized ye ix of Feb (9 Feb 1606/7). N.S. 9 Feb 1607. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of death shortly before probate: 14 Jan 1677/78.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Members Spouse Joan Savage Earle 1609–1680 Children Mary Earle Timberlake 1631–1717 Ralph Earle 1632–1716 William Earle 1634–1715 Sarah Earle Cornell 1640–1690
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Christening Feb. 9, 1606. Raulphe arrived in Boston, MA. about 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT 'English Origins of New England Families'; 1st Series (Baltimore 1984) 2:635-6. Background of Earle Families.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT @R351077827@  Ancestry Family Trees  Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com.  Original data:  Family Tree files submitted by Ancestry members. 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Ancestry Family Tree  http://trees.ancestry.com/pt/AMTCitationRedir.aspx?tid=56830191&pid=1280 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT LifeSketch:	From http://hylbom.com/family/paternal-lines/paternal-dy-to-gi/earle-5600/
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Ralph Earle (born about 1606) and his wife, Joan Savage[3] (born about 1595), came to the New World from Stortford, Hertfordshire, England.  There is little doubt that they were married in England and came together to America
2 CONC .  According to a tradition among his descendants, they came to Boston in 1634.  They may have come with a group of Puritans led by John Cotton[4] (a Puritan minister), which included William and Anne (Marbury) Hutchinson[5], who 
2 CONC arrived in 1633, with approximately 200 other passengers, on the ship Griffin.  The passenger list for this voyage is incomplete, so we do not know for certain whether they were on this ship or another.
2 CONT
2 CONT We also do not know for sure whether Ralph Earle was a Puritan or whether he came to the new world to flee religious persecution.  It is known that he joined himself with the Puritans of Massachusetts and later followed William an
2 CONC d Anne Hutchinson to Rhode Island, where he settled and lived for many years. The earliest trace of Ralph Earle hitherto discovered is in the records of Newport, Rhode Island.  His name is there found under date of first of eighth
2 CONC  month [October], 1638, in a list with fifty-eight others, arranged as,
2 CONT
2 CONT "A catalogue of such persons who, by the General consent of the company, were admitted to the Inhabytants of the Island now called Aqueedneck, having submitted themselves to the Government that is, or shall be established accordin
2 CONC g to the word of God therein."
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph Earle appears to have been a person of some consequence in the colony. The records show that he was a large landholder, as many sales are found.  He was chosen to keep an inn in 1647.  In 1649, he was elected treasurer and a
2 CONC lso overseer of the poor. On 29 Apr 1650, Ralph Earle and five others were chosen for the committee for the General Assembly of Newport in May next.  In 1651 he was elected one of the committee to proportion every man’s farm, and 
2 CONC in the same year he was again chosen town treasurer.  He served later as juryman and grand juryman.  He was appointed in 1655 to keep a house of entertainment.  In 1667 joined the troope of horse of which subsequently he became ca
2 CONC ptain.   He fulfilled various other offices, served as grand juror and witnessed deeds and other instruments.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ralph Earle died 1678 at Dartmouth, Bristol County, Massachusetts.  Ralph’s will was dated 19 Nov 1673 in Portsmouth, Rhode Island.  This will, of which his friend John Tripp was the overseer, after providing for his widow, leaves
2 CONC  two-thirds of his real estate to his son Ralph, and one-third to his grandson Ralph, the son of his son William.  William received only one shilling, probably due to the fact that he had already provided for him.  Indeed, in Apri
2 CONC l 1655, he conveyed to him a homestead in Portsmouth near John Tripp’s property.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan died in 1679.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earle Family: Ralph Earle and his Descendants compiled by Pliny Earle of Northampton, Massachusetts (Worcester, Massachusetts, privately printed for the family by Charles Hamilton) 1888.
