2 CONT 1638. He and others, were admitted inhabitants of Aquidneck, having submitted themselves to the government, that is or shall be established.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1639, Apr 30. He and twenty-eight others signed declaration of allegiance. "We, whose names are underwritten, do acknowledge ourselves the legal subjects of his majesty, King Charles, and in his name, do hereby bind ourselves into
2 CONC  a civil body politicke, unto his laws according to matters of justice."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1640, Jan 07. It was ordered that he and his co-partner, Mr. Wilbur, shall serve the town with good sufficient stuff, viz: well sawn boards at 8s. the hundred, and half inch boards at 7s., to be delivered at the pit by the watersi
2 CONC de.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1640, Mar 21. He sold William Baulstone, certain land.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1647 He was chosen to keep an inn, to sell beer and wine, and to intertain strangers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1649 Treasurer
2 CONT
2 CONT 1649 Overseer of the poor
2 CONT
2 CONT 1651, Jan 16. He with three others, was chosen to apportion a tax. He was again elected treasurer, this year.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1651, May 21 He deeded Nicholas Hart, of Portsmouth, and Joan, his wife, 8 acres and messuage, for valuable consideration.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1652, Jun 24 He sold William Arnold, of Providence, land formely owned by Francis Weston's wife, Margaret, near Pawtuxet Falls.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1654, Apr 11 He and another, were chosen to oversee the work of the prison, &c.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1655 Freeman
2 CONT
2 CONT 1655, May 05 Juryman
2 CONT
2 CONT 1655, May 25 He was appointed by the court of Commissioners, to keep a house of intertainment. A convenient sign was to be set out at the most perspicuous place, to give notice to strangers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1658, Dec 13 He sold William Cadman, 20 acres
2 CONT
