2 CONT *'''A Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Commoners of Great Britain and ...     by John Burke
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/agenealogicalan00goog
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/agenealogicalan00goog#page/n286/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.264
2 CONT *   SIR WILLIAM PETRE, of Tor Brian, Devonshire, secretary of state to HENRY VIII. married, for his second wife, Anne, daughter of Sir William Browne, lord mayor of London in 1514, and was father of 
2 CONT *   SIR JOHN PETRE, who was elevated In the peerage as Lord Petre, Baron of Writtle, in 1603. His Iordship married Mary, daughter of Sir Edward Waldegrave, knt. of Barclay, in Essex, and was s. at his decease in 1637 by his son, 
2 CONT *   WILLIAM, second Lord Petre, of Writtle, who married Catherine, second daughter of Edward, fourth Earl of Worcester, and had 
2 CONT **	I. ROBERT, third Lord Petre. 
2 CONT **	II. William, ancestor of the Petres of Bellhouse. 
2 CONT **	lII. Thomas. 
2 CONT **'''	IV. JOHN, of whom presently. 
2 CONT **	V. Henry.
2 CONT **	VI. George
2 CONT **	I. Elizabeth, m. to William Sheldon, esq. of Beoley, in Worcestershire. 
2 CONT **	II. Mary, m. to John, Lord Teynham. 
2 CONT **	III. Catherine, m. to John Carrel, of Harting, in Sussex. 
2 CONT *'''The fourth son, 
2 CONT *'''   THE HON. JOHN PETRE, was of Fidlers, in Essex. He married thrice. By his first wife, Elizabeth (who d. in 1658), daughter of Thomas Pordage, esq. of Radmersham, he left, at his decease in 1696, a son and successor, 
2 CONT *   JOHN PETRE, esq. of Fidlers, who married Mary, daughter of Sir Francis Mannock, bart of Gifford's Hall, and was father of 
2 CONT *   JOSEPH PETRE, esq. of Fidlers, who m. Catherine, daughter of Sir William Andrews, bart. of Hildersham, in the county of Cambridge, and left at his decease, in 1721, a son and heir, 
2 CONT *   JOHN PETRE, esq. of Fidlers, who m.  Anne, daughter of Sir Robert Throckmorton, bart of Weston, and had (with other issue, who d. s. p.) a daughter, MARY, who married FRANCIS CANNING, esq. of Foxcote, and was ultimately sole h
2 CONC eir to the family of Petre of Fidlers.  _________________
2 CONT *William PETRE (2° B. Petre of Writtle)
2 CONT *Born: 24 Jun 1575, West Horndon, Essex, England
2 CONT *Died: 5 May 1637, West Horndon, Essex, England
2 CONT *Buried: 12 May 1637, Ingatestone, Essex, England
2 CONT *Notes: Student at the Middle Temple in 1593. Member of Parliament for Essex 1597-8. Married in 1596 to Catherine, 2nd daughter of Edward Somerset, 4th Earl of Worcester. Male heir born 1599. Succeeded 1613.
2 CONT *Father: John PETRE (1° B. Petre of Writtle)
2 CONT *Mother: Mary WALDEGRAVE
2 CONT *Married: Catherine SOMERSET (B. Petre of Writtle) 8 Nov 1596, Essex House
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Mary PETRE
2 CONT **2. Robert PETRE (3º B. Petre of Writtle)
2 CONT **3. William PETRE
2 CONT **4. Edward PETRE
2 CONT **5. Thomas PETRE
2 CONT **6. Henry PETRE
2 CONT **7. George PETRE
2 CONT **8. Catherine PETRE
2 CONT **9. Elizabeth PETRE
2 CONT **'''10. John PETRE
2 CONT **11. Anne PETRE (b. 2 Jul 1609 - d. 18 Oct 1610)
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/PETRE.htm#William PETRE (2° B. Petre of Writtle) _____________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 45
2 CONT *Petre, William (1602-1677) by Thomas Seccombe
2 CONT *PETRE, WILLIAM (1602–1677), translator, the third son of William, second lord Petre (1575–1637) of Writtle in Essex, and great-grandson of Sir William Petre [q. v.], was born in his father's house at Ingatestone, Essex, 28 July 1
2 CONC 602. His mother, who died in 1624, was Catherine, second daughter of Edward Somerset, fourth earl of Worcester. His family, who remained Roman catholic, had been steady benefactors of Exeter College, Oxford, whither he was sent as
2 CONC  gentleman commoner, matriculating on 5 Feb. 1612, at the early age of ten. In the following year, however, when Wadham College was completed by his great-aunt, Dame Dorothy Wadham, he migrated thither, and ‘became the first noble
2 CONC man thereof’ (Wood). '''In October 1613 his eldest brother John died''', and the society of Exeter dedicated a threnody to the family (Madan, Early Oxford Press, p. 92). About the same time he was joined at Wadham by his elder bro
2 CONC ther Robert, and the two brothers, both of whom left without taking degrees, presented to the college two fine silver tankards, which were sacrificed to the royal cause on 26 Jan. 1643. After leaving Oxford he was entered of the I
2 CONC nner Temple. Subsequently he travelled in the south of Europe, and, according to Wood, ‘became a gent. of many accomplishments.’ In 1669 he issued from St. Omer a translation of the then popular ‘Flos Sanctorum’ of the jesuit Pedr
2 CONC o de Ribadeneira, originally published at Barcelona in 1643, fol. The translation, which was entitled ‘Lives of the Saints, with other Feasts of the Year according to the Roman Calendar,’ is continued down to 1669. The first editi
2 CONC on soon became scarce, and a second, corrected and amended, was issued at London in 1730, folio. Petre's rendering has been commended by Southey and Isaac Disraeli. Petre died on the estate at Stanford Rivers in Essex which had be
2 CONC en given him by his father, and he was buried in the chancel of Stanford Rivers church. His wife Lucy, daughter of Sir Richard Fermor of Somerton, Oxfordshire—by whom he had three sons and two daughters—was buried by his side in M
2 CONC arch 1679.
2 CONT *[Wood's Athenæ Oxon. ed. Bliss, iii. 1144; Gardiner's Register of Wadham, i. 21; Collins's Peerage, vii. 36; Dodd's Church Hist. iii. 278; Morant's Hist. of Essex, ‘Hundred of Ongar,’ p. 152; Disraeli's Curiosities of Literature;
2 CONC  Howard's Roman Catholic Families of England, pt. i. p. 44.]
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Petre,_William_(1602-1677)_(DNB00)
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/dictionaryofnati45stepuoft#page/95/mode/1up to https://archive.org/stream/dictionaryofnati45stepuoft#page/96/mode/1up _______________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Fourth son of William Petre, 2nd Baron Petre of Writtle. Married three times. Son and heir by his first wife, Elizabeth (d. 1658), daughter of Thomas Pordage, Esq. of Radmersham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Burke, Genealogical and Heraldic History of the Commoners of Great Britain, vol. 3, p. 264.
2 CONT
