1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education b. c.1592, 2nd but o. surv. s. of Sir Richard Smythe* and his 1st w. Elizabeth, da. of Sir Thomas Scott of Scot’s Hall, Kent, wid. of John Knatchbull of Mersham Hatch, Kent.1 educ. ?travelled a
2 CONC broad 1610-13.2 m. 3 Mar. 1617 (with £4,000),3 Mary (d. 11 May 1678), da. of Richard Franklin of Willesden, Mdx., ?1s. d.v.p.4 kntd. 28 Feb. 1617;5 suc. fa. 1628. d. 20 May 1632.6
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT Commr. sewers, Maidstone, Kent 1628, Ticehurst, Kent 1630, Kent and Suss. Feb. 1632-d.7
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography According to Chamberlain, Smythe’s knighthood in 1617 was a precondition of his marriage to Mary Franklin, who brought him a substantial dowry of £4,000. The union was possibly not childless as is usually supposed, for t
2 CONC he financial records of Mary’s brother, Sir John Franklin*, include payments during the mid-1620s for the education of a boy named John Smith.8 Despite his lack of a university training, Smythe, who may have been the John Smith ‘o
2 CONC f London’ licensed to travel abroad for three years in 1610, was evidently well educated. In 1623 he lent a number of French and Italian manuscripts to John Howell*, who also borrowed a copy of a work by Balzac from him two years 
2 CONC later.9
2 CONT
2 CONT Returned for Mitchell to the first and second Caroline Parliaments, Smythe probably owed his seats to the influence of his cousin, Sir Richard Buller*, who belonged to the same Cornish gentry faction as John Arundell*, the borough
2 CONC ’s principal patron. Smythe left no trace on the records of either session.10 He succeeded to his patrimony in 1628, but played only a minor role in local government. In his will of 30 Dec. 1631 he appointed Sir John Franklin one 
2 CONC of his overseers.11 He died without male heir five months later, aged 40, and was buried at Leeds Castle. His widow subsequently married Anthony Crofts*. Smythe’s two sisters, who were his coheirs, sold Leeds Castle to Sir Thomas 
2 CONC Culpeper*.12
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1604-1629 Authors: Peter Lefevre / Andrew Thrush Notes 1. Arch. Cant. xx. 78. 2. SO3/4, unfol., April 1610. 3. D. Lysons, Environs of London, iii. 622; Chamberlain Letters ed. N.E. McClure, ii. 62. 4. Arch. Cant. xx. 
