1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/STRANGE.htm#Nicholas Le STRANGE of Hunstanton (Sir Knight)1
2 CONT *''''''Nicholas Le STRANGE of Hunstanton (Sir Knight)
2 CONT *Christened: 13 Mar 1562/3, St. Margaret's, Kings Lynn, Norfolk, England
2 CONT *Died: 15 Dec 1591
2 CONT *Buried: 22 Dec 1591, Hunstanton, Norfolk, England
2 CONT *Father: Hamon Le STRANGE of Hunstanton (Sir)
2 CONT *Mother: Elizabeth HASTINGS
2 CONT *''''''Married 1: Margaret (Mary) BELL 6 Aug 1582, Outwell, Norfolk, England
2 CONT **1. Hamon Le STRANGE (Sir)
2 CONT **2. Roger STRANGE (Drowned, Emanuel College, Cambridge)
2 CONT **3. Henry STRANGE
2 CONT **4. Alice STRANGE
2 CONT **5. Paston STRANGE
2 CONT *''''''Married 2: Anne PASTON 1591
2 CONT *_________________
2 CONT *''''''Sir Robert Bell and his early Virginia Colony descendants: a compilation of ... By James Elton Bell, Frances Jean Bell
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=AXdzoxq2NX8C&pg=PA108&lpg=PA108&dq=Beaupre'+Bell+1569&source=bl&ots=BjjPKewdM7&sig=jNIb54wwvYSKSMEmW00XzDALlOY&hl=en&ei=DDdbTeq6EoyosQOCv8WlCg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=6&ved=0CDUQ6AEwB
2 CONC Q#v=onepage&q=BEAUPRE&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 7
2 CONT *Sir Robert Bell's second family;
2 CONT *By Robert's third wife, Dorothy Beaure', Lady Bell; She was the adopted daughter and heiress of Sir Edmund Beaupre'.
2 CONT *''''''5 Margaret Bell, Lady Le Strange born about 1560 in probably Norfolk, died 1591. Married Sir (Knight) Nicholas Le Strange who succeeded his brother Thomas in 1578. He was admitted to Peterhouse in 1580, and married two year
2 CONC s later. After Margaret's early death he married (2) Anne, daughter of Sir William Paston, and (3) Lady Ann Chaworth.
2 CONT *''''''  Margaret had five children; the most notable of this line was her grandson Sir Roger Le Strange, a Royalist who spend a short refuge time in Holland. He returned and remained in England where he produced many witty writin
2 CONC gs some of which are exlant; toyed with and outsmarted both the Puritans and Sir Oliver Cromwell; but eventually served three years in Newgate prison; excaped, and helped start a new Royalist movement that proved successful by 166
2 CONC 0 with the crowning of Charles II. He, along with his father and brothers were avid Cavaliers, and he may have had contact with his Bell cousins in Deptford and Tenterden, Kent, during the late 1640's where he was taking refuge. A
2 CONC  good detail account of Royalist decline and subsequent harsh treatment by the Puritans is described in his writings.
2 CONT *Pg. 111
2 CONT *1. Sir (Knight) Robert Bell (father was William Bell) was born between 1520-1537 in Norfolk Co., or Worcester Co., England, and died 22 July 1577 in Leominster, Herefordshire, England (on circuit). He married (1) Mary Chester. He
2 CONC  married (2) Elizabeth (Darnell) Anderson, Lady Anderson widow or daughter-in-law of Sir Edmund Anderson. He married (3) Dorothy Beaupre' Lady Bell 15 October 1559 near King's Lynn, Norfolk, England, daughter of Sir Edmund Beaupre
2 CONC ' and Catherine Bedingfield, Dorothy was born about 1537 between King's Lynn/Wisbech, Beaupre' Hall, Outwell, Norfolk, England and died February 1602/3.
2 CONT *Pg. 112
2 CONT *Children of Robert Bell and Dorothy Beaupre' are:
2 CONT **''''''+ 6 i. Margaret Bell, Lady Le Strange, born about 1560 in Outwell, Norfolk, England: died 14 September 1591 in Hunstanton, Norfolk, England.
2 CONT *Page 114 is not part of this book preview.
2 CONT *____________________
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Robert_Bell
2 CONT *Robert Bell (Speaker of the House of Commons)
2 CONT *Sir Robert Bell SL (died 1577) of Beaupre Hall, Norfolk, was a Speaker of the House of Commons (1572–1576), who served during the reign of Queen Elizabeth I.
2 CONT *Marriages
2 CONT *Bell is reported to have married:
2 CONT *1. Mary Chester, daughter of Anthony Chester.[1][3]
2 CONT *2. Elizabeth Anderson (d.1556-58?), widowed daughter in law of Edmund Anderson, Chief Justice of the Common Pleas.[3]
2 CONT *3. Dorothie, daughter and co-heiress of Edmonde Beaupre, Esq., d. 1567, and Katherine Wynter, daughter of Phillip Bedingfeld of Ditchingham, Norfolk.[1][4][5]
2 CONT *Descendants
2 CONT *"Amongst the many great families with whom the Bells were connected by their various marriages, we may mention.... Beaupre, [Montfort] , De Vere, Bedingfeld, Knyvett, Oldfield, Osbourne, Wiseman, Deering, Chester, Oxburgh, Le Str
2 CONC ange, Dorewood, Oldfield, Peyton, and Hobart, all persons of great eminence and distinction."[6][19]
2 CONT *''''''6. His daughter, Margaret Bell b. before 1561, d. 14 September 1591, married Sir Nicholas Le Strange of Norfolk; the son of Sir Hamon Le Strange (c.1534-1580) and Elizabeth Hastings; daughter of Sir Hugh Hastings of Elsing,
2 CONC  14th Lord Hastings (d. c.1540) and Catherine Le Strange (d. 2 February 1558).
2 CONT *Sources
2 CONT *1.^ a b c d e f g h i j k l m n o p q r Hasler, P. W., HoP: House of Commons 1558-1603, HMSO 1981, p. 421-4 [1]
2 CONT *2.^ a b Foss, E., Lives of the Judges, Vol. V, London 1857, p. 458-61
2 CONT *3.^ a b c d e f g h i Graves, M. A.R., ‘Bell, Sir Robert (d. 1577)',ODNB, OUP, 2004 accessed 13 Feb 2005
2 CONT *4.^ a b c d e O'Donoghue, M.P.D., Report, 'Arms' and ‘pe de gree's' of Bell [Sir Robert Bell], 15 August 2005
2 CONT *5.^ Waters, R.E.C., Genealogical Memoirs of the Extinct Family of Chester of Chicheley, their Ancestors and Descendant Vol. 1, page 120
2 CONT *6.^ a b c d e Manning, J. A., Speakers, pb. Myers and Company, London p. 242, 245
2 CONT *7.^ Bell, H. W. Notes and Queries, 12 S. VIII. FEB. 26, 1921 p. 175
2 CONT *8.^ , Notes and Queries, Vol. 193 (24), 1948, pp. 515-6, OUP, Wilberforce-Bell, ‘Sir Robert Bell: Speaker of the House of Commons,1572.,’ Lord Chief Baron of the Exchequer 1576- 7 [2]
2 CONT *9.^ a b c d Bell, R.R.L., Tudor Bell's Sound Out, Rolls-privately pb, 7 September, 2006
2 CONT *10.^ a b c MacCaffrey, W. T., 'Cecil William, first Baron Burghley (1520/21–1598),’ODNB, OUP, 2004 accessed 15 April 2005
2 CONT *11.^ Bell, Robert in Venn, J. & J. A., Alumni Cantabrigienses, Cambridge University Press, 10 vols, 1922–1958.
2 CONT *12.^ Williamson, J. B., The History of the Temple of London, London, pb. John Murray (2nd ed. 1925)
2 CONT *13.^ Summerson, H., Correspondence, 4 February 2007, (Opinion; in matters of religion) [3] [4]
2 CONT *14.^ House of Commons, Journal Volume 1, 6 March 1559, pb. 1802, Sponsor BHOL: History of Parliament Trust
2 CONT *15.^ MacCaffrey, W. T., ‘Hatton, Sir Christopher (c.1540-1591)’, ODNB, OUP, 2004 accessed 7 May 2005
2 CONT *16.^ Bawlf, S., The Secret Voyage of Sir Francis Drake 1577-1580, pb. Walker Publishing Co. 2003, p. 67
2 CONT *17.^ Hussey, C., Beaupre Hall Wisbech, Coventry Homes and Gardens Old & New, pb. Country Life, 1923
2 CONT *18.^ Evans, H. M. E., ‘Peyton, Sir John (1544-1630)’, ODNB, OUP, 2004 accessed 7 May 2005
2 CONT *19.^ Coll Arm Ms, The Visitations of Norfolk, 1563, William Hervey 1589, Robert Cooke and 1613, John Raven, p. 33-34 Bell. Beaupre., Ed. Walter Rye, London 1891
2 CONT *20.^ The National Archives, UK, Catalog Reference Prob. 11/111, Image Reference 565 (C)
2 CONT *21.^ Kupperman, K., Puritan Colonization from Providence Island through the Western Design, The William and Mary [5]
2 CONT *22.^ Woodcock, T., and, Robinson, J. M.,Heraldry in Historic Houses of Great Britain, The National Trust, pb. 2000 [6]
2 CONT *23.^ MacDonald, W., Documentary source book of American History, 1606-1913,1910-20-21 [7]
2 CONT *24.^ Salt, S. P., ‘Dering, Sir Edward, first baronet (1598-1644)’, ODNB, OUP, 2004 accessed 23 May 2005
2 CONT *Likeness
2 CONT *NPG, London. (1) Robert Bell, Esq., Speaker 1572, possibly by the artist T. Athlow, (2) Sir Robert Bell, Chief Baron of the Exchequer 1577, by William Camden Edwards, after unknown artist, and the British Museum [10]
2 CONT *____________________
