1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Tufton,_2nd_Earl_of_Thanet
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tufton, 2nd Earl of Thanet (15 December 1608 – 7 May 1664) was an English nobleman and supporter of Charles I of England. He was the eldest son of Nicholas Tufton, 1st Earl of Thanet and Lady Frances Cecil granddaughter of Wi
2 CONC lliam Cecil, 1st Baron Burghley .
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 April 1629, he married Lady Margaret Sackville (1614–1676), daughter of Richard Sackville, 3rd Earl of Dorset and Lady Anne Clifford. They had eleven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas Tufton, 3rd Earl of Thanet (1631–1679)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Margaret Tufton (b. 13 July 1636), married George Coventry, 3rd Baron Coventry on 18 July 1653
2 CONT
2 CONT John Tufton, 4th Earl of Thanet (1638–1680)
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Tufton, 5th Earl of Thanet (1640–1684)
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Tufton, 6th Earl of Thanet (1644–1729)
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Sackville Tufton (c.1647–1721)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne Tufton, died young
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Frances Tufton, married Henry Drax, died without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Cicely Tufton (2 June 1648 – 30 December 1672), married on 12 February 1667 Christopher Hatton, 1st Viscount Hatton
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Mary Tufton (d. February 1674), married Sir William Walter, 2nd Baronet (d. 1693)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Anne Tufton, married Samuel Grimston
2 CONT
2 CONT Thanet was a staunch Cavalier, and in 1642 led a regiment to try to raise a rebellion in Sussex in support of Sir William Brockman in Kent. However, Brockman's revolt quickly collapsed and the Earl was forced to surrender. He suff
2 CONC ered considerably from confiscations and sequestrations of his large estates during the English Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among his properties was Bodiam Castle, purchased from the Levett family in 1639, which Thanet sold for £6,000 in 1644.
2 CONT
2 CONT Curiously, he was succeeded as Earl of Thanet by four of his sons in succession, the first three having no issue.
