1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Stewart,_9th_Seigneur_d%27Aubigny
2 CONT
2 CONT '''George Stuart''', 9th Seigneur d'Aubigny was born on 17 July 1618 at Bath House, Holborn, London, England.1 He was the son of Esmé Stuart, 3rd Duke of Lennox and Katherine Clifton, Baroness Clifton (of Leighton Bromswold).1 He 
2 CONC married Lady Katherine Howard, daughter of Theophilus Howard, 2nd Earl of Suffolk and Lady Elizabeth Home, in 1638 in a secret marriage.1 He died on 23 October 1642 at age 24 at Edgehill, killed in action.1 He was buried at Christ
2 CONC  Church Cathedral, Oxford, Oxfordshire, England.1 He died intestate and his estate was administered to Katherine Lady Aubigny on 8 June 1647.George Stuart, 9th Seigneur d'Aubigny was educated at Paris, France.1 He succeeded to the
2 CONC  title of 9th Seigneur d'Aubigny, de Berry [France, 1423] in 1632.  He fought in the Battle of Edgehill on 23 October 1642.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT George Stewart (or Stuart), 9th Seigneur d'Aubigny (17 July 1618 – 23 October 1642) was a Scottish nobleman and Royalist commander in the English Civil War. He was the son of Esmé Stewart, 3rd Duke of Lennox, and his wife Katherin
2 CONC e Clifton, 2nd Baroness Clifton, and the brother of James Stewart, 1st Duke of Richmond and 4th Duke of Lennox, and of the Royalist commander Lord Bernard Stewart.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart, his older brother Henry, and younger brother Ludovic were brought up at Aubigny in France as Roman Catholics under the charge of their paternal grandmother, the old Duchess-Dowager, Katherine de Balsac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart's father, the Duke of Lennox, died in 1624, and Stewart became a ward of his cousin, King Charles I of England. He inherited the Lordship of Aubigny at the age of 14 on the death of his elder brother Henry in 1632. By 1633
2 CONC , he was a student at the Collège de Navarre, part of the University of Paris, and he did homage to Louis XIII of France for the lordship of Aubigny on 5 August 1636, shortly after his eighteenth birthday. Later that year he moved
2 CONC  to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1638 he secretly married Katherine Howard, the daughter of Theophilus Howard, 2nd Earl of Suffolk, and Elizabeth Home, without her father's consent, offending his guardian the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT The portrait of Stewart by Anthony van Dyck, now in the National Portrait Gallery, London, may have been painted to mark his marriage; the Latin inscription can be translated "Love is stronger than I am" which may allude to his co
2 CONC nflicting loyalties. His younger brothers Lords John and Bernard are shown in a still more famous double Van Dyck portrait in the National Gallery, London - the iconic image of Cavaliers. They were also both killed in the Civil Wa
2 CONC r.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Stewart and Katherine Howard had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Charles Stewart, later 3rd Duke of Richmond and 6th Duke of Lennox (1638–1672)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Katherine Stewart, later Baroness Clifton of Leighton Bromswold (1640–1702)
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought with the French against the Spanish in the Battle of Montjuïc (1641).
2 CONT
2 CONT As civil war loomed in England, Stewart joined the forces of King Charles at York where he was knighted on 18 April 1642 along with his brother Bernard. He was mortally wounded in the first pitched battle of the war at Edgehill on
2 CONC  Sunday 23 October 1642, aged 24. He was buried in Christ Church Cathedral, Oxford. The lordship of Aubigny passed to his next brother, Ludovic, on his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Stewart,_9th_Seigneur_d%27Aubigny
2 CONT
2 CONT George Stewart (or Stuart), 9th Seigneur d'Aubigny (17 July 1618 – 23 October 1642) was a Scottish nobleman and Royalist commander in the English Civil War. He was the son of Esmé Stewart, 3rd Duke of Lennox, and his wife Katherin
2 CONC e Clifton, 2nd Baroness Clifton, and the brother of James Stewart, 1st Duke of Richmond and 4th Duke of Lennox, and of the Royalist commander Lord Bernard Stewart.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart, his older brother Henry, and younger brother Ludovic were brought up at Aubigny in France as Roman Catholics under the charge of their paternal grandmother, the old Duchess-Dowager, Katherine de Balsac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stewart's father, the Duke of Lennox, died in 1624, and Stewart became a ward of his cousin, King Charles I of England. He inherited the Lordship of Aubigny at the age of 14 on the death of his elder brother Henry in 1632. By 1633
2 CONC , he was a student at the Collège de Navarre, part of the University of Paris, and he did homage to Louis XIII of France for the lordship of Aubigny on 5 August 1636, shortly after his eighteenth birthday.[3] Later that year he mo
2 CONC ved to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1638 he secretly married Katherine Howard, the daughter of Theophilus Howard, 2nd Earl of Suffolk, and Elizabeth Home, without her father's consent, offending his guardian the king.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT George Stewart and Katherine Howard had two children:[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Charles Stewart, later 3rd Duke of Richmond and 6th Duke of Lennox (1638–1672)
2 CONT     Katherine Stewart, later Baroness Clifton of Leighton Bromswold (1640–1702)
