1 NOTE of "Pick-thorn Farm in the com of Shropshire,Eng; later Malvern Hill one of his
2 CONT father's plantations. Pickthorn is a township in the parish of Stottesden, co.
2 CONT Salop, 6 miles SW of Bridgnorth. Given land on the ridge, and appears to have
2 CONT been the first to have lived at "Malvern Hills". Will probated 1 Apr. 1697,
2 CONT buried in his garden at Malvern Hills as directed in his will.
1 NOTE Justice of Henrico Co.1678,sheriff,1680,1688, coroner,1680, and Burgess,1677.
1 NOTE WILL: VA, Henrico Co, Book I, pg 166:
2 CONT Thomas Cocke:
2 CONT Wife: Margaret
2 CONT Dau. Mary Cocke who married William Randolph.
2 CONT Grandsons: Peter Wynn, Major Joshua Wynn, Robert Wynn, William Wynn, Francis
2 CONT    Wynn.
2 CONT Dau: ____ Jones wife of Abraham Jones
2 CONT Grandchildren: Margaret Jones and Peter Jones
2 CONT Dau or son in law:___Jones or Peter Jones.
2 CONT
2 CONT VIRGINIA GENEALOGIES, CD-ROM DISK, VOL. 1-20:
2 CONT Vol. 6, pg 143: Quieries:
2 CONT WYNN (WINN)-COCKE-GOODRICH.  Margaret Wynn, dau. of Joshua and granddau. of
2 CONT Margaret Cocke, married Edward Goodrich, Brunswick Co., VA, ca 1715.  Want
2 CONT Marriages of their children and any other information...Mrs. James B. Burch,
2 CONT 703 Gordon Ave, Thomasville, GA.
2 CONT
2 CONT VA GENEALOGIES OF VIRGINIA FAMILIES II, Cl-FI, PG 109
1 NOTE NOTE: Our Kin: Captain Thomas Cocke was born in 1638 and died in 1696. He was Justice of the Peace 1678-80; Sheriff of Henrico 1688; and member of House of Burgesses. He married Mrs. Margaret (Wood) Jones, widow of Peter Wood, who
2 CONC  came to Virginia in 1620 on the vessel "Margaret and John" when ten years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~coxfamily/pdffamilygenealogys/margaret%20wood.pdf
2 CONT Thomas succeeded to his fathers estate and lived at Bremo. He was one of the members of the Henrico Co. Court in 1680 and High Sheriff from that year to 1688.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES FOR THOMAS COCKE: M.H./B. (this must refer to Malvern Hill and Bremo) Sheriff, Henrico Co. Owned early ferry, tannery, flour mill:
2 CONT Malvern Hill Plantation, part of Father's Bremo Estate, was battle ground in the Civil War, July 1862. Built Malvern House late 1600's.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: heritagequestonline.com
2 CONT Also listed as a child of Thomas is William (the 4th child) born abt. 1670 died 1717 married Sarah Dennis , probably a daughter of Richard Dennis a member of the House of Burgess; Captain Robert Dennis who was sent over by Oliver 
2 CONC Cromwell in 1652 to establish his authority in the Colony, may have been immigrant ancestor of the Dennis Family. Children: William Temperanace, Catherine, Mary and Sarah. (I had previous information that listed this William as so
2 CONC n of Richard b. 1602)
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: http://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/va/henrico/wills/wills003.txt
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen, I Hugh MACKMYELL (Mackgill), being of body sick and weak, but through
2 CONT Gods goodness in perfect health of mind and memory----------------------make this my Last Will and
2 CONT Testament this 7th of November 1678 in manner and form following.
2 CONT I bequeath my soul unto the hands of Almighty who gave it me and my body to the ground from
2 CONT whence it came.
2 CONT I do give and bequeath unto Agnes COCKE daughter of Thomas COCKE, my good friend and
2 CONT neighbor, one black cow calf, of this last years breed whose name is known by the name of
2 CONT strawberry, to be disposed of by her after my decease as she shall thinks good of.
2 CONT I do --------------, and bequeath unto Temperance COCKE, daughter unto the aforesaid COCKE,
2 CONT one brown cow calf likewise of this last years breed to be delivered to her aforesaid father or
2 CONT guardians after my decease. and by him or them to be improved by him and them for increase until
2 CONT she shall otherwise dispose thereof according as she thinks good..........
2 CONT ....... Proved in Henrico Court the 1 Feb 1679.
2 CONT *****
2 CONT In the Name of God Amen. I John CUMBER, make this my last Will and Testament, being sick
2 CONT in body, but in sound and perfect memory, thanks be to God.
2 CONT Item: First I bequeath my soul into the hands of his maker.......
2 CONT Item: I make Mr. Thomas COCKE my whole and sole executor desiring him to except of the
2 CONT trouble and to take all my estate into his hands of what natural or orginal source and----------
2 CONT ---------as my estate will go to satisfy all my just debts that that shall appear to be justly due by law.
2 CONT The testfication of this to do my last will and testament , there hereunto set my hand and seal
2 CONT this 30th of March 1679.
2 CONT John Cumber
2 CONT Witnessed in the Presence of
2 CONT Francis Radford
2 CONT Samuel F. Moody
2 CONT Charles White
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: <http://www.usahistory.com/wars/malvern.htm>
2 CONT U S A History - WARS - Civil War - Battle of Malvern Hill - 1862
2 CONT The Seven Days Campaign ended with the indecisive Battle of Malvern Hill (Virginia). General Lee had started the campaign with an attack on McClellan's Union army at Mechanicsville, Virginia, on June 26. The Confederates gradually
2 CONC  pushed the timid McClellan back to the safety of his base on the James River. McClellan's Peninsular Campaign had failed and his army was withdrawn. Casualties for the week stood at Union, 15,849; Confederate, 20,141.
2 CONT Carruth, Gorton. "The Encyclopedia of American Facts and Dates". 10th Ed. New York: Harper Collins Publishers. ©1997.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Information on the will of Thomas Cocke, d. 1697, can be found at:
2 CONT http://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/va/henrico/wills/wills1655-1800.txt
2 CONT HENRICO COUNTY - INDEX TO WILLS AND ADMINISTRATIONS, 1655-1800
2 CONT Contributed by: Joan Renfrow
2 CONT The Library of Virginia
2 CONT [http://eagle.vsla.edu/torrence/virtua-basic.html]
1 NOTE [georgeholt.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT From Edward Cocke file the following:
2 CONT Born only 6 years after the family arrived in America, and only 34 years after Jamestown was settled. Per Compendium of American Genealogy (pg.261) he was the eldest son of Richard Cocke and succeeded to his father's estate. He li
2 CONC ved in Bremo, Henrico County, VA. Owned over 5,000 acres of land, a flour mill, tow tanneries and a wool textile mill. Appointed Sheriff of Henrico County between 1680-1688. Also a member of the Virginia House of Burgesses.
2 CONT In his own words he said "My plantation, Malvern Hills, was on the James River opposite Turkey Island. Here we had a flour mill, tobacco houses, orchards, two tanneries, and looms to make quality linen. Two centuries later, my pro
2 CONC perty was the site of the bloody Civil War battle of 1 July 1862. For a seventeenth century colonist, I was quite wealthy. My estate, including what I advanced to my children before my death, included some 5,000 acres of land.  I 
2 CONC had busy public life, serving as burgess, justice, Sheriff, and coroner at various times. After the mother of my four sons and two daughters died, Peter Jones’ widow, Margaret, became my wife. Margaret’s stepfather was one of Virg
2 CONC inia’s principal explorers and traders, Abraham Wood. I was a sixty-year-old grandfather when I died. Among the legacies in my will was 1,000 pounds of tobacco towards purchasing a bell for the church (http://www.virginians.com/re
2 CONC direct.htm?topics&3434)
2 CONT He was Thomas Cocke of Pickthorn Farm in 1688 when he sold land he and his brothers inherited from their father to Thomas East.  
2 CONT Cocke was a burgess representing Henrico County in 1677. His other community service was as a county justice (1678), sheriff (1680, 1688), and county coroner (1680). 
2 CONT On 4 October 1675 Mr. Thomas Cocke got patents for two tracts of land: 1,983 acres in Charles City County and 3,087 acres in Henrico County.  The governor granted the land in consideration of Thomas having transported 102 individu
2 CONC als to the Colony. As Thomas Cocke Sr. he added 1,650 aces for 33 headrights 20 October 1688.  
2 CONT The identity of the first wife of Thomas Cocke, the mother of his children, is unknown. We do know he married second Margaret—, a stepdaughter of Gen. Abraham Wood and the widow of Peter Jones. 
2 CONT The 1678-will of Hugh Mackmyell left to the daughters of Thomas Cocke, (my good friend and neighbor,) a black calf each.  In 1687 Thomas Cocke Sr. and Margaret, his wife, gave a 200-acre portion of (Malvern Hills” to his son of th
2 CONC e same name. 
1 NOTE 1685 LAND RECORD: VA, Henrico Co., Abstracted, part I, pg 30:
2 CONT p. 328.  John Pleasants of Curles, Henrico Co., for love, good will & affecton to my three children John, Elizabeth, and Jospeh Pleasants, all born of the body of my dear wife Jane, formerly wife and executrix of Samuel Tucker, de
2 CONC c'd as follows:
2 CONT To son John, 2 negroes, Toby and his wife Betty, and 2 mares.
2 CONT To my daughter Elizabeth Pleasants, 2 negroes, Will and Nanne. and 3 mares branded "EP"
2 CONT To son Joseph, 2 negroes, Kate and Tony, and a mare purchased of Ben Hatcher.If any child dies under 16, his part to the other tow 
2 CONT 1 Aug 1685.
2 CONT Signed: John Pleasants.
2 CONT
2 CONT LAND RECORDS OF HENRICO COUNTY, VA; 1677-1705:
2 CONT pg 69:  p. 297.  James Cocke, son of Thomas Cocke, Sr., of Henrico Co., has taken to wife Elizabeth Pleasants, my daughter, born of body of my loving wife Jane Pleasants, formerly relict and executrix of Samuel Tucker, dec.  I Joh
2 CONC n Pleasants, therefore for love & affection to said Elizabeth, give to said James and Elizabeth cocke a plantation purchaed by me of Abraham Childres on Curles Swamp, 70 acres, and also a plantation I purchased of Henry Rowen and 
2 CONC Robert Woodson, Sr., 400 acres, for their lives, and then to their oldest surviving son.  May 1692
2 CONT Wit: Charles Bartholomew, Will'm (MP) Peirce
2 CONT Signed: John Pleasants   Recorded 16 May 1692.
1 NOTE From Cocke Family Notes by Judy Canant October 2000
2 CONT Thomas COCKE, Capt b: 1638 in of "Pickthorne Farm", Henrico Co. 
2 CONT (Title is for County Military Establishment) d: Aft. December 10, 1696 in Henrico Co, VA (date of Will, probated April 1, 1697) (Was living at "Malvern Hills")
2 CONT (His father gave him Malvern Hills in his will by deed. about 1663 near time of his first marriage. When Thomas died, more than 30 years afterwards, no doubt he likewise was buried in the Garden at Malvern HIlls in complian with t
2 CONC he wish expressed in his will dated 10 Dec 1696, probated 1 Apr 1697 (Henrico Co Recds, Book 5, 1684-1697, pp 684, foll, Archives Div., VA State Library.) Accrding to Dr Moore, a historian of this family, no tragrave can be found 
2 CONC now at Malvern Hills. a Justice of Henrico, Along wi his bro, Richard the Elder, Genalogies of Va Families, Vol II, Genealogy of the COCKE Family in Virginia, by James C Southall, p 106; Sheriff of Henrico Co and Coroner; Thomas &
2 CONC  Richard own "Cocke's Ferry" (still known as CF in 1810) and an "Ordinary" at the Ct Hs in Varina; Thomas deeds 625 acres of land to Wm Randolph (very probably Turkey Island); Was living on NS of Turkey Creek.
1 NOTE Colonial Wills of Henrico County, Virginia Part One 1654-1737
2 CONT by Benjamin B. Wesiger III  (1976)
2 CONT page 44
2 CONT Henrico County Wills and Deeds 1688-1697
2 CONT p. 684
2 CONT Will of THOMAS COCKE
2 CONT To loving wife MARGARET, my plantation I Live on for life, a mulatto woman Betty and her sons Harry and Daniel.
2 CONT To son STEPHEN COCKE, Jack Long, with all tools belonging to him and all things that belong at the mill and 400 acres of that patent, of which I sold part to Thomas Davis, joining the land of Davis; all land north of the White Oak
2 CONC  Swamp not given, and to his daughter AGNES, a feather bed, etc., at age 18.
2 CONT To son JAMES COCKE, 625 acres and part of a divident which Capt. Randolph and John Watson had of me out of a patent where my son THOMAS COCKE was seated, near Daniel Price's plantation; also negroes, 2 of which to atay with my wif
2 CONC e and daughter for life, also furnishings; and to his daughter ELIZABETH, the indian girl Cate.
2 CONT 1000 lbs. tobacco to buy a bell for the Henrico Parish Church.
2 CONT To son WILLIAM COCKE, 1200 acres of land, including a patent, out of which I sold John Pleasants a part, also livestock, and to his daughter also livestock at age 18 or marriage.
2 CONT After the death of my daughter AGNES HARWOOD, I give to grandson THOMAS HARWOOD, a mulatto girl Sue, but to serve my daughter during her life, but she is not to do "man-like" work. If she be abused, she is to choose which of my so
2 CONC ns to serve.
2 CONT To other grandsons JOSEPH and SAMUEL HARWOOD, a grey mare.
2 CONT To grandaughter AGNES HARWOOD, a cow & cow calf.
2 CONT To grandaughter JOYCE HARWOOD, a ewe and ewe lamb.
2 CONT To grandson THOMAS HARWOOD, a young mare.
2 CONT To daughter TEMPERANCE HARWOOD, a negro girl Pegg (one of Mingo's daughters) plus household items including a silver tankard purchased by her own mother.
2 CONT To grandson THOMAS COCKE, the plantation I live on and all land on south side of White Oak Swamp Run included in patent of my Father's and Mr. John Beauchamp and according to division made between my brother RICHARD COCKE and John
2 CONC  Pleasants, Attorneys of said John Beahchamp and myself, reserving to my wife what is due her; also my son, his father THOMAS COCKE, to have privileges during life to make  any use of said house and furniture.
2 CONT To grandson JAMES COCKE, a son of THOMAS COCKE, the land where Gill Fugitt now lives, with all land joining thereto on south side of western branch of Herrin Creek.
2 CONT To son STEPHEN COCKE, all my wearing clothes.
2 CONT To grandson HENRY COCKE, all the residue of land within that patent of Monquies that shall be found to belong to me after Davis, STEPHEN, and JAMES COCKE have their parts.
2 CONT To grandson _________ COCKE, calves at age 18.
2 CONT To loving wife and dutiful son JAMES COCKE, all residue of my estate, 1/2 to each, and they to be executors.
2 CONT Dated 10 Dec. 1691
2 CONT Codicil: Void the part of will mentioning leaving mulatto Sue to THOMAS COCKE and his mother and give to grandson THOMAS COCKE 4000 lbs. tobacco at age 22. The mulatto Sue is to remain with my wife for her lifetime and then to go 
2 CONC to my executors.
2 CONT Wit: Jacob Ware, Thomas Smythe, Thomas Topping
2 CONT Probated 1 April 1697
1 NOTE Another version of his will
2 CONT
2 CONT Henrico Co., Va. Will Book Part I 1677-1737
2 CONT Pages 684 – 689
2 CONT Henrico County April the first 1697
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of god amen. I Thomas Cocke being at this present time ____ be it _____ of sound mind ____ is fact necessary I thanks be to god but considering the instability of all mankind have made this my last will and testament.
2 CONT
2 CONT First I give and bequesth my soul to you my maker hoping that for the _______ of Jesus Christ his only Son and my only Saviour and Redeamer that he will pardon and forgive me all my Sins _____
2 CONT
2 CONT ______ of his Death and Sufferings as a full satisfaction for all mine offences according to his faithful promises and being well assured that his word is true and _____ all mighty forever suffiliant to _____ unto my body from ___
2 CONC _ such and reunite it to my Soul again it is both body and Soul may enter into Eternall Joy through Jesus Christ my lord and only Saviour to dwell with him in perfect bliss for ever and ever amen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I bequesth my body to the Earth to be decently buryed at ye descression of my Executors (in my Garden)
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my loving wife Margaret Cocke my plantation I now live on with its ____ lands and all other housing _____ (Except the Farm house and that Tobacco house below the hill and also that land and farm yard 
2 CONC between the Creek from my son Thomas lives to the main Road that goes down the hill to the poplar ___ including the poplar Neck) but all the rest of my land belonging to this ______ I _____ in with all orchids, Gardens and other _
2 CONC ______ thereunto belonging I give unto my loving wife with the housing above not exacpted I ____ in as followith. That she my said wife during the time of her widowhood shall peaceably enjoy the ______ to her own _____ use and ben
2 CONC efitt provided she shall live & abide herself in person upon the said plantation and the sd. All housing and other things belonging to the said plantation in good repair as all widows ought to do but in case she shall either marry
2 CONC  or remove herself from living on the sd. Plantation above sd. ______ ______ is ________ ________ ________ _______ it part thereof disreing ____ _____ at _____ life. I also give unto my wife the Mulatto woman by name Betty and her
2 CONC  two sons Harry & Daniell with the two beds in her Chamber with all the furniture belonging to them I above give her all my loggs Except those that belong to the Mill and I give her my mare Riding horse and her Choice of my other 
2 CONC lands on Mar__ belonging to me further my will is although I have said that I give the two boys to my wife it is with this provision that my grandson ______ Wy---- shall have one of them which she pleaseth before ____ her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give unto my Son Stephen Cocke Jack Long with all the tools belonging to him at Mill with his ____ and bed and pott and all other things that doth Really belong to the Mill and I also give unto him my above son four hundred
2 CONC  Acres of land out of that patent of land which I sold part of unto Thomas Davis Joyning in that land of Davis and I’ve to take its breadith and length prosson ____ was the land there bounded by patient will _______ to him the sd.
2 CONC  Stephen Cocke and his heirs forever and I _____ give unto him my son Stephen all my land on North Side of White Oak it at is not given from that Marica Runn of the sd. White Oak until it Joyns in that I gave my Son William to him
2 CONC  the said Stephen and his heirs forever. And I give to his daughter Agness one _______ feather bed banister and all other Descent furniture and ___ to be del’d to her when She shall attain to the age of Eighteen years or marry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my Son James Cocke and to his heirs forever that Six hundred and twenty five acres of land that doth belong to me and is part of that divident of land which Capt. Randolph and John Watson had of _____
2 CONC  out of that patient where my Son Thomas once seated with Daniell Price plantation. I also give unto him my Son James these Negroes and slaver that is Jack White and Indian Bob with Minger and then only these two last are to remai
2 CONC n and be with my wife by this to be his pledge as for her own if represent and ______ of not during the lives of her widowhood but not longer. She my sd. Wife to find the negroes clothing and dyatt and all other ______ during thei
2 CONC r abode with her for if also it all sister Mary or dye before the sd. Two negroes then they are both to Return to him my sd. Son, further my will is that my Son James shall have the one half of the hides that shall be in the tan p
2 CONC itts _______ at my decease provided he let Jack White finish the tanning of them all. I give to my Son James one foot tubed and furniture as it is usually furnished being the bed over the hall and I give unto sd. Son James the Ind
2 CONC ian Girle Hannah and to my daughter Elizabeth the Indian Girle Cate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give to _______ for the buying of a bell for Henrico Parrish Church one thousand pounds of Tabacco and cask.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give unto my Son William Cocke all that land be it more or less item twelve hundred acres that doth belong to me includes that patient out of which I sold Mr. John Pleasant a part to him and his heirs forever. I also give u
2 CONC nto my Son William one yoke of Oxen and four Ewes besides what I have already given him into his possesion and I give to his daughter two young Ewes with lamb or lambs by their sides to be delivered her at ye day of her marriage o
2 CONC r when she shall attain to the age of Eighteen years which shall first happen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath (after the death of my Agness Harwood) unto my grandson Thomas Harwood one Mulatto girle called Sue the which sd. Mulatto girle is to be my Exec’d delivered unto my sd. Daughter Agness within one month aft
2 CONC er my decese to serve stay and abide with her my siad daughter to be imployed by her in any service or imployment that she shall think fitt to imploy her in Excepting beating at mortar or working in the Ground or such man like wor
2 CONC k without ________ my will is that she may ____ to my daughters own person and that the ______ may be well and kind by _____ for she hath been a great help to me in my ______ . I also give to her the ____ as Loan and all the slave
2 CONC s and harness to the same with all other operationale thereunto belonging all which is to be equally enjoyed by my Daughter for to be by the Girle Sue used if she my daughter shall see fit but at the death of my daughter the said 
2 CONC Mulatto Sue with the ______ and its appertenances doth belong unto her Son Thomas and it is my will that they ______ by delivered to him and if my sd. Daughter shall think fitt at any time before her death to surrender the said Mu
2 CONC latto Sue and _______ unto her Son Thomas to whome they are Given it at then She may without any ____ or _____ provided she does it volentary and of her own free will without any _____ of compultion and further my will is that ye 
2 CONC Girle Sue be well used in all her time of service whoever shall happen to be her Master or Mistress for if she shall be by any of them Notoriously abused my will is that She have liberty to choose which of my Sons she pleases for 
2 CONC her Master to live with.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give my two other Grandsons Joseph & Samuel Harwood a young Mare to Run as a Joyest Stock between them. I give my Grandaughter Agness Harwood one young cow and cow calf. I give my Grandaughter Joyce Harwood one Ewe and Ewe lamb.
2 CONC  I give unto my Grandson Thomas Harwood one young mare.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my daughter Temperance Harwood one negro Girle called Pegy being one of Mingoe’s daughters. I also give her the furniture bed over my wives chamber with all the usuall furniture there unto belonging t
2 CONC o and four of the turkey cloth chairs which usually are in that chamber where the bed commonly called hers doth stand. I also give her the Silver Tankard, it being purchased by her own Mother and was her desire that she her daught
2 CONC er should have it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give unto my grandson Thomas Cocke all the land housings, orchards, Garden and all the things affixed to the same that is to say the plantation I now live on and the land belonging to use of this resident and also I give un
2 CONC to him all the land and in the South side of White Oak Swamp run included in that patient of my Father and Mr. John Beauchamp and according to the division made between my two brothers Richard Cocke and Mr. John Pleasants the atto
2 CONC rney of the said John Beauchamp & myself to him and his heirs forever allways Reserving to my wife what I have given to her out of the said land and also that my Son Thomas Cocke to further have priviledge during his naturall life
2 CONC  to make use of any of the said land to plant or for timber for his own proper use that shall not be planted on or inclosed within a fence by his son for his use. I give unto my sd. Grandson the long table & two _______ in the hal
2 CONC l but my wife to have the use of items as long as she doth abide in the house if she desire it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give and bequeath unto my grandson James Cocke the son of Thomas Cocke the land and plantation where Gill F. Fugitt now lives on with all the land belonging to me adjoining thereto on the inward or South Side of the Western
2 CONC  branch of Herrin Creek to the uppermost on the North Side of Mangines Runn and _____ till it comes to the bounds thereof near Gilly’s path and the breadth on that side of Mangines Runn to extend to the Eastward most branch of the
2 CONC  two branches of Herrin Creek above the Mouth of Mongiries Runn and on up its sd. Runn till it comes to the head line of the sd. Land in that place and so along the sd. Line till it come to the Corner tree near Gilly’s path, all w
2 CONC hich land as above sd. I give to my sd. Grandson and his heirs for ever be there more or less four hundred acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give unto my son Stephen Cocke all my wearing clothes of what quality soever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give unto my grandson Henry Cocke all the Residence of land included within that Patient of Monguies that shalt be found to belong to me after Davis Stephen and James Cocke have their parts out of it be it more or less to h
2 CONC im and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give unto my grandson Wm. (?) Cocke two cows and calves when he shall attaine to ye age of sixteen years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I give unto my loving wife & dutiful Son James Cocke all the residue of my estate which is undisposed of my this my will to be theirs divided between them the one half to my wife and other half to my sd. Son James makind him 
2 CONC and my loving wife my Executor and Excutress of this my will and testament as witness my hand and seal this tenth day of december one thousand six hundred ninety six.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Cocke
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed and Sealed in presence of
2 CONT
2 CONT Before signing and sealing this my will I do Revoke and make wholly this sd. If it had never been that part of my will wherein is exprest the Gift of a Mulatto wench called Sue unto Thomas Harwood and his mother and instead thereo
2 CONC f give unto my grandson Thomas Harwood four thousand pounds of Tobacco to be paid him when he shall attain to the age of two and twenty years and that the sd. Mulatto Girle doth remaine with my wife during my wives natural lifeand
2 CONC  after her decease I doth give her to my Excutor and all their parts & clauses of my will to stand inaltrable.
2 CONT
