1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Richard Cocke===
2 CONT * Birth:  	12/10/1639  	
2 CONT * Death: 11/20/1706, Bremo, Henrico, Virginia 
2 CONT * Parents: 	Lt. Col. Richard Cocke, of Bremo and Temperance Bailey
2 CONT * Married: Elizabeth b 1643
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth is said to have been the daughter of John Hartwell of 'Swann's Point', Surry Co., VA; gr dau of William Hartwell, J.P. James City. But this is not possible; John Hartwell son of William of James City was born about 1681,
2 CONC  some 50 years after this Elizabeth was born.  It is a mixup with '''A later Elizabeth (Hartwell) Cocke'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Richard Cocke II and Elizabeth are:
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. +Richard Cocke III, b. 1672, BREMO,VA, d. 1720.
2 CONT *2. +Elizabeth Cocke, b. 1675, d. date unknown.
2 CONT *3. +Martha Cocke, b. 1677, d. 1757.
2 CONT *4. +John Cocke, d. 1699.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT Known as Richard "the elder" Cocke to distinguish him from his young half-brother of the same name. He inherited the 640 acre "Bremo" plantation in Henrico from his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT had 3 surviving children mention in Richard's will: Richard, Elizabeth, and Martha.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Will bequeaths slaves to children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1895 The Virginia magazine of history and genealogy estimated his estate to be worth $35,000. According to Measuring Worth, in 2010 $35,000 from 1895 was worth:
2 CONT
2 CONT * $4,350,000.00 using the unskilled wage
2 CONT * $7,440,000.00 using the Production Worker Compensation
2 CONT * $7,430,000.00 using the nominal GDP per capita
2 CONT * $32,900,000.00 using the relative share of GDP
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned, with his brother Thomas, a Ferry and an Ordinary at the courthouse It was still "Cocke's Ferry" in 1810.
2 CONT
2 CONT SERVICE: a surveyor of the county and justice, 1678. A member of the county court 1678, 1680, 1699, and probally during all of this period.
2 CONT
2 CONT VA Gen 40: 243-248 Oct 1996 apparently shows that this Cocke family and the one of Princess Anne Co. seen in early Moseley records may be all the same one of Constantine area in Cornwall England.
2 CONT
2 CONT From: "Adventurers of Purse and Person":
2 CONT
2 CONT Called "the elder" to distinguish him from his young half-brother by the same name.
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited the 640 acre "Bremo" plantation in Henrico from his father and served as surveyor of the county and justice, 1678.  He was associated with his brother Thomas is various enterprises, among them the operation of a ferry
2 CONC  across the James River, known as "Cocke's Ferry" as late as 1810, and the operation of an ordinary near the courthouse at Varina.  He held 2180 acres in Henrico County, 1704.
2 CONT
2 CONT His will, 19 Nov 1706-02 Dec 1706, named his children and grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT RICHARD COXE (THE ELDER), b. December 10, 1639, Bremo, Curles, Henrico County, VA; d. November 20, 1706, Bremo, Fluvanna County, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT He resided at Malvern Hill:  http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~marshall/esmd66.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Father:  Richard COCKE , Sr. Lt Col. 1602 - 1665
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: : Temperance BAILEY
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers:
2 CONT
2 CONT *   1.  Thomas COCKE , I
2 CONT *    2.  Richard COCKE , the Elder  1639-1706 
2 CONT *    3.  Elizabeth COCKE 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father's Family 2 : Mary ASTON
2 CONT
2 CONT *    1.  William COCKE 
2 CONT *    2.  John COCKE 
2 CONT *    3. +Richard COCKE , the Younger 
2 CONT *    4.  Edward COCKE 
2 CONT
2 CONT .
2 CONT
