2 DATE 7 JAN 1714
2 PLAC Chelsea, London, England
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT http://www.cyberancestors.com/cummins/ps110/ps110_358.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Capell
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=79189445
2 CONT
2 CONT http://histfam.familysearch.org/getperson.php?personID=I62154&tree=EuropeRoyalNobleHous
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p1378.htm#i13776
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================
2 CONT
2 CONT from ''Gardening Women'' by Catherine Horwood  2010
2 CONT
2 CONT pp11
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mary grew up surrounded by garden lovers; her eldest brother, Arthur, later Earl of Essex, had a passion for trees and, in consultation with John Evelyn, created what was probably the first 'wooded' garden in the country at Cassi
2 CONC obury in Hertfordshire. ... Another brother, Sir Henry Capel, built what may have been the first conservatory in Britain in the late 1670's at his gardens in Kew, on the site that was to become the Royal Botanic Gardens, and he wa
2 CONC s certainly a source of seeds for his sister"
2 CONT
2 CONT She had a collection of plants running into thousands, and she kept detailed records of them all.   Her contribution to late 17th Century British horticulture was inestimable.   She cultivated extensive gardens at Badminton, north
2 CONC  of Bath, and Beaufort House in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT She corresponded regularly with Sir Hans Sloane, and was later to bequeath to him the 12 albums of her herbarium - dried and preserved specimens - volumes now in the British Museum's natural history Collection.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is commemorated by ''Beaufortia decussata'' - an exotic variety of Australian Myrtle named for her in 1812 by Robert Brown, plant explorer and botanist-librarian to Sir Joseph Banks.
