2 CONT *''''''Lady Elizabeth Wriothesley was born in 1646.2 She was the daughter of Thomas Wriothesley, 4th Earl of Southampton and Lady Elizabeth Leigh. She married, firstly, Joceline Percy, 11th Earl of Northumberland, son of Algernon 
2 CONC Percy, 10th Earl of Northumberland and Lady Elizabeth Howard, on 23 December 1662. She married, secondly, Ralph Montagu, 1st Duke of Montagu, son of Edward Montagu, 2nd Baron Montagu of Boughton, on 24 August 1673. She died on 19 
2 CONC September 1690.
2 CONT *''''''     Her married name became Percy. Her married name became Montagu.
2 CONT *''''''Child of Lady Elizabeth Wriothesley and Joceline Percy, 11th Earl of Northumberland
2 CONT ** 1.Lady Elizabeth Percy+ b. 26 Jan 1666/67, d. 23 Nov 1722
2 CONT *''''''Children of Lady Elizabeth Wriothesley and Ralph Montagu, 1st Duke of Montagu
2 CONT ** 1.Ralph Montagu2 b. 1679, d. 1689
2 CONT ** 2.John Montagu, 2nd Duke of Montagu+ b. 1690, d. 5 Jul 1749
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S332] Artcyclopedia, online http://www.artcyclopedia.com/artists. Hereinafter cited as Artcyclopedia.
2 CONT * 2.[S3409] Caroline Maubois, "re: Penancoet Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger Lundy, 2 December 2008. Hereinafter cited as "re: Penancoet Family."
2 CONT *http://www.thepeerage.com/p1047.htm#i10468
2 CONT *________________________
2 CONT *''''''A general and heraldic dictionary of the peerages of England, Ireland, and ... By John Burke
2 CONT *[http://books.google.com/books?id=aB0IAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA310&lpg=PA310&dq=Alice+Coverall+Barker&source=bl&ots=dilchU_XlT&sig=ow-5Xj0nUSuQy1HG3RDUzec8ky8&hl=en&ei=OheJTfCOE5H2tgPHpICJDA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CBMQ6
2 CONC AEwADgU#v=onepage&q=LEIGH&f=false/ A general and heraldic dictionary of the peerages of England, Ireland, and ... By John Burke]
2 CONT *Pg. 583
2 CONT *   HENRY WRIOTHESLEY, third Earl of Southampton. . . . m. Elizabeth, daughter of John Vernon, Esq., of Hodnet, in the county of Derby, and dying in 1624, left issue,
2 CONT *The earl was s. by his son,
2 CONT **   THOMAS WRIOTHESLEY, fourth Earl of Southampton. . . .  His lordship m. first, Rachel, daughter of Daniel de Massey, Baron de Rouvigny, in France, by whom he had two sons, who both died young, and three daughters, . . . 
2 CONT **He wedded, secondly, Frances, daughter of Francis Leigh, Baron Dunsmore, which nobleman was created 3rd June, 1644, EARL OF CHICHESTER, with remainder, failing his own male issue, to his son-in-law, the Earl of Southampton and t
2 CONC he heirs male of his body by his lordship's daughter, the said Frances Leigh. He died in 1653, and the honours of Lord Southampton, were then augmented by the Earldom of Chichester. By this lady, his lordship had four daughters,
2 CONT ***      Audrey, who d. unmarried.
2 CONT ***      Penelope, d. young.
2 CONT ***''''''      Elizabeth, m. first Joceline, Earl of Northumberland, and secondly, to Ralph, Lord Montagu, of Boughton.
2 CONT ***      Penelope, d. in infancy.
2 CONT **The earl espoused, thirdly Frances, daughter of William, Duke of Somerset, and widow of Richard, Viscount Molineux, but had no issue. He died at Southampton House, "near Holburne, in the suburbs of London," 16th May, 1667, when 
2 CONC all his HONOURS, including the Earldom of Chichester, became EXTINCT.
2 CONT *__________________________ Elizabeth Monck, Duchess of Albermarle (and later Elizabeth Montagu, Duchess of Montagu), was born Lady Elizabeth Cavendish (22 February 1654–11 September 1734) and known for most of her life as the Mad
2 CONC  Duchess of Albemarle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was the eldest daughter of Henry Cavendish, 2nd Duke of Newcastle and his wife Frances Pierrepont, daughter of William Pierrepont.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 30 December 1669, she married Christopher Monck (later the Duke of Albemarle) at Whitehall, London. Monck was appointed Governor of Jamaica in 1687 and Elizabeth went with him to Jamaica where Monck amassed a small fortune, whi
2 CONC ch Elizabeth acquired and brought with her back to the Kingdom of England in 1688 when her husband died.
2 CONT
2 CONT From this time, Elizabeth had declared that she would only marry into royalty and was convinced that the Kangxi Emperor of Qing Dynasty China wished to marry her; suitably dressed as the Emperor of China, her sister-in-law Elizabe
2 CONC th's stepfather, the 1st Duke of Montagu asked for her hand in marriage and they were wed on 8 September 1692 in Newcastle House, London. Elizabeth died in 1734 and was buried in Westminster Abbey.
