1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He "(II) William Earle, son of Ralph Earle (1), was born in England. The first known record of William Earle is dated April 2, 1654, when he and his wife Mary sold to James Sands their interest in fourteen acres th
2 CONC at came by right of the late Widow Walker, the land having come by marriage of Mary Walker, daughter of the widow, to William Earle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was admitted a freeman at Bristol, Rhode Island, May 11, 1658, and on May 18th he was admitted a freeman of the colony at the general court held at Warwick. On June 8th, in the same year, he was chosen to represent Bristol in t
2 CONC he general court of trials at Newport. He was given a grant of land in partnership with William Cory May 1, 1665, provided they maintain a windmill for the use of the town. In 1668 the windmill had been erected. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Earle removed to Dartmouth about 1670, and stayed there several years, his interests there being large. He owned more than two thousand acres from his claims in the original division of the land. "
2 CONT
2 CONT He signed a will Second Generation; page 24 in 1713. In his will, executed Nov. 13, 1713, he gave to his son William, a brass milk pan, and a like gift to his sons Thomas, Ralph and John (our line), and his daughters Mary Borden, 
2 CONC Mary Hix and Prudence Durfee, and to the last a negro girl, "Kate," also, to his son John, all the rest of the estate, real and personal, he paying legacies as follows: to grandson Caleb Earle, 40 acres, or L40 [40 pounds], at the
2 CONC  age of 21. To granddaughter Joan Earle, daughter of Caleb, L10 [10 pounds], at 20 years of age. His son John was to allow his mother comfortable and sufficient maintenance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since he appears to have been about 6 when his Dad came over, I am assuming that he came over with his dad in 1638.
2 CONT
2 CONT Story is that he returned to England at least once to help his brother-in-law acquire with respect to some property:
2 CONT
2 CONT Unusually, William (Cory), accompanied by his brother in law, William Earle returned to Bristol on a visit in 1669/70 to claim property deeded to him by his grandmother. An entry in the first volume of the Colony Records for 28th 
2 CONC February 1667, recorded that Ann Roome, widow of John Roome of Portsmouth, Rhode Island had deeded to William Corrie of Portsmouth "for consideration of full and just satisfaction and true payment by mee in hand Received from 'Wil
2 CONC liam Corrie of Portsmouth, house carpenter, deeded to him' two certain mansion houses with the lands thereto belonging . . . in St James Parish in the street called St. James back in the Citty of Bristol, in Old England" which had
2 CONC  been left to her by her late husband [John Correy] and by him "put into the hands and care of Mr. Thomas ffloyd [sic] of Bristol." 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 October 1667 she gave to him a commemoratory deed, and immediately gave to him another document (both recorded R.I. [Archive MS 0020 9], 2:354, Rhode Island Colony Records 1646 to 1669) stating that: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * "William Correy, being of aged about fower and thirtye yeares being now personally present and intending a voyage to England upon occasion of demanding and Receiving the Estate that his grandmother, Ann Roome hath made sale and 
2 CONC surrender of unto him by the written deed: I say this certifieth that the bearer here of William Correy aforesaid is the reputed son of one John Correy deceased who was somtime inhabitant in or near the city of Bristol in England 
2 CONC and son of Ann Roome aforenamed: and further this certifieth that the said William Correy is bound for England in company with one William Earll inhabitant of the same town of Portsmouth on Rhode Island and the aforesaid William E
2 CONC arll may be able to testifie in England and to the parson that shall ther Exhibett these presantes whether he be the same to whome they are now committed and in whose behalfe these lines are written . . . Voted at Newport . . . th
2 CONC e thertenth day of November . . .1669 . . under the hand of the governor [Benidict Arnold]. Joseph Torrey recorder." 
2 CONT
2 CONT On Dec. 4, 1669, Cory had a deed of a third of a share in Dartmouth from William Earle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT No records have come to light in Bristol to confirm the success or otherwise of the 1669 pilgrimage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From: [http://books.google.by/books?id=LA7ntaS11ocC&pg=PA69&lpg=PA69&dq=Mary+Walker+%26+William+Earle&source=bl&ots=ZrgwoBwsTa&sig=V143x315ZytxKibe8UJfdRHbCNQ&hl=en&sa=X&ei=IsSiUqzMKI2AygHayoCgAg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=Mary%20Wal
2 CONC ker%20%26%20William%20Earle&f=false The Genealogical Dictionary of Rhode Island: Comprising Three Generations of Settlers who came before 1690 (with many families carried to the fourth generation), by John Osborne Austin (1887), P
2 CONC g. 69]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===11. William (Earle)===
2 CONT
2 CONT *b. Portsmouth RI, Dartmouth Mass., Portsmouth RI (Note: this entry is certain to be incorrect: Portsmouth was settled only in 1638, and Dartmouth was settled only in 1650. Other sources indicate birth in England, with immigration
2 CONC  at age 5, name of ship not indicated.)
2 CONT *d. 1715, Jan. 15
2 CONT *'''m1'''. Mary Walker
2 CONT **b. of John and Katherine ( )
2 CONT *'''m2'''. Prudence
2 CONT **b. of (Unknown)
2 CONT * '''1654, Apr. 2'''. He and wife Mary sold James Sands their interest in 14 acres that came by right of late widow Walker; the land having come by marriage of Mary Walker (daughter of widow) to William Earle. 
2 CONT * '''1658, May 11'''. Freeman 
2 CONT * '''1658'''. Juryman. 
2 CONT * '''1665, May 1'''. He and William Cory were given 1 acre on Briggs' Hill and 1/4 acre near the town pond to have so long as they maintained a windmill for the town's use. Three years later an exchange was made (between town and 
2 CONC Earl and Cory) of 2 acres near the mill for 2 acres elsewhere. (In 1684, the site was called Windmill Hill.) 
2 CONT * '''1667, Dec. 5'''. His ear mark for cattle was recorded.
2 CONT * '''1670'''. Dartmouth, Mass. He probably removed there about this time and stayed for a number of years, his interest there being large. He owned 2,000 acres, from his claims in the original division. 
2 CONT * '''1680'''. Portsmouth. Taxed 8s 6d., with his mother. 
2 CONT * '''1688, June 2'''. He deeded son Ralph and Mary, his wife, of Dartmouth, for love, etc. 1/2 a share of land in Freetown, east side of Taunton River. 
2 CONT * '''1691, May 6'''. The Assembly met at his house, being removed from Newport on account of the distemper.
2 CONT * '''1692, Oct. 6'''. He deeded son Thomas of Dartmouth 1/4 share of land there. 
2 CONT * '''1693-1704-1706'''. Deputy. 
2 CONT * '''1713, Nov. 13. Will - proved 1715, Feb. 8'''. Executed by son John. Overseers, James Tallman of Portsmouth and Benjamin Durfee of Tiverton. 
2 CONT ** To '''son William''', a brass milkpan, and like gift '''to sons Thomas, Ralph, and John''', and '''daughters Mary Borden, Mary Hix, and Prudence Durfee''', and to the last a negro girl "Kate" also. To '''son John''', all rest o
2 CONC f estate, real and personal, he paying legacies as follows: 
2 CONT ** To '''grandson Caleb Earle,''' 40 acres of 49 pounds at 21. 
2 CONT ** To '''granddaughter Joan Earle, daughter of Caleb''', 10 pounds at 20 years of age. 
2 CONT ** Son John to allow wife comfortable and sufficient maintenance. 
2 CONT ** The '''daughter Mary Hix''', mentioned in will, '''was widow of his son Caleb''', she having married Joseph Hicks, for her second husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children by first wife:''' [Mary Earle (Walker)]
2 CONT *1. Mary (b. 1655)
2 CONT *2. William
2 CONT *3. Thomas
2 CONT *4. Ralph (b. 1660)
2 CONT *5. Caleb
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children by second wife:''' [Prudence Earle (???)]
2 CONT *1. John
2 CONT *2. Prudence (b. 1681)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT There is no firm evidence that these are the father and mother of Mary Earl(e) Borden, but the dates and location are appropriate.
2 CONT
2 CONT  *************************** From:  http://hylbom.com/family/paternal-lines/paternal-dy-to-gi/earle-5600/
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Ralph Earle and Joan Savage is William Earle, born about 1635 and died 1 Jan 1715, probably in Portsmouth, Rhode Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Noted events in the life of William Earle:'''
2 CONT *1654: William married Mary Walker[7].  I am not confident in Mary’s dates, but it seems in many genealogies she is confused with another Mary Earle (1636-1718), the daughter of Ralph and Joan.  This is not surprising since Ralph 
2 CONC had both a daughter and a daughter-in-law who were known as “Mary Earle”, who would have been approximately the same age.  
2 CONT *1654, Apr. 2: The first known record of William was when he and his wife Mary sold to James Sands their interest in 14 acres that came by right of the late widow Walker, the land having come by marriage of Mary Walker (daughter o
2 CONC f the widow) to William Earle.
2 CONT *1655: Eldest child Mary is born. She marries John Borden and dies in 1734 at age 79.
2 CONT *1656, Dec. 3: The proprietors of Portsmouth ordered that William Earl and James Sands shall have 50 acres of land it being an old grant to John Walker deceased.
2 CONT *1657: Eldest son William is born at Portsmouth. He marries Elizabeth [maiden name unknown]
2 CONT *1658, May 11: William was Received a freeman of Portsmouth.  
2 CONT *1658, May 18: Att the Generall court of Commissioners held for the Collony, at Warwicke, he was admitted as a freeman of the colony.  
2 CONT *1658, Jun. 8: William and two others were chosen Jurymen to attend the General court of Tryels, at Newport. 
2 CONT *1659, Dec. 3: Phillip Delano Sr. with the consent of Mary his wife sold one half his purchase lands at Coaksett or Cushena or both to William Earle. 
2 CONT *1660: Ralph is born. He marries Mary Carr in 1689, and dies at Leicester, Massachusetts, in the same year as his wife, 1757.
2 CONT *1665, May 1: At a town meeting, it was ordered that William Earle and William Correy (Cory) should have one acker of land on the hill cauled Briges hill, or some other conveniant place in this townes Comons, and a quarter of an a
2 CONC cker of land lying aganst ye towne pond over against William Earle’s new dwelinge house, and these two pearcells of land they are to have and to enjoy to them and theres, so long as they maintain a wind mill in this towne for the 
2 CONC towns use, Provided that if they maintain not the said mill then the said pearcells of land is to be returned and laid downe to the townes use and dispose.
2 CONT *1665: Thomas is born. He eventually marries Mary Taber.
2 CONT *1668: The windmill had been erected, and the town, at the request of said Earle and Cory, annulled the above order, and exchanged two acres of ground near the mill for two acres belonging to the said parties. The history of this 
2 CONC quasi-public enterprise is rather complicated and occupies considerable space in the town records.  Numerous transfers and retransfers of land between the town and Earle and Cory and Cory’s widow were necessary to straighten thing
2 CONC s out, but in the end it seems to have been satisfactorily resolved. 
2 CONT *1670: William Earle removed to Dartmouth about this time and stayed several years, his interest there being large.  He owned more than 2,000 acres from his claims in the original division of the land.  Copies of many conveyances 
2 CONC both to and from him are upon the record.  
2 CONT *1673, Jun. 24: Henry Langstarr and Anthony Nutter were sued by William Earl for a debt against John Partridge at court.
2 CONT *1673: Caleb is born.
2 CONT *Before 1676: William's wife, Mary, died.
2 CONT *1677: William marries a second time, to Prudence, by this date. He had his two youngest children with her, including John, born this year in Portsmouth. He marries Mary Waite, and dies in August 1759.
2 CONT *1680, Jun. 26: In one of the conveyances from William, in consideration of naturall Love and affection, he conveyed to his son Ralph and his wife Mary, of Dartmouth, a parcel of Land Scituate Lying and being in the Township of Fr
2 CONC eetown, in the county of Bristoll aforesaid, on the Eastward side of Tanton River, containing half a share, that is to say, half that whole share Adjoyning to the Fall River, two thirds whereof I do give to my said son Ralph Earll
2 CONC  his heirs and Assigns forever; and the other Third thereof I do give to my Daugther in Law Mary Earll her heirs and Assigns forever.  This deed was executed in Portsmouth, and the land conveyed is now the site of the central and 
2 CONC earliest settled part of the city of Fall River.
2 CONT *1681: Prudence, William's youngest child is born. She marries Benjamin Durfee, and dies in 1732 at age 49.
2 CONT *1684: The site of the windmill is mentioned as windmill hill, and in 1685, the grounds were re-exchanged, the town and the parties taking their former property respectively.
2 CONT *1689: William's son, Ralph, age 29, marries Mary, widow of John Hicks and daughter of Robert Carr of Newport.
2 CONT * 1691, May 6: The General Assembly for their Majesties Collony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations, in New England, in Portsmouth on said Rhode Island, for the Election of General Officers for the said Collony, was held at
2 CONC  the house of William Earle, it being removed from Newport by reason of the distmper.  It was adjourned until 23 June, and then to meet again at the house of William Earle in Portsmouth, except the Governor sees cause to meet soon
2 CONC er, if urgent occasion should present.
2 CONT *1692: William was on the grand Inquest of Newport; and on 6 October he conveyed to his wel beloved son Thomas Earll, of the Town of Dartmouth, one full quarter of a share of upland in Dartmouth.
2 CONT *1705, Oct. 25: William was a deputy from Portsmouth to the General Assembly held at Providence.
2 CONT *1706, May 1 and July 3: William was a deputy from Portsmouth to the General Assemblies held at Newport.
2 CONT *1713, Jan. 15: William Earle dies (presumably this year, as his will would not have been executed before his death).
2 CONT *1713, Nov. 13: William's will is executed. In it, he gave to his son William, a brass milk pan, and a like gift to his sons Thomas, Ralph and John, and his daughters Mary Borden, Mary Hix and Prudence Durfee, and to the last a ne
2 CONC gro girl, Kate.  Also, to his son John, all the rest of the estate, real and personal.  Legacies as follows: To grandson Caleb Earle, forty acres, or 40 pounds] at the age of twenty-one.  To granddaughter Joan Earle, daughter of C
2 CONC aleb, 10 pounds, at twenty years of age. His son John was to allow his mother comfortable and sufficient maintenance.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The likely children of William and Mary Walker are:'''
2 CONT * 	1.	'''Mary''', born 1655 and died 1734.  She married John Borden
2 CONT * 	2.	'''William''', born 1657 at Portsmouth, Rhode Island married Elizabeth [surname unknown]
2 CONT * 	3.	'''Ralph''' Earle, born about 1660 and married Mary Carr (the widow of John Hicks and daughter of Robert Carr of Newport, Rhode Island) in 1689.  Ralph Earle died at Leicester, Massachusetts in 1757, as did his wife.
2 CONT * 	4.	'''Thomas''', born about 1665 and married Mary Taber[8]
2 CONT * 	5.	'''Caleb''', born 1673 and married Mary [name unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The likely children of William and Prudence are:'''
2 CONT * 	1.	'''John''' Earle, born about 1677 at Portsmouth and died August 1759.  He married Mary Waite
2 CONT * 	2.	'''Prudence''', born 1681 and died 1732.  She married Benjamin Durfee.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
