2 CONT
2 CONT moved from Portsmouth to Darmouth and back again Earle book p. 23
2 CONT
2 CONT first know record of William is under date April 2 1634.  [ this date cannot be right he was only 2 years old- guessing should be 1654)  He and wife Mary sold to James Sands their interest in 14 acres that came by rich of the late
2 CONC  widow Walker, land having come by marriage of Mary Walker to William.  the northern extremity of the island, at Bristol Ferry (pg 19)  James Sands was brother- in law marrying Mary’s sister Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 5, 1655 Ralph conveyed to son William 18 acres of land on the northern extremity of the island, at Bristol Ferry.
2 CONT
2 CONT may 11 1658 was freeman in Portsmouth Rhode Island.  At the General Court of Commissioners held in Warwick May 18th he was admitted as a freeman of the colony.  On June 8 same year he and two others were chosen Jurymen to attend t
2 CONC he” General Court of Tryels at Newport”
2 CONT
2 CONT May 1, 1665 it was ordered that William Earle and William Corry{Cory was brother-in-law] were to have “ one acker of land on the hill caused Briges hill, or some other convenient places in this townes Commons, and a quarter of an 
2 CONC acker of land lying against ye town ond over against William Earle’s new dewelinge house, and these two pearcells of land they dare to have and to enjoy to them and theres, so along as they maintain a wind mill in this town for th
2 CONC e towns use.  Provided that if they maintain not the said mill then said pear cells of land is to be returned and laid down to the townes use and dispose.”  The wind mill had been erected by 1668. At request of Early and Cory the 
2 CONC above order was annulled and exchanged two acres of ground near the mill for two acres belonging to the said parties.In 1685 the grounds were reexchanged, the town and the parties taking their former property respectfully   operat
2 CONC ed a windmill
2 CONT
2 CONT the Eare mark of William Earl’s cattell is a hapeny under the side of ye further Eare and a slit on the Nere Eare of 12 years standing and recorded by me Richard Bulgar, town Clarkd, December 5, 1667.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moved to Dartmouth about 1670 and stayed several years.  He owned more than two thousand acres from his claims in the original division of land. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1680 back in Portsmouth taxed by Portsmouth
2 CONT On may 6 1691 the General Assembly for the Majesties Collony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations in New England were to be held at William Earle house in Portsmouth.  There to hold Elections of General Officers of the said 
2 CONC Collony.  [The elections were removed from Newport for the reason of distemper].  The meeting was adjourned until June 23 when it would again be held at William Earle’s house.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1692 on the Grand Inquest at Newport.  He was a deputy from Portsmouth to General Assembles held at Providence Oct 25, 1704 and Newport May 1 1706 and July 3, 1706
2 CONT
2 CONT Will executed Nov 13, 1713 gave to son William a brass milk pan and a like gift to his sons, Thomas, Ralph and John and daughters Mary Borden, Mary Hix and Prudence, and to the last negro girl Kate.  John received all the rest of 
2 CONC the estate real and person, he paying legacies as follows: Caleb Earle, grandson 40 acres or £40 at age twenty-one.  granddaughter John Earle, daughter of Caleb £10 at age twenty.  John was to allow his mother comfortable and suff
2 CONC icient maintenance.
