2 CONT
2 CONT 139. He is described in Lodge’s Peerage of Ire. (Archdall ed. 1789) Vol. II, p.35 as “Sir Roger O’Shaughnessy of Gortinhegourah, Co. Galway, Bart.” This description is, however, a mistake for, though his father, gr.f. and gr.gr.f.
2 CONC  were knights, he himself was neither a knight nor a baronet. He was Capt. in Clare’s Dragoons, and was attainted as “Esquire” in 1691. G.E.C’s Complete Baronetage, Vol. IV, 1665-1707, p.224. For the O’Shaughnessy pedigree see The
2 CONC  Genealogies, Tribes and Customs of Hy-Fiachcrach, by O’Donovan (who made use of the pedigree complied by Peter O’Connell and Cornet Butler) and Blake-Forster, Ir. Chieftains, p.705. See also nn. 46 and 62 (supra).
2 CONT
2 CONT 140. History of Diocese of Kilmacduagh. See also Blake-Forster, ibid. p.436, note 20.
2 CONT
2 CONT 141. Edward Wakefield, author of Ireland, Statistical and Political (1812). Father of Edward Gibbon Wakefield (D.N.B.).
2 CONT
2 CONT 142. When O’Donovan visited the neighbourhood in 1839 the sentor representative of the once powerful chiefs was know locally as “Heynes the process-server” from his modest calling. He was fifth in descent from Martin O’Heyne, who 
2 CONC was driven from Ardrahan Castle by Ireton. Survey Letters, Vol. 1, p.27.
2 CONT
2 CONT 143. Deed dated 15 Jan., 1737. The lands settled on Francis Butler were Ballygegan, Cregg, Creggboy, Drumbleane, Burra, Scariff, Knockobode, Knockgillereagh, Rathlough and Annagh, all in Co. Galway.
2 CONT
2 CONT 144. Cas. Crine Rental, 1720; denominations 2-5.
2 CONT
2 CONT 145. See Appendix III. (1).
2 CONT
2 CONT 146. Carrucate, i.e. an ancient measurement based on an estimate of what a single team could plough within a year. It varied according to the district. Boytonrath actually contained 20 acres “old measurement” equal to 605 acres, I
2 CONC .P.M., or 935 Statute acres. Garrancaninane (conveyed by the same deed) contained 7 a. O.M., 200a., I.P.M. or 310 a. Stat.
2 CONT
2 CONT 147. Richard O’Grady was father of Morough O’Grady, Ensign in Col. Charles O’Brien’s Regt. of Infantry, 1689-90, who mar. Elizabeth Butler, dau. of James Butler of Shranagollen. The O’Gradys were among the principal landowners in 
2 CONC Inchicronan before the Cromwellian Settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT 148. Mrs. C. Bennett, The Galweys of Lota. O’Hara, as usual, cites no authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT 149. Inquisition taken at Knockenegan, 22 Nov, 1577.
2 CONT
2 CONT 150. Frost, History of Clare. p.347.
2 CONT
2 CONT 151. Third s. of John Galwey of Lota, and bro. of Richard Galwey of Fort Richard. See nn. 83, 98 [supra].
2 CONT
2 CONT 152. See n. 98 [supra].
2 CONT
2 CONT 153. Sarah (Sally Moore, dau. of Edward Moore of Mooresfort, Co. Tipp. by Elizabeth dau. of Hon. and Rev. Maurice Crosbie, third s. of the 1st Lord Brandon.
2 CONT
2 CONT 154. Wm. Purefoy was M.P. for Limerick City and Kimallock in Cromwell’s Parliament. J. Grene-Barry, The Liberties of Limerick, Journal N.M.A.S., Vol. 11, No. 4 (Jan., 1913), p.235.
2 CONT
2 CONT 155. Cas. Crine Rental, 1720.
2 CONT
2 CONT 156. Second s. of Henry Butler of Cas. Crine. See n. 217 [infra].
2 CONT
2 CONT 157. Murrough. 1st Earl of Inchiquin (1614-74).
2 CONT
2 CONT 158. Ellen, dau. of William Butler III, of Bunnahow. She mar. 1st, Capt. Geo. O’Brien of Birchfield, son of the well-known ‘Corney’ O’Brien, M.P., for Clare, and 2nd. Charles Vyse.
2 CONT
2 CONT 159. The Return of 1876 describes the owner of the Clare estate as “reps. Wm. Butler. 27 South Ter., Cork”; of the Galway a “Wm. Butler Bunnahow Ho”; and of the Cork as “Margaret Butler, Dublin”. The compiler’s confusion was proba
2 CONC bly due to the fact that Wm. Butler IV died in Cork in 1867 and that in 1876 Bunnahow Ho. was unoccupied (see App. XV and his widow was living in Dublin. The Cork property formed part of her dowry and ultimately descended to the w
2 CONC riter.
2 CONT
2 CONT 160. Sir Toby had a grant of 2,863 acres in Co. Tipp. from the 2nd Duke of Ormonde on 18 Aug., 1697. Lodge’s Register, Q. Anne, No. 383. He augmented his Clare property in 1711 by the purchase of 4,311 acres from his bro., James B
2 CONC utler. T. Blake Butler, The O’Shaughnesssy Woods, N.M.A. Journal, Vol. VI, No. 3. The estates were heavily mortgaged owing to litigation with the Prendergasts; and in 1775 James Butler, with a rent roll of £4,000 p.a., was describ
2 CONC ed as “greatly distressed”. Anal. Hib. Vol. 12.
2 CONT
2 CONT 161. The 1364 acres held by the Millbrooke branch from the Butlers of Kilcommon are excluded, as their interest in the lands was purchased by the latter in 1808.
2 CONT
2 CONT 162. [The footnote for this reference is not given in the original text.]
2 CONT
2 CONT 163. Sir Cormac Mac Tiege MacCarthy, 14th lord of Murkerry, described by Ld. Dep. Sidney as ‘the rarest man that ever was born in the Irishry’. He marr. Joan, dau. of Piers Butler of Grallagh. In recording Sir Cormac’s death in 15
2 CONC 83 the Four Masters refer to “the young sons of the deceased and their mother Joan, the dau. of Piers-na-Buille”. There was a dispute over Joan’s jointure, and the Council of Munster adjudged her to have the castle and lands of Da
2 CONC rraghoid for life in lieu of the famous Blarney Castle, which Sir Cormac had left her. Harl. MSS. 697. W.F. Butler. Gleanings from Irish Hist. pp 251, 262. H. W. Gilman, art. Journal C.H.A.S. Vol. I No. 1 Q p. 103.
2 CONT
2 CONT 164. Margaret, dau. of O’Mulrian (anglice Ryan) of Annagh, Co. Limerick, chief of his name. She mar. John Butler, yr. s. of Piers of Grallagh.
2 CONT
2 CONT 165. The Funeral Certificates of Ireland (preserved in G.O.) cover the period 1588-1729, and contain the names, parentage, arms, marriages, issue, date and place of burial of those of the Irish nobility and landed gentry whose fun
2 CONC erals were attended by the Ulster King or Arms of his deputy.
2 CONT
2 CONT 166. Née Hon. Eleanor Butler, eld. dau. of 2nd Viscount Mountgarret. She mar. Walter, 11th Earl of Ormonde.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Funeral of Eleanor, Countess of Ormond, who died 28th January, 1631, in Kilkenny Castle, 27 March 1632. Chief mourner, Countess Dowager of Ormond.[167] Her train borne by Mrs. Ellin Butler of Upper Court. Pallbearers: Mr. James B
2 CONC utler of Ikerrin, Mr. Edmond Butler of Mount Garrett, Sir George Hamilton and Lord Dunboyne.[168] Present. Mr. John Butler fitz. Edmond, Mr. Edmond Butler fitz Pierce, and Mr. Richard Butler of Kilcash, Mr. Butler of Poleston,[169
2 CONC ] Mr Butler of Mayaille, Mr. Tobie Butler and Mrs. James fitz Edmond Butler of Crehanagh, Mr. Edmond Butler fitz James,[170] Mr. Pierce Butler, son of Sir Edward, Mr. Butler of Moinhere……Mr. Pierce Butler fitz Edward and Mr. James
2 CONC  of Darilouscan,[171] Mr. James Butler of Grantstown,[172] Lady Ikerrin, old Lady Thurles, young Lady Thurles and Lady Kathren, Lady Ellen and Lady Jane, Lady Mary and Lady Eliz., Lady Eleanor and Lady Upper Ossory, Lady Ikerrin's
2 CONC  daughter, Lady Anne's daughter-in-law. (Funeral Entries, (G.O.), Vol. p. 184).
