1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1648, Mary together with her half sister, Elizabeth, left England for Belgium to join one of the orders of English nuns in exile. The record from the book of clothing records her entry.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the yeare of 1648, the 13 of iune Marie Rooper In Religion called Marie Peter toke the habit of probation at the hands of Rnd Father Nicholas Day Custos of the Province, she was daughter to Mr Antony Rooper Esqr her mothers nam
2 CONC e was Mary Garret (sic) and was born at Eltome otherwise called Wellhall, at the age of 21, this good Religious Brought unto this Monestary 3000 pou : starling , and had not ye tymes been very bad in England by wars she had brough
2 CONC t much more.[1] (Note 1 from transcriber of published transcription .This date was originally written as 1658 but the 5 has been altered into a 4 in a later hand and in darker ink. Note 2, spellings as in printed transcription) Th
2 CONC e entry of her half sister followed on the 16th June. This second entry does not mention a dowry so it is possible that the £3000 was for both sisters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Three years later, Mary became a fully professed nun as recorded in the book of professions
2 CONT
2 CONT 1649 Sister Mary Roper by the name of Mary Peter in the 24 Year of her age made her vowes and Profession of the third Rule of St Francis in the hands of the Rnd Mother Abbess Sr Margarit Clare the Rnd Father receiuing her Progessi
2 CONC on the 13 day of June the Year of our Lord 1649 in testimony that this is truth she hath vnder written her name the the witnesses accustomed [2] The profession book also records in a side note that Sister Mary Peter died on the 18
2 CONC th April, 1668. Her place of burial is recorded in the book of the dead:
2 CONT
2 CONT Within ye newe Cloyster at Bruges neere ye Cloyster doore lyeth Sister Mary Peter als Rooper with her face towards ye high Altar, 1668[3] A research project into the English nuns in exile notes that in addition to her half sister 
2 CONC she was related to 3 other nuns , all members of the Roper family. She was the cousin of Mary and Margaret Roper daughters of Thomas Roper of London and Susan Winchcombe of Henwick, Berks) and Barbara Constable (daughter of Sir Ph
2 CONC ilip Constable of Everingham, Yorks and Anne Roper of Eltham
2 CONT
2 CONT There are 15 nuns in total, with the surname Roper in the data base. Their dates of profession ranging from 1619-1770 [4]
