1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Anne Wentworth, Baroness Wentworth1
2 CONT *'''F, #472405, b. 29 July 1623, d. 7 May 1697
2 CONT *Last Edited=19 Jun 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.78%
2 CONT *'''     Anne Wentworth, Baroness Wentworth was baptised on 29 July 1623.1 She was the daughter''' of Thomas Wentworth, 1st and last Earl of Cleveland and Anne Croftes.1 '''She married John Lovelace, 2nd Baron Lovelace of Hurley''
2 CONC ', son of Richard Lovelace, 1st Baron Lovelace of Hurley, '''on 11 July 1638.1 She died on 7 May 1697 at age 73.1
2 CONT *'''      From 11 July 1638, her married name became Lovelace.1 She succeeded to the title of 7th Baroness Wentworth [E., 1529] on 23 April 1686, suo jure.1 She succeeded to the title of 11th Lord Despenser [E., 1387] on 23 April 
2 CONC 1686, de jure.1
2 CONT *'''Children of Anne Wentworth, Baroness Wentworth and John Lovelace, 2nd Baron Lovelace of Hurley
2 CONT **John Lovelace, 3rd Baron Lovelace of Hurley+1 b. c 1640, d. 27 Sep 1693
2 CONT **Hon. Margaret Lovelace+1 b. c 1644, d. 14 Apr 1671
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 2442. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p47241.htm#i472405 __________________
2 CONT *'''John Lovelace, 2nd Baron Lovelace''' (February 1616 – 25 November 1670 was a British peer and Royal servant.
2 CONT *John was born in Hurley, Berkshire the son of Richard Lovelace, 1st Baron Lovelace and his wife, Margaret, the daughter of London merchant William Dodworth, and educated at Christchurch College, Oxford. He lived at Ladye Place at
2 CONC  Hurley which he inherited on the death of his father.
2 CONT *He was an ardent Royalist and was committed by the Parliamentarians to the Tower of London and made to pay a heavy fine of 18,373l. 1s. 10d. After the restoration of the Monarchy he was appointed Lord Lieutenant of Berkshire from
2 CONC  28 August 1660 to 25 November 1670. In 1670 he was made steward of the old Royal palace at Woodstock and died in the palace gatehouse on 25 September 1670. He was buried in the old priory church in Hurley.[1]
2 CONT *After his death he was succeeded by '''his son''' John Lovelace, 3rd Baron Lovelace. '''His wife Anne''', daughter of Thomas '''Wentworth''', 1st Earl of Cleveland, '''succeeded her niece as 7th Baroness Wentworth, which after he
2 CONC r death passed to their granddaughter''' Martha Johnson.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Lovelace,_2nd_Baron_Lovelace ____________________
2 CONT *John Lovelace, 3rd Baron Lovelace (1641 – 27 September 1693) was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons from 1661 to 1670 when he inherited the peerage as Baron Lovelace.
2 CONT *Lovelace was born at Hurley, Buckinghamshire, the son of '''John Lovelace, 2nd Baron Lovelace, and Lady Anne, 7th Baroness Wentworth and Baroness Le Despenser'''.[1] He matriculated at Wadham College, Oxford, on 25 July 1655, and
2 CONC  was awarded MA on 9 September 1661.[2] 
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *Lovelace married in 1662 Martha Pye, the daughter and coheiress of Sir Edmund Pye, 1st Baronet, of Bradenham, Buckinghamshire. They had one son, John, who died in infancy, and three daughters. The peerage passed to his cousin Wil
2 CONC liam’s son, John Lovelace, 4th Baron Lovelace, who became a Governor of the New York colony.[3] Their daughter Martha married Henry Johnson, said to be 'the greatest shipbuilder and shipowner of his day'; and '''succeeded her gran
2 CONC dmother''' as the 8th Baroness Wentworth.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Lovelace,_3rd_Baron_Lovelace ____________________
2 CONT *LOVELACE, Hon. John (c.1642-93), of Water Eaton, Oxon. and Hurley, Berks.
2 CONT *b. c.1642, 1st s. of '''John, 2nd Baron Lovelace of Hurley by Lady Anne Wentworth''', da. and h. of Thomas, 1st Earl of Cleveland. educ. Wadham, Oxf. 1655, MA 1661. m. 28 Aug. 1662, aged 20, Martha, da. and coh. of Sir Edmund Pye
2 CONC , 1st Bt., of Bradenham, Bucks., 1s. d.v.p. 3da. suc. fa. as 3rd Baron 25 Sept. 1670.1
2 CONT * .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/member/lovelace-hon-john-1642-93 _____________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 34
2 CONT *Lovelace, John          by Thomas Seccombe
2 CONT *LOVELACE, JOHN, third Baron Lovelace of Hurley (1638?–1693), was grandson of Sir Richard Lovelace (1568–1634) of Hurley, Berkshire, who was knighted at Dublin on 5 Aug. 1599, and elevated to the peerage by Charles I on 30 May 162
2 CONC 7. His father was '''John Lovelace, second baron''' (1616–1670), '''and his mother, Lady Anne, daughter and eventual heiress''' of Thomas '''Wentworth''', first earl of Cleveland. '''It was this ‘Lady Anne’ to whom Richard Lovelac
2 CONC e dedicated''' his ‘Lucasta.’ Of his grandfather, Sir Richard, Fuller says: ‘He was a gentleman of mettal; and in the reign of Queen Elizabeth, making use of letters of mark, had the successe to light on a large remnant of the Kin
2 CONC g of Spain's cloth of silver, I mean his West Indian fleet; wherewith he and his posterity are warmer to this day’ (Worthies, 1811, i. 112). Of the same man, Garrard, in a letter to the Earl of Strafford, dated 3 June 1634, says: 
2 CONC ‘Lovelace being my neighbour, born near Windsor, I knew him well, though he was born but to 400l. a year, yet he left to his only son, aged near 20, near 7,000l. a year: All got by a fortunate marriage with a rich citizen's daught
2 CONC er (of which an early example), she was worth to him 50,000l.’ (Strafford, Letters and Despatches, ed. Knowles, i. 260; Hist. MSS. Comm. 11th Rep. App. vii. p. 213). His father was a staunch royalist, who signed the declaration in
2 CONC  favour of Charles I in June 1642, and joined the king at Oxford in August 1643 (Clarendon, Hist. v. 346, vii. 174). He came in to compound for delinquency on 24 March 1645, was assessed to pay a fine of 18,373l. 1s. 10d., and aft
2 CONC er numerous petitions, reviews, abatements, and delays, succeeded in getting his sequestration suspended after payment of about 4,000l. (Cal. Comm. Comp. ii. 1188). He was lord-lieutenant of Berkshire from 1660 to 1668, died at Wo
2 CONC odstock 25 Sept. 1670, and was buried at Hurley (Ashmole, Antiq. of Berkshire, p. 207; Whitelocke, Memorials, 1682, pp. 76, 352; Cal. State Papers, Dom. 1660–1667 passim). Details of some clumsy attempts at intrigue made by him du
2 CONC ring 1643 and 1644, in which he was the dupe of Sir Harry Vane and other parliament men, are given in Baillie's ‘Correspondence’ (Bannatyne Club, ii. passim).
2 CONT *The son, who was born at Hurley about 1638, was educated at Wadham College, Oxford, whence he matriculated 25 July 1655, was created M.A. 9 Sept. 1661 (Foster, Alumni Oxon. 1500–1714), travelled in France and the Low Countries (c
2 CONC f. Thurloe State Papers, ed. Birch, vi. 151), and represented Berkshire in the House of Commons from 1661 until his father's death in 1670, when he succeeded to the peerage .... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Lovelace,_John_(DNB00)
2 CONT * https://archive.org/stream/dictionarynatio57stepgoog#page/n178/mode/1up to https://archive.org/stream/dictionarynatio57stepgoog#page/n180/mode/1up _______________________
2 CONT *Thomas WENTWORTH (1° E. Cleveland)
2 CONT *Born: 1591
2 CONT *Died: 25 Mar 1667, Toddington, Bedfordshire, England
2 CONT *Buried: 4 Apr 1667, Toddington, Bedfordshire, England
2 CONT *Father: Henry WENTWORTH (3° B. Wentworth of Nettlestead)
2 CONT *Mother: Anne HOPTON
2 CONT *Married 1: Anne CROFTS BEF 1612
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **1. Thomas WENTWORTH
2 CONT **'''2. Anne WENTWORTH (B. Wentworth & Dow)'''
2 CONT *Married 2: Lucy WENTWORTH (C. Cleveland) BEF 25 Oct 1638
2 CONT *Children:
2 CONT **3. Catherine WENTWORTH
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/WENTWORTH.htm#Thomas WENTWORTH (1° E. Cleveland) ____________
2 CONT *'''The Wentworth genealogy, comprising the origin of the name, the family in England, and a particular account of Elder William Wentworth, the emigrant, and of his descendants (1870)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgenealo01inwent#page/n107/mode/2up
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgenealo01inwent#page/n178/mode/1up
2 CONT *(19) Sir Thomas Wentworth, Kt., 2d Baron Wentworth, who is said in the Peerages to have been summoned to Parliament "from 23 January, 1552 to 4 February, 1489." This is, however, an error; as the letters of Administration granted
2 CONC  on his estate to his son Henry, on the 18 January, 1583-4, have been found. He died at Stepney, Co. Middlesex. He was one of Queen Mary's Privy Council, and Deputy of Calais when that place was surrendered, in 1558. He was subseq
2 CONC uently tried by his peers and honorably acquitted. He was one of the noblemen who sat in judgment on the Duke of Norfolk, and also upon Mary, Queen of Scots.
2 CONT *He was twice married. His first wife was Mary, daughter of Sir John Wentworth, Kt., of Gosfield, Co. of Essex, to whom he was married at Gosfield, 9 February, 1545-6 (For her descent, see back to No. 2, of XIV., of this note).
2 CONT *She died without issue, and he remarried Anne, daughter of Henry Wentworth, Esq., of Co. Suffolk. She was buried at Stepney, Co. Middlesex, in 1571. By her he had issue as follows:
2 CONT **1. Thomas, who married Elizabeth, daughter of Wm. Cecil, Lord Burghley, but died without issue in his father's lifetime.
2 CONT **2. Henry, 3d Baron, of whom hereafter.
2 CONT **3. Elizabeth, who married William Hynde, Esq., son and heir of Sir Francis Hynde, Kt., of Madingley, Co. Cambridge.
2 CONT *The line was continues by the 2d son
2 CONT *(20) Henry Wentworth, 3d Baron Wentworth, who is said in the Peerages to have been summoned to Parliament 19 February, 1593, probably 1592-3, as  administration was granted on his estate the 30 August, 1593. His wife, who suvived
2 CONC  him, was Anne, daughter of Sir Owen Hopton, Kt., Lieutenant of the Tower of London and relict of Sir Wm. Pope, Kt.
2 CONT *They had issue as follows:
2 CONT **1. Thomas, 4th Baron, etc., of whom hereafter.
2 CONT **2. Henry, a Major General in the service of King Charles I., who died in 1644.
2 CONT **3. Jane, who married Sir John Finet, Kt., of West Keele, Co. Lincoln.
2 CONT *The line was continued by
2 CONT *(21) Thomas Wentworth, 4th Baron Wentworth, who was created, 7 February, 1625-6, Baron Wentworth of Nettlestead, and Earl of Cleveland.
2 CONT *He suffered greatly for his loyalty to King Charles I. He died 26 March 1667, in his 76th year, and was buried at Toddington, Co. Bedford. He was twice married. His first wife was Anne, daughter of Sir John Crofts, Kt., of Saxmun
2 CONC dham, Co. Suffolk, who was buried at Toddington aforesaid, in January 1637-8. His second wife was Lucy (wrongly called Catherine in the Peerages), second daughter and co-heir of Sir John Wentworth, Kt., and Bart. of Gosfield Hall,
2 CONC  Co. Essex.
2 CONT *Her descent is briefly as follows:
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/wentworthgenealo01inwent#page/n180/mode/1up
2 CONT *Sir Roger Wentworth, of Codham Hall, by his wife Anne Tyrrell (see back to number 2 of XIV. of this note) had a second son, Henry Wentworth, Esq., Mountnessing, Co. Essex, and died in his father's lifetime, leaving by his wife An
2 CONC ne, daughter of Reginald Hammond, Esq. (with other issue), a son, John Wentworth, Esq., born 1540, buried at Gosfield, 3 April, 1588. He inherited Gosfield Hall from his uncle Sir John Wentworth, Kt. (see back to number 2 of XIV. 
2 CONC of this note), and by his first wife Elizabeth, daughter of Sir Christopher Heydon, Kt., had with other issue a son, John Wentworth, Esq., born 1564, buried in Gosfield church, 11 February, 1613-4, who married Cecelia, daughter of
2 CONC  Sir Edward Unton, by Anne, Countess of Warwick, and had with other issue Sir John Wentworth, Kt., of Gosfield, created a Baronet, 29 June, 1611, and died in October 1631, when, having no surviving male issue, the Baronetcy expire
2 CONC d.
2 CONT *He married Catherine, daughter of Sir Moyle Finch, Kt., who died in September 1639. They had, with several other daughters, a daughter Lucy, who married Thomas, 4th Baron Wentworth, and 1st Earl of Cleveland, as above stated.
2 CONT *The Earl of Cleveland, by his first wife had issue as follows:
2 CONT **1. Thomas, of whom hereafter.
2 CONT **2. William, who died in 1623, aged six years, and was buried at Toddington, Co. Bedford.
2 CONT **3. Charles, who died in 1622, in his second year, and was buried at Toddington aforesaid.
2 CONT **4. Anne, who died in infancy.
2 CONT **5. Mary, who died unmarried in January 1632-3, aged 18, and was buried at Toddington.
2 CONT **'''6. Anne, of whom hereafter.
2 CONT *The Earl by his second wife, Lucy, had an only daughter
2 CONT **7. Catherine, who married William Spencer, Esq., of Cople, Co. Bedford, and died without issue.
2 CONT *The second wife, Lucy, died 23 November 1651, and was buried at Toddington.
2 CONT *The line was continued through
2 CONT *   (22) Thomas Wentworth, who was summoned to Parliament, 16 Chas. I., as Lord Wentworth of Nettlestead. He was also knight, and died 7 March, 1664-5, in his father's lifetime and without male issue. He married Philadelphia, daug
2 CONC hter of Sir Ferdinand Carey, Ktn., who died 4 May 1696, and was buried, with her husband, at Toddington aforesaid. Their only issue was a daughter
2 CONT **   (23) Henrietta Maria, who, according to the terms of the patent, succeeded her grandfather as Baroness Wentworth. She survived his execution only a few months, dying 23 April 1686, and was buried at Toddington.
2 CONT *The male line ceased of course on the death of her grandfather, the Earl of Cleveland, and that title expired; but the Barony of Wentworth passed from her, at her death, to her '''Aunt Anne (see back to number 6 of (21) of this n
2 CONC ote), who married, 11 July 1638, John, Lord Lovelace. They had a son''' John, '''who died in his mother's lifetime''', leaving a daughter, Martha, who succeeded to the Barony of Wentworth. _______________________
2 CONT *'''Pedigrees of the county families of Yorkshire (1874) Vol. 2 Pg.n258 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/pedigreesofcount02fost
2 CONT **Pedigree of Wentworth, of Elmsall, Bretton and Baron Wentworth, of Nettlested.
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/pedigreesofcount02fost#page/n265/mode/1up
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/pedigreesofcount02fost#page/n266/mode/1up
2 CONT *SEE DOCUMENTS OR SOURCES __________________
