2 FORM txt
2 TITL "The Overby Story"
1 NOTE Nicholas Overbey was born in England. He came to America during the time of Bacon's Rebellion, in the year of 1676 and lived in a fort near Jamestown, VA. After that he went into farming. His farm was where the city of Petersburg,
2 CONC  VA now stands (1895).
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT This Nicholas Overby is the immigrant from England.  His name was orginally Nicholas Overbury, but Overbury was gradually changed to Overby.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas OVERBY, born in Worcester County England in 1622 , died in Prince George County, VA. Notes: Dates of death include 1704, 1706/7, 1714
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The following taken fron the book "Foot Print in Time" by Helen Edwards
2 CONT
2 CONT      Nicholas Overby, Overbey, Overbee, Overbury, the immigrant the first ancestor of your line to arrive in Virginia, has had many spellings of his last name over the years and generations.  It started out as Overbury, bit since 
2 CONC most people of that day spelt names the way they sounded, it was not long before Overbury had become Overbey.  It has had many spellings but finally settled on Overby.  The spelling of the name might be spelled two different ways 
2 CONC in a single document.
2 CONT      The family were originally from the small village of Overbury from which they possibly took their names hundreds of years earlier. 
2 CONT      This Nicholas Overbury would have been about 26 years old in the early 1650s which was
2 CONT usually considered the best age in which to strike out for a new land to "make your fortune".  Being a younger son of Sir Giles he could not count on an inheritance for his security because of the laws of primogeniture, i.e. the o
2 CONC ldest son always inherited the home and lands of their father.  Nicholas had to make his own way in the world.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Nancy cnnewby@ind.tds.net
2 CONT The spelling of OVERBEY-OVERBY originally was spelled OVERBURY and numerous other spellings have been found.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the North Carolina marriage application, dated July 1, 1799, for one Peter 'Overbee' and Susanna Stovall. In the body of the document, spelling is OVERBEE, but at the bottom for the signature, the spelling is OVERBEY.
2 CONT In the will of Adam (a son of Nicholas Overby and Jane ?) who died in 1780 the name is spelled OVERBY. In the Book, CUMBERLAND PARISH Lunenburg Co., Virginia, Vestry Book 1746-1816, by Landon C. Bell, the following item is stated 
2 CONC on page 370:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ordered that Richard Yansey (,) Wm Coalbreath, Wm Royster, Thomas Wiles, Peter Overby, & Nicholas Overby, do meet at the Mouth or (of) Grassey Creek on the 12th of Novemb(e)r next, & Procession all the Lands between Grassey Creek
2 CONC , County Line, Arons Creek & Roanoak River & make return according to Law."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the book, Life by the Roaring Roanoke, by Susan L. Bracey, there are both spellings: OVERBY & OVERBEY.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Lou Lisenbe marylou1@flash.net
2 CONT The Overbey family cemetary in Mecklenburg, Co., Virginia has grave stones of Robert Yancy Overbey. Ann Overby (born October 28, 1775, died June 17, 1859.) The letter "e" is missing between the "b" & "y" in the last name of Ann.
2 CONT
2 CONT A stone about 3 ft wide & 5 ft high near the front of the cemetary has the 
2 CONT following on it:
2 CONT
2 CONT Erected To The Memory Of The Overbey Family
2 CONT Robert Yancey Overbey Mary P.Poole
2 CONT Children married
2 CONT Minerva James E. Haskins
2 CONT Mary Adeline James Shelton, M.D.
2 CONT Rosalie Charles E. Williams
2 CONT Narcissa James A. Carr, M.D.
2 CONT Sarah Eugenia James T. Page
2 CONT Cornelia Caroline Benjamin W. L. Holt
2 CONT Major Johnstons Co.
2 CONT Tennessee Heavy Artillery
2 CONT Robert Camillus Anna Nelson Williams
2 CONT Capt. Cp. G. 59th Virginia
2 CONT Inf Wise's Brigade
2 CONT Ann Celestia William L. Trice
2 CONT Alice Rebecca Thomas D. Jefferess
2 CONT Capt. Co. G 56th Virginia Inf.
2 CONT Picketts Div.
2 CONT Emma John A. Coke
2 CONT Capt. Lee Artillery
2 CONT 1st Virginia Artillery
2 CONT Pendletons Brigade"
2 CONT
2 CONT He emigrated to America the year after his father died.  Our Nicholascame to Virginia in 1654 as a headright for Colonel Abraham Wood whowas a merchant, explorer and land trader. Based on ship records fromBristol, England, the p
2 CONC ort from which Nicholas sailed, he was notindentured or apprenticed like most other emigrants. Nicholas wasemployed in some capacity by Colonel Wood who was well known by theOverbury family. Virginia records show business transact
2 CONC ions byNicholas Overbey and Thomas Chamberlayne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Wood operated his trading business from Fort Henry which wasbuilt in 1645 for the defense of the citizens on the south side of theJames River. It was on a bluff near the falls not far from where theAppomatox River enters t
2 CONC he James River. The garrison had 45 soldiersfrom the surrounding counties and Abraham Wood was placed in command.The Fort and 600 acres were granted to Abraham Wood. The area is northof what is now called Petersburg, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT From this little city of Fort Henry, Colonel Wood began exploratoryjourneys with pack trains of 50-100 horses loaded with trinkets,blankets and guns. In the year, 1650, he went on expeditions to thewest and south with Edward Bland
2 CONC . In 1652, by order of the Assembly,Abraham Wood (then a Major) was granted the right to discover andtrade for 14 years , places where no Englishman had ever been.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the year 1654, our ancestor Nicholas Overbey, Sr., had been broughtfrom England by Abraham Wood to help him in some way with his growingbusiness interests. By 1675, Wood had expanded his business journeysover the Great Smokies 
2 CONC and into the powerful Cherokee Nation.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no records of our Nicholas Overbey, Sr. owning any land ofhis own between 1654 and 1680. It is assumed that he traveled a lotand spent all these years helping Colonel Wood. He provided skilledlabor and intelligence for t
2 CONC he development of the trading empire andthe management of the land holdings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our Nicholas Overbey, Sr., was the father of a son born about 1659 inVirginia. The son was called Nicholas "ye younger" and "ye son". Ishall call him Nicholas, Junior.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1685 , our Nicholas, Sr. purchased a developed tract of land forhis son Nicholas, Jr. with all houses, edifices, buildings, orchards,gardens, forests and all profit on this same tract of land andplantation. (Henrico Co., VA Dee
2 CONC ds, 1677-1692) This frontier land wasan area that was administered by Charles City County, VA, but wasformed into Prince George Co., VA in 1705. The land was probably nearFort Henry, just south of the Appomatox River, in what was 
2 CONC calledBristol Parish.The land was a wedding gift to Nicholas Overbey, Jr.Witnesses to the deed were Dysons, who lived near the site of WoodsChurch , five miles north of Petersburg, Viginia. It is considered the"Mother Church" of B
2 CONC ristol Parish and was named for Abraham Wood. OurNicholas Overbeys son (Jr.) learned well from his father and fromColonel Wood and by the year 1690, (Jr.) was earning more land forhimself, by transporting his own "headrights" fro
2 CONC m England and takingland grants for their emigration.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were no further records of our Nicholas Overbey, Sr. after 1690and I assume he died shortly after 1690, in Bristol Parish, Virginia(originally part of Charles City County), some where between theAppomatox River and the town 
2 CONC of Petersburg, VA, not far from FortHenry.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Please contact me with any additional information! Thanks!!!
