2 CONT
2 CONT America's First Families
2 CONT Ancestor Roll Of Honor
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT NICHOLAS OVERBEY, SR.
2 CONT VIRGINIA
2 CONT (1628-c1691) 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas Overbury was born at Isleworth, in Middlesex, England and baptized November 4, 1628 at the parish church in Middlesex. He died after1690 in the colony of Virginia in America. In Middlesex England, his family name was Over
2 CONC bury, but in America it became Overbey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas was the third son of Sir Giles Overbury and Anne Shirley, who had a total of eight (8) children. The oldest son, Sir Thomas inherited the family fortune, the second son Giles, Jr. died at age 10 and our Nicholas emigrated
2 CONC  to America to seek his fortune, the year after his father died. His father was buried at Bourton-on-the-Hill, Gloucestershire, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There were many reasons for emigration to America. The bloody civil war had raged in the Cotswold Hills, home of the Overburys and with the fall of the Monarchy, it was a good time for Royalists to leave England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Our Nicholas came to Virginia in 1654 as a headright for Colonel Abraham Wood who was a merchant, explorer and land trader. Based on ship records from Bristol, England, the port from which Nicholas sailed, he was not indentured 
2 CONC or apprenticed like most other emigrants. Nicholas was employed in some capacity by Colonel Wood who was well known by the Overbury family. Virginia records show business transactions by Nicholas Overbey and Thomas Chamberlayne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Wood operated his trading business from Fort Henry which was built in 1645 for the defense of the citizens on the south side of the James River. It was on a bluff near the falls not far from where the Appomatox River enter
2 CONC s the James River. The garrison had 45 soldiers from the surrounding counties and Abraham Wood was placed in command. The Fort and 600 acres were granted to Abraham Wood. The area is north of what is now called Petersburg, Virgini
2 CONC a. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From this little city of Fort Henry, Colonel Wood began exploratory journeys with pack trains of 50-100 horses loaded with trinkets, blankets and guns. In the year, 1650, he went on expeditions to the west and south with Edward Bl
2 CONC and. In 1652, by order of the Assembly, Abraham Wood (then a Major) was granted the right to discover and trade for 14 years , places where no Englishman had ever been. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By the year 1654, our ancestor Nicholas Overbey, Sr., had been brought from England by Abraham Wood to help him in some way with his growing business interests. By 1675, Wood had expanded his business journeys over the Great Smoki
2 CONC es and into the powerful Cherokee Nation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no records of our Nicholas Overbey, Sr. owning any land of his own between 1654 and 1680. It is assumed that he traveled a lot and spent all these years helping Colonel Wood. He provided skilled labor and intelligence fo
2 CONC r the development of the trading empire and the management of the land holdings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Our Nicholas Overbey, Sr., was the father of a son born about 1659 in Virginia. The son was called Nicholas ye younger and ye son. I shall call him Nicholas, Junior. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1685 , our Nicholas, Sr. purchased a developed tract of land for his son Nicholas, Jr. with all houses, edifices, buildings, orchards, gardens, forests and all profit on this same tract of land and plantation. (Henrico Co., VA 
2 CONC Deeds, 1677-1692) This frontier land was an area that was administered by Charles City County, VA, but was formed into Prince George Co., VA in 1705. The land was probably near Fort Henry, just south of the Appomatox River, in wha
2 CONC t was called Bristol Parish.The land was a wedding gift to Nicholas Overbey, Jr. Witnesses to the deed were Dysons, who lived near the site of Woods Church , five miles north of Petersburg, Viginia. It is considered the Mother Ch
2 CONC urch of Bristol Parish and was named for Abraham Wood. Our Nicholas Overbeys son (Jr.) learned well from his father and from Colonel Wood and by the year 1690, (Jr.) was earning more land for himself, by transporting his own he
2 CONC adrights from England and taking land grants for their emigration. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There were no further records of our Nicholas Overbey, Sr. after 1690 and I assume he died shortly after 1690, in Bristol Parish, Virginia (originally part of Charles City County), some where between the Appomatox River and the to
2 CONC wn of Petersburg, VA, not far from Fort Henry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Child of Nicholas Overbey, Sr. and his unknown wife, was: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Nicholas,  b. abt.1659, in Charles City Co. VA d.c.1740,  m. c1685 in Bristol Parish in Prince George Co. VA 
2 CONT His children were; Nicholas III, William, Richard, Ptere, James, Ludwell and Abraham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference "The Overby Story" A wonderful Book by Libby Overbey and Gateway Press Inc. 
2 CONT Submitted by Harold Eugene Rainey of Goodlettsville, TN 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
