1 NOTE  Jill Herndon 	Date: March 12, 2001 at 09:44:35
2 CONT My ancestor William Edward Herndon m. Mary Elizabeth Woolfolk. New research shows that she was the daughter of Susan Cole Woolfolk and her cousin William Woolfolk. Susan's mother was Mary Cole, the daughter of William Cole and Sus
2 CONC annah Digges, and this Susannah was the descendant of Dudley Digges, brother to Catherine Digges who married the first William Herndon.
2 CONT William Edward Herndon is son of David Herndon and Mary Moorman, grandson of Edmond and Letticia (Moseley) Herndon, great grandson of David Herndon b. 1712 to Edward and Mary Waller.
2 CONT William Edward and Mary Elizabeth had 5 sons -- I come from the third one Edward Beverly Herndon Sr/Jr/and my Dad is a IIIrd.
2 CONT There is another EBH line that goes to the IV but it starts with a younger nephew of my EBH, I think from Jesse Moorman Herndon the youngest of WE and ME's children.
1 NOTE Notes
2 CONT Member of House of Burgesses, Colonel of Warwick County militia, prominent tobacco planter. Four children. 
2 CONT This is to the memory of his son Dudley, who married Miss Cole, of Denbigh:-- Old Churches, Ministers, and Families of Virginia. Article XVIII. 
2 CONT "Sub hoc marmore requiescit in pace Dudleus Digges, armiger, Susannµ Digges juxta depositµ maritus amantissimus. Vir et virtute, et pro sapientia, vere inclytus, qui hujusce Coloniµ primo Consilioris, dein ad Auditoris dignitatem,
2 CONC  erectus est. Obiit, omnibus desideratus, 27 Januarii, 1710, µtatis suµ 47. Justorum animµ in manu Dei sunt." 
2 CONT Which is thus rendered:-- "Under this marble rests in peace Dudley Digges, gentleman, the most loving husband of Susannah Digges, buried near him. He was a man very eminent for virtue and wisdom, who was first raised to the dignit
2 CONC y of Councillor and then Auditor of this Colony. He died, lamented by all, the 27th of January, 1710, in his forty-seventh year. 'The souls of the righteous are in the hand of God.' " 
2 CONT Dudley Digges of York county, son of Edward Digges, governor of Virginia, was born about 1665. Sometime in 1698 Gov. Andros appointed him a member of the council, but for some reason he was not continued in office by Gov. Nicholso
2 CONC n, and on Jan. 4, 1699-1700, the lords of trade and plantations wrote to Nicholson that they approved of his action in not admitting Col. Digges. Whatever the objection may have been it was removed in a few years, for on Feb. 23, 
2 CONC 1703-04, the Queen appointed Col. Dudley Digges to the council, as had been recommended by Gov. Nicholson. He was also included in a new commission dated Feb. 23, 1709-10. In 1705 Digges was appointed auditor and surveyor- general
2 CONC  of Virginia, offices which he held until his death, Jan. 18, 1710-11. 
