1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/GEN-MEDIEVAL/2001-01/0979159898
2 CONT
2 CONT In Will Book 4, starting on page 44, is the inventory of Daniel Harrison. The inventory was taken on 3 October, 1677, and was recorded on 12 Dec. 1677 and was presented by Mary Harrison. The inventory is  extremely long and takes 
2 CONC up about 7 pages in the will book. I will post the beginning of this inventory and the ending, as the middle consists of items in the estate, debts, etc., again the exact spelling.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==A True and Pefecte Inventory and Acct. of the Estate of Mr. Daniel
2 CONT Harrison  (deced.) as it was taken and apprized this 3d day of October 1677, by us whose names are hereunto subscribed, being sworne according to an Ordr. of > Cort. by Mr. Edw. Dale.==
2 CONT
2 CONT Next is the inventory, debts, etc., followed by:
2 CONT
2 CONT  "Exhibit in Cur. Com. Lancastr: 12 die Dec: 1677  p.  Oaths of Mary Harrison, widdow and relict of Dan: Harrison deced., and admitted to record.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Teste John Stretchley, Cl Cur"
2 CONT
2 CONT  Also of interest in reference to Mary Harrison are the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Lancaster County Orders, 1666-1680:   Court 11 July 1677
2 CONT *  P. 379, "Com. of Adm. on the estate of Mr. Daniel Harrison to Mary Harrison  the widdow and relict of same and inventory to be exhibited."
2 CONT * Lancaster County Orders, 1666-1680:  Court 13 March 1677/8
2 CONT *  P. 416, Cert. to Mary Harrison widdow and relict of Mr. Daniell
2 CONT * Harrison for  the trans. of Katherine Moone, Jane Wilson, Jeremiah Thornton, Richard Asale,  Richard Lambert, John Willet, John Panmer, Edmund Nugent, Elizabeth Godsell  and Priscilla Howles.
2 CONT * 
2 CONT
2 CONT  On the subject of the term, son in law in the colonial period could have been used as describe a foster child, stepchild, etc., BUT not always. I discussed this with Mr. Preston Haynie, a director of the Northumberland County His
2 CONC torical Society. Preston devotes most of his time to genealogy on the Northern Neck of Virginia (Northumberland, Lancaster, Westmoreland and Richmond  counties). He is extremely knowledgeable on the subject. If he isn't at the his
2 CONC torical society, he can be found in the record room of the clerk of the county office in the Northumberland County courthouse or somewhere in the Library of Virginia doing research. Preston told me that the term son in law in colo
2 CONC nial records has to be taken on a case by case basis and all facts determined to decide exactly what the term means in relationship to the parties involved.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thank you. Joan Burdyck
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/VA-NORTHERN-NECK/2014-08/140 8392788
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 3, 1684/85, Middlesex County DB 2-177, Edward Dale petitioned for custody of Humphrey Jones, Jr., and stated that "Mr. Humphrey Jones of this county lately deceed did intermarry with youre petitionrs daughter desed". E
2 CONC dward Dale said that the protection of the child of right belonged to him as being the next of kin. He said that he would care for him and educate him until he reached his full age. The court gave the custody of young Humphrey to 
2 CONC his older half sister, Mary Jones Segar, a child of a previous marriage of Humphrey Jones, Sr. Mary Jones Segar, wife of Randolph Segar passed away on November 8, 1690 and was buried in the Upper Chapel (Christ Church, Middlesex C
2 CONC o.).
2 CONT
