2 CONT *LEWIS, William (c.1625-61), of Bletchington, Oxon. and The Van, Glam.
2 CONT *b. c.1625, '''2nd s. of Sir Edward Lewis (d.1630) of The Van and Edington Priory, Wilts.''', and bro. of Richard Lewis. educ. Jesus, Oxf. matric. 12 Oct. 1638, aged 14; travelled abroad (France, Italy) 1642-6. m. 24 June 1649, Ma
2 CONC rgaret, da. and h. of Lawrence Banastre of Boarstall, Bucks., 2s. 2da. suc. bro. 1647.1
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT **Commr. for assessment, Oxon. Jan. 1660-d., militia Mar. 1660, j.p. Mar. 1660-d., dep. lt. c. Aug. 1660-d.
2 CONT *Lewis was head of a very old, but thoroughly Anglicized Glamorgan family, distinguished chiefly for prudent marriages, but not previously aspiring to a seat in Parliament. '''His father, a courtier, married the widow of the 1st E
2 CONC arl of Hertfords heir, and bought the remainder of a long lease of Edington in 1629, dying there in the following year. Lewiss mother was careful to preserve neutrality in the Civil War, sending her eldest sons abroad''' lest thei
2 CONC r uncles, the Marquess of Hertford and the 4th Earl of Dorset, should engage them in the royalist cause. The Glamorgan committee testified in 1648 that Lewis was well-affected and had made large contributions to their funds; but i
2 CONC t was probably a paternal uncle of the same name who sat with them throughout the Interregnum. Lewismarriage to a Buckinghamshire heiress enabled him to buy an estate at Bletchington in 1656 for ÃÂÃÂ£10,000. He probably '''owed
2 CONC  his election for Devizes, ten miles from Edington, to his mother, whose lavish hospitality at the Priory was notable chiefly for her excellent brew of beer. As she also secured the return for Westbury of another son''', it is imp
2 CONC ossible to distinguish his record in the Convention, but he was clearly inactive. Lord Wharton seems to have regarded him as a moderate Anglican. One or other of the brothers was appointed to the committees for altering the entail
2 CONC  on Lord Hertfords estates, attainting Oliver Cromwell, and considering the petition of a former serjeant-at-arms; but they made no speeches. He lost his seat at the general election, and died later in the same year. His widow bro
2 CONC ught Bletchington and other lands worth in all 38,000 to her second husband, the 5th Duke of Richmond; but on her death in 1667 the Boarstall estate went to her son Edward Lewis.2
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/member/lewis-william-1625-61 __________________________
2 CONT *EDWARD LEWIS was the first that took the surname. He built the older portions of Van , near Caerphilly, Glam. , and enclosed the park . He purchased the manor of Roath-Keynsham , part of the estate of Keynsham abbey , and was she
2 CONC riff of Glamorgan in 1548 , 1555 , and 1559 . His wife was Ann , daughter of Sir William Morgan , of Pencoyd, Mon. , a member of the Tredegar family . 
2 CONT *THOMAS LEWIS , his son, was sheriff of Glamorgan in 1569 . His first wife was Margaret Gamage of Coity , at the time widow of Miles Mathew of Llandaff . He added to the Van and built the Lewis house in S. Mary Street , Cardiff , 
2 CONC which was finally demolished about 1865 . He d. at Cardiff 2 Nov. 1594 . The ÃÂ¢ÃÂÃÂinquisitionesÃÂ¢ÃÂÃÂ taken on his death are of considerable local interest. 
2 CONT *Sir EDWARD LEWIS ( 1560- 1628 ), eldest son of Thomas Lewis [the man who gave the living of Llanfaches to William Wroth , q.v. ], was knighted in 1603 , and was sheriff of Glamorgan in 1601 and 1612 . He indulged in a good deal o
2 CONC f litigation and added considerably to the family estates. The most interesting of his purchases was S. Fagans castle , bought from Sir William Herbert of Cardiff in 1616 , and now the Folk Museum of Wales . This included the pres
2 CONC ent house built by Dr. John Gibbon about 1590 . He d. 9 Jan. 1628 . 
2 CONT *'''Sir EDWARD LEWIS (d. 1630 ), the second, was also knighted in 1603 . He lived at Edington, Wilts. , of which he was lessee. He was Gentleman of the Privy Chamber to prince Henry in 1610 and afterwards to prince Charles . He d.
2 CONC  at Edington , 10 Oct. 1630 two years after his father. 
2 CONT *WILLIAM LEWIS (d. 1661 ), succeeded to the estate and d. in 1661 . His wife was Margaret , heiress of the estates of Brill and Boarstal , to both of which he added. He was a strong Parliamentarian . 
2 CONT *EDWARD LEWIS ( 1650- 1674 ), who was born 30 July 1650 , bequeathed the Glamorgan estate to his uncle Richard Lewis in tail male. 
2 CONT **The uncle, RICHARD LEWIS ( 1623- 1706 ), purchased the manor of Corsham and was buried there. He certainly neglected and possibly dismantled Van . He d. 7 Oct. 1706 . 
2 CONT *From: http://wbo.llgc.org.uk/en/s-LEWI-VAN-1548.html ___________________________________
