1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "Sir Herbert and Sir Thomas Lunsford, both of Lunsford, Sussex, the first said by the enemies to be the fairer, ther other the shrewdest adversary." "Both the biggest men, though twins, you could likely see....both
2 CONC  bred in the Dutch and German wars, both in command in the Scotch war....The first mention...of Sir Herbert Lunsford is in 1633, when, as an accomplice with his brother in his outrage on Sir Thomas Pelham, he was sent up to London
2 CONC  by the magistrates of Sussex.. On the 2nd of December, 1637, he administered to the effects of his late father. At the muster at York he held the rank of Captain in his brother Sir Thomas's regiment." etc. On the 6 July 1644 or 1
2 CONC 645, he was knighted.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the twin of Thomas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his brother Thomas he was "bred in the Dutch Dutch and Grrman wars, and was concerned with him in the outrage on Pelham in 1633. At the muster at york in 1640 he was captain in his brother's regiment, and was present at the b
2 CONC attle of Edgehill. In 1643 he distinguished himself at Rupert's capture of Cirencester. He was then made governor of Malmesbury, but was taken prisoner when Waller captured that place, 23 March 1643." He succeeded Thomas in the go
2 CONC vernment of Monmouth. In October of the same year he yielded up Monmouth to Col. Morgan, governor of Gloucester. "He subsequently passed over to France, where in 1568 he was temporarily in command of three regiments. he returned t
2 CONC o England evidently some time after the Restoraton, he presented a petition to Charles in 1665, and was in command of a company of foot in 1667. "
2 CONT
2 CONT "miles, a Colonel in the army of Charles I, now commander-in-Chiefe of 3 regiments in France in ye absence of General Edw. Cooke, knighted 6 July 1644; administered to his father's effectes 2 Dec. 1637" [6, 8, 9, 10]
2 CONT
