1 NOTE {geni:about_me} September 28, 1662 could be the date of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT From "Martha's Extended Family" family tree page on Mary: 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://martisgenes.info/p18.htm#i172
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary (?)[1],[2]
2 CONT *F, b. 1632
2 CONT *Birth* Mary was born in 1632 in England.[2]  
2 CONT *Marriage* She married Richard Browne in 1656 in England.[2]  
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Richard Browne b. s 1625, d. 9 Aug 1662 
2 CONT *Children 
2 CONT **1. Daniel Brown+ 
2 CONT ***b. s 1654, 
2 CONT ***d. c 1714
2 CONT **2. James Brown+ 
2 CONT ***b. 27 Mar 1656, 
2 CONT ***d. 1715
2 CONT **3. William Brown+ 
2 CONT ***b. 29 Jan 1658, 
2 CONT ***d. 1746
2 CONT **4. Jeremiah Brown 
2 CONT ***b. 19 Aug 1660
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations:
2 CONT *1.[S187] Warren E Pickett, John Piggott Sr. (1680 ?- 1738) of Susquehannah Hundred in Cecil County MD. Together with some account of the Browne and Clayton families from whom his wife Margarey Brown Piggott descended.
2 CONT *2.[S1047] World Family Tree Volume 57, tree 1712.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Popular consensus among the many family historians rests with Mary Masters whose parents William and Alice Masters resided at Cirencester’s Abbey and were prominent in Gloucestershire’s history. To accept Mary Masters is to acknow
2 CONC ledge her age at marriage at around 14 years.   Not unusual for the times.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now this is where I teeter on the edge of fact and fiction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Official records of Quaker births, deaths and marriages in England weren’t kept until 1746 with a resulting black hole regarding the Browne family in the 1600’s. There are several entries in early American Quaker records referring
2 CONC  to James and William’s father having been a prominent businessman before becoming a Quaker...(and I should imagine later suffering because of his beliefs and as a result being reduced to poverty.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Both brothers were under the age of 10 years when their father died in 1662, their only memories of him from their time in the village of Podington. The brothers have been described as corders,  involved in the wool industry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT So  it is not beyond the realms of possibility for Richard to have met and married Mary Masters in Cirencester, a noted wool market of the time.  The Abbey itself was built in the 12th century with money provided by the wool trade
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother, the former Alice Escott, provides another strong link to a Browne connection. Alice Escott’s family were well known in Devon.  Her pedigree included the Glanville family, the Mildmay’s, Bouchiers and at least two famil
2 CONC ies of Brownes, including a Thomasine Browne who married John Glanville and whose son Sir John Glanville became a Judge in the Court of Common Pleas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With such a preponderance of Brownes floating about in England’s history there is every chance our Quaker Brownes, on the maternal side at least, descended from one of the Devon Browne's through Alice Masters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Richard’s provenance, there are so many likely Browne's I would need another lifetime and a few stiff drinks to research them all.  For the present he remains a mystery.
2 CONT
2 CONT ... These are just two snippets to be found in British Archives.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Glanville of Tavistock. Buried 1579. Will proved 1 Feb 1580. Crest of the stag granted by Robt. Cooke, Herald, 1576. + Thomasine (probably da. Of Thomas Browne, Esq., of Tavistock). Mentioned in her husband’s will.
2 CONT                                                                     ... Petition of Henry Mildmay, to the King (now in HL/PO/JO/10/4). 6 September 1660 – Application for an order for removal of Wm. Browne and his wife and others, 
2 CONC who are in possession of the Duchy House in the Strand, and refuse to render possession or to pay any rent. Lords Journals, XI. 159.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The brothers Browne remaining behind in England remained staunch Quakers. Well into the 1740’s their names, Daniel, Joseph and John are mentioned in English Archives being censured because of their Quaker beliefs. Mary their mothe
2 CONC r died in 1676 in Wiltshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT In America William and James Browne will be well documented with only the provenance of their parents in doubt.  No doubt somewhere in the Quaker records at Wellingborough someone will eventually find an obscure reference to Richa
2 CONC rd and Mary, and perhaps even her surname.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT As you can imagine, I  found it difficult to place my Grandfather, ChasBert. in the midst of this Quaker background.  
2 CONT
2 CONT A man who punched a news editor on the nose and then poured ink over his head, a man who tarred and feathered another would be viewed with absolute horror by his many times removed Quaker Grandfather, Richard Browne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the circumstances I don’t think even Quaker Richard would consider a mouth wash with soap sufficient punishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT But I am extraordinarily proud that somewhere way back in the mists of time I can claim connection to a family with such staunch beliefs enshrined in peace and goodwill to others, that they were willing to send two sons to the end
2 CONC s of the earth to preach their creed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though not a member of the Society of Friends, I feel privileged to acknowledge a Quaker family occupying such a prominent perch in my family tree.
2 CONT
2 CONT It’s just a pity some of this piety didn’t rub off on Grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Should any of my readers have additional information, or even wish to correct, please contact me through the comments box.
2 CONT
