2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/18536019/thomas-fenner
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on the Thomas Fenner house:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Fenner_House
2 CONT
2 CONT The Thomas Fenner House or the "Sam Joy Place" is an historic stone-ender house on 43 Stony Acre Drive in Cranston, Rhode Island. It is one of the oldest surviving houses in Rhode Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT The house was built as a farmhouse around 1677 after King Philip's War. The house was added to the National Historic Register in 1990. It is privately-owned and is not open to the public.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Thomas Fenner house was built by Captian Arthur Fenner for his son Major Thomas Fenner.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Arthur Fenner['s] first home was burnt to the ground in King Phillips War where he was appointed Captain of the militia and was "one who staid and went not away" in the defense of Providence. His original homestead was bui
2 CONC lt in 1662. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, he rebuilt his home in 1677 and he also built, for his son, the Major Thomas Fenner house. It is an historical landmark located on Stony Acre Drive in Cranston, R.I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Fenner Castle" stood until 1895 when the chimney was demolished. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His grandson was Governor Arthur Fenner who donated a piece of wood from the "Fenner Castle" for what is now the RI Mace. He did so to honor his grandfather, Captain Arthur Fenner who so bravely defended Providence from the Indian
2 CONC s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My reference for this is from The Rhode Island Historical Magazine, Sketch of Capt. Arthur Fenner of Providence, A paper read before the RI Historical Society, March 23 and April 6, 1886 by Rev.J.P. Root.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Source http://www.gaspee.com/Mace.htm )
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Fenner-58
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 15 Oct 2024 | Created 20 Aug 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT A cemetery record indicates that Major Thomas Fenner was born in or about 1653;[1] James Pierce Root's 1887 "Genealogy of the Fenner Family, however, dates his birth specifically to October 27, 1652, a son of Captain Arthur and Me
2 CONC hitable (Waterman) Fenner. It adds,
2 CONT
2 CONT "He swore allegiance to the English government in May, 1671, about which time, being of age, he probably married. He undoubtedly married as his first wife Alice Ralph, daughter of Thomas Ralph, who was born January 13, 1657. An an
2 CONC cient town record in the Foster Mss., under date of the '11th of ye first month (March) 1676', makes mention of a son born to Tho. Fenner (that date). Thomas Ralph in his will mentions his grandson William Fenner, who could have b
2 CONC een none other than son to Maj. Thomas."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The son noted, William, is the one recorded child of Thomas Fenner's first marriage; that he "died early" is concluded, in Root's Genealogy, "as he has no other record in the family history." William's 1676 birth had taken place i
2 CONC n "the time of the Indian war"; the boy's father, Root notes, "was one who 'staid and & went not away' during the hostilities. Thomas Fenner subsequently acquired significant land beyond what he inherited, which was half of his fa
2 CONC ther's estate, divided between Thomas and his brother Joseph.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Fenner married second, on the 26th of July, 1682, Dinah Borden -- a daughter of Thomas and Mary (Harris) Borden, born in October of 1664 .
2 CONT
2 CONT He died, age 65, in late February of 1718.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will
2 CONT
2 CONT The Root Genealogy records Thomas Fenner's death on 27 February, 1718, adding that his will had been made on the 19th:
2 CONT
2 CONT "In this document he makes due provision for his wife Dinah, and his 'poor helpless child Eleazar,' bestows £5 apiece on his three daughters Freelove Westcotte, Mehitable Starkweather, and Mary Abbott, and divides his remaining pr
2 CONC operty between his five sons Thomas, Richard, Joseph, Arthur and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT "His inventory of personal property amounting to 430 pounds.19. 09."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Arthur was born about 1658. He passed away about 1725. [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was born in 1652. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was born about 1652. He passed away in 1725.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Fenner (1652 - 1718)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Major Thomas Fenner===
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 27 Oct 1652 in Providence, Providence, Rhode Island
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Arthur Fenner and Mehitable (Waterman) Fenner
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Freelove (Fenner) Crawford, Arthur Fenner II, Samuel Fenner, Bethiah (Fenner) Kilton and Phebe (Fenner) Latham
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Alice (Ralph) Fenner — married about 1675 [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Dinah (Borden) Fenner — married 26 Jul 1682 in Providence, Rhode Island, British Colonial America
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of William Fenner, Freelove (Fenner) Stone, Thomas Fenner II, Mehitable (Fenner) Blodgett, Mary (Fenner) Abbott, Joseph Fenner, Richard Fenner, Sarah (Fenner) Antram, Arthur Fenner, Eleazer Fenner and John Fenner
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 27 Feb 1718 at age 65 in Providence, Providence, Rhode Island
2 CONT
2 CONT
