1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ==brief biography and family==
2 CONT From an unidentified source:
2 CONT
2 CONT >Ensign Thomas Lee II was born in 1639 and baptized September 29, 1644 in Rusper, Sussex County, England. He was the son of Thomas Lee I and his wife Phoebe Brown Lee.  Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Lee and their three children Jane, Sarah,
2 CONC  and Thomas II left England in 1645 for America together with Phoebe’s father William Brown. The senior Thomas Lee died of small pox during the crossing. The widow Phoebe married two more times.  First to Greenleaf Larabee and was
2 CONC  the mother of five children: Greenfield, John, Elizabeth, Joseph, and Sarah Larabee. Sarah, the half sister of Thomas II, was the grandmother of the diarist Joshua Hempstead. Phoebe married again to a man named Cornish and had tw
2 CONC o more children, James Cornish and a stillborn. She died in childbirth at Northampton, Massachusetts in 1664. William Brown, his daughter the widow Phoebe Lee, and her children arrived in Saybrook in 1645. According to Glimpses of
2 CONC  Saybrook in Colonial Days, by Harriet Chapman Chesebrough “Their afflicted and distressed condition commended to the sympathies of those at the fort and Thomas II was particularly cared for by Matthew Griswold, and followed him t
2 CONC o Lyme, where in later years he became a prominent citizen and received on arriving his majority a grant of land on the East side of the river”. A Saybrook 1650 division of land lists Thomas Lee (then about age 11) as a grantee. N
2 CONC o documentation of the grant details has been found. A close relationship between the Lee and the Griswold families continued throughout the colonial period. About 1670 Thomas Lee II married Sarah Kirtland, daughter of Nathaniel K
2 CONC irtland of Lynn, Massachusetts. It is probable that the first stage of the Lee house was built at this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT In ''Essays on the Lee Family'' by Wilbur Beckwith, East Lyme Town Historian (downloaded 2011 from eastlymehistoricalsociety.org), Thomas Lee is styled "Ensign Thomas Lee II, the First Lee in America."
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The East Lyme Historical Society maintains the home of this Thomas Lee as a museum: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Lee_House
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT Surname has also been reported to be '''[https://www.geni.com/surnames/laigh Laigh]'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been (erroneously?) reported to be 1639 at Rusper, Horsham, Sussex, England <attestation unknown, conflicts with his Find A Grave memorial>.
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been (erroneously?) reported to be May 30, 1705 at Old Lyme, New London County, Connecticut, British Colonial America <attestation unknown, conflicts with his Find A Grave memorial>.
2 CONT
