1 NOTE Phillip was a member  of Captain Samuel Gallup's Company in the expedition against  Canada. He was a merchant in Providence; in 1705 he was a justice  of the peace. Commencing 1707 and ending 1731 he served twelve  terms as a Depu
2 CONC ty to the General Assembly of Rhode Island from  Providence, and served on several important special committees. In  1714 he was chosen an Assistant to the Governor. Commencing 1719  and ending 1731 he was a selectman in the town 
2 CONC council of Providence,  serving eleven terms. He died March 14, 1732. The inventory  of his estate totaled œ4964 10s., a very large estate, which  included two negro slaves--the negro woman Nimfo, to daughter  Sarah, and the negro
2 CONC  boy, Primus, to son Elisha. His will devised  his entire estate to his fifteen children, his wife, Martha, having died  three years before.
