2 TITL The Tillinghast line 
1 NOTE Pardon removed  from Providence to East Greenwich, where he purchased property  and was made a freeman October 11, 1699. Commencing 1702 and  ending 1725, he served eleven terms as a Deputy to the General  Assembly of Rhode Island
2 CONC . 1705 to 1710 he was a justice of the  peace. He died October 3, 1743, testate. The inventory of his  estate amounted to œ3,089 8s. 14d., a large estate for those days,  including two negro men slaves, at œ120; one of them, "negr
2 CONC o Jack,"  was bequeathed to his son Joseph "to serve him six years and then  to have his freedom." After bequests to the "well beloved brethren  of the Baptist Church" his silver cup for their use forever and œ25  toward defraying
2 CONC  their necessary charges in spreading the gospel,  and "25 to the poor of the Baptist Church," he distributed the remainder  of his estate among his children and grandchildren, naming each  one. The items of his inventory indicate
2 CONC  that he carried on the business  of farming extensively, including stock-raising of all kinds.
