2 CONT Notes about Edward HAZEN:
2 CONT
2 CONT NameEdward HAZEN33, pgs. 215-16,26, 9G Grandfather
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthbef 14 Dec 1614, Cadney, Lincolnshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Deathbef 22 Jul 1683, Rowley, Essex, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial22 Jul 1683, Rowley, Essex, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Christen14 Dec 1614, Cadney, Lincolnshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT FatherThomas HAZEN (HASSEN) (1580-1628)
2 CONT
2 CONT MotherElizabeth (ca1585-<1647)
2 CONT
2 CONT Misc. Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT He came to New England and settled at Rowley, Mass. where the first record of him was on 10 Jan 1643/4 when, in a survey of the town, he is shown as having 4-1/2 acres of upland laid out at the Great Plain. He does not appear on t
2 CONC he list of those who had house lots at that time. A little later he was shown as having 3 acres laid out for him in Crane Meadow.166
2 CONT
2 CONT He was on the lists in 1650 as having two oxen and one calf. He had some small land parcels and by Feb. 1661 had acquired 7 and 1/4 gates, or cattle rights, in the town commons, a good number for those times. He received a share i
2 CONC n the Hog Island marshes when they were divided and laid out in 1667, also other land parcels from time to time.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Jan 3 1650/1, at a town meeting, he was chosen one of the four overseers whose duties were to oversee the execution of town orders and highways. He was chosen again in five later years and was a selectman in 1669, and served on
2 CONC  jury and other court duties.
2 CONT
2 CONT An inventory of his estate taken 24 Sep 1683 totalled L374, 4s, 3d. It included his home lot, house, buildings, and land, 15 other parcels of land of from 1 to 60 acres totally 127 acres, 2 pair oxen, 3 horses, cattle, pigs and sh
2 CONC eep, and the usual tools, household items, etc. There were no special tradesman’s tools which indicated that he was a farmer.148 167 168 88 169 11, No. 74, pg. 120, 1920
2 CONT
2 CONT “The origin of this family beyond the sea has not been traced. Recent information locates a family bearing the name in Newcastle-on-Tyne, in the last century, and possibly the fact may afford a clue to the English home of the Puri
2 CONC tan Edward.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Edward Hazen was the immigrant ancestor. The first mention of the name, which has been found, occurs in the records of Rowley. “Elizabeth, wife of Edward Hassen, was buried 1649 Sept. 18.” He was a man of substance and influenc
2 CONC e in the town; was overseer or Selectman in 1650, 1651, 1654, 1660, 1661, 1665 and 1668, and judge of delinquents 1666. In the records of surveys, Feb. 4, 1661, he appears entitled to “seven gates.” These related to cattle rights 
2 CONC on the town commons; the average number being three, and no one had more than seven. The inventory of his estate amounted to L404:7:8.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his first wife, Elizabeth, nothing is known; or where or how long they had been married.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married (2) in March, 1650, Hannah, dau. of Thomas and Hannah Grant. He was buried in Rowley, July 22, 1683. His widow m. (2) March 17, 1683-4, George Browne of Haverhill, who Sept. 9, 1693, adopted her youngest son Richard as 
2 CONC the sole heir to his large estate. Lieut. Browne d. Oct. 31, 1699, aged 76; his wife d. Feb. 1715-16.”165
2 CONT
2 CONT (I) Edward Hazen, the immigrant ancestor, was born in England. The origin of the name is not known, but a family of the name was located at Newcastle-on-Tyne in the last century. He settled in Rowley, Massachusetts, before 1649, f
2 CONC or his wife Elizabeth was buried there September 18, 1649. He was a man of substance and influence in the town; was overseer of the poor, or selectman, in 1650-51-54-60-61-65-68, and a magistrate in 1666. In the records of surveys
2 CONC , February 4, 1661, he appears entitled to “Seven Gates.” These related t cattle rights on the town commons, the average number being three, and no one having more than seven. The inventory of his estate amounted to over four hund
2 CONC red pounds, a large sum for that period. He married (second), in March, 1650, Hannah, daughter of Thomas and Hannah Grant. He was buried in Rowley, July 22, 1683. his widow married (second) March 17, 1684, Lieutenant George Browne
2 CONC , of Haverhill, who adopted her youngest son Richard as sole heir to his large estate, September 9, 1693. Lieutenant Browne died October 31, 1699, aged seventy-six years, his wife February, 1715-5. Children of Edward and Hannah (G
2 CONC rant) Hazen, all born in Rowley: Elizabeth, March 8, 1651; Hannah, September, 1653; John, September 22, 1655; Thomas, Edward, September 10, 1660; Isabel, July 21, 1662; Priscilla, November 25, 1664; Edna, June 20, 1667; Richard, A
2 CONC ugust 6, 1669; Hepzibah, December 22, 1671, died November 29, 1689; Sarah, August 22, 1673.
2 CONT
2 CONT 81, “The Hazen Line”, pgs. 595-960,
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses
2 CONT
2 CONT 1Elizabeth
2 CONT
2 CONT DeathSep 1649
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial18 Sep 1649
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage1649, Rowley, essex, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT 2Hannah GRANT22,148, 9G Grandmother
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthbef 16 Oct 1631, Cottingham, Yorkshire, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Death15 Feb 1715, Haverhill, Essex, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT Christen16 Oct 1631, Cottingham, Yorkshire, ENGLAND
2 CONT
2 CONT FatherThomas GRANT (<1601-<1643)
2 CONT
2 CONT MotherJane HABURNE (<1602-1696)
2 CONT
2 CONT Misc. Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to America in 1638
2 CONT
2 CONT MarriageMar 1650, Rowley, Essex, Massachusetts170,171
2 CONT
2 CONT ChildrenElizabeth (1651-)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Hannah (1653-<1683)
2 CONT
2 CONT  John (1655-<1683)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Thomas (1657-1735)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Edward (1660-1748)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Isabella (Isabel) (1662-1735)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Priscella (1664-1752)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Edna (1667->1727)
2 CONT
