1 TITL Governor
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Edward Cranfield''' (flourished 1680–1696) was an English colonial administrator. ''(Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0)''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * This Edward Cranfield is presumed to have been born in Barbados between 1633 when his father Edward Cranfield arrived and 1649 when his father died. His mother was Elizabeth Parker, daughter of William Parker, 13th Lord Morley a
2 CONC nd 4th Baron Monteagle. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * His father's will though does not mention a son Edward Cranfield, although it does list an eldest son Francis Cranfield and a younger son William Cranfield. HOWEVER, the 1656 will of Francis Cranfield [as he headed off to Barbad
2 CONC os] DOES mention a brother Edward Cranfield. Possibly, their mother Elizabeth was pregnant with Edward when his father died. His father's will also mentions his wife Elizabeth, his mother Mary Peasley of London als Cranfield & Unc
2 CONC le William Goodman of Compton in the Hole, Warwickshire
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2536.   Warrant signed by James, Earl of Suffolk (deputy to Henry, Earl of Norwich, Earl Marshal) directed to the Kings and Officers of Arms, to grant arms to Edward Cranfeld, esq., gentleman usher to the King, who has performed
2 CONC  good services to the King in the English plantations in the West Indies. 1 doc., 27 Jan. 1674/5. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Edward Cranfield (fl. 1680-1696) was an English colonial administrator. His mother Elizabeth was a daughter of William Parker, 4th Baron Monteagle.
2 CONT
2 CONT * He was governor of the Province of New Hampshire from 1682 to 1685, in an administration that was marked by hostility between Cranfield and the colonists. He left New Hampshire in 1685 for Barbados, where he was appointed commis
2 CONC sioner of customs, where he introduced a 4.5% tax on sugar exports, and sat on the council in the 1690s.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sir Robert Southwell wrote in 1686 that Edward Cranfield told him "his grandfather was the first that discovered the gunpowder treason," and, as this was Lord Morley & Monteagle, father of Elizabeth Parker, wife of Cranfield, th
2 CONC is line seems proved.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Jeremy Belknap wrote 'The History of New Hampshire' in the 1700s, and gives some further information on Edward Cranfield in a footnote (2nd Edition, pub. 1813, Vol. II, p. 369):
2 CONT The information includes the intriguing facts that Cranfield was "of the family of Lord Monteagle", that he went to Barbados after New Hampshire, that "he died about the beginning of the present century", that "he was buried in th
2 CONC e Cathedral Church, at Bath, in England", and, most interesting, is that he left descendants, including at least one in Jamaica, Belknap's informant.
2 CONT
2 CONT * An Edward Cranfield Esq became a Bristol burgess on 21st June 1681 by vote of council.
2 CONT
2 CONT *An Edward Cranfield, Gent died on 5 November 1700 and was buried at Bath Abbey on the 8th November 1700 plot A. 539.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT * "Edward Cranfield." ''Wikipedia'', [https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Edward_Cranfield&oldid=1145167717 revision] of 17 March 2023. < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Cranfield link] > Accessed 20 July 2024.
2 CONT
