1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Family and Education==
2 CONT b. c.1633, 1st s. of Sir John Bampfield, 1st Bt., of Poltimore by Gertrude, da. and coh. of Amyas Coplestone of Warley. educ. Corpus, Oxf. 1651. m. (1) 16 Nov. 1655, Margaret, da. of John Bulkeley of Nether Burgate, Hants, 2s. d.v
2 CONC .p. 1da.; (2) lic. 21 Oct. 1674, Jane, da. of Sir Courtenay Pole, 2nd Bt., of Shute, Devon, s.p. suc. fa. as 2nd Bt. and in Warley estate 24 Apr. 1650, gdfa. in Poltimore estate c.1650.1
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Offices Held==
2 CONT J.p. Devon 1656-July 1688, Oct. 1688-?d., Tiverton 1680-1, South Molton 1684-Oct. 1688; commr. for assessment, Devon 1657, Jan. 1660-80, 1689-90, militia Mar. 1660, col. of militia ft. Apr. 1660-c.1685, oyer and terminer, Western 
2 CONC circuit July 1660; sheriff, Devon Nov. 1660-1, dep. lt. 1661-c.1687, commr. for corporations 1662-3, loyal and indigent officers 1662, recusants 1675, inquiry into customs frauds, Lyme Regis 1678; freeman, Plymouth 1684; j.p. and 
2 CONC alderman, Tiverton 1684-7.2
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT Bampfylde’s ancestors acquired Poltimore in the reign of Edward I, and one of them sat for Devon in 1429. His father, a timorous Parliamentarian, abstained from the House after Pride’s Purge, and two of his uncles were nonconformi
2 CONC sts. But with regard to Bampfylde (as he chose to spell his name) we are assured that ‘as he always lived in the profession, so he died in the communion of the Church of England’. He took a prominent part in the Devon petition for
2 CONC  a free Parliament in February 1660, and to avoid arrest went into hiding in the home of John Drake. On the return of the secluded Members, Bampfylde and Sir William Courtenay
2 CONT
2 CONT raised each a gallant troop of about 120 gentlemen (most of them persons of quality and estate), in the head of which they rode themselves, securing some and disarming others. They brought all the disaffected in those parts into a
2 CONC  due subjection to the Government in a little time. He had earned nomination as knight of the Royal Oak, for which his estate was reckoned at £1,900 p.a. As sheriff at the general election of 1661 he appeared with so formidable a 
2 CONC troop of retainers that an order was made limiting future sheriffs to 40 followers. He told the electors
2 CONT
2 CONT I hope you all desire as well as myself to be represented in Parliament as loyal subjects and good Christians. It concerns us all therefore to choose such as are and always have been such. He seems to have had an efficient publici
2 CONC ty organization at his command, for his speech was printed in Mercurius Publicus. In the following year, hearing reports of an intended rising at Exeter, he swooped down on the city from his seat at Poltimore, ransacked the guildh
2 CONC all for arms and garrisoned the castle, again to the applause of royalist newsmen.3
2 CONT
2 CONT On the withdrawal of the original court candidate in 1671, Bampfylde stood successfully for the county in the Church interest. He was moderately active in the Cavalier Parliament, serving on 30 committees, the most important being
2 CONC  on the corruption of Members and the prevention of the growth of Popery. He was teller with his county colleague, (Sir) John Rolle, against the bill for the trial of peers on 23 Feb. 1674, but he made no speeches. When present, h
2 CONC e usually voted with the Court; in 1675 his father-in-law Pole promised to make sure of him, and Edward Seymour was also to be asked to influence him. An opposition satirist described him as much addicted to tippling, and alleged 
2 CONC that he had been presented to the King by his pretended wife, Betty Roberts, in Pall Mall; while Shaftesbury marked him as ‘thrice vile’.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Bampfylde, a safe Tory by 1678, found the atmosphere of the Popish Plot so oppressive that he was ordered to be sent for as a defaulter. He stood again for the county in the ensuing general election, but was sacrificed by his ‘tri
2 CONC mming’ colleague Seymour in an electoral bargain with Courtenay. He helped to present the loyal address from Devon in 1681, accepting the King’s reasons for dissolving Parliament, and he was ordered to examine Courtenay in 1683 ab
2 CONC out his knowledge of the Rye House Plot. He regained the county seat in 1685, and was listed by Danby among the Opposition in James II’s Parliament. He was appointed only to one private bill committee. His replies to the questions
2 CONC  on the repeal of the Test Act and Penal Laws followed the standard negative set by Sir Edward Seymour; and shortly afterwards ‘he, with a great many other loyal gentlemen, had the honour of being turned out’ of the lieutenancy an
2 CONC d the commission of the peace.5
2 CONT
2 CONT Bampfylde excused himself from attending William of Orange at Exeter in 1688 on the grounds of ill-health, but he sent his son to
2 CONT
2 CONT congratulate his arrival, as one come to preserve our laws and religion, and maintain the established government. But when at length he apprehended that matters were carried beyond all imaginations, fearing a change would be made 
2 CONC in the fundamental constitution of the government, he so far declared against these proceedings as to refuse payment of any new-made rates and taxes, and the collectors were enforced to levy them by distress against his goods. Nev
2 CONC ertheless he seems to have remained a j.p. He died of gout on 9 Feb. 1692, and was buried at Poltimore. On his deathbed he enjoined his family ‘that they should always continue faithful to the religion of the Established Church of
2 CONC  England, and be sure to pay their allegiance to the right heirs of the Crown’. Well over six feet tall, strongly built and handsome, with ‘a ready wit and a good judgment’, he had lived for many years beyond his income,
2 CONT
2 CONT always keeping about him a great retinue and a noble equipage. He was further (what is the badge of a true gentleman) of a courteous, obliging carriage to all, but very condescending to his inferiors, willing to befriend and grati
2 CONC fy them, if in any thing they applied themselves unto him. ... In a word, he was every way a gallant gentleman, and was the honour of his time and our country. His grandson, the 3rd baronet, was a Jacobite who sat for Exeter and D
2 CONC evon from 1710 till his death in 1727.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1660-1690
2 CONT
2 CONT Author: John. P. Ferris
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT This biography is based on J. Prince, Worthies of Devon, (published 1701).
2 CONT * 1. Vivian, Vis. Devon, 40; Soc. of Genealogists, Fordingbridge par. reg.
2 CONT * 2. SP44/335/234, 373, Parl. Intell. 16 Apr. 1660; HMC 14th Rep. IX, 274; A. Jenkins, Hist. ExeterCal. Treas. Bks. v. 1109; HMC 4th Rep. 281; SP29/62/1; PC2/72/535.
2 CONT * 3. Keeler, Long Parl. 95; D. Underdown, Pride’s Purge, 82, 367; Clar. SP, iii. 682; Devon and Cornw. N. and Q. xviii. 316; HMC Popham, 193; CSP Dom. 1661-2, p. 538; Merc. Pub. 25 Apr. 1661.
2 CONT * 4. Trans. Devon Assoc. lxxvi. 26.
2 CONT * 5. HMC 15th Rep. VII, 106; Luttrell, i. 222; CSP Dom. Jan.-June 1683, pp. 353, 377
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/member/bampfylde-sir-coplestone-1633-92
2 CONT ______________
2 CONT '''Sir Coplestone Bampfylde, 2nd Bt., DL, JP (ca. 1633 – 9 February 1692) of Poltimore and North Molton and Warleigh, Tamerton Foliot, in Devon, was an English politician who sat in the House of Commons at various times between 16
2 CONC 59 and 1689.'''
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT '''Bampfylde was the eldest son''' of Sir John Bampfylde, 1st Baronet (1590–1650), of Poltimore and North Molton, by his wife, Gertrude Coplestone, 4th daughter of Amyas Coplestone[1] and co-heiress of her brother John Coplestone[
2 CONC 2] of Copplestone in the parish of Colebrooke and of Warleigh in the parish of Tamerton Foliot, Devon. '''His brother-in-law''' was Sir William Morice, 1st Baronet, husband of '''his sister''' Gertrude Bampfylde.[3]
2 CONT Career
2 CONT '''He matriculated at Corpus Christi College, Oxford on 20 March 1651,[4] where he befriended Sir John Drake, 1st Baronet.'''[5]
2 CONT '''He succeeded to the baronetcy in 1651 on the death of his father. He was nominated Justice of the Peace for Devon in 1656 and one year later became a Commissioner for Assessment.'''[6]
2 CONT '''In 1659, Bampfylde was elected Member of Parliament for Tiverton, Devon, in the Third Protectorate Parliament.[6] Although his father and two of his uncles were considered Roundheads (Parliamentarians), Bampfylde himself was a 
2 CONC very active Royalist.'''[5]
2 CONT '''In February 1660, he delivered a petition from Devon's population for more rights to the king's general George Monck, on the discovery of which by Parliament he was temporarily imprisoned in the Tower of London.'''[1]
2 CONT '''In 1660, he was appointed a Commissioner of Militia, serving subsequently as colonel of the Devon Militia. He became the first High Sheriff of Devon after the Restoration of the Monarchy and toured the Western Circuit as a Comm
2 CONC issioner of Oyer and Terminer. He was a Deputy Lieutenant of Devon from 1661 and worked as Commissioner for Corporations in the following two years.'''[6]
2 CONT '''In 1671, Bampfylde was elected MP for Devon in 1671 in a by-election to the Cavalier Parliament which seat he held until 1679.[6] He was reasonably diligent as an MP, until the outbreak of the Popish Plot in the autumn of 1678,
2 CONC  when the hysterical political atmosphere caused him to retire to his home. He generally voted with the Court party, especially after his second marriage, when he was much influenced by his father-in-law, Sir Courtenay Pole, who e
2 CONC njoyed the personal regard of Charles II. He was again elected MP for Devon in 1685 and held the seat until 1689. He initially welcomed the Glorious Revolution, but in his last years became a stern critic of the new regime.'''[6]
2 CONT Greets Grand Duke of Tuscany
2 CONT '''Sir Coplestone Bampfylde is mentioned in the Travel Journal of Cosimo III de' Medici, Grand Duke of Tuscany (1642–1723) in connection with his visit to Plymouth on 5 April 1669''':[7]
2 CONT *"The governor then came to take leave, and afterwards Sir Richard Edgecumbe and Mr. Prideaux came in, to wish his highness a good journey. About three they dined, and towards five, took their departure; his Highness being attende
2 CONC d by the governor on horseback, who, when they had got two miles from Plymouth, appeared at the coach-door, to take leave once more. He had wished to have paraded the military, as was done on his highness's arrival, but the latter
2 CONC  courteously declined it. When they had proceeded about a mile after the governor's departure, there came galloping up to the coach, '''Sir Copleston Bampfylde, with his wife and sister. They happened to be hunting in that neighbo
2 CONC urhood and wished not to lose the opportunity of performing an act of respect to his highness'''. The serene prince stopped the carriage, and received their compliments, but did not alight to salute them, not knowing, till afterwa
2 CONC rds, who the ladies were".[citation needed]
2 CONT Marriage and progeny
2 CONT '''He married twice''':
2 CONT '''Firstly on 16 November 1655 to Margaret Bulkeley''', daughter of Francis Bulkeley of Burgate, Hampshire,[8] '''by whom he had two sons and a daughter''':[3]
2 CONT *Col. Hugh Bampfield (d. 1690), eldest son and heir apparent, who predeceased his father having died in a fall from his horse.[8] He married Mary Clifford, daughter of James Clifford of Ware, by whom he had a son Sir Coplestone Ba
2 CONC mpfylde, 3rd Baronet.
2 CONT *Coplestone Bampfylde (1659–1669), 2nd son, a precocious scholar who died young aged 10 and whose monument survives on the south side of the chancel in St Mary's Church, Tamerton Foliot. His effigy, dressed like an adult man,[9] i
2 CONC s shown seated at a desk with hand on a book and wears a gown and band with a large bushy wig.[10] Below are elaborate inscriptions in Latin and Greek.
2 CONT *John Coplestone Bulkeley Bampfield, 3rd son, who died without issue.
2 CONT *Margaret Bampfield, died an infant.
2 CONT '''Secondly at Houghton, South Devon, on 21 October 1674[3] to Jane Pole''', daughter of Sir Courtenay Pole, 2nd Baronet of Shute, Devon and his wife Urith Shapcote; '''without progeny. She remarried''' Edward Gibbons.[3]
2 CONT Character
2 CONT '''He was tall, strongly built and handsome, with "ready wit and good judgment"; in manner "a true gentleman, courteous and obliging". His luxurious way of life caused him to live beyond his income for many years.'''
2 CONT Death and burial
2 CONT '''Bampfyle died of gout at Warleigh and was buried at Poltimore.[6] On his deathbed, he required his assembled family to pledge loyalty to the Church of England and to the crown.'''[5]
2 CONT Succession
2 CONT '''His eldest son''' Hugh Bampfield having '''predeceased him by one year, he was succeeded in the baronetcy by his grandson''' Sir Coplestone Bampfylde, 3rd Baronet.[11]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Coplestone_Bampfylde,_2nd_Baronet
2 CONT _________
2 CONT '''Sir Coplestone Bamfylde, 2nd Bt.'''1 
2 CONT M, #130330, '''b. 1638, d. 9 February 1691/92'''
2 CONT Last Edited=27 Jun 2013
2 CONT '''     Sir Coplestone Bamfylde, 2nd Bt. was born in 1638.1 He was the son of''' Sir John Bamfylde, 1st Bt. and Gertrude Coplestone.1 '''He married, firstly, Margaret Bulkeley''', daughter of Francis Bulkeley.2 '''He married, seco
2 CONC ndly, Jane Pole''', daughter of Sir Courtenay Pole, 2nd Bt. and Ureth Shapcote, '''circa 21 October 1674 at Houghton, Devon, EnglandG.1,3 He died on 9 February 1691/92 at Warleigh, Devon, EnglandG.1 He was buried at Poltimore, Dev
2 CONC on, EnglandG.1 His will was proven (by probate) on 2 May 1692.'''1
2 CONT '''     He succeeded as the 2nd Baronet Bamfylde, of Poltimore, co. Devon [E., 1641] in April 1650.1 He matriculated at Corpus Christi College, Oxford University, Oxford, Oxfordshire, EnglandG, on 20 March 1650/51.'''1
2 CONT '''     He was an active Royalist, and presented to General Monk a 'petition of right' from Devon, for which he was imprisoned.1 He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Tiverton in 1659.1 He held the office of Sherif
2 CONC f of Devon from 1660 to 1661.1 He gained the rank of Colonel in the Devon Militia.1 He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Devon between 1671 and 1679.1 He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Devon be
2 CONC tween 1685 and 1687.1 His last will was dated 24 August 1691.'''
2 CONT '''Child of Sir Coplestone Bamfylde, 2nd Bt. and Margaret Bulkeley'''
2 CONT 1. Colonel Hugh Bamfylde+4 b. c 1663, d. 16 Jun 1691
