1 NOTE From Ancestral File (TM), data as of 2 January 1996.
1 NOTE WILL: William Cox's will was written 24 Feb 1711 and probated 02 Aug 1712, [Record 1710-1714, part 1, p,131 Henrico County, Virginia]
2 CONT
2 CONT COMMENT: "Hopkins of Virginia" states that the will of William Cox was written 24 Feb 1711. Lillian Etter states it was the 25 Feb 1711.
2 CONT
2 CONT RESEARCHER: Lillian Etter and Marcia Kay (Pinkston) Bignall. They obtained their information from Hopkins of Virginia.
1 NOTE Note by Lou Ann Murphy:
2 CONT WILLIAM COX  was born Est bef 1685.  William died bef 1717? in Henrico Co. VA.  He apparently died bef Sarah Cocke's father died according to one source.  Need to look into this a little further. 
2 CONT 	He married SARAH COCKE in Henrico Co. VA?, ca 1710.  Sarah was born in Henrico Co. VA aft 1695.  She was the daughter of William COCKE and Sarah PERRIN.  She married THOMAS JORDAN  in Henrico Co. VA, bef 1750.  Sarah died ca 1750
2 CONC  in Henrico Co. VA.  Per the court record of 1754.  The William & Mary Quarterly misquoted the date of the court document which they cited as 1734, s/b 1754.  Sarah had died about four years previous to the case. The document file
2 CONC d in Henrico in 1754 confirms the Wm Cox marriage as a first marriage.  Note: It must be noted that the 1734 date often given is in error and should be 1754. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Mary College Quarterly Historical Magazine, Vol. 27, No. 2. (Oct., 1918), pp. 132-143.
2 CONT p.140-141
2 CONT The Children of William Cocke of Henrico.---The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Vol., page 90, in a genealogy of the Cocke Family, by James C. Southall, says that "William Cocke (son of Thomas2; and grandson of Richard
2 CONC 1, of Bremo, first of the family in Virginia)... had issue: William, Temperance, Catharine, Mary and Sarah."  The followiing record in Goochland County Court May 1734 (Order Book 7, page 402) shows who these children married.  Jor
2 CONC dan vs Cox. William Cocke, his life time was possessed of 4 negros as his own, one of which was a slave named in the declaration in this suit; William Cocke died sometime about the year 1736 leaving issue 2 children, infants, said
2 CONC  infants both dying not more than 8 years old; at death of said infants  said negros descended to Sarah, wife of Thomas Jordan, Temperance, wife of Abraham Bailey, Mary, wife of John Redford, sisters and co-heiresses of said Willi
2 CONC am Cocke, and John Burton, Jr., son and heir of Catherine, then deceased, late wife of John Burton, said Catherine being a sister of said William Cocke.  Sarah, wife of the plaintiff (i. e. Thomas Jordan) in this suit, was at the 
2 CONC time of her intermarriage with the said plaintiff, the widow and relict of one William Cox, and that said William Cox died  before the death of the infants of aforesaid William Cocke. The defendant n this suit, John Cox, is son an
2 CONC d heir at law to said William Cox by the said Sarah; the said Thomas Jordan, the plaintiff, and said Abraham Bailey, John Redford and John Burton, jr., on the death of the infants of said William Cocke, made a division of the said
2 CONC  slaves into four lots and cast lots for choice and the negro Sam (mentioned in the declaration) fell to Sarah, wife of Said Thomas Jordan, and said Jordan became possessed of said Sam.  About 4 years since Sarah, wife of the said
2 CONC  Thomas Jordan departed this life and the defendant, John Cox, thinking that he had a right to the aforesaid negro applied to the said Jordan, and Jordan being advised that the right rested in the said John Cox turned over the sai
2 CONC d negro to him and said Sam is now in his possession.  There is more of the evidence in the foregoing suit; but, the facts culled and given, as above, are the vital ones from a genealogical point of view as showing the names of th
2 CONC e parties whom the daughters of William Cocke married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Article provided by Mike & Bushy Hartman
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT "Some researchers think the son of William and Sarah is the John known as John of Bluestone.  This researcher disagrees.  The evidence is overwhelming that John who married Elce Hix is the correct son.  Land records and family ass
2 CONC ociations bear testimony to this relationship.  There are close ties to Cockes (and neighboring Curds with their allied lines), Baughs, neighboring Davises, Hatchers, etc. in all of the relationships and dealings of John and Elce 
2 CONC Hix and their children."
2 CONT Note by Lou Ann Murphy
1 NOTE Listed with 300 acres on the 1704 Quit Rent roll of Henrico Co., VA. This was probably the land he had been given by his father, John COX, on Feb 5, 1685/6.  the land was adjacent Thomas TAYLOR and included a boat landing on the J
2 CONC ames River up towards Curles.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM2 COX (John1) was born in Henrico Co. VA bef Feb 63/64.  William died Feb 24, 1711/12 in Henrico Co. VA (Will dated Feb. 24, 1711, probated Aug. 2, 1712 (Record 1710-1714, part 1, p. 131, Henrico Co.  Wit: Robert Burton, Jr
2 CONC . and William Cox.  William and Mary College Quarterly, 2nd series, Vol. 6, p. 133:  Dr. Philip T. Southall, b. 1791, d. 1857, left a paper in his own handwriting stating that the data was taken from the journal of his grandfather
2 CONC , Valentine Wood who was a son of Henry Wood and Martha Cox).  Will Feb 10, 1711; probated June 1712.  Wit by Robert Burton Jr. and William Cox. Henrico Co. VA Will book, 4, pg 131. (Note by Lou Ann Murphy)
2 CONT
2 CONT Henrico Wills & Deeds 1710-1714
2 CONT p.87 & p. 131
2 CONT Will of William Cox of Henrico Co., planter; Made 20 Feb 1711; To son Stephen, the plantation I live on, a negro man Dick, and furniture; To dau. Martha Cox, items; To dau. Mary Cox, items; To dau. Prudence Cox, 150 acres of land 
2 CONC in the woods four years from this date, and furniture; To dau. Judith Cox, a colt; To dau. Ede Cox, items; To dau. Elizabeth Jamson, an ell of Hollan; To wife Sarah, negro men Will and Peter, and all the rest to be in hands of wif
2 CONC e until children are of age or marry, and she is to be executrix; Wit: Robert Burton Jr., William Cox; Rec. 2 June 1712.
