1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Do not confuse with his uncle, https://www.geni.com/people/Richard-Cocke/6000000000613953960
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/K6Q7-MG8
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Richard Cocke (d 1706) and Elizabeth are:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Richard Cocke III, born 1672 in "Bremo", Henrico Co., VA; died Bef. October 1720 in "Bremo", Henrico Co., VA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married 
2 CONT * (1) Rebecca Unknown. * (2) Ann Bowler Abt. 1695. *(3) unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT It would appear that Richard also married Anne Bowler or Boulware. A great number of records for this county were destroyed during the Civil War, however, a gravestone gives evidence of the marriage. Anne was the daughter of Thoma
2 CONC s Boulware and wife Tabitha Underwood. She was born in 1675 and died in 1705, and was buried at Malvern Hill. Her gravestone reads: "Here Lyeth Inter'd the body of Anne, the wife of Richard Cocke the younger of Bremo in this count
2 CONC y and daughter of Thomas Bowler late of the county of Rappahannock Esq. She was borne the 23d day of Jan., 1675 and departed this life the 24 day April 1705 aged 30 years, 3 months, 1 day"
2 CONT
2 CONT >comment from https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=60500229
2 CONT >>"Some people have mistakenly placed Ann Bowler as another wife of THIS Richard Cocke The Younger. Ann did marry a man named Richard Cocke. But he was the son of Richard "The Elder" (Richard "The Younger's half brother.) In other
2 CONC  words, Ann Bowler married Richard the Younger's 1/2 nephew. Confusing the matter is that Ann's tombstone states that her husband was "Richard Cocke the Younger." This is in reference to her husband's father being named Richard al
2 CONC so. But it is easy to see how it could be confused with Elizabeth's husband."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=60507200
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime after his first wife Ann died in 1705, he married again. The name of his second wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Richard Cocke, of "Bremo," was born in 1672, and died in 1720." - Earl Gregg Swem, The William and Mary Quarterly, page 152, 1932.
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 1714 (Dr. William Cocke was at this time secretary of the colony ) Richard Cocke bought of Lt.-Governor Spotswood, as appears by a deed recorded at Williamsburg, for the sum £12 10s. (about $350 at present), 2,447 acres of lan
2 CONC d on the north side of James river, in what is now Goochland county, adjoining the lands of Nicholas Meriwether and Joseph Lewis. A part of this tract, 1,100 acres, was bought in 1770 by the Rev . Wm. Douglas, the teacher of Mr. J
2 CONC efferson, and given to his grandson, Thomas Meriwether." - The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Vol. 4; "The Cocke Family, Third Generation Continued" page 91.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We have not the will of Richard Cocke, but there is a memorandum in the Order-Book of Henrico county that it was proven October, 1720, with Ebenezer Adams, Nathaniel Harrison, and Henry Harrison as executors. Nathaniel Harrison w
2 CONC as son of Benjamin Harrison, of Surry county." - The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Vol. 4; "The Cocke Family, Third Generation Continued" page 91.
2 CONT
2 CONT The above proves that the Will of Richard Cocke that was brought to Court in October 1720, was this Richard Cocke, the husband of Ann (Bowler) Cocke, as Ebenezer Adams was his son-in-law. Ebenezer having married his daughter Tabit
2 CONC ha about two years before he died in 1718.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is where this information can be found. Note it is from an 1895 issue of The William and Mary College Quarterly. http://files.usgwarchives.net/va/schools/wmmary/quarterly/v03n3/pg202-209.txt
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://books.google.com/books?id=uMc9AQAAMAAJ&lpg=PA323&ots=YU5TcLvrv6&dq=tabitha%20bowler%20Cocke%20children&pg=PA323#v=onepage&q&f=false page 323] of The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Volume 4 edited by Phil
2 CONC ip Alexander Bruce, William Glover Stanard "V. DESCENDANTS OF RICHARD COCKE,a SON OF RICHARD“, (FOURTH GENERATION)."
2 CONT
2 CONT >Richard Cocke3 left three sons and four daughters, to-wit: Bowler,‘ Richard,‘ Benjamin,‘ Martha‘ (married Thomas Adams, who became quite prominent afterwards); a daughter who married \Villiam Acrill, of Charles City, member House
2 CONC  of Burgesses 1736; Mary, who married Eppes, ancestor of Senator John W. Eppes; and a daughter named Tabitha. We notice them in order:  ....
2 CONT
2 CONT *1. Bowler Cocke,‘ born 1696, died 1771, at “Shirley,” in Charles City. He married twice: 1. Sarah ; 2. Mrs. Elizabeth Carter, widow of Colonel John Carter, of “ Co'rotoman,” and daughter of Colonel Edward Hill, of “Shirley.”
2 CONT * 2. RICHARD COCKE,‘ son Of Richard,“ born c. 1706 (by second wife), died 1772; married Elizabeth Hartwell, daughter of John Hartwell, of Swan‘s Point, Surry county (opposite James City), and great-niece of Hon. Henry Hartwell, Cl
2 CONC erk of Council in I675—95.
2 CONT * 3. BENJAMIN COCRE,‘ son of Richard.” Born c. 1710, died 1763. He married Catharine Allen, daughter of Arthur Allen, of Surry county.* She was a' daughter of Mrs. Elizabeth Stith, who married three times: 1. Arthur Allen, of Surr
2 CONC y; 2. Arthur Smith, Jr., of Isle of Wight; 3. — Stith. (See William c? Illary Quarterly, Oct., 1896, p. 113.) Her maiden name was Elizabeth Bray, Sister of Thomas and James Bray.
2 CONT * 4. MARTHA COCKE,‘ daughter of Richard Cocke.“ She married Thomas Adams
2 CONT *5. MARY COCKE,‘ daughter of Richard,I married—Eppes c. 1730.
2 CONT * 6. ANNE COCKE,‘ daughter of Richard,‘ married William Acrill, of Charles City county, member House of Burgesses 1736. He died in 1737, and Richard Cocke‘ and Benjamin Cocke‘ were his executors. She died about 1755. They had issu
2 CONC e (amongst others) Susanna, Rebecca, and Hannah.
2 CONT * 7. TABITHA COCKE,* daughter of Richard.5 We know nothing of her.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
