2 CONT Lady Diana Bruce (d. 15 July 1672), married firstly, Sir Seymour Shirley, 5th Baronet, on 29 January 1666;[6] married secondly, on 10 November 1671, John Manners, Lord Roos, who became Duke of Rutland after her death.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''Lady Diana Bruce1 
2 CONT *''''''F, #11777, d. 15 July 1672
2 CONT *Last Edited=5 Apr 2011
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.0%
2 CONT *''''''     Lady Diana Bruce was the daughter of Robert Bruce, 2nd Earl of Elgin and Lady Diana Grey.1 She married, firstly, Sir Seymour Shirley, 5th Bt., son of Sir Robert Shirley, 4th Bt. and Catherine Okeover, on 29 January 166
2 CONC 6.2 She married, secondly, John Manners, 1st Duke of Rutland, son of John Manners, 8th Earl of Rutland and Hon. Frances Montagu, on 20 November 1671. She died on 15 July 1672.2
2 CONT *''''''      From 29 January 1666, her married name became Shirley. Her married name became Manners.
2 CONT *'''''' Child of Lady Diana Bruce and Sir Seymour Shirley, 5th Bt.
2 CONT ** 1.Sir Robert Shirley, 6th Bt.3 b. Jan 1667/68, d. c 8 Mar 1708/9
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 1, page 1295. Hereinafter cited as Burke's 
2 CONC Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT * 2.[S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition, volume 1, page 1416.
2 CONT * 3.[S37] Charles Mosley, Burke's Peerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *http://www.thepeerage.com/p1178.htm#i11777
2 CONT *________________
2 CONT *''''''John Manners, 1st Duke of Rutland and 9th Earl of Rutland (Boughton, 29 May 1638 – 10 January 1711, Belvoir Castle) was the son of John Manners, 8th Earl of Rutland and Frances Montagu. His maternal grandparents were Sir Ed
2 CONC ward Montagu, 1st Baron Montagu of Boughton and his wife Elizabeth Jeffries. He was styled Lord Roos from 1641 until 1679.
2 CONT *He married, firstly, Lady Anne Pierrepont, daughter of Henry Pierrepont, 1st Marquess of Dorchester, on 15 July 1658. The failure of their marriage attacted considerable attention, as divorce was not generally available at the ti
2 CONC me. He obtained a "separation from bed and board" in 1663 on grounds of her adultery, and private Acts of Parliament in 1667 bastardizing her issue since 1659 and granting him permission to remarry in 1670. This process required c
2 CONC onsiderable expenditure and trouble.
2 CONT *''''''He married, secondly, Lady Diana Bruce, daughter of Robert Bruce, 2nd Earl of Elgin, on 10 November 1671. She died on 15 July 1672 in childbed.
2 CONT *He married, thirdly, Catherine Wriothesley Noel (d. 1733), daughter of Baptist Noel, 3rd Viscount Campden, on 8 January 1673 They had three children:
2 CONT ** John Manners, 2nd Duke of Rutland (1676–1721)
2 CONT ** Lady Catherine Manners (c. 1682–1722), married John Leveson-Gower, 1st Baron Gower and had issue.
2 CONT ** Lady Dorothy Manners (c. 1690–1734), married Baptist Noel, 3rd Earl of Gainsborough and had issue.
2 CONT *He served, rather passively, as Member of Parliament for Leicestershire from 1661 until 1679. Politically he was a Whig, but did not attend court after 1689, preferring the life of a country magnate.
2 CONT *Lord Roos succeeded his father as Lord Lieutenant of Leicestershire in 1677, and proved an effective deputy of the crown. His invitation to Lord Sherard to stand with him for Leicestershire instead of a gentry candidate upset the
2 CONC  Leicestershire gentry, and the Commons disallowed Roos' election. He was created Baron Manners of Haddon on 30 April 1679 and sent to the House of Lords instead, but succeeded as Earl of Rutland on 29 September 1679 at the death 
2 CONC of his father. He retained his lord lieutenancy in 1681, despite supporting the Exclusion Bill, but was turned out by James II in 1687. Reappointed in 1689 after the Glorious Revolution, he resigned in 1702, to protest Government 
2 CONC promotion of Tory interests in Leicestershire. He was briefly Custos Rotulorum of Leicestershire thereafter (22 August 1702 – 22 March 1703). On 29 March 1703, his long support of Whig government was rewarded by his creation as Du
2 CONC ke of Rutland and Marquess of Granby. Rutland was reappointed to the lord lieutenancy in 1706, which he retained until his death on 10 January 1711. 
2 CONT *See also
2 CONT *List of deserters from James II to William of Orange
2 CONT *References
2 CONT * Morrin, Jean (2004). "Manners, John, first duke of Rutland (1638–1711)". Oxford Dictionary of National Biography. Oxford University Press. Retrieved 2006-10-14.
2 CONT *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Manners,_1st_Duke_of_Rutland
2 CONT *__________
