1 NOTE {geni:about_me} When the 3rd Baronet of Llantarnam, Edward III, died without male heirs,  the title went to his uncle, Sir James Morgan (4th Baronet).
2 CONT * Ref: [http://wbo.llgc.org.uk/en/s-MORG-LLA-1561.html Welsh Biography Online]
2 CONT * Ref: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_Edward_Morgan,_1st_Baronet Wikipedia article on the Baronet's of Llantarnam]
2 CONT * Ref: [http://books.google.com/books?id=K1kBAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA370&lpg=PA370&dq=baronetLlantarnam&source=bl&ots=E8TtZ2olal&sig=sPTN7EzpqKq_KY8tYZSOL-hCsOA&hl=en&sa=X&ei=HYjNUuW9M-ir2gWbjoEo&ved=0CEEQ6AEwBAv=onepage&q=baronet%20llantar
2 CONC nam&f=false John Burke's genealogy of baronetcies]
2 CONT * [http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/member/morgan-sir-edward-1682 History of Parliament - MORGAN, Sir Edward, 3rd Bt. (d.1682), of Llantarnam Abbey, Mon.]
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com/books?id=Ey0UAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA41&lpg=PA41&dq=Margaret+Chidiocke+1503&source=bl&ots=1WIVR5DmXc&sig=6rYofhZCSWqYrLLiXumqml3t7zI&hl=en&ei=48CeTN_sHIn6sAP6uJzWAQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=7&ved=0CCUQ
2 CONC 6AEwBjgU#v=snippet&q=morgan&f=false The peerage of England. 3 vols. [in 4. Sig. N6,7 of vol. 1, 3B1 of vol. 2 ...  By Arthur Collins] Pg.328
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com/books?id=veg-AQAAMAAJ&pg=PR11&dq=A+History+of+Monmouthshire&hl=en&sa=X&ei=GNrUUsCOKYWIogTQv4LADw&ved=0CGEQ6AEwCTgU#v=onepage&q=Morgan&f=false The History of Monmouthshire  By David Williams] Pg.128
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com/books?id=K1kBAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA370&lpg=PA370&dq=baronetLlantarnam&source=bl&ots=E8TtZ2olal&sig=sPTN7EzpqKq_KY8tYZSOL-hCsOA&hl=en&sa=X&ei=HYjNUuW9M-ir2gWbjoEo&ved=0CEEQ6AEwBAv=onepage&q=baronet%20llantarnam&f
2 CONC =false#v=onepage&q&f=false A genealogical and heraldic history of the extinct and dormant baronetcies ...  By John Burke, John Bernard Burke (sir.)] Pg.370
2 CONT * http://www.thepeerage.com/p25689.htm#i256885
2 CONT * [https://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhera01inburk#page/92/mode/1up A genealogical and heraldic history of the landed gentry .... Vol. 1] Pg.92
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________
2 CONT *'''A genealogical and heraldic dictionary of the peerage and baronetage of the British Empire (1869)
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/genealogicalhera00inburk
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhera00inburk#page/1115/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.1115
2 CONT *   LLEWELLYN AP IVOR, lineally descended from Cadivor Vawr, Lord of Kilsaint, m. Angharad, dau. and co-heir fo Sir Morgan Meredith, Lord of Tredegar (descended from Rhys, Prince of South Wales), and thus acquired the Tredegar est
2 CONC ates. Llewellin was father of three sons, viz,
2 CONT **	MORGAN, his heir.
2 CONT **	Ivor, of Werncleppa, or Gwerney Cleppa, near Tredegar, ancestor of the MORGANS of Werncleppa, whose last male heir, Roger Morgan, Esq., d. in 1632, when his estate passed to the Tredegar family.
2 CONT **	Philip, whose son, DAVID AP PHILIP AP LLEWELLIN, seated at St. Pierrer, in the county of Monmouth, was ancestor of the family of LEWIS of St. Pierre. (See BURKE'S Landed Gentry.)
2 CONT *The eldest son,
2 CONT *   MORGAN AP LLEWELLIN, Lord of St. Clere, in Carmarthenshire, and of Tredegar, co. Monmouth, m. Maud, dau. of Rhys ap Gronwy, Lord of Kybor, and had two sons, 
2 CONT **	LLEWELLYN, his heir.
2 CONT **	Philip, who acquired the estate of Langston, co. Monmouth, in marriage with Gwenllian, 3rd dau. and co-heir of Sir John Norris, Knt. of Penllyne Castle, Glamorganshire, and was ancestor of the MORGANS of Llantarnam, (raised to 
2 CONC the degree of a Baronet, in 1642,) now represented by the family of BLEWITT,* and the MORGANS of Tredunnock, whose heir is MRS. JANE HAWKINS, of The Garn. (See BURKE'S Extinct Baronetage.)
2 CONT *The elder son,
2 CONT *   LLEWELLIN AP MORGAN, Lord of St. Clere and Tredegar, was grandfather of SIR JOHN MORGAN, Knt. of St. Clere and Tredegar, whose grandson, ....
2 CONT **	* FRANCES, dau. and co-heir of SIR EDWARD MORGAN, Bart. of Llantarnam, m. Edmund Bray, Esq., and was mother of MARY BRAY, who m. John Blewitt. Esq. of London, and was great- grandmother of REGINALD - JAMES BLEWITT, Esq. of Llan
2 CONC tarnam Abbey, late M.P. for the Monmouthshire boroughs. _____________________________________________
