1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Wikitree https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Alston-84
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alston, Gent., of Middlesex, was baptized 13 Aug 1637 at Odell, Bedfordshire, son of John Alston, Esq., by his wife Dorothy Temple.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Thomasine Brooks, daughter of Unknown Brooks, Esq.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT William was admitted to St. John's College, Cambridge in 1654.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Thomasin had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT William, baptized 1666, at Pavenham, Bedfordshire[2] (recorded as 21 October 1666 in Pavenham Parish Register).[3] John, baptised 25 February 1668/9, at Pavenham, Bedfordshire.[2][3] additionally
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomasin, baptized 28 November 1667, Pavenham.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomasin, wife of William Alston, Gent., was buried at Pavenham on 29 January 1669/70.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Research Notes'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Death:
2 CONT
2 CONT A death date and location have been given as 26 Apr 1736 and South Carolina.[citation needed]. Douglas Richardson's Royal Ancestry does not show him immigrating to South Carolina (which would have made William a Gateway Ancestor),
2 CONC  nor does he show a death date.[2] (South Carolina category was removed.) Bedfordshire Parish records include burial at Pavenham of three William Alstons:[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alston, Esq., was buried 16 November 1708
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alston, Gent., was buried 1 December 1713.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alston, "Attorney-at-Law", was buried 26 April 1736.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son John: Richardson's footnote in Royal Ancestry discusses WIlliam's son, John, noting that he was apprenticed to James Jones, merchant of Charleston and immigrated to Charleston, SC sometime around 1682, settling in St. John's, 
2 CONC Berkeley, South Carolina and leaving a will dated 2 January 1718.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The following sources were included in the footnote:<rev name="raV139" />
2 CONT
2 CONT Lionel Cresswell, Stemmata Alstoniana, (1905): 19, 236, 399-400 (Online PDF).
2 CONT
2 CONT South Carolina Mag. of Ancestral Research 34 (2006): 63-68.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cresswell's Stemmata Alstoniana (page 400) concludes that the son of William (eldest son of John Alston and Dorothy Temple) could have "[c]onceivably... been the John Allston of St. John's, Berkeley S. Carolina, whose will is date
2 CONC d ... January 2nd, 1718 or the John Alston of Chowan County, N. Carolina, whose will is dated September, 1754. But in my opinion the mere existence of two Johns—contemporaries—in America disposes of the claims of both in the absen
2 CONC ce of prima facie evidence. The fact that one of the daughters of the first John was called Tomassin is not evidence."[6]
2 CONT
