2 CONT John Cory Sr. was born on 12 May 1658 in Portsmouth, Newport Co, RI. He died on 12 May 1712 in North Kingstown, Washington Co, RI (Now Wickford).  He was buried in 1941 in Quidnessett Cemetery, North Kinstown, RI.  Re-burial 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY
2 CONT *On May 7, 1679 on his petition was granted, by assembly, land in East Greenwich out of shares not taken up. 1686 made a Freeman.
2 CONT *22 March 1686 John of Portsmouth, had 10 acres of land laid out to him in East Greenwich.
2 CONT *4 April 1705, Bought of Daniel Thurston and wife Mary, of Newport, 90 acres of land in East Greenwich for 40 pounds.
2 CONT *10 April 1705. He and wife Elizabeth, sold Jonathan Nichols, of Newport 12 acres for 20 pounds.
2 CONT *16 April 1705 He and Wife Elizabeth, sold to William Brightman, of Newport, 50 acres, for 350 pounds.
2 CONT *4 October 1707 he deeded to son William, for love etc 90 acres, house and meadow, at East Greenwich. ( Both he and son were of Portsmouth, at this date).
2 CONT *1707 Kings Town Deputy.
2 CONT *1710, his house was built which had a chimney 8 square feet.
2 CONT *12 May 1712. Will-Proved 14 July 1712. Exx. Wife Elizabeth. To Wife, certain land and all personal. To son John, house and land, he paying certain legacies. (The rest of will illegible or destroyed.)
2 CONT *26 June 1713 Widow Elizabeth, of Kings Town, sold John Mumford, of Newport, 91 acres in east Greenwich, for 16 pounds.
2 CONT *15 Dec 1713 Son Thomas, with consent of mother Elizabeth, put himself apprentice to Christopher Lindsey, of Newport, house carpenter, for the term of four years, eight months and seventeen days.
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived in Portsmouth and Kingstown, Rhode Island. On 7 May 1679 his petition was granted by the assembly for land in East Greenwich out of shares that had not been taken up. He was made a Freeman in 1686 , which seems to indicat
2 CONC e that he might have been an indentured servant or an apprentice. In that year he had 10 acres of land laid out t o him in East Greenwich. (Sources are not clear as to whether this wa s an affirmation of the 1679 grant or an addit
2 CONC ion to that property.) He bought 90 acres of land in East Greenwich for 40 pounds from Daniel Thurston and wife Mary. On 10 April 1685 he and his wife, Elizabeth, sold Jonathan Nichols 12 acres for 20 pounds. On 16 April 1705 he a
2 CONC nd Elizabeth sold 50 acres for 350 pounds to William Brightman of Newport. On 4 October 1707 he deeded to his son William, for love etc. 90 acres, a house and meadow at East Greenwich. (Records indicate that both he and his son we
2 CONC re of Portsmouth of this date. This ma y account for the discrepancy in the listings of his place of death.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1707-1708 he was a Kingstown Deputy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his will he left his son, John, the farm on Quonset Peninsula at East Greenwich, Kent County, Rhode Island, one half mile from Naragansett Bay. That was where "The Old Corey House" was built (in 1713, one year after John Sr.'s 
2 CONC death). His will was dated 12 May 1712 and proved 14 July 1712. (Other sources indicate that he might have died 3 1 May 1712 or 1713 in Portsmouth.) He named his wife, Elizabeth, executrix of his will, an unusual direction to be g
2 CONC iven in those days o f male dominance of financial matters. To his Wife he left certain land and all personal belongings. To his eldest son, John Jr., he left the house and land, John Jr. to pay certain legacies. The rest of the w
2 CONC ill was illegible or destroyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried on the family farm, as were his wife and many of his descendants. However, all of the graves in the family cemeteries on Quonset Peninsula were moved by the U.S. Navy to Quidnessett Cemetery, Section 29, in North Kin
2 CONC gstown (Wickford) in 1941.
2 CONT
2 CONT Additions were built to the Corey house between 1730 and 1800. In 1941 when the U.S. Navy bought all of the land on Quonset Peninsula in order to build a base where Quonset huts were built. The "newer" vacation cottages on the pen
2 CONC insula were used as officers' quarters. The farm houses were used to house laborers or were torn down. The fate o f "The Old Corey House" is unknown by this researcher. It may have burned down.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Cory Sr. and Elizabeth (Minnetinka) Gasesett were married in Sep 1678 in Portsmouth, Newport Co, RI. Elizabeth (Minnetinka) Gasesett1 (daughter of Chief Gasesett Naragansett Sachem) was born about 1660 in Newport Co, RI. She 
2 CONC died about Dec 1713 in North Kingstown, Washington Co, RI (Now Wickford). She was buried on 19 Dec 1713 in Quidnessett Cemetery, North Kinstown, RI. There are questions about the names and spellings of Elizabeth's and her father's
2 CONC  names. The main sources used here refer to the daughter as Elizabeth Gasesett (others spell it Gassoset or Gassett) and her father as Minnetinka, a Narragansett Indian sachem. An article in t he April 2000 issue of the "Cory Fami
2 CONC ly Society Newsletter" quote s a writer named Edwin Frances Corey saying that a newspaper article which he recalled reading years previously stated that the daughter's name was "Minnetan koo." A reference found by a descendent of 
2 CONC Lillis Baker Corey, (Richard_Kretsinger@macnexus.org) at another web site followed a line of Coreys from Robert in England, dated 1554. It ap peared to use "Minnetinka" as Elizabeth's Indian name and refered to her father as Chief
2 CONC  Gasesett. This web site, however, was no longer valid when tried by this researcher.
2 CONT
2 CONT Whether Gasesett was the father's name, the adopting parents' name or Elizabeth's Indian name is unknown. She is listed herein with Gasesett as her maiden name and that of her sachem father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Documentation states that Elizabeth Gasesett was an Indian "Princess; " Her mother having died when Elizabeth was young, she was orphaned w hen her father was killed in a battle with the Mohegan Indians during King Philip's War (1
2 CONC 675-76). The Mohegans were allied at that time with the English who wished to destroy the Pequot tribes and their allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth was reportedly adopted by a white Quaker family whose identity is unknown. They reared and educated her.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband died he made her the sole executor of his estate. There is a question as to whether Elizabeth was John Corey's first or second wife. Although some records state that he fathered eight children by one wife and two 
2 CONC by another, there is only one wife, Elizabeth , buried with him and their headstones are unusual and identical as though quarried from the same rock source and carved by the same hand.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was originally buried in the family cemetery on Quonset Peninsula , but when the Navy bought the land in 1941 in order to build Quonse t huts, the family graves were moved to Quidnessett Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Cory Sr. and Elizabeth (Minnetinka) Gasesett had the following children:
2 CONT *i. William Cory.
2 CONT *ii. John Cory Jr..
2 CONT *iii. Elisha Cory.
2 CONT *iv. Joseph Cory.
2 CONT *v. Dinah Cory.
2 CONT *vi. Hopestill Cory.
2 CONT *vii. Elizabeth Cory was born about 1693 in Portsmouth, Newport Co, RI. She died about 1693.
2 CONT *viii. Anthony Cory was born about 1694 in North Kingstown, Washington Co, RI (Now Wickford). He died about 1715.
2 CONT *ix. Thomas Cory.
2 CONT *x. Anne Cory.	
2 CONT *xi. Patience Cory was born in 1703 in North Kingstown, Washington Co, RI (Now Wickford).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Freeman 1686. Had alot of property in east Greenwich, R.I. John was Kingstown's Deputy in 1707.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. John Cory was born on 26 Jan 1666/67 in Chelmsford, Middlesex Co, MA.1 He is listed twice in Corys of America, in Book 4 and Book5. In Book5, his birth date is given as Jan 1679/80(?). He died on 15 Nov 1747 in Chelmsford, Midd
2 CONC lesex Co, MA.2 In Book5, his death date is given as abt 1730(?) in Roxbury, Suffolk Co, MA. He signed a will. The inventory of his will is dated 19-Nov-1730. He lived in Roxbury, Suffolk Co, MA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Cory and Elizabeth were married in 1690. Elizabeth was born on 1 Feb 1667/68 in Chelmsford, Middlesex Co, MA. She died on 23 Dec 1727 in Chelmsford, Middlesex Co, MA. John Cory and Elizabeth had the following children: 
2 CONT
