1 NAME Katherine /Fitzgerald/ suo jure Viscountess Grandison
2 GIVN Katherine
2 SURN Fitzgerald
2 NSFX suo jure Viscountess Grandison
2 _MARNM Villiers
2 NICK Katherine Villiers
2 NICK Lady Grandison
1 NAME Katherine Fitzgerald, Viscountess Grandison
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE AUG 1660
2 PLAC Dromana House
2 ADDR
3 CITY Villierstown
3 STAE Ireland
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 DEC 1725
2 ADDR
3 CITY London
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 TITL The Hon. Lady
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Source:  
2 CONT
2 CONT https://goodgentlewoman.wordpress.com/2012/11/04/katherine-fitzgerald-villiers-viscountess-grandison/
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine Fitzgerald Villiers, Viscountess Grandison Posted on November 4, 2012 In the 1660s the war torn medieval castle of Dromana high above the River Blackwater in County Waterford commanded stunning views across oak woodlands
2 CONC .  The vast estate had been the seat of the Fitzgerald family for more than four hundred years and when Sir John Fitzgerald died without a male heir in 1664, all eyes were on his three year old orphaned daughter Katherine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine became first the ward of Charles II and then of her ambitious uncle Richard Le Power.  Keen to extend his land holdings and increase his wealth, Baron Le Power married Katherine to his son John. The ceremony was performe
2 CONC d by the Archbishop of Canterbury Gilbert Sheldon in his chapel at Lambeth Palace on May 20, 1673.  Katherine was just short of her 13th birthday and her groom was eight years old. But Katherine was no pushover.  Two years later, 
2 CONC still only 15 years old, she appealed to the Archbishop for an annulment, citing that she had been married against her consent due to ‘immoderate importunity, threats, fear and the false suggestion of losing her estate.’ Power’s f
2 CONC ather in law, Lord Anglesey, who had been instrumental in arranging the marriage, described Katherine as a ‘jadish viper, fair flirt, wicked urchin,’ in other words – a girl who would not be messed with. 
2 CONT
2 CONT And two years after this she eloped with the man of her choice dashing guardsman Edward Villiers, eldest son of George Villiers, 4th Viscount Grandison of Limerick, who would rise to the rank of Brigadier General in The Queen’s Ba
2 CONC ys.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Grandison title had come into the Villiers family via the marriage of Edward’s grandparents, Sir Edward Villiers and Barbara St John, one of the six daughters of Sir John St John and Lucy Hungerford of Lydiard House.  The visc
2 CONC ountcy was created in 1620 for Sir Oliver St John, Lord Deputy of Ireland, with special remainder to the male issue of his niece Barbara.  First to inherit the title was Barbara’s eldest son William, father of the Royal mistress B
2 CONC arbara, Countess of Castlemaine.  Upon his death in 1659 it went to her second son John who died in 1659 when Barbara’s third son became 4th Viscount Grandison.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Edward and Katherine had six children, Mary, Katherine, Harriott, Elizabeth, William and John who became the 5th Viscount and 1st Earl Grandison. Following Edward’s death in 1693 Katherine set about providing for herself and her 
2 CONC children.  In a letter to the Rt Hon Thomas Keightley, Vice Treasurer of Ireland, Katherine accuses Lord Grandison and the Villiers family of ill treatment. In a direct challenge to her father in law, George 4th Viscount Grandison
2 CONC , plucky Katherine obtained a patent from King William granting her the privilege to enjoy the same title and precedence ‘as if her husband had survived his father.’  Through a private act of parliament Katherine established her r
2 CONC ights, provided for her younger children and regulated the descent of the family estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having secured her family’s fortune, Katherine married again.  Her third husband was William Steuart, MP for Waterford, Privy Councillor in Ireland and commander in chief of the army during the Duke of Ormonde’s absence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katherine died in 1725 aged 64 according to some accounts, insane.  She was buried in Westminster Abbey with other members of the Villiers family.  Her last husband William joined her there a year later.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katherine_FitzGerald,_Viscountess_Grandison
