2 CONT Ormond was freed in 1625 and large parts of his estates were restored to him.[31] For some while he lived in a house in Drury Lane, London, with his grandson James, afterwards Duke of Ormond. In 1629, on the projected marriage of 
2 CONC his grandson with Elizabeth Preston, Preston's only child, Charles I of England granted her marriage and the wardship of her lands to him by letters patent dated 8 September. After the marriage Ormond was recognised, on 9 October 
2 CONC 1630, heir to the lands of his uncle, Earl Thomas, as well as of Sir John Butler of Kilcash, his father.
2 CONT Ormond also suffered problems within his own family. His son Thomas, styled viscount Thurles, married the daughter of Sir John Poyntz of Gloucestershire against his wish. In 1619 Thomas was accidentally drowned at The Skerries, Is
2 CONC le of Anglesey,[32] at the beginning of Walter's long imprisonment in the Fleet Prison. Viscount Thurles had been a prominent Catholic and at the time of his death, was being sent to England on charges of having garrisoned Kilkenn
2 CONC y.
2 CONT Death and timeline
2 CONT Ormond died at Carrick-on-Suir on 24 February 1633 and was buried in St. Canice's Cathedral, Kilkenny, on 18 June 1633.[33] His eldest son having predeceased him, he was succeeded by his grandson, James Butler, later the 1st Duke 
2 CONC of Ormond.
2 CONT Timeline 
2 CONT Notes and references
2 CONT etc.
