1 NAME John /Herring/
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Herring
2 _MARNM Herring
2 NICK John Little
2 NICK John Herring
2 NICK John Little Herring
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1620
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bristol
3 CTRY England
1 DEAT
2 DATE BEF 10 JUN 1672
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 OCCU Tobacco farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Herring is the earliest Herring family member that can be reliably identified. The Herrings trace their American roots to John Herring who appears in the records of Isle of Wight County, VA in the early 1640's
2 CONC . (Isle of Wight County is across the river from Jamestown and a little downriver toward its mouth.) John most certainly came to VA on one of the Bristol ships -- a number of ships that made periodic round trips between Bristol, E
2 CONC ngland and the early Virginia settlements. There were, however, several John Herrings who appear in British records about that time and several who perhaps came to America and so which British John Herring this was and on which pa
2 CONC rticular ship and in what year he came cannot be said with certainty, though a 1641 or early 1642 arrival seems likeliest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just from the typical ages of men coming to America, it is assumed that John was born in England possibly about 1620 and came to America at about age 20. He settled and grew tobacco near the Blackwater River northwest of present d
2 CONC ay Windsor, Isle of Wight County. His will is in the Herring book (and I have seen it abstracted in published sources). It was proved 10 Jun 1672. He gave his son Anthony livestock, a gun, housewares and half the tobacco crop then
2 CONC  growing, but no land. It gave everything else to his wife Marjorie and one young heifer to John Whitfield. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The mention of John Whitfield in the will hints that Marjorie may have been Marjorie Whitfield prior to her marriage to John, likely a widow [of a Whitfield].
2 CONT
2 CONT John Whitfield is mentioned two more times in early Isle of Wight wills. When his mother dies in 1675, she leaves everything to John Whitfield, orphan, and designates Clement Creswell as more or less the guardian for John. She cal
2 CONC ls Clement John’s father-in-law. Your father-in-law is your spouse’s father, but the assumption is John was still a minor so obviously he was not married. The use of the word father-in-law here meant something different to Marjori
2 CONC e. Then when Clement Creswell writes his will in 1682, he passes everything that Marjorie held on to John Whitfield. There were several men named Whitfield among the earliest settlers of Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In any event, John Whitfield must have been quite young in 1672, meaning that Marjorie could not have been Anthony’s mother, so John must have been married earlier to someone whose name is not known. Source: http://www.mertzgeneal
2 CONC ogy.com/reunion/Family_Tree/ps02/ps02_309.htm
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT He came to America from Bristol, England, either in 1642, or in 1635 on the ship Alexander. In any event, he then settled near the Blackwater River in the vicinity of Horse Swamp, northwest of present day Windsor, Isle of Wight Co
2 CONC unty, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a tobacco farmer and made tar.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a book on wills and administrations of Isle of Wight County found at NHCL, it says John Herring left things to son Anthony and wife Margerie and to John Whitfield.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-1-187650342-3-596/john-herring-sr-in-myheritage-family-trees?indId=externalindividual-b9f3e9d96555a3c207b88d21378f8e68&mrid=1c92fd641fb50e28f3c67d5ddfd02c51 MyHeritage Famil
2 CONC y Trees] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 2 2019, 2:21:58 UTC''
