1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was a member of the House of Burgesses for York, VA colony and Colonial Councillor
2 CONT *<nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> The house known as "The Nancy Camp House" (also known as the James Anderson House) is located on the south side of Duke of Gloucester Street in Colonial Williamsburg, VA.  On early Williamsbu
2 CONC rg maps this lot is No. 18 and is marked "Anderson." (Copy of maps in Department of Research. The first definite information as to the ownership of lot 18 is noted in 1723 when Thomas Ravencroft conveyed two lots to Cole Digges: I
2 CONC t appears from a later deed, apparently to this property, that Cole Digges conveyed the lots to his son, Dudley Digges.
2 CONT
2 CONT from: http://research.history.org/DigitalLibrary/View/index.cfm?doc=ResearchReports\RR1224.xml
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Cole Digges married Elizabeth Power [Source from William and Mary Quarterly Vol l by Lyon Gardiner Tyler, Richard Lee Morton William and Mary College., 1892 - United States] Lists their children as: 1. Col Edward Digges of Be
2 CONC llfield (heir) Married Anne Harrison (Daughter of Nathaniel Harrison dcd) 2. William Digges of Denbigh married Frances Robinson 3. Col Dudley Digges married Martha Armstead, Elizabeth Wormley 4. Mary married Nathaniel Harrison of 
2 CONC Brandon 5. Susannah married Benjamin Harrison.
2 CONT
