1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Sackville,_5th_Earl_of_Dorset
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Sackville, 5th Earl of Dorset (16 September 1622 – 27 August 1677) was an English peer and politician. Before his father's death in 1652 he was known as Lord Buckhurst.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born at Dorset House, the second of three children of Edward Sackville, 4th Earl of Dorset and Mary Curzon, daughter and heiress of Sir George Curzon of Croxall Hall, Derbyshire. His elder sister Mary died in 1632; his youn
2 CONC ger brother Edward participated in the English Civil War, and was captured and killed by Parliamentary forces in 1646.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sackville sat in the House of Commons, 1640-3, as Lord Buckhurst, representing East Grinstead in Sussex; he was involved in the political events leading to the English Civil War, and was arrested by Parliament in 1642 and fined £1
2 CONC 500 in 1644. After that point, however, he played no active role in the conflict. He resumed a political career in 1660; he sat in the new parliament or convention that managed the Restoration, and, among other posts, chaired the 
2 CONC committee that was in charge of the reception of King Charles II. The new King appointed Sackville lord lieutenant of Middlesex in 1660. In the 1660s he was able to restore many of the possessions and privileges that his family ha
2 CONC d lost in the Interregnum.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sackville was an occasional poet; a poem in mourning of Ben Jonson was included in the memorial volume Jonsonus Virbius (1638), published in the year after the poet laureate's death. John Aubrey reproduced a report that Sackville 
2 CONC translated Corneille's Le Cid. Sackville was elected a fellow of the Royal Society in 1665.
2 CONT
2 CONT For about  40 years he was married to Lady Frances Cranfield, daughter of Lionel Cranfield, 1st Earl of Middlesex; they had seven sons and six daughters. His eldest son, Charles Sackville, succeeded him as the 6th Earl of Dorset.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried at St Michael and All Angels at Withyham, Sussex "the 7th day of September was buried the Right honnorable Richard Earl of Dorset was Buried"
2 CONT
2 CONT This monument was designed to be Erected
2 CONT before the decease of the Rt Hon Richard
2 CONT Earl of Dorset, Father of this youth
2 CONT who departed this life the 27th of August
2 CONT in the year of our Lord God 1677
2 CONT And in the 55th year of his age. And the
2 CONT Rt Hon Frances Countess Dowager of
2 CONT Dorset, Relict of the said Father
2 CONT And mother of the said youth
2 CONT Erected the same to perpetuate the memory
2 CONT of her husband and son in the year
2 CONT of our Lord 1678
2 CONT
2 CONT
