1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1639, Grandison married Mary Bayning (1623-1672), heiress to a fortune of £180,000; they had one daughter, Barbara Villiers (1640-1709), who was later mistress to Charles II.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''Hon. Mary Bayning1
2 CONT *''''''F, #105609, b. circa 1623, d. before 20 January 1671/72
2 CONT *Last Edited=11 Mar 2009
2 CONT *''''''     Hon. Mary Bayning was born circa 1623.1 She was the daughter of Paul Bayning, 1st Viscount Bayning of Sudbury and Anne Glemham.1 She married, firstly, William Villiers, 2nd Viscount Grandison of Limerick, son of Sir Ed
2 CONC ward Villiers and Barbara St. John, on 31 October 1639.2 She married, secondly, Charles Villiers, 2nd Earl of Anglesey, son of Christopher Villiers, 1st Earl of Anglesey and Elizabeth Sheldon, on 25 April 1648 at St. Batholomew's-
2 CONC the-Less Church, London, England.1 She married, thirdly, Arthur Gorges, son of Sir Arthur Gorges, after 1661.1 She died before 20 January 1671/72.2 She died intestate and her estate was administered to to her daughter, Barbara on 
2 CONC 26 January 1671/72.1
2 CONT *''''''      From 31 October 1639, her married name became Villiers. As a result of her marriage, Hon. Mary Bayning was styled as Dowager Viscountess Grandison.1 From after 1661, her married name became Gorges.1
2 CONT *''''''Child of Hon. Mary Bayning and William Villiers, 2nd Viscount Grandison of Limerick
2 CONT **1.Barbara Villiers, Duchess of Cleveland+3 b. 17 Nov 1640, d. 9 Oct 1709
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extin
2 CONC ct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 133. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *2.[S37] Charles Mosley, editor, Burke's Peerage, Baronetage & Knightage, 107th edition, 3 volumes (Wilmington, Delaware, U.S.A.: Burke's Peerage (Genealogical Books) Ltd, 2003), volume 2, page 2095. Hereinafter cited as Burke's P
2 CONC eerage and Baronetage, 107th edition.
2 CONT *3.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume III, page 91.
2 CONT *http://thepeerage.com/p10561.htm#i105609
2 CONT *______________
2 CONT *Viscount Bayning, of Sudbury in the County of Suffolk, was a title in the Peerage of England. It was created on 8 March 1628 for Paul Bayning, 1st Baron Bayning. He had already been created a Baronet, of Bentley Parva in the Coun
2 CONC ty of Essex, in the Baronetage of England on 24 September 1611,[1] and Baron Bayning, of Horkesley in the County of Essex, on 27 February 1628, also in the Peerage of England. He was succeeded by his son, the second Viscount. On h
2 CONC is early death in 1638 the titles became extinct.
2 CONT *The substantial Bayning estates in Essex and Sussex devolved on the Honourable Anne Bayning, daughter of the first Viscount. In 1674 the viscountcy was revived in favour when she was made Viscountess Bayning, of Foxley in the Cou
2 CONC nty of Berkshire, for life, in the Peerage of England. She was the wife of firstly Henry Murray, Groom of the Bedchamber to Charles I, and secondly of Sir John Baber. The life peerage became extinct on her death in 1678. The Bayni
2 CONC ng title was revived once again in 1797 in favour of the Viscountess Bayning's great-great-grandson Charles Townshend, who was made Baron Bayning in the Peerage of Great Britain. See this title for more information.
2 CONT *The Honourable Elizabeth Bayning, daughter of the first Viscount, married Francis Lennard, 14th Baron Dacre, and was created Countess of Sheppey for life in 1680. ''''''The Honourable Mary Bayning, daughter of the first Viscount,
2 CONC  married William Villiers, 2nd Viscount Grandison, and was the mother of Barbara, Duchess of Cleveland, mistress of Charles II''''''.
2 CONT *Viscount Bayning (1628)
2 CONT *Paul Bayning, 1st Viscount Bayning (1588–1629)
2 CONT *Paul Bayning, 2nd Viscount Bayning (1616–1638)
2 CONT *Viscountess Bayning (1674)
2 CONT *Anne Baber, Viscountess Bayning (1619–1678)
2 CONT *See also
2 CONT *Baron Bayning
2 CONT *Duke of Cleveland
2 CONT *References
2 CONT *1.^ George Edward Cockayne Complete baronetage Volume 1 1900
2 CONT *Leigh Rayment's Peerage Pages [self-published source?][better source needed]
2 CONT *From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viscount_Bayning
2 CONT *______________
2 CONT *Barbara Palmer, 1st Duchess of Cleveland, Countess of Castlemaine, also known as Lady Castlemaine (27 November [O.S. 17 November] 1640[1] – 9 October 1709) was an English courtesan and perhaps the most notorious of the many mistr
2 CONC esses of King Charles II of England, by whom she had five children   .....
2 CONT *Born Barbara Villiers at the parish of St. Margaret's, Westminster, London, she was the only child of the ''''''2nd Viscount Grandison, William Villiers (a half-nephew of the 1st Duke of Buckingham), and his wife, Mary Bayning, h
2 CONC eiress of the 1st Viscount Bayning. On 20 September 1643, her father died in the English Civil War from a wound sustained at the Battle of Newbury while fighting for the Royalists. He had spent his considerable fortune on horses a
2 CONC nd ammunition for his Cavalier regiment; his widow and daughter were left in straitened circumstances. Shortly after Lord Grandison's death, Barbara's mother the Lady Mary remarried to Charles Villiers, 2nd Earl of Anglesey, a cou
2 CONC sin of her late husband''''''.[3]
2 CONT *Upon the 1649 execution of King Charles I, the impoverished Villiers clan  ....
2 CONT *From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbara_Villiers,_1st_Duchess_of_Cleveland
2 CONT *______________
