1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Book of the Lockes: A genealogical and historical record of the descendants ... By John Goodwin Locke
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=2twUAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA358&lpg=PA358&dq=Matthew+Michael+Locke+1550&source=bl&ots=dd3fJR-MCD&sig=mWoBIujyABMkQ7CMo5siY7b88Y0&hl=en&ei=nqcrTYa9L4zSsAPq0ZWmBw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0C
2 CONC BkQ6AEwADgU#v=onepage&q=Matthew%20Michael%20Locke%201550&f=false
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/bookoflockesgene00lock
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/bookoflockesgene00lock#page/342/mode/1up
2 CONT * .... John Locke, sheriff of London, 1460, is the first in a pedigree in my possession. Thomas, his son, was a merchant in London, who died anno 1507, and by Joan his wife (who was the only daughter and heir of Mr. Wilcock of Rot
2 CONC heram in Yorkshire) left three sons,  John, William, and Michael. John is said to have died without issue, and buried in Mercer's Chapel, 1519, ....  William, married two wives; (b) first, Elizabeth daughter and heir of Mr. Spence
2 CONC r, a citizen and fishmonger of London; secondly, Catharine, daughter of William, and sister and co-heir of
2 CONT **	(b) He had four wives.
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/bookoflockesgene00lock#page/343/mode/1up
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Cook, of Wiltshire, knight.  ..... etc.
2 CONT *Michael Locke, younger brother (a) of Sir William, according to a pedigree now before me, was father amongst other children of Matthew Locke, who became seated at Pensford in Buckinghamshire, and had two sons, Richard and Christo
2 CONC pher. The former was of Bedminster in the County of Somerset, and, dying 1617, left John Locke, his son, Mayor of Bristol 1641, father of another John Locke of Bristol, merchant, whose son, Samuel Locke, became a merchant in Londo
2 CONC n, and of whose family and death I have not been able to procure any account. Christopher Locke, the youngest son, soon after Abbot Whiting's lands at East Brent were granted to the Whitmore family, was sent into that 
2 CONT **	(a) English antiquarians have supposed that the Michael Locke here spoken of was a son of Sir William and not his brother, and so I have represented him in the chart that follows this article ; but I have strong suspicion that 
2 CONC the statement in the text is true, and that Sir William had both a brother and a son named Michael. If I am right in my supposition, Matthew of Pensford, who is represented in the chart as the son of Michael, and gr. son of Sir Wi
2 CONC lliam, was in fact the son of Michael and nephew of Sir William. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/bookoflockesgene00lock#page/344/mode/1up
2 CONT *parish (which as I before observed adjoins to Lockstown), to divide the forfeited estates into farms; and having laid out a valuable farm for himself at a place called Pilrow, built a large house and became the stem of a very res
2 CONC pectable family, whose baptisms, as by the parish register penes me, are as follows : 
2 CONT *   1. Christopher .... etc,
2 CONT *   5. Lewis Locke, baptized 13th July 1601; was buried at Taunton Saint Mary Magdalen, March 27, 1692. By four wives he had thirty-five children, most of whom lived to be men and women; and what is more remarkable, his eldest son
2 CONC  John, born 1625, was fifty-nine years of age, when his youngest son Christopher was born at Taunton, anno 1684. It is reported in the family that John had a great-grandson as old as his younger brother; which is, perhaps, the chi
2 CONC ef reason why this branch of the family cannot truly ascertain their respective degrees of kindred. It is, however, generally believed, that all the Lockes of Somersetshire and Devonshire derive themselves from this Lewis; but whe
2 CONC ther so or not, we know that Allen, one of the younger sons by the last wife, was father of another Allen who was father of the present Mr. Thomas Locke of Taunton, Maltster, who has six sons, three of whom are settled in London a
2 CONC nd three in Taunton, the youngest being a Master of Arts of the University of Oxford, also one daughter not yet married.
2 CONT *'''   William Locke of Pitminster, near Taunton, was another son''' of Lewis by his fourth wife. '''He married Dorothy''', daughter of Richard Cooksley of Dunstar, Gentleman, '''and died Jany. 25, 1719, aged 49. His only son''' R
2 CONC ichard married Hannah, the only daughter of John Dod, of Burnham in the County of Somerset, Gentleman, and became an inhabitant of that parish. He died 1765, aged 59, leaving the present Richard Locke, Esq. of Highbridge House in 
2 CONC Burnham, his son and heir. This last gentleman is well known in the literary line as an Antiquary and Agriculturist, he having in the course of forty years written many essays and short pieces, upon various subjects, in the differ
2 CONC ent periodical publications of the day, some with, but more without his real signature. His two essays in the fifth volume of the Bath Agricultural Society's papers, (one on the improvement of meadow land ; the other, an historica
2 CONC l account of the flat part of Somersetshire, as it has been and still is capable of improvement,) are originals in their kind. He has been announced in your present volume, (p. 241,) as the 
2 CONT **	(a) That Capt. Locke was killed as above related, has been staled by several authors ; but Lord King, in the life of John, the son of Capt. Locke, speaking of a letter written by John the son to his father, says "It is without 
