2 CONT Thomas Pelham, 1st Baron Pelham of Laughton Bt (1653 – 23 February 1712) was a moderate English Whig politician and Member of Parliament for several constituencies. He is best remembered as the father of two British Prime Minister
2 CONC s Henry Pelham and the Duke of Newcastle who between them served for eighteen years as first minister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pelham was born in Laughton, Sussex, the son of Sir John Pelham, 3rd Baronet and his wife Lucy Sidney, the daughter of Robert Sidney, 2nd Earl of Leicester. Pelham was educated at Tonbridge Grammar School and Christ Church, Oxford
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He sat for East Grinstead from October 1678 till August 1679. In October 1679, he was returned for Lewes, serving until 1702 when he was returned for both Lewes and Sussex, and he subsequently chose to sit for Sussex, a seat he he
2 CONC ld until 1705.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 26 November 1679, Pelham married Elizabeth Jones, with whom he had two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Elizabeth Pelham (died 11 May 1711), married Charles Townshend, 2nd Viscount Townshend
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy Pelham, died unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth died in October 1681. In 1686, he married Lady Grace Holles (daughter of Gilbert Holles, 3rd Earl of Clare), with whom he had two sons and five daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Grace Pelham (died 1710), married George Naylor
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Pelham-Holles, 1st Duke of Newcastle-upon-Tyne (1693–1768)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Henry Pelham (1696–1754)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Frances Pelham (died 27 June 1756), married Christopher Wandesford, 2nd Viscount Castlecomer
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Gertrude Pelham, married Edmund Polhill
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Lucy Pelham (died 20 July 1736), married Henry Clinton, 7th Earl of Lincoln
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Margaret Pelham (died 23 November 1758), married Sir John Shelley, 4th Baronet
2 CONT
2 CONT Both of his sons ultimately went on to serve as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pelham served as a Lord Commissioner of the Treasury for three distinct periods (March 1690–March 1692; May 1697–June 1699; and March 1701–May 1702) and in 1706 was elevated to the peerage as Baron Pelham of Laughton, having previ
2 CONC ously succeeded to his father's baronetcy in 1703.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Oxford Dictionary of National Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Leigh Rayment's Peerage Pages
2 CONT
