2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Pelham, 1st Baron Pelham of Laughton Bt (c. 1653 – 23 February 1712) was a moderate English Whig politician and Member of Parliament for several constituencies. He is best remembered as the father of two British prime minis
2 CONC ters (Henry Pelham and the Duke of Newcastle) who, between them, served for 18 years as prime minister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pelham was born in Laughton, Sussex, the son of Sir John Pelham, 3rd Baronet and his wife Lucy Sidney (daughter of Robert Sidney, 2nd Earl of Leicester). Pelham was educated at Tonbridge School and Christ Church, Oxford.
2 CONT
2 CONT He sat for East Grinstead from October 1678 until August 1679. In October 1679 he was returned for Lewes, serving until 1702; he subsequently chose to sit for Sussex, a seat he held until 1705.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal life
2 CONT On 26 November 1679 Pelham married Elizabeth Jones,[1] daughter of Sir William Jones, Attorney General from 1650 to 1679 and his wife Elizabeth Alleyn, with whom he had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Elizabeth Pelham (died 11 May 1711), married Charles Townshend, 2nd Viscount Townshend
2 CONT Hon. Lucy Pelham, died unmarried
2 CONT Pelham's wife Elizabeth died in October 1681. In May 1686, he married Lady Grace Holles[1] (daughter of Gilbert Holles, 3rd Earl of Clare and Grace Pierrepoint), with whom he had two sons and five daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hon. Grace Pelham (died 1710), married George Naylor
2 CONT Thomas Pelham-Holles, 1st Duke of Newcastle (1693–1768)
2 CONT Hon. Henry Pelham (1694–1754)
2 CONT Hon. Frances Pelham (died 27 June 1756), married Christopher Wandesford, 2nd Viscount Castlecomer
2 CONT Hon. Gertrude Pelham, married Edmund Polhill
2 CONT Hon. Lucy Pelham (died 20 July 1736), married Henry Clinton, 7th Earl of Lincoln
2 CONT Hon. Margaret Pelham (died 23 November 1758), married Sir John Shelley, 4th Baronet
2 CONT Both of Pelham's sons went on to serve as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom. He served as a Lord Commissioner of the Treasury for three separate periods (March 1690–March 1692; May 1697–June 1699 and March 1701–May 1702); in 17
2 CONC 06, he was elevated to the peerage as Baron Pelham of Laughton (having previously succeeded to his father's baronetage in 1703).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/details/9CN1-1F5
