1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Name 	John Stapley<br/>
2 CONT Event Type 	Christening<br/>
2 CONT Event Date 	29 Jun 1628<br/>
2 CONT Event Place 	Patcham, Sussex, England<br/>
2 CONT Gender 	Male<br/>
2 CONT Father's Name 	Anthony Stapley<br/>
2 CONT Mother's Name 	Anne<br/>
2 CONT "England, Sussex, Parish Registers, 1538-1910", database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:KFGR-XRS : 7 December 2017), John Stapley, 1628.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Grave here at St Mary the Virgin, Ringmer: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/104933723/john-stapeley<br/>
2 CONT Inscribed memorial stone here: https://images.findagrave.com/photos/2013/187/104933723_137320299809.jpg<br/>
2 CONT Also shown here: http://www.sussexrecordsociety.org/dbs/esm/church/2120/
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Wikipedia contributors. "<em>[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Stapley John Stapley]</em>." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [http://archive.org/stream/cu31924092524390#page/n115/mode/2up Complete baronetage]; Cokayne, George E. (George Edward); 1903; Vol. III; page 97
2 CONT ----
2 CONT [http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1660-1690/member/stapley-john-1628-1701 History of Parliament - STAPLEY, John (1628-1701), of Patcham and Broyle Place, Ringmer, Suss.]<br/>
2 CONT (Published in The History of Parliament: the House of Commons 1660-1690, ed. B.D. Henning, 1983.)<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ''bap. 29 June 1628, 2nd but 1st surv. s. of Anthony Stapley† of Patcham by 1st w. Anne, da. of George Goring of Hurstpierpoint. m. c.1651, Mary (d.1709), da. and coh. of Herbert Springet of Broyle Place, 2s. d.v.p. 8da. suc. fa. 
2 CONC 1655; cr. Bt. 28 July 1660; kntd. 6 Aug. 1660.''<br/>
2 CONT ...<br/>
2 CONT ''Stapley’s family was established in East Sussex by the end of the 15th century. His father, a Presbyterian, moved to Patcham, some seven miles west of Lewes, about 1626, and sat for the borough in several Parliaments. He became 
2 CONC a regicide and a Councillor of State during the Interregnum. Stapley outwardly conformed to the Protectorate, but he and his brother took their politics from their mother’s side of the family. They became leaders of a royalist con
2 CONC spiracy in 1658, which they abjectly betrayed to the Protector.''<br/>
2 CONT ...<br/>
2 CONT ''and remained active in local government until his death. Although forced to sell Patcham, he retained Broyle Place and his wife’s share of the Springet estate, which she valued at £350 p.a. He died on 22 Aug. 1701 and was buried
2 CONC  at Ringmer, the last of the family.''
2 CONT ----
