1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Cornelius Turner (c. 1615 – 1675) was an early English immigrant and settler who established a lineage in colonial Virginia. Most historical and genealogical significance surrounding his life is preserved through h
2 CONC is connection to the Henley family of James City County, one of the foundational families of early Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Biographical Information-
2 CONT Birth:
2 CONT Born circa 1615 in Oldham, Lancashire, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT Recorded in family trees as the son of Robert Turner and Janet (Jonet) Sanders. Alternate records occasionally point to Edward Turner and Marjorie Johnston.
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration:
2 CONT He arrived in the Virginia colony during the mid-17th century, where he established a residence in James City County/Jamestown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage:
2 CONT He married Margaret Wilkinson around 1640 in James City, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children & Legacy:
2 CONT He is primarily recognized as the father of Susan Turner (sometimes spelled Torner). Susan married Reynold (Reginald) Henley, establishing a multi-generational legacy in Virginia. 
2 CONT The name "Cornelius Turner" was consistently passed down through the family line, most notably seen in his great-grandson, Cornelius Turner Henley (born 1728).
2 CONT
2 CONT Death:
2 CONT He passed away in 1675 in Virginia (frequently cited as Jamestown).
2 CONT
2 CONT Essential Source Material & Primary Documents:
2 CONT Because early James City County court records were almost entirely destroyed by fires (most notably during the Richmond evacuation of 1865), direct records for Cornelius Turner are scarce. Genealogists rely on indirect land regist
2 CONC ries and family compilations to verify his existence.Virginia Land Patent Books (Land Office Records): While Cornelius's physical patent records are fragmented, his presence is confirmed through the land patents of his son-in-law 
2 CONC and grandchildren. Specifically, Virginia Land Patent Book 8, folio 397 (issued October 26, 1694, to his grandson Leonard Henley) traces land ownership back to December 15, 1661, confirming the family's deep roots in James City Co
2 CONC unty during Cornelius’s lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Passenger and Immigration Lists:
2 CONT Early iterations of Virginia immigration indices detail the transport of the Turner family. Susan Turner's arrival and headright tracking can be found compiled within early Virginia Land Office Records
2 CONT
2 CONT The Henley Family Papers:
2 CONT Biographical links linking Cornelius Turner to the Henleys of "Merry Oaks" are validated through private family bibles and court depositions abstracted in early historical periodicals.
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT
2 CONT May not be same as contemporary Cornelius Adam Turner.  More investigation needed.
