1 NAME Philadelphia /Carey/
2 GIVN Philadelphia
2 SURN Carey
2 _MARNM Wentworth
1 SEX F
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sir Ferdinando Carey (1590–1638), married Philippa, daughter of Sir John Throckmorton. 
2 CONT They were the parents of '''Philadelphia Carey, Baroness Wentworth'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_Carey
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Philadelphia Carey1
2 CONT *'''F, #472402, d. 4 May 1696
2 CONT *Last Edited=24 Aug 2014
2 CONT *Consanguinity Index=0.01%
2 CONT *'''     Philadelphia Carey was the daughter of Colonel Ferdinand Carey and Philippa Throckmorton.1,2 She married Thomas Wentworth, 5th Lord Wentworth, son of Thomas Wentworth, 1st and last Earl of Cleveland and Anne Croftes, befo
2 CONC re 11 March 1657/58.1 She died on 4 May 1696.1
2 CONT *'''      From before 11 March 1657/58, her married name became Wentworth.1
2 CONT *'''Child of Philadelphia Carey and Thomas Wentworth, 5th Lord Wentworth
2 CONT **Henrietta Maria Wentworth, Baroness Wentworth1 b. 11 Aug 1660, d. 23 Apr 1686
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S37] BP2003 volume 2, page 2442. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37] [S7234] Joan Labouchere, "re: Labouchere Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger LUNDY (101053), 24 August 2014. Hereinafter
2 CONC  cited as "re: Labouchere Family."
2 CONT *From: http://thepeerage.com/p47241.htm#i472402 _____________________
2 CONT *'''Thomas Wentworth, KB, PC (bapt. 2 February 1612 – 1 March 1665)''' was an English soldier and politician who supported King Charles I in the English Civil War. He served the king during two parts of the English Civil War and a
2 CONC ccompanied the young Prince Charles (later King Charles II) in exile.
2 CONT *Wentworth was born in 1612, the eldest son of the 1st Earl of Cleveland and his first wife, Anne. His exact birthdate is unknown, but parish records show that he was baptised on 2 February 1612.
2 CONT *In 1640, he was elected Member of Parliament (MP) for Bedfordshire to the Short Parliament in April and again to the Long Parliament in November. However, before he took his seat in November, he was called up to the House of Lord
2 CONC s by writ of acceleration in his father's barony of Wentworth.
2 CONT *'''He was married by mid-March of 1658 to Philadelphia Carey (d. 1696), daughter''' of Sir Ferdinando Carey. '''Together they had one child''': Henrietta Maria Wentworth was born on 11 August 1660.
2 CONT *Honors awarded to Wentworth include the Order of the Bath (KB) and appointment to the Privy Council of England (PC).
2 CONT *Wentworth died at age 53 on 1 March 1665, thereby predeceasing his father by two years. He was buried six days later at Toddington. His daughter Henrietta succeeded to the barony upon her father's death; she would have an affair 
2 CONC with James Scott, 1st Duke of Monmouth but no children. After her death at 26 the barony passed to her aunt, Anne Lovelace, 7th Baroness Wentworth, the wife of John Lovelace, 2nd Baron Lovelace.
2 CONT *During the English Civil War, Wentworth was the Sergeant-Major-General of Horse and commanded the Prince of Wales's Regiment of Horse. He saw action at Tipton Green, Cropredy Bridge, and possibly at Newbury, all in 1644; at Langp
2 CONC ort in 1645; and at Worcester, the final battle of the English Civil War in 1651. He fought most battles alongside his father. When the Royalists were defeated, Lord Wentworth accompanied the court into exile. He raised a regiment
2 CONC  (which later became the Grenadier Guards) at Bruges as a bodyguard to the exiled Charles II.
2 CONT * ... etc.
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Wentworth,_5th_Baron_Wentworth ____________________
2 CONT *'''Dictionary of National Biography, 1885-1900, Volume 60
2 CONT *'''Wentworth, Thomas (1613-1665)'''         by Herbert Edward Douglas Blakiston
2 CONT *'''WENTWORTH, Sir THOMAS, Baron Wentworth (1613–1665)''', eldest son, by his first wife, of Thomas Wentworth, fourth baron Wentworth of Nettlestead and first earl of Cleveland [q. v.], was born at Todding- ton, knighted on 2 Feb.
2 CONC  1625–6, and entered at Trinity College, Oxford, in 1628; in 1631 he was at The Hague, at the court of the Queen of Bohemia, who frequently mentions him in her letters (see Evelyn, Letters, passim). He was with his father at Berwi
2 CONC ck in 1640, and was in the same year returned to both the Short and Long parliaments; but on 25 Nov. 1640 was summoned to the upper house in his father's barony of Nettlestead. During the early part of the civil war (1642–5) he co
2 CONC mmanded a troop of horse, first under Charles, viscount Wilmot [q. v.], against whose dismissal he protested, and then under Lord Goring; was present at the battles of Cropredy Bridge and Newbury in 1644, and shared the revels and
2 CONC  intrigues of Prince Charles's disastrous campaign in the west in 1645. In 1646, on Goring's flight to France, the chief command fell to Wentworth, who, according to Bulstrode (Memoirs, pp. 93–4, 149–53), ‘was not thought either o
2 CONC f interest, experience, courage, or reputation enough for that trust.’ He was mainly responsible for the defeat and surrender at Torrington on 14 March 1646. He also presumed to talk ‘imperiously and disrespectfully’ to the prince
2 CONC ; and, after being driven from his quarters at Ashburton, was placed as general of the horse under the chief command of Lord Hopton, with whom and the prince he eventually escaped to the Scilly Isles and Jersey. In 1649 he attende
2 CONC d Charles to Paris, was with him in Scotland and at Worcester, and formed one of the council till the Restoration, being gentleman of the chamber and master of the ceremonies. His principal services were a diplomatic mission from 
2 CONC Cologne to Denmark in 1653, and the organisation and command of the ‘royal regiment of guards’ in 1656, though he seems not to have been present at the battle of the Dunes in 1658. After the Restoration he retained this colonelcy,
2 CONC  received 500l. from the king in November 1663, and died in his father's lifetime on 28 Feb. 1665. '''By his wife Philadelphia (d. 4 May 1696), daughter''' of Sir Ferdinando Carey, '''who was naturalised in 1662 and received a pen
2 CONC sion of 600l., very irregularly paid, he had an only child''', Henrietta Maria Wentworth [q. v.], who succeeded his father in the barony. A portrait of Wentworth, painted in 1640, belongs to Mr. H. R. Clifton of Clifton Hall, Nott
2 CONC ingham, and is reproduced in F. W. Hamilton's ‘Grenadier Guards.’ Lloyd credits him with ‘a very strong constitution and admirable parts for contrivance.’
2 CONT *[Authorities cited under Wentworth, Thomas, Earl of Cleveland, and F. W. Hamilton's Grenadier Guards, caps. i. and iii.]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Wentworth,_Thomas_(1613-1665)_(DNB00)
2 CONT * https://archive.org/stream/dictionaryofnati60stepuoft#page/283/mode/1up to https://archive.org/stream/dictionaryofnati60stepuoft#page/284/mode/1up _____________________________
2 CONT *'''Pedigrees of the county families of Yorkshire (1874) Vol. 2 Pg.n258 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/pedigreesofcount02fost
2 CONT **Pedigree of Wentworth, of Elmsall, Bretton and Baron Wentworth, of Nettlested.
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/pedigreesofcount02fost#page/n265/mode/1up
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/pedigreesofcount02fost#page/n266/mode/1up
2 CONT *SEE DOCUMENTS OR SOURCES __________________
2 CONT *Links
2 CONT * http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=wentworth&GSfn=thomas&GSbyrel=all&GSdy=1667&GSdyrel=in&GScntry=5&GSob=n&GRid=139432673&df=all&
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________________
