2 CONT
2 CONT William (Rayment) Raymond (abt. 1638 - 1709)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Capt. William Raymond formerly Rayment===
2 CONT
2 CONT Born about 1638 in Glastonbury, Somerset, England
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of George Raymond and Mary (Shell) Raymond
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother of Dorothy (Rayment) Robyns [half], George Rayment [half], Maurice Raymond [half], John Rayment [half] and Elizabeth Rayment [half]
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Hannah (Bishop) Raymond — married 1661 [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Ruth (Hull) Rayment — married 1681 [location unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of William Raymond, Edward Raymond, George Rayment, Hannah (Raymond) Hutchinson, Abigail (Raymond) Giles, Mary (Rayment) Batchelder, Ruth (Rayment) Batchelder and Ebenezer Raymond Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 29 Jan 1709 at about age 71 in Beverly, Essex County, Massachusetts Bay
2 CONT
2 CONT Profile last modified 19 Jul 2023 | Created 11 Sep 2012
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT William Rayment (or Raymond) was born about 1638, presumably in Glastonbury, Somerset, England.[1][2] He was the son of George Rayment (or Raymond) of St. John's Parish in Glastonbury. His English origins are proven by his father'
2 CONC s will in 1651, which names children Dorothy Robyns, Maurice Rayment, William Rayment, Elizabeth Rayment and John Rayment.[3] William, John and Elizabeth were specifically noted to be living in New England.
2 CONT William Rayment himself deposed that he immigrated to New England in 1652, however, it was more likely in the spring of 1651.[4] His father's will places him as being already in New England by June of 1651. He would have been only
2 CONC  12 to 14 years old, and almost certainly would have traveled with his brother John Rayment who was considerably older (born about 1622). Both he and his brother settled across the Bass River in Salem on land which had originally 
2 CONC been granted to the Old Planter William Trask.[5] This land was set off from Salem to create the new town of Beverly in 1668.
2 CONT William Rayment spent his entire life in New England in Beverly and was active in the civil affairs of the town. In March 1662, he bought a home lot with 4 acres of land which had belonged to had belonged to William Ellet for £30;
2 CONC  this land was adjacent to his father-in-law Edward Bishop.[6] He petitioned the town for additional land in 1668.[7] He was chosen to serve on the county jury in 1667, 1676, 1679 and 1681.[8][9][10][11] He was also the deputy to 
2 CONC the General Court in 1683, 1685 and 1686.[12][13][14][15] He was the constable in Beverly in 1675.[16][17],He was also a surveyor in 1678.[18] William Rayment was a selectman for the town in 1670, 1683 and 1689.[19] He was on the 
2 CONC committee to determine the town boundaries in a dispute with the neighboring towns of Salem and Wenham.[20] His name is frequently found as a witness to deeds and in giving testimony in various legal disputes.
2 CONT William Rayment is most noted for a long military career. He was in the company of Capt. Lothrop at the capture of Saint John and Port Royal in the French colony of Acadia in 1654.[21][22] During King Philip's War, he was present 
2 CONC at the Great Swamp Fight in December 1675. In 1683, Lt. William Raymond was made commander of the Beverly militia unit. In 1690, he was the Captain of the Beverly Company in the ill-fated Phipps Expedition to Canada. About 2,000 N
2 CONC ew Englanders left in the spring of 1690 to attack Quebec with the belief that success would force all of Canada to submit, and bring a large financial gain to the colonies. The combination of disease, and little to no leadership 
2 CONC doomed the endeavor to failure.[23][24] From this point forward, he was commonly called Capt. William Rayment in the records.
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: About 1638 (1637-1639).
2 CONT If we use the three depositions in 1671 and 1680 as the most precise and consistent, we would suppose that he was born between March and June 1638. This obviously does not fit all of the data points.
2 CONT Deposed aged about 30 years in March 1669/70.[25]
2 CONT Deposed aged 32 years in March 1671.[26]
2 CONT Deposed aged 42 in June 1680.[27]
2 CONT Deposed aged 42 in September 1680.[28]
2 CONT Deposed aged 43 in June 1682.[29]
2 CONT Deposed aged 60 or thereabouts in December 1697.[30]
2 CONT Aged about 72 years at death in January 1708/9.[31]
2 CONT Note: There is no evidence to support a birthdate of 11 May 1637 as is often found on the internet. The parish records for St. John's Glastonbury no longer exist in this time period so this must be considered an error.
2 CONT Marriage and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 1st - Hannah Bishop about 1661, presumably in Salem, Massachusetts.[32] She was the daughter of Edward Bishop of Salem.[33] They had five children. Hannah died between 1676 when her last child was baptized and 1681 when h
2 CONC er husband remarried.
2 CONT Married: 2nd - Ruth Hull by 1681 as their child Mary was born in May 1682.
2 CONT Children of William Rayment and and Hannah Bishop:
2 CONT William Raymond. Born by May 1662 based on his admission as a freeman in May 1683.[34] He married Mary Kettle, the daughter of John and Mary (Allen) Kettle. William was killed by a falling tree shortly before 23 January 1700/1.[35
2 CONC ]
2 CONT Edward Raymond. Baptized on 12 July 1668 in Beverly.[36] Edward died 6 November 1727 (no known children).
2 CONT George Raymond. Baptized on 30 October 1670 in Beverly.[37] He married Jerusha Woodbury on 28 March 1698 in Beverley.[38] He was dead by December 1705 when his father made his will, which also notes that he had an only child named
2 CONC  Hannah.
2 CONT Hannah Raymond. Baptized on 18 May 1673 in Beverly.[39] She married Nathaniel Hayward, Jr.[40]
2 CONT Abigail Raymond. Baptized on 23 July 1676 in Beverly.[41] She married John Giles on 29 March 1694 in Marblehead, Massachusetts.[42]
2 CONT Children of William Rayment and Ruth Hull:
2 CONT Mary Raymond. Born on 3 May 1682 in Beverley.[43] She married Josiah Batchelder on 19 Dec 1700 in Salem, Massachusetts, and had eight children.[44] Mary died on 26 October 1763 in Beverly.
2 CONT Ruth Raymond. Born say 1684, presumably in Beverly. Named as youngest daughter in her father's will. She married Jonathan Batchelder on 25 February 1702/3.[45]
2 CONT Ebenezer Raymond. Born about 1690 (died in his 58th year). He married Joanna Herrick on 6 January 1714/5 in Beverly.[46] Ebenezer died on 21 May 1747.[47]
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 29 January 1708/9 in Beverly, Massachusetts.[48]
2 CONT Will: of William Rayment of Beverley[49][50]
2 CONT Dated 8 December 1705; proved 25 February 1708/9 (administration of the estate would continue for decades).
2 CONT Summary:
2 CONT Item: ...my loving wife Ruth...her father Hull...my sons Edward and Ebenezer...
2 CONT 2ndly: ...to my three grandchildren William, Daniel and Paul, sons of my son William, lately deceased, my eldest son
2 CONT 3rdly: ...my son Edward being my second son...
2 CONT 4thly: ...to my son Ebenezer being my youngest son...
2 CONT Note: ...to my two sons and three grandsons...
2 CONT 5thly: ...to my granddaughter Hannah the only child of my son George late deceased...
2 CONT 6thly: ...to my daughter Hannah my eldest daughter widow and relic to Nathaniel Hayward...
2 CONT 7thly: ...to my daughter Abigail my second daughter wife of John Giles...
2 CONT 8thly: ...to my daughter Mary wife of Josiah Bathelder...
2 CONT 9thly: ...to my daughter Ruth being my youngest daughter wife of Jonathan Bathelder...
2 CONT 10thly: ...my loving wife Ruth and my two dutiful sons Edward and Ebenezer to be joynt executors...
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Name at Birth: The name can be found with a number of variations including Rayment, Reyment, Raiment, Reayment, Raiement, Raymont, Raymond, Raymound, Raimond, Reymond and Raymund. The transcription of his father's will uses t
2 CONC he spelling Rayment. Where recorded, the births and baptisms of his children were (in order) Raymond, Raymond, Raiment, and Raiment. It is Rayment in his death record and in his will. It is reasonable to use Rayment as the name he
2 CONC  was most likely baptized with and most commonly used in records during his lifetime, and switch to Raymond for his children as found in several of their baptisms, and commonly used in their records. At no time was the spelling co
2 CONC nsistent for either William Rayment or his children.
2 CONT Family associations: Further proof that John Rayment of Beverly and Capt. William Rayment of Beverly were brothers is found in a deed of John's son John Rayment who named Capt. William Rayment as his uncle.[51]
2 CONT Richard Rayment: He is sometimes said in older secondary sources to be related to Richard Rayment, also of Salem.[52] There is absolutely no evidence to suggest this true. Richard Rayment was in Salem by 1634, well before William 
2 CONC Rayment was even born. While there are numerous sources which directly prove John and William Rayment were brothers, there are no sources to connect Richard Rayment to John or William Rayment.
2 CONT Birth/baptism as 11 May 1637. A baptismal date as Glastonbury, 11 May 1637, is frequently association with William Rayment (FindAGrave; FamilySearch FamilyTee, PID LTD4-6Z2; WikiTree). According to George Lansing Raymond in 1918, 
2 CONC "All the [St. John's] records are missing between about 1621 and 1653."[53]
2 CONT A source for this baptismal date was found on Robert Raymond's family pages for " as "International Genealogy Index, v4.01, British Isles, film #455250 (17 March 2001)." The link provided returned a 404-like notice in 2021; a simi
2 CONC lar notice was returned in a search for the referenced FHL film number (455250). No such data was returned in a general IGI search. Multiple matching entries as to date, location (St. John's Glaston, Somerset, England) and name we
2 CONC re found in a 2021 search of "contributed International Genealogical Index" entries (accessed from here; these submittals seem now referred to as "Genealogies"). The William Raymond returns examined referred to a submittal by chur
2 CONC ch member "ratolman5983"; submission ID MM4P-B7T.
2 CONT A search for "Will* Ray*" returned no such entry in the indexed collection, "All Somerset, England, Church of England Baptisms, Marriages, and Burials, 1531-1812," by subscription, Ancestry.com.
2 CONT Record Extraction
2 CONT From Vital Records of Beverly:
2 CONT Raiment, Abigall, d. William and Hannah, bp. 23: 5m: 1676. CR1 (1:273)
2 CONT Raiment, Hannah, d. William and Hanah, bp. 18: 3m: 1673. CR1 (1:273)
2 CONT Raymund, Edward, s. William and Hannah, bp. 12: 5m: 1668. CR1 (1:278)
2 CONT Raymund, George, s. William and Hannah, bp. 30: 8m: 1670. CR1 (1:278)
2 CONT Name of mother: The name of his mother is sometimes given as Abigail Maurice. This comes from the speculation by
2 CONT Historical Note: Raymond, Maine: In 1735 Massachusetts granted land to descendants of those who served in the Phipps expedition (1690) but who weren’t paid (or were paid with paper money that rapidly declined in value). Nine towns
2 CONC hips were thusly granted, e.g., 36 sq. miles to troops from Dorchester, becoming known as Dorchester-Canada, later Ashburnham. MA. A similar grant was made for service of the Barnstable (MA) militia; it is now known as Dunbarton, 
2 CONC NH. A grant in 1735 of 36 sq. miles in NH to soldiers of the Beverly troop became known as Beverly-Canada or Halestown, but the land wasn’t owned by MA so the grant was changed in 1765 to land in SW Maine (not then a State). The t
2 CONC own formed there by the shore of Lake Sebago was named Raymondtown (now Raymond, ME) in honor of Capt. William Raymond ca. 58 years after his death. See Gage, Arthur, Ancestry of Lyman Gage, NEHGR 53: 204 plus websites for the tow
2 CONC ns named in this Note.
