1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education bap. 7 Oct. 1649, 1st s. of Vincent Randyll, of Chilworth, Surr. by Dorothy, da. of John Duncombe of Weston, Surr.  educ. Wadham, Oxf. matric. 1666, BA 1670; M. Temple 1670, called 1677.  m. li
2 CONC c. 5 Feb. 1678, Anne, da. and coh. of Sir Thomas Gould, Draper, of Aldermanbury, London, alderman of London 1676–83, 2 da.  suc. fa. 1673.
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held Freeman, Guildford 1679; sheriff, Surr. 1686–7.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Randyll’s interest at Guildford arose from the proximity of the town to his family’s seat at Chilworth, which lay less than three miles away. His grandfather Sir John Morgan had originally acquired the manor and its powd
2 CONC er mills in the early 17th century, and Randyll’s father (Vincent) had developed it into a considerable estate by the time of his death. Vincent Randyll’s steady adherence to the Royalist cause had earned him a nomination for the 
2 CONC abortive Order of the Royal Oak, but his son was to pursue a far more idiosyncratic political career which saw his allegiance fluctuate between Whig and Tory camps. On first gaining entrance to Westminster, he was regarded as a Wh
2 CONC ig, thereby following the example of his father-in-law rather than that of his father. However, such support contributed greatly to his defeat at the election of 1685, a setback which may have influenced his decision to undertake 
2 CONC local office under James II. Such political inconsistency was punished by his failure to gain election to the Convention, a defeat which was compounded by the Commons’ rejection of his petition against John Weston* on 24 Apr. 1689
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT At the election of March 1690 Randyll gained his revenge over Weston, and for the next 15 years he appeared content to share the Guildford seats with the powerful Onslow interest. Such repeated success, however, was not gained by 
2 CONC any consistent political stance on his part, for politicians at Westminster gave conflicting impressions of his allegiance. At the outset of the 1690–5 Parliament, Lord Carmarthen (Sir Thomas Osborne†) classed him as a Whig, but b
2 CONC y April 1691 he had been described as a Country supporter by Robert Harley*, a verdict which the latter subsequently amended to ‘doubtful’. Such confusion was also represented in the calculations of Samuel Grascome, who, while cla
2 CONC ssifying him as a pensioner or placeman (for some unknown reason) after the spring of 1693, concluded that he was not a Court supporter. However, on 1 Mar. 1693 he appeared in the Whig camp when acting as a teller in opposition to
2 CONC  a bill to satisfy the debts which the London corporation owed to the orphans under its care. Partially explaining these uncertainties about his politics is the fact that Randyll remained an inactive Member throughout his career.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Randyll’s political outlook was still unclear early in the next Parliament, his antipathy towards the Court became much more evident as the 1695–8 Parliament progressed. In January 1696 he was initially described as doubt
2 CONC ful on the issue of the proposed council of trade but was then forecast as one of the Court’s likely opponents on the issue. More worryingly for him, even though the Association did not present him with any crisis of allegiance on
2 CONC  27 Feb., his name featured in the course of the subsequent investigations into the Assassination Plot. On 12 June one of the interrogated suspects, Peter Cook, testified that Randyll had furnished him with written accounts of par
2 CONC liamentary proceedings. Cook informed the inquiry that Randyll’s good friend, the late Sir Thomas Clarges*, had also provided him with similar reports, but both of them were cleared of any involvement in the Jacobite plot. When th
2 CONC e fate of Sir John Fenwick† was debated in Parliament in November, Randyll voted against the attainder, and for the rest of that Parliament showed himself much more openly opposed to the Court. In August 1698 he was forecast as a 
2 CONC likely opponent of the maintenance of a standing army, and a month later, in the wake of his victory in the contested Guildford election, another parliamentary observer classed him as a Country supporter.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, once the disbanding issue had been resolved, Randyll again returned to the political fringes, a move indicated by the failure to identify him with any particular parliamentary interest in 1700. His position on the back be
2 CONC nches was in little doubt, although he acted as a teller in favour of a private bill involving his sister-in-law’s husband, Sir Walter Clarges, 1st Bt.* However, after Robert Harley had identified him as a Tory in December 1701, h
2 CONC is political allegiance became much more predictable. On 26 Feb. 1702 he voted to vindicate the Commons’ proceedings in the impeachment of the Junto ministers the previous session, and maintained these apparent Tory sympathies und
2 CONC er the new monarch on 13 Feb. 1703 when dividing against the Lords’ proposal for an extension of the period permitted for taking the abjuration oath. He was then forecast on 30 Oct. 1704 as one of the Members likely to support the
2 CONC  Tack, although in the crucial division of 28 Nov. he either voted against the measure or was absent from the House.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately for Randyll, the surest indication of his identification with the Tories was his electoral defeat at the hands of Robert Wroth* in May 1705. Lord Halifax delighted in Wroth’s victory over ‘a bosom friend of Lord Guer
2 CONC nsey (Hon. Heneage Finch I*)’, and the result signalled the demise of Randyll’s electoral partnership with the Onslows. At the subsequent county election such antagonism was revealed by Randyll’s decision to plump for their rival 
2 CONC Edward Harvey*, and a series of contested elections at Guildford was the most direct consequence of this local split. Even though he had lost his seat, Randyll’s involvement in parliamentary affairs was not completely abated, for 
2 CONC in February 1707 he lent his support to a petition to introduce a private estate bill involving Surrey properties. Before the year was out his eagerness to return to Westminster was revealed by rumours of his possible candidacy fo
2 CONC r Ludgershall. Only a month before the election of May 1708, it was reported that he ‘does design to stand’ for the Wiltshire constituency on the interest of his sister-in-law Lady Clarges, but he withdrew from the contest after h
2 CONC e had gained electoral success at Guildford.2
2 CONT
2 CONT Randyll’s comfortable victory over Wroth was regarded by the Earl of Sunderland (Charles, Lord Spencer*) as a setback for the Whigs, a view which merely endorsed the verdict of another parliamentary list compiled earlier in the ye
2 CONC ar. His opposition to the impeachment of Sacheverell was his only activity of any note in that Parliament, but his support for the Church’s champion did not secure him an easy victory at the ensuing general election. Despite the T
2 CONC ory tide which swept both county and country in 1710, his rival Wroth was returned ahead of Randyll by a margin of just three votes. Within days of the poll Randyll was reported to be resolved to petition, ‘thinking the right to b
2 CONC e very plainly on his side’, a confidence which was eventually vindicated on 3 Feb. 1711 when the Commons ruled in his favour. By way of repayment to his Tory allies, he was later cited as one of the ‘worthy patriots’ who in the c
2 CONC ourse of the first session of that Parliament took part in exposing the mismanagements of the previous administration, and was also identified by Boyer as a member of the October Club in February 1712. His attention to other aspec
2 CONC ts of parliamentary business was unremarkable, although he did act as a teller on 10 Mar. 1711 in favour of hearing the city of Exeter’s petition against the bill to repeal the ban on French wine imports. Furthermore, the followin
2 CONC g month he was ordered to carry up to the Lords a bill to discharge a debt to the crown, and later reported on a bill to maintain the Equivalent for the corporation of London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his recent support for High Tory initiatives, Randyll’s independent spirit was once again highlighted in the course of the third session of the 1710 Parliament, and on 18 June 1713 he voted against the French commerce bill
2 CONC . On account of his behaviour in this division he was bracketed with the ‘whimsical’ Tories, and for the rest of Anne’s reign his allegiance was regarded as volatile. The grant of an 11-year lease in August 1713 to raise wrecks in
2 CONC  the West country and America may well have been designed by the ministry as a means of securing his support, the value of which was attested a month later by the ease with which he was returned at Guildford. Crucial to that victo
2 CONC ry was his rapprochement with the Onslows, a development which paralleled his strengthening ties with the Whigs at Westminster. Three parliamentary observers, including the compiler of the Worsley list, cited him as a Whig soon af
2 CONC ter the Hanoverian succession, and although his subsequent opposition to the Whig ministries casts doubt on his adherence to the party line, he evidently had little difficulty in accommodating himself to the new regime.
2 CONT
2 CONT The ease of his victory at the Guildford election of 1715 suggested that Randyll’s local influence was unaffected by the change of dynasty, but his fortunes were to decline rapidly as the cost of frequent campaigning over the spac
2 CONC e of 40 years finally took its toll. In 1720 he was forced to sell his Chilworth estate to a director of the South Sea Company for £29,335, a desperate move which robbed him of an annual income of some £1,000. Unrepentant, he cont
2 CONC ested Guildford for the last time in 1722, only to be defeated by the increasingly powerful Onslow interest. He then fell into complete political obscurity and the last mention of him came from his grandson Henry Vane† who was inv
2 CONC olved in making arrangements for Randyll’s maintenance in the Fleet prison in July 1735. The marriage of his daughter Mary to Gilbert Vane, 2nd Lord Barnard, may have served as an indication of Randyll’s influence 30 years before,
2 CONC  but it was probably a pauper’s death that he suffered at a date as yet undetermined.3
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1690-1715 Author: Perry Gauci Notes 1. Manning and Bray, Surr. ii. 118; J. R. Woodhead, Rulers of London (London and Mdx. Arch. Soc.), 77; Surr. RO, BC/OC/1/3, f. 9. 2. G. Holmes, Pol. in Age of Anne, 275; HMC Lords, 
2 CONC n.s. vii. 43; Wilts. RO, Ailesbury mss 1300/1338A, 1344, Charles Becher to Ld. Bruce (Charles*), 9 Dec. 1707, 27 Apr. 1708. 3. Ailesbury mss 1300/1287, Anne, Lady Bruce, to Ld. Bruce, 8 Oct. 1710; Boyer, Pol. State, iii. 120; Cal.
2 CONC  Treas. Bks. xxvii. 326–7; Manning and Bray, ii. 118; Add. 70397, Henry Vane to 2nd Earl of Oxford (Edward Harley*), 4 July 1735.
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Morgan Randyll1
2 CONT *'''M, #107010, b. 7 October 1649, d. after 1735
2 CONT *Last Edited=24 Sep 2012
2 CONT *'''     Morgan Randyll was baptised on 7 October 1649.2 He married Anne Gould, daughter of Sir Thomas Gould and Anne (?), on 5 February 1678.3,2 He died after 1735.2
2 CONT *'''     He held the office of Member of Parliament (M.P.) for Guildford.1 He lived at Chilworth, Surrey, England.1
2 CONT *'''Child of Morgan Randyll and Anne Gould
2 CONT ** 1.Mary Randyll+1 b. 30 Jan 1682, d. 4 Aug 1728
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Exti
2 CONC nct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume I, page 425. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT * 2.[S6361] Rod Kerr, "re: Randyll Family," e-mail message to Darryl Roger LUNDY (101053), 21 September 2012. Hereinafter cited as "re: Randyll Family."
2 CONT  3.[S35] BLG1965 volume 3, page 387. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S35]
2 CONT *From: http://www.thepeerage.com/p10701.htm#i107010 ___________________
2 CONT *'''RANDYLL, Morgan (b.1649), of Chilworth, nr. Guildford, Surr.
2 CONT *'''b. 7 Oct. 1649, 1st s.''' of Vincent Randyll by Dorothy, da. of John Duncombe. '''educ. Wadham, Oxf. 1666; M. Temple 1670, called 1677. m. 5 Feb. 1678, Anne, da. and coh. of Sir Thomas Gould of St. Mary Aldermanbury, London, 2
2 CONC da.''' of whom one married Gilbert Vane, 2nd Baron Barnard. suc. fa. 1673.
2 CONT *Offices Held
2 CONT *'''Randyll’s grandfather''', Sir Edward Randyll of Albury, acquired Chilworth by marrying the only child of Sir John Morgan (d. 1621). The family owned considerable property round Guildford, including two gun-powder mills. '''A T
2 CONC ory, and a member of the October Club, Randyll voted consistently against the Administration after 1715. Owing to a succession of contested elections he ‘became so much in debt ... that in 1720 he sold his estate ... to ... one of
2 CONC  the directors of the South Sea Company for £29,335’.1 He contested Guildford again in 1722, but was defeated by Thomas Brodrick, put up by Lord Onslow.2 On 14 July 1738 his grandson''', Henry Vane, wrote to the 2nd Lord Oxford:
2 CONT **'''	My grandfather Randyll’s ... unhappy affairs oblige him to go to the Fleet to prevent a worse prison. I hope your Lordship will sign the enclosed note ... for £25, a sum absolutely necessary to pay the warden’s fees and buy 
2 CONC a few necessaries.3
2 CONT *'''The date of his death is unknown.
2 CONT *From: http://www.historyofparliamentonline.org/volume/1715-1754/member/randyll-morgan-1649 ____________________
