1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Burke, Bernard, Sir.  [https://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhera01byuburk#page/302/mode/2up A genealogical and heraldic history of the landed gentry of Great Britain & Ireland 6th ed]. London : Harrison 1879. V
2 CONC ol I. page 303
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In 1539, King Henry VIII awarded the Monkstown lands to Sir John Travers, Master of the Ordnance in Ireland. John Travers lived in his Castle at Monkstown from 1557 to his death in 1562 and is buried in the Carrickbrennan Graveyar
2 CONC d where the property fell to James Eustace 3rd Viscount Baltinglass through his marriage to Mary Travers. In 1580, the Castle was used as a rebellion stronghold, after which it was awarded to Sir Henry Wallop, Vice-Treasurer of Ir
2 CONC eland. The lands were later returned to Mary, the widow Baltinglass, who later married Gerald Alymer. On her death in 1610 the Castle was transferred to the Chevers family through the marriage of Mary Travers's sister Catherine to
2 CONC  John Chevers, and the property passed directly to his second son Henry Chevers, who married Catherine, daughter of Sir Richard Fitzwilliam. Henry and Catherine Chevers lived here with their four children (Walter, Thomas, Patrick,
2 CONC  Margaret). Upon the death of Henry in 1640, the castle and lands were passed to Walter Cheevers. Walter and family were given command to vacate Monkstown in 1653 by the Cromwellian Commissioners, and transplanted to Killyan, Coun
2 CONC ty Galway. In 1660, Walter Chevers was restored to his estate at Monkstown Castle, until his death in 1678.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT As the oldest son of Henry and Catherine (FitzWilliam) of Monkshead Castle, Dublin County, Ireland, Walter was ordered to proceed to Connaught with his family and descendants on 16 December 1653.  His wife, Allison, and their five
2 CONC  children all under the age of seven accompanied him along with four women servants and seven manservants.  He was required to supply to the English authorities a statement of his goods and description of all who were to accompany
2 CONC  him.  Walter is described:  "Walter Cheevers, of sanguine complexion, brown haire, and indifferent status." Their titles and land holdings were confiscated by representatives of Oliver Cromwell as part of his plan to punish Catho
2 CONC lics for the Ulster Protestant massacres of 1641.  Eleven million acres were confiscated and redistributed between soldiers and the investors who had financially supported the parliamentary armies. The displaced Catholics escaped 
2 CONC to the continent, were sold as slaves to the West Indies, or were sent to the poor infertile lands in Connacht which is the province that covers the West of Ireland, with the counties of Galway, Leitrim, Mayo, Roscommon, and Sligo
2 CONC . By 1655 no Catholic landowners remained in the East of Ireland.
