1 NOTE {geni:about_me} no proof he's related to Lord Rathmore.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ten Thousand Plunketts" by Emma Plunkett Ivey discusses this John Plunkett on Page 12 in Volume 1 (published 1969) and on Page 11 in Volume 2 (published 1974).
2 CONT
2 CONT The conclusion from the letters posted in the Ten Thousand Plunketts was that this John Plunket was NOT the brother of Captain Peter Plunkett
2 CONT
2 CONT Following are excepts from two letters on this topic:
2 CONT
2 CONT March 10, 1971 (written by Joan Phillips) on page 13 Volume II
2 CONT Richmond County, Virginia records indicated that John Plunkett was an orphan without estate and indentured the 3rd of January 1697/1698 to John and Saints Hawksford. (Later she mentions the bondage was to terminate when John was 2
2 CONC 1) Wonder if this might be how John became "landed and a planter". A good portion of Richmond County became part of King George County. Later, I found John and Frances Plunkett of Hanover Parish purchasing land from John Grinning 
2 CONC (Greening) whom I believe was the nephew of Frances Plunkett.
2 CONT
2 CONT The wife of John Plunkett, Jr. was Mildred Hawkins and daughter of Benjamin and Sarah Willis Hawkins. Additionally, Richmond County has reference to a Henry Plunkett who married the Widow Munden in August 1696.
2 CONT
2 CONT I think, without a doubt that this eliminated John Sr. as a brother of Captain Peter Plunkett.
2 CONT
2 CONT (In a 1974 letter Joan adds) I presume this to be the same John Plunkett who is given as executor of the estate of Robert Frank of Westmoreland County wills 1725. William Plunkett is mentioned as grandson of Robert Frank.
