1 NOTE {geni:about_me} HISTORY OF THE TOWN OF STONINGTON, County of New London, Connecticut, from its first settlement in 1649 to 1900, by Richard Anson Wheeler, New London, CT, 1900, p. 445
2 CONT ----
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=8943257
2 CONT
2 CONT *''''''Hyde genealogy: or, The descendants,in the female as well as in the male ...    By Reuben Hyde Walworth
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=rIKhN5aMelcC&pg=PA9&lpg=PA9&dq=Richard+Lord+1647+Hyde&source=bl&ots=HDmWGOQIoU&sig=x9JDNLoIGzqhLbE10r0c44vu5vc&hl=en&ei=q2zkTtP9DMariAKUtsWdBg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=5&ved=0CDQQ6AEwB
2 CONC A#v=onepage&q=Richard%20Lord%201647%20Hyde&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 9
2 CONT *''''''   III. ELIZABETH HYDE(9), born at Norwich, Conn., Aug., 1660, the eldest daughter of Samuel Hyde (2) and Jane Lee, was a granddaughter of William Hyde the first of Norwich. She m. in 1682 Lieut. Richard Lord, b. in May 164
2 CONC 7, at Saybrook, the third son of William Lord of Saybrook.
2 CONT *   [This William Lord, born in England about 1623, was third son of Thomas Lord (born in England in 1583) and Dorothy  his wife, who came to Newton, Mass., in 1635, and went from there to Hartford, Conn., in 1636, and had lands a
2 CONC ssigned to him there in 1639, where she d. in 1676. This William Lord was among the early settlers of Saybrook. he d. there 17 May, 1678. he had 14 children: 1, William, b. October, 1643, who married Mary Shaylor, and settled at E
2 CONC ast Haddam; 2, Thomas, b. in Dec., 1645, who married Mary Lee, daughter of the first Thomas Lee of Lyme, and settled at Lyme; ''''''3, Lieut. Richard''''''; 4, Mary, b. in May 1649; 5, Robert, b. in Aug., 1651; 6, John, b. in Sept
2 CONC ., 1653; 7, Joseph b. in Sept., 1656; 8, Benjamin, b. in 1666, father of Rev. Benjamin Lord, D.D., of Norwich; 9. Daniel; 10, James; 11, Samuel; and three daughters.]
2 CONT *''''''   Lieut. Richard Lord settled at Lyme, where he held a considerable tract of land. he d. there 20 August, 1727. His wife survived him, and d. there 22 July, 1736. They were both buried in the cemetery at old Lyme, below th
2 CONC e hill (where his brother, Thomas Lord, and Mary Lee his wife were also buried).
2 CONT *______________
2 CONT *''''''A genealogical history of the Kelley family : descended from Joseph Kelley of Norwich, Connecticut, with much biographical matter concerning the first four generations and notes of inflowing female lines (1897)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/genealogicalhist00inkell
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/genealogicalhist00inkell#page/19/mode/1up
2 CONT *   "From Hotten's 'Original Lists', we learn that on April 29, 1635, were registered for transportation from London to New England in the ship 'Elizabeth and Ann', Robert Cooper, master, Thomas Lord, aged 50, his wife Dorothy, ag
2 CONC ed 46, and their children, Thomas, aged 16, Ann, aged 14, William, aged 12, John, aged 10, Robert, aged 9, Aymie, aged 6, Dorothy, aged 4."
2 CONT *   The story of this Thomas Lord in the new world and the descent of Lydia Lord from his is as follows:
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/genealogicalhist00inkell#page/20/mode/1up
2 CONT *   I. THOMAS LORD, born about 1585, m. Dorothy ___, born about 1589; came to America 1635 and first settled in Newtown (now Cambridge) Mass., where his eldest son Richard had already established himself in 1632. He remained in Ne
2 CONC wtown a year or more, but in 1636-37 he and his family were a part of that large company, led by Rev. Thomas Hooker, from Massachusetts to form a new settlement on the Connecticut River. "they travelled more than a hundred miles t
2 CONC hrough a hideous, trackless wilderness to Hartford," of which they were the founders. Thomas Lord became one of the original propietors and first settlers of Hartford. he lived on the north side fronting Mill River. The date of hi
2 CONC s death is not known, but he died early. His wife Dorothy died 1675,
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/genealogicalhist00inkell#page/21/mode/1up
2 CONT *aged 86. Her will is given by Salisbury in full and shows that they had accumulated considerable property. The children of Thomas and Dorothy Lord are named above. The fourth of these, William, was our ancestor.
2 CONT **   II. WILLIAM LORD, born in England about 1623; died May 17, 1678; settled at Saybrook about 1645; ws there at the division of lands 1648; became a large landed proprietor both in Saybrook and Lyme. he bought great tracts of th
2 CONC e Indians. He married twice. The name of his first wife is unfortunately not preserved. His second married occurred in 1664 to Lydia Brown. By his first wife he had seven children: William, b. 1643; Thomas, Jr., b. 1645; ''''''Ric
2 CONC hard (our ancestor), b. 1647''''''; Mary, b. 1649; Robert, b. 1651; John, b. 1653; and Joseph, b. 1656. By his second wife he also had seven children.
2 CONT ***''''''   III. LIEUTENANT RICHARD LORD, born at saybrook 1647, settled in Lyme, where he was a landowner as early as 1680. The situation of the Lord estates was very fine. They stretched from the east side of the Connecticut riv
2 CONC er for several miles toward the north and north-east. In 1682 Elizabeth Hyde, eldest daughter of Samuel Hyde and Jane (Lee) Hyde, of Norwich. Accounts of the Hyde and Lee families are given below. In 1705 Richard Lord was appointe
2 CONC d Justice of the Peace and Quorum, and again Judge and Justice in 1706, as "Richard Lord, Gent." He held various other appointments of responsibility and honor in the public service. He was generally known in later years as Lieute
2 CONC . Richard Lord. The original commission of lieutenancy given him by Gov. Saltonstall in 1708, is still preserved. He died August 20, 1727, at Lyme. His wife survived him and died at Lyme July 23, 1736.
2 CONT ***''''''   Richard Lord and Elizabeth (Hyde) Lord had nine children: Elizabeth, b. Oct. 28, 1683; Phebe, Jane, Judge Richard, Mary, Lydia (our ancestress), Deborah, Abigail and John.
2 CONT *___________________________________
2 CONT *''''''George Hayes of Windsor and his descendants     By Charles Wells Hayes
2 CONT *http://books.google.com/books?id=dJ9RAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA61&lpg=PA61&dq=Richard+Lord+1647+Hyde&source=bl&ots=nWVmevecS1&sig=WjsbUXlMu6pHtwj1XjLb0qHSYXQ&hl=en&ei=q2zkTtP9DMariAKUtsWdBg&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=9&ved=0CEkQ6AE
2 CONC wCA#v=onepage&q=Richard%20Lord%201647%20Hyde&f=false
2 CONT *Pg. 61
2 CONT *   NATHAN, b. Rowley, Sept. 9, 1706, d. n. Lyme, Ct., Feb. 10, 1762; rem. c. 1729 to Lyme, farmer, with large estate (see an interesting account of his family, farm, servants, &c., by the Rev. J. Bailey, in Bartlett's "Frontier M
2 CONC issionary," Coll. P. E. Hist. Soc. II.); m. Lyme, Dec. 23, 1729 DEBORAH LORD, (''''''6th dau. Lieut. Richard (s. Wm., s.Thomas I. of Hartford) and Elizabeth (Hyde, dau. Sam., s. Wm. of Norwich'''''') L. of Lyme, b. c. 1698, d. Nov
2 CONC . 19, 1777.   .....
2 CONT *______________
2 CONT *''''''George Burritt Vanderpoel. The Ely ancestry: lineage of Richard Ely of Plymouth, England who came to .... (page 9 of 62)
2 CONT *http://www.ebooksread.com/authors-eng/george-burritt-vanderpoel/the-ely-ancestry-lineage-of-richard-ely-of-plymouth-england-who-came-to--goo/page-9-the-ely-ancestry-lineage-of-richard-ely-of-plymouth-england-who-came-to--goo.shtm
2 CONC l
2 CONT *   21. Samuel Ely, eldest son of Richard Ely and Mary Marvin, was born Oct, 21, 1686, in Lyme. His wife was Jane Lord, born about 1686, ''''''daughter of Lieut. Richard Lord and his wife, Elizabeth Hyde. Lieut. Lord's will, an ab
2 CONC stract of which is printed in E. E. & E. M. Salisbury's "Family Histories and Genealogies," Vol. L, page 287, is dated 1725, and contains legacies "to two grandchildren, Samuel and Elizabeth Ely, children of daughter Jane, decease
2 CONC d." All the known descendants of this Samuel Ely were included in Walworth's "Hyde Genealogy," published in 1864, occupying many pages of that voluminous work.'''''' The date of Mr. Ely's death has not been ascertained. 
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=8943257
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Samuel & Jane (Lee) Hyde. Information from Barbara Lea: According to Ruben H. Walworth (1864) The Hyde Genealogy, "Samuel Hyde and his wife came to Norwich in 1660. Their eldest daughter, Elizabeth, was bor
2 CONC n in August of that year and was the first white child born in Norwich." This quote is from Edith Drake Hyde's book The Descendents of Andrew Hyde. 
2 CONT
