2 CONT Hezekiah HERENDEEN, born 1687
2 CONT Hezekiah HERENDEEN was born in 1687, to Joseph HARRINGTON and Sarah HARRINGTON (born TILLINGHAST). Joseph was born in 1652, in Providence, RI. Sarah was born on November 17 1654, in Providence, Providence Co, RI. Hezekiah had 6 si
2 CONC blings: Richard HARRINGTON, Job HARRINGTON and 4 other siblings. Hezekiah passed away on date.
2 CONT
2 CONT MANUSCRIPT - HARRINGTON FAMILY GENEALOGICAL [2]
2 CONT Daughter Walling1 [3]
2 CONT Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The name, Harrington, with varied orthography, is one of the oldest surnames known in history, dating back to the time of William the Conqueror, (1066-1087). In the Latin census taken by this monarch to learn the resources of the 
2 CONC people for purposes of taxation, and in the book, Doomsday", we find the name record as Harginton, Hargington, Herynrton. Papworth, in his Directory of Arms, varied the spelling: Harrington, Herrington, Harington, Herendeen, etc.
2 CONT THE FIRST GENERATION
2 CONT BENJAMIN HARRINGTON, (HERENDEEN), PROVIDENCE, R.I. By Grant W. Harringotn, Barker Bldg., Kansas City, Kan., May 3, 1928. The Rhode Island branch of the Harrington and Harrington family tree runs back to Benjamin Herndon, one of th
2 CONC e earliest settlers of Rhode Island. Few names in American history have been given such a variety of spelling. For at least two or three generations, all of the family signatures, estant, are made by marks so that the bad spelling
2 CONC  cannot be charged up to the family, but rests on the more or less learned clerks who wrote the papers in which the name is used. The early records of the town of Providence, R.I. are printed in 20 volumes. They fairly bristle wit
2 CONC h the family name. In the five or six hundred times the name appears, it is spelled Arendeen, Hearnden, Hearndon, Hearnton, Hernden, Herenden, Herendeen, Herindin, Herendell, Herinton, Hearndale, Herndon, Hernton, Hernton. Often t
2 CONC he name will appear in the same paper spelled in two or more different ways. Book Notes, a paper published at Providence, R.I., once gave seventeen varieties of spelling for the name, and then did not exhaust the subject. Robert W
2 CONC illiams, perhaps the best educated of all the early Rhode Islanders, spelled it Hernden. (1), By 1790, when the first government census was taken, Harrington and Herrington were the recognized forms of spelling in the four or five
2 CONC  states into which the family had scattered although the Rhode Island census takers still found a few families of Harrendens and Herrendens. (2). {http://myplace.frontier.com/~chucknan3/appendixmain.htm)
2 CONT
