1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eleazer GILES was born 27 NOV 1640 in Salem, Essex Co., MA, and died ABT 1726. He was buried 1726 in Salem, Essex Co., MA. He was the son of Edward GILES and 3. Bridget Scudder (VERRY).
2 CONT
2 CONT Was husbandman (small landowner or free tenant farmer), then Yeoman (free man who owned his own farm).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===family===
2 CONT
2 CONT He married
2 CONT #  Sarah MORE 25 JAN 1664/65 in Salem, Essex Co., MA. She died 9 MAY 1676 in Salem, Essex Co., MA.
2 CONT # Elizabeth BISHOP 25 SEP 1677 in Salem, Essex Co., MA, daughter of James BISHOP and Mary Lewin (CURTIS). She was born 3 JUL 1657 in New Haven, New Haven Co., CT, and died 1733 in Salem, Essex Co., MA.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Sarah More
2 CONT #Sarah GILES b: 1 JAN 1665/66
2 CONT #  Elizabeth GILES b: 7 DEC 1667
2 CONT #  Hannah GILES b: FEB 1669/70
2 CONT #  Mary GILES b: 14 FEB 1671/72
2 CONT #  Susanna GILES b: 1 MAR 1673/74
2 CONT #  Eleazar GILES b: 3 MAR 1675/76 died young
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Elizabeth Bishop
2 CONT #  James GILES b: 15 NOV 1679 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  John GILES b: 31 AUG 1681 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  Abigail GILES b: 7 DEC 1684 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  Ruth GILES b: 12 JUL 1687 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  Edward GILES b: 28 APR 1689 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  James GILES b: 15 MAY 1691 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  Samuel GILES b: 17 DEC 1694 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  Eleazer GILES b: 8 JUL 1698 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT #  Mehitable GILES b: 11 APR 1701 in Salem, Essex Co., MA
2 CONT
2 CONT ===biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Giles Memorial [https://archive.org/stream/gilesmemorialgen00vint#page/8/mode/2up page 8 - 11]
2 CONT
2 CONT ELEAZAR GILES, 2 son of Edward 1 and Bridget Giles; bap. Nov. 27, 1640 ; m. 1, Sarah More of Lynn, Jan. 25, 1664-5 ; who d. May 9, 1676. 2, Elizabeth Bishop of New Haven, Ct., Sept. 25, 1677. They were married by her father, a mag
2 CONC istrate of the Connecticut Colony. She was b. July 3, 1657, and was the dau. of James Bishop, Esq., of New Haven, who was Secretary of the New Haven Colony, 1661, before its union with Connecticut; Representative of New Haven, 166
2 CONC 5 ; Assistant of Connecticut Colony, after the union, 1668; and Deputy Governor of Connecticut, from 1683 to 1690 — "until his death," says Mr. Savage, — except when the government and charter were suspended by the usurpation of S
2 CONC ir Edmund Andros, in 1687 and 1088. He died June 22, 1691. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazar Giles lived in Salem, the part which is now South Danvers, all his days. He was a "husbandman," or "yeoman;" a man of respectable standing, as appears from his alliance with the family of Lieut. Gov. Bishop; and possessed 
2 CONC a competent, though not large, estate. His name often occurs in conveyances of land, as grantor or grantee. Some of these I will here quote, as illustrative of his condition and relations : — 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1663. Eleazar Giles of Salem, for £8, conveys to John King of Salem ten acres of land in Salem, "which my mother gave me," bounded W. on land of Samuel Very, and S. on my own land and my brother's. [Essex Deeds, Salem, 5 : 62. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1671, May 22. Eleazar Giles of Salem, husbandman, and Sara his wife, for £13, convey to John Southwick of Salem, husbandman, about 13 acres of land in Salem, bounded in part by land of John Giles. [Ibid, 3 : 151. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1671, July 10. William Lord of Salem, for £12, sells to Eleazar Giles of Salem, husbandman, two acres of meadow-land on Cedar Pond, in Salem, which land was formerly excepted and reserved out of the farm now owned by Samuel Very; 
2 CONC it is desci'ibed as being "at the north end of the Gi-eat Meddow, on the westward side of a brooke that runs down through the medow." It was "formerly excepted and reserved out of the farm of Samuel Very by Mr. Philip Veren, who w
2 CONC as the first possessor thereof." For Philip Veren, see pp. 5, 6. In this deed, Veren and Very occur as two distinct names. [Ibid, 5 : 111. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1681, March 28. Eleazar Giles of Salem, yeoman, and Elizabeth his wife, convey to John Nurse of Salem, two acres of meadow land in Salem, being the same Avhich was conveyed to him in the last preceding deed. [Ibid, 10 : 27. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1695-6, Feb. 11. Eliezer Giles of Salem, yeoman, and Elizabeth his wife, for £100 currant money of New England, convey to William Brown, Esq., merchant, of Salem, my present homestead in Salem, containing about 20 acres, more or l
2 CONC ess, dwelling-house, out-houses, orchard, and the land adjoining. [Ibid, 11 : 74. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1695-6, Feb. 28. Thomas Baker of Topsfield and Priscilla his wife, for £100 currant money of New England, convey to Ensign Eleazar Giles of Salem, yeoman, 40 acres of land, more or less, in Salem, bounded N. on Stone's Plain and I
2 CONC pswich road, E. on John King's land and Mr. Parkman's ; S. on Very's meadow and Mr. Parkman ; W. on the form called Henry Bartholomew's. No house or other building is mentioned. [Ibid, 11 : 221. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Same day, Eleazar Giles, for £39.2, mortgages one half of the above described premises to Thomas Baker of Topsfield. Mortgage discharged, Nov. 11, 1718. [Ibid, 11 : 173. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1698, March 31. Eleazar Giles, for £20, sells five acres of the above land (apparently) to Samuel Gardner of Salem. He also sells eight acres for £30 to Samuel King of Salem, April 6, 1699. [Ibid, 12 : 190, and 15 : 288. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1698, June 17. Eliezer Giles of Salem sells to George Loskier of Salem, 4 acres and 26 poles of meddow land in Salem. [Ibid, 13 : 200. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1704, Nov. 27. Eliezer Gyles of Salem, yeoman, for £52, mortgages to William Brown, Esq., 30 acres of land, bounded on the north by the road from Ipswich to Boston. Mortgjige discharged, Sept. 3, 1716. [Ibid, IG : 152. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In both of the two deeds, last quoted, the name is spelled Giles and Gyles in the same document, but the signature to both is Giles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1724, June 23. Eleazar Giles of Salem, yeoman, gives his personal estate to his wife Elizabeth Giles. [Ibid, 43 : 251. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1725, Nov. 24. Eleazar Giles of Salem and Elizabeth Giles his wife, in consideration of a bond for the maintenance of them, &c., convey to their sons Edward Giles and Samuel Giles, their dwelling-house, land, &c. [Ibid, 45 : 177. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1725-6, March 8. Eleazar Giles and Elizabeth his wife give their consent to a deed whereby Edward Giles, their son, sells to Joseph Putney, Jr., of Salem, a shop in Salem, with the land it stands on. [Ibid, 53 : 157. This Joseph P
2 CONC utney married Mehitable, dau. of Eleazar Giles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the interval between the deed last quoted, and that which I shall next quote, the death of Eleazar Giles must have occurred. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1727, June 29. Elizabeth Giles of Salem, widow of Eleazar Giles of Salem, deceased, for £30 in Province Bills, conveys part of the homestead to her son Samuel Giles of Salem, " Joyner." [Ibid, 46 : 116. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1729, May 2. Elizabeth Giles of Salem, widow, conveys land to her son Edward Giles of Salem, bricklayer. [Ibid, 54 : 16. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Eleazar Gyles appears in a List, made 1714, of those who were "legall proprietors of y'^ comon and undevided Lands in y^ township of Salem, by y^ Laws of this province, y' had Cottages and Dwelling places before y^ yea
2 CONC r 1661." The name of Benjamin Very, his nephew, also appears ; but neither uncle or nephew was married in 1661, and it would seem that Benjamin Very was not then born! [Geneal. Reg., Vol. VIL, p. 151. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazar Gyles and his brother John Gyles signed a Petition to the General Court in 1668, against Imposts. It was also signed by 228 other inhabitants of Salem, whose names are printed, with the petition, in the Geneal. Reg., Vol. 
2 CONC IX., pp. 81-85. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was constable of Salem in 1676. He presented a petition to the General Court, Oct. 22, 1680, praying for indemnity for losses sustained by him in the fiill of Indian Corn received by him for rates. This petition is still extant
2 CONC , in the Massachusetts Archives. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazer Gyles and Elizabeth Gyles, with thirty-eight others, were dismissed, June 25, 1713, from the First Church in Salem, in order to become a distinct church at the Middle Precinct, now South Danvers. His nephew, Thomas Very, w
2 CONC as dismissed at the same time. Before this time, they must have gone three or four miles — and the younger people, at least, must have walked that distance — to meeting. Of the new church. Rev. Benjamin Prescott w^as ordained past
2 CONC or, Sept. 23, 1713. He was b. in Concord, Sept. 16, 1687; H. C. 1709; dismissed Nov. 16, 1756; d. May 28, 1777, a. 90.   There had previously been a church in North Danvers, then called " Salem Village "; famous in the history of 
2 CONC the Witchcraft delusion, 1692. Hence the church formed in 1713, came to be in the "Middle Precinct." This was sometimes called " Salem Precinct." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazar Giles died probably in the year 1726, a. 86. I have found no will of his, or settlement of his estate, on record.  His widow Elizabeth d. in 1733, a. 76. Samuel Giles was appointed, Oct. 29, 1733, administrator on the esta
2 CONC te of his mother Elizabeth Giles, late of Salem, widow, deceased, intestate. [Essex Prob. 21 : 6. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Eleazar Giles, all b. in Salem, (South Danvers,) and all baptized in the First Church ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT * http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=chester&id=I2063
2 CONT **1.Title: Thompson Family - Ancestors and Relatives Author: Thompson, Keith Publication: Worldconnect.Genealogy.Rootsweb.Com
2 CONT **2.Title: The Giles Memorial Genealogical Memoirs Author: Vinton, John Adams Publication: Henry W. Dutton & Son, Boston, MA. 1684 Repository:  Media: Book
2 CONT **3.Title: Vital Records of Salem, MA to 1850 Author: Transcribed by Judith Wolff Publication: Essex Institute, Salem, MA 1910 Repository:  Note: freepages.books.rootsweb.com/~rbrown/salem/birth-39.htm Media: Book
2 CONT **4.Title: The Great Migration Begins, Immigrants to New England, 1620-1633, Vol I-III Author: Anderson, Robert Charles Publication: NEHGS, Boston, 2000 Repository:  Note: HT Family History book shelf Media: Family Archive CD
2 CONT * http://trees.wmgs.org/getperson.php?personID=I92486&tree=Schirado
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazer Giles first wife was Sara More, married at Salem, MA on 25: 11m: 1664.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazer and Elizabeth Bishop were married on Sept. 25, 1677 at Salem, MA.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazer (alternate spelling Eleazar) Giles (alternate spelling Gyles) was born on November 27, 1640 in Salem, Massachusetts, the third of four children of Edward Gyles and Bridget _____ Verry, who were married October 6, 1635. [3]
2 CONC  He married, in Salem, (1) Sara More, of Lynn, married January 25, 1664-5, who died in Salem on May 9, 1676, and (2) Elizabeth Bishop on September 25, 1677. [4] Eleazer Giles is named in the will of his mother, Bridget Giles (will
2 CONC  made on January 14, 1668, proved November 30, 1680). Also named are her sons Samuel Very, Thomas Very and Thomas’s daughter Bridget Very. Vinton comments that “From this will it is evident that Edward Giles was the second husband
2 CONC  of Bridget, and that she lived till the age of about eighty years. She must have been born about the year 1600.” [5] “The children of Edward and Bridget Giles, b. in Salem, were . . . Eleazar, bap. Nov. 27, 1640; m. 1, Sarah More
2 CONC ; 2. Elizabeth Bishop.” [6] Vinton documents that “Eleazar Giles lived in Salem, the part which is now South Danvers, all his days. He was a ‘husbandman,’ or ‘yeoman;’ a man of respectable standing, as appears from his alliance wi
2 CONC th the family of Lieut. Gov. Bishop; and possessed a competent, though not large, estate. His name often occurs in conveyances of land, as grantor or grantee.” [7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleazer Giles served on nine juries in which the accused faced charges of witchcraft in Salem, Massachusetts in 1692-3. Seven of the defendants (Rebekah Jacobs, Job Tookey, Sarah Hawkes, Mercy Wardwell, Hannah Post, Sarah Bridges,
2 CONC  and Elizabeth Johnson Sr.) were found not guilty. The other two, Elizabeth Johnson Jr. and Mary Post were found guilty of witchcraft and sentenced to death, but they were pardoned by Gov. William Phips and released. [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT “Eleazer Giles died probably in the year 1726, a. 86. I have found no will of his, or settlement of his estate, on record. His widow Elizabeth d. in 1733, a. 76. Samuel Giles was appointed, Oct. 29, 1733, administrator on the esta
2 CONC te of his mother Elizabeth Giles, late of Salem, widow, deceased, intestate. [Essex Prob. 21 : 6.” [9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the children of Eleazar Giles by his first wife, Sarah More, was Mary Giles, born February 14, 1671-2, in Salem (South Danvers), and baptized in the First Church.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT
