0 @I6000000016661155240@ INDI
1 NAME George /Wade/
2 GIVN George
2 SURN Wade
2 _MARNM Wade
1 NAME Field Marshal George Wade
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Westminster Abbey, Collegiate Church of St Peter at Westminster, City of Westminster, London
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1673
2 PLAC Killavally, Westmeath, Ireland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 MAR 1748
1 TITL General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Wade
2 CONT
2 CONT Field Marshal George Wade (1673 – 14 March 1748) was a British Army officer who served in the Nine Years' War, War of the Spanish Succession, Jacobite rising of 1715 and War of the Quadruple Alliance before leading the constructio
2 CONC n of barracks, bridges and proper roads in Scotland. He went on to be a military commander during the War of the Austrian Succession and Commander-in-Chief of the Forces during the Jacobite rising of 1745.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early career
2 CONT
2 CONT Born the son of Jerome Wade in Killavally, Westmeath in Ireland, Wade was commissioned into the Earl of Bath's Regiment on 26 December 1690 and served in Flanders in 1692, fighting at the Battle of Steenkerque in August 1692 durin
2 CONC g the Nine Years' War and earning a promotion to lieutenant on 10 February 1693. He transferred to Sir Bevil Granville's Regiment on 19 April 1694 and was promoted to captain on 13 June 1695.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the War of the Spanish Succession he first served under Marlborough, seeing action in Flanders at the Battle of Kaiserwerth in April 1702, the Battle of Venlo in September 1702, the Battle of Roermond in October 1702 and Ba
2 CONC ttle of Liège also in October 1702, gaining promotion to major on 20 March 1703 and to lieutenant colonel in October 1703. In 1704 he joined the staff of Henri de Massue, Earl of Galway as adjutant-general in Portugal, and disting
2 CONC uished himself as colonel of the Huntingdon's Regiment during the Battle of Alcántara during which he was wounded in April 1706. He repelled a large force of cavalry at Vila Nova and then commanded the 3rd infantry brigade during 
2 CONC the Battle of Almansa in April 1707. He won promotion to brigadier general on 1 January 1708.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as second-in-command to James Stanhope in Minorca in 1708, leading one of the storming parties on Fort St. Philip, before returning to Spain in 1710, where he fought at the Battle of Saragossa in August 1710. He earned a
2 CONC  promotion to major-general on 3 October 1714 and became commander of the British forces in Ireland in November 1714.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wade returned home to join in the suppression of the Jacobite rising of 1715 and undertook security duties in Bath where he unearthed a haul of Jacobite weapons. He entered politics serving as Member of Parliament for Hindon in 17
2 CONC 15. On 19 March 1717 he became colonel of the Earl of Plymouth's Regiment of Horse.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1719 he served as second in command to Viscount Cobham during the War of the Quadruple Alliance when Cobham led a force of 4,000 troops on a raid on the Spanish coastline which captured Vigo and occupied it for ten days before 
2 CONC withdrawing.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became Member of Parliament for Bath in 1722 retaining the seat for 25 years. His house there is now a grade I listed building.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT The government of George I sent Wade to inspect Scotland in 1724. He recommended the construction of barracks, bridges and proper roads to assist in the control of the region and on 10 May 1725 received appointment as Commander in
2 CONC  Chief of His Majesty's forces, castles, forts and barracks in North Britain, tasked with carrying out his own recommendations. Over the next twelve years Wade directed the construction of some 240 miles (390 km) of roads, plus 30
2 CONC  bridges (including the Tay Bridge at Aberfeldy). General Wade's military roads linked the garrisons at Ruthven, Fort George, Fort Augustus, and Fort William. A reference in verse is said to be inscribed on a stone at the start of
2 CONC  one of his military roads in Scotland:
2 CONT
2 CONT If you had seen this road before it was made.
2 CONT
2 CONT  You would lift up your hands and bless General Wade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wade also organised a militia named "Highland Watches", calling on members of the landed gentry to sign up[10] and raising the first six companies in 1725 (three of Campbells and one each of Frasers, Grants, and Munros). Also in 1
2 CONC 725, Wade put down an insurrection after the Government attempted to extend the "Malt tax" to Scotland and enraged citizens in Glasgow drove out the military and destroyed the home of their representative in parliament. He was pro
2 CONC moted to lieutenant general on 15 April 1727.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 June 1732 he became Governor of Berwick upon Tweed and on 19 June 1733 he became Governor of Fort William, Fort George and Fort Augustus. He was promoted to general of horse on 17 July 1739.
2 CONT
2 CONT He raised four more "highland watch" companies in 1739: these were subsequently reorganized as the Black Watch regiment. He still had the time to sign his support to the Foundling Hospital which was established in 1939 in London. 
2 CONC On 22 June 1742 he was appointed Lieutenant-General of the Ordnance and on 24 June 1742 he was appointed a member of the Privy Council.
2 CONT
2 CONT War of the Austrian Succession
2 CONT
2 CONT On 17 December 1743 he became a field marshal with his appointment to the joint command of the Anglo-Austrian force in Flanders against the French in the War of the Austrian Succession. Wade organised an advance towards Lille in J
2 CONC uly 1744 but the action became stalled in the face of logistical problems. He resigned from his command in March 1745, returning home to become Commander-in-Chief of the Forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacobite rebellion
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1745 during the Jacobite rising Wade focussed his troops on Newcastle upon Tyne on the East coast of England; however the Jacobite forces advanced from Scotland down the West coast of England via Carlisle into Lancashir
2 CONC e and the speed of their advance left Wade scrambling. In freezing conditions and with his men starving, he failed to counter their march into England or their subsequent retreat back from Derby to Scotland; Wade was replaced as C
2 CONC ommander-in-Chief by Prince William, Duke of Cumberland who led the army to success at the Battle of Culloden in April 1746.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wade received mention in a verse added to God Save the King circa 1745, but dropped by the time the song gained adoption as the British national anthem towards the end of the eighteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord, grant that Marshal Wade
2 CONT
2 CONT  May, by thy mighty aid,
2 CONT
2 CONT  Victory bring.
2 CONT
2 CONT  May he sedition hush
2 CONT
2 CONT  And, like a torrent, rush
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rebellious Scots to crush.
2 CONT
2 CONT  God save the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wade died, unmarried, on 14 March 1748 and is buried at Westminster Abbey where his life his recognised by monument created by Louis-François Roubiliac.
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * "<em>[http://www.westminster-abbey.org/our-history/people/george-wade George Wade]</em>", Westminster Abbey
