0 @N6000000004969805797@ NOTE Built first Baptist church in New England
0 @N6000000002932538042@ NOTE Built his own exp. the first meeting-ho. and gave it to the soc. in 1711, with the lot it stood on; and d. 29 Jan 1718. By his sec. w. Lydia, prob. d. of Philip Tabor of Tiverton, he had Pardon; Philip; Joseph, b. 1677; Benjamin; 
1 CONC Mary; Abigail; Mercy, b. a. 1679; Hannah; and Eliz. He had three ch. by a former w. whose names are unkn. Mary m. one of the numerous Carpenters at Pautuxet, prob. a s. of William; Abigail m. Nicholas Sheldon; Mercy m. the third N
1 CONC icholas Power, as his sec. w. had nine ch. and d. 13 Nov 1769, aged 91; Hannah m. a Hale of Swanzey; and Eliz. m. Tabor of New London. He is, I believe, founder of a long line, eight of wh. had, says Farmer, ben gr. at Brown Univ.
1 CONC  in 1834.
1 CONT {Savage, James Genealogical Dictionary of the First Settlers of New England, . 1998,
1 CONT Publication: Genealogical Publishing Co., Baltimore, Maryland. v4p303}
1 CONT
1 CONT Pardon Tillinghast was a noted benefactor of the growing town of Providence, and the Baptist meeting house and its grounds were his gift. The building was noted as one of the finest samples of architectural skill, and was designed
1 CONC  by Joseph Brown. (Wallace.ged; Author: Robert Almon Wallace)
1 CONT
1 CONT The following stories & information from Dick Bentley, 3-4-03 LBM via Ancestors of Robert Mackay Howard Sr. and Margaret Braun Howard GED; Beverly Bode Howard
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT TILLINGHAST, Pardon (1622-1718), from Eng. to Providence, R.I., ca. 1643, where built at own expense, 1st Bapt. church in America, and gave it to the society; pastor 40 years; dep. Gen. Ct 1672, 80, 94, 97, 1700; m 2d Lydia Taber.
1 CONC  From: ABRIDGED
1 CONT COMPENDIUM, Frederick Virkus Vol. I, 1925 , F. A.
1 CONT VIRKUS & COMPANY
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------
1 CONT PARDON TILLINGHAST was born at Seven Cliffs, Sussex, Eng., 1622, and died at Providence, R. I., January 29, 1718. He was the most prominent merchant of his day, and a very useful citizen, being Deputy six years, and Overseer of th
1 CONC e Poor, a member of the Town Council, etc. In 1681 he became the pastor of the First Baptist
1 CONT Church, the building for which, as well as the land, he is said to have given. He married Lydia, the daughter of Philip and Lydia (Masters) Taber, on April 16, 1664, and died 1718. Issue, among others:
1 CONT Mercy, who married NICHOLAS POWER III., and a son: PHILIP TILLINGHAST, born at Providence, R. I., October, 1669, and died March 14, 1732, was a wealthy merchant, and took part actively in public life. He joined Captain Gallup's ex
1 CONC pedition against Canada, 1690; was a Deputy for twelve terms, Assistant to the Governor 1714, and a member of the Town Council for eleven years. His wife was Martha, the daughter of Jonathan and Sarah (Borden) Holmes, and they wer
1 CONC e married May 3, 1692. She was born 1675, and died March 10, 1729. Their daughter Anne, married her cousin, NICHOLAS POWER IV. From: Ancestral Records and
1 CONT Portraits vol.1 The Grafton Press. New York. 1910. This book is a compilation from the archives of chapter I, of the Colonial Dames of America.
1 CONT ====================================================
1 CONT Pardon Tillinghast, b. 1622, at Seven Cliffs, Sussex Co., England. He served in Cromwell's Army. Came to Providence abt. 1646. He was Deputy 1672-1700. Member of Town Council 1688-1707. April 14, 1711, he deeded his house called t
1 CONC he Baptist meeting house, "situated between the Town Street and salt water, together with the lot whereon said meeting house standeth, to the Church and their successors, for the Christian love, good will and affection which I bea
1 CONC r to the Church of Christ in said Providence, the which I am in fellowship with, and have the care of as being elder of said Church."
1 CONT
1 CONT He d. Jan. 29, 1718. His will, proved Feb. 11, 1718, says: "I do bequeath my life and spirit into the hands of the Fountain of life and Father of spirits, from whom I have received it, and my body to the dust from whence it came, 
1 CONC in hope of a resurrection to eternal life."
1 CONT
1 CONT Inventory,1,542, 4s. 3d. He was buried in his own lot at the south end of the town. He m. (2nd) April 16, 1664, Lydia, dau. of Philip and Lydia (Masters) Taber.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bowler, N. P. Record of the descendants of Charles Bowler. Forman-Bassett-Hatch Co. Cleveland, OH. 1905.[Desc Pardon Elisha Tillinghast from 2523840.ged]
0 @N6000000003525436681@ NOTE
1 CONT Known as "The Aged". Came to Mass. from England sometime in the autumn of 1642. Earliest date recoreded is in Dedham 25 Nov. 1642 when he purchased land, probably on the "Great" or Dedham Island. Various other grants in the area w
1 CONC ere also made.. He joined the church at the time of the baptism of his eldest child, 2 or 11 Oct. 1646 and was made a freeman 26 May 1647. He lived and died in Dedham, but three sons, John,, Nathaniel and Robert moved to Wollomono
1 CONC poag, established in 1661 and later incorporated as Wrentham in 1673. His name is second on the Dedham tax list, indicating wealth. His will reprinted in "Ware Genealogy" by Emma F. Ware, also the source of above information.
