0 @N6000000000334567015@ NOTE [Shipp 2260823.ged]
1 CONT MARRIAGE: Marriage took place in old Rappahannock Co., VA.  Old Rappahannock Co existed from 1656-1692 and took in the territory which later was split into Essex and Richmond counties.
1 CONT
1 CONT Posted by: Norman Peters Date: February 08, 2002 at 19:49:43 
1 CONT In Reply to: Re: Looking for Josiah Shipp's parents... by Jim Shipp  of 1091  
1 CONT
1 CONT Thanks for your notes. Much of what you mentioned is included in the article I noted in my original post, including some of the following comments:
1 CONT 1. When Ralph Shipp wrote his first book he was unaware of the immigration record for Josiah in 1687, plus Ralph did not have access to the original records or good abstracts of. When he was revising his book for a second printing
1 CONC  a group of us [4] wrote his with the documentation about Josiah immigration. Even though Ralph liked the idea that all Shipps were descended from William, he did accept our research.
1 CONT 2.. The Josiah I. S. Shipp on Josiah's will is another misinterpretation of a document. People who could not read or write signed records with their initials, with an X, or with a particular mark [as did Mary Beckley on her record
1 CONC s]. Their initials, mark or X was placed between the given name and the family name.
1 CONT 3. Something of interest about Josiah's immigration record. He was imported with a man named John Manning. On 19 November 1687 John Manning was one of the witnesses to the deed from Joseph and Mary Beckley to Josias Shipp. [Have a
1 CONC lways planned to work on this John Manning, but never have.]
1 CONT 4. In the old records the name Joseph, Josiah, and Joias were all used for the same name. In fact one of the pieces of land that Josiah bought has him listed as Joseph.
1 CONT 5. A good idea of Josiah's age may, may, have been the year that he registered his cattle mark with the local court. Most young men did this at the first court after their 21 birthday. Josiah registered his on 12 September 1666.
