0 @N6000000004558342121@ NOTE
1 CONT 1st Hazen in America, came in 1638
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Hazen, born in the parish of Cadney, Lincolnshire, England, baptized there 14 Dec 1614, son of Thomas Elizabeth (_____) Hazen, died at Rowley, Essex Co, Mass, 22 July 1683 [probate record], buried there 22 July 1683 [vital 
1 CONC record]; marries first, Elizabeth _______, buried at Rowley, 18 Sept 1649; married second, at Rowley, in Mar 1650, HANNAH GRANT, baptized at Cottingham, Yorkshire, England, 16 Oct Oct 1631, died at Haverhill, Mass, in Feb 1715/16,
1 CONC  daughter of Thomas Jane (Haburne) Grant. She married second, as his second wife at Haverhill, 17 Mar 1683/84, Lieutenant [later Captain] George Browne, baptized at St Edmunds, Salsbury, Wiltshire, England, 29 Aug1622, died at Hav
1 CONC erhill, 31 Oct 1699, aged 76 {vital records], died 30 Oct 1699, aged 78 [gravestone], son of George Christian (Hibbert) Browne.
1 CONT 12 children were born at Rowley, Mass, all by the second wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT THE IDENTIFICATION OF EDWARD HAZEN
1 CONT What are the reasons for concluding that Edward Hazen of Rowley, Massachusetts, the founding ancestor of the American family, was the same "Edwardus Hasson filius Thomae" who "fuit baptizatur 24 die Decembris 1614" as entered in t
1 CONC he registers of Cadney, Lincolnshire?
1 CONT 1. The surname was not a very common one in England, and a great deal of research in Northumberland and Lincolnshire has not disclosed any other Edward Hazen of suitable age.
1 CONT 2. No other history has been found for Edward Hazen (baptized 24 Dec. 1614), who was living in 1628 when his father made his will. No record of burial has been found in the search of many Parish registers of Lincolnshire. In July 
1 CONC 1641, Parliament passed an act that Every clergyman should take a census of males over eighteen in his Parish, presenting to them for signature a paper upholding the Protestant faith. ; This "Protestation Roll" is very compete for
1 CONC  Lincolnshire. It shows at Cadney, William Hassen, first cousin of Edward; at Great Limber, Richard Hason, Edward's brother; and at South Ferraby, a Thomas Hason, servant to William Bromby. Edward does not appear in this Roll, ind
1 CONC icating that unless he had died with record, he had left Lincolnshire before 1641.
1 CONT 3. The date of birth is about what we should expect for Edward of Rowley, and makes him in his sixty-nineth year at death.
1 CONT 4. The names which Edward of Rowley gave his children are very significant. The first child was Elizabeth named after his mother, and also his grandmother who lived until he was fifteen years old. The next child, Hannah, was named
1 CONC  for Edward's wife. Then came John, the eldest son, which was the name of Edward's grandfather and elder brother. The next child, Thomas, was named after both grandfather, Edward's father and Hannah's father both bearing the name 
1 CONC of Thomas. The next son, Edward, was named for himself, and the youngest, Richard, for Edward's brother of that name. The names of the other children, who were daughters, are not significant, since Edward had no sisters for whom t
1 CONC hey could have been named.
1 CONT 5. Other settlers in Rowley were from Lincolnshire, and after Edward Hazen married Hannah Grant, her sister Anna married Robert Emerson, who was, like Hazen, a native of Cadney.
1 CONT This from the records of Tracy Elliott Hazen.
1 CONT In the political life of the town also, the Rowley records show that Edward Hazen was a man of influence and importance.
1 CONT On 3 Jan. 1650, at a town meeting, Edward Hazen was chosen as on of the four overseers, and was again chosen to this office, 19 Dec. 1651 and 12 Dec. 1654. He was also overseer for the years 1660, 1661, and 1662, and was a selectm
1 CONC an for the year 1669. These overseers were not the same as selectmen, as sometimes has been stated, but were always named after and in addition to the select men, or "prudentiall men," and in 1649 are described as "ouerseers for t
1 CONC he execution of towne orders and Hy wayes." On 10 Jan. 1660/70, "Thomas Tene" and "Edward Hasne" werre chosen "ouerseers for vuiin fences and hywayes and vuiin Chimneys." Among the "Towne Charges for the year past 1651: for John S
1 CONC mith for going to Court 0-4-6: alsoe for Edward Hasen the lyke worke 0-3-6." "Town charges 1665: Edward hasen a Day Jury Man." The Ipswich Court Records and Files show that Ed. Hassen served on Trial Jury 30: 7: 1651 and also 26:7
1 CONC :1654. On 9 Jan. 1665/66, Edward Hazon was chosen one of the four judges of delinquents "for not comeing to towne meetings"; 9 Jan. 1666/67, "Edward hasen Judge for yeare ensuing." In a list [of town charges?] 1662: "Ed hason 0-10
1 CONC -2." Towne charges 1667: "Edward Hason for ueiwinge fence 0-3-0." In an undated record probably referring to King Phillip's War and to Edward Hazen's sone: "Work done for Samuel person in ye war--James Tenny 1 day: Thomas Hasen an
1 CONC d Edward each a day." [Early Records of the Town of Rowley (1894), pp 70, 71, 73, 91, 106, 118, 132, 146, 159, 162, 165, 185, 200, 205, 224; Quarterly Courts of Essex County, 1:232, 362.]
1 CONT The follow account of the settlement of Edward Hazen's estate is taken from the original papers, in two sheets, on file at Salem, and differs somewhat from the clerk's book copy.
1 CONT Edward Hazen and his wife Hannah appear in the following deeds:
1 CONT Peter Eyers and wife Hannah of Haverhill convey to Edward Hasen of Rowley 6 1/2 acres of puland, Peter Eyers' 3d division, with land abutting on the Merrimack River, (date not give in copy). Witnesses, Jno. Carlton, Jno. Gryffyn [
1 CONC Old Norfolk Co. Deeds, 3:336, p. 367 in copy.]
1 CONT Edward Hassen and wife Hannah of Rowley convey to John Tennie of Rowley 100 acres in the division called "Merimake land," the northwest end abutting on the Merrimack River, and 4 acres of meadow called Crane meadow; also a parcel 
1 CONC of land granted to John Harris, John Tod, Richard Longhorne, Richard Holmes, and Edward Hassen by the town of Rowley, dated 20 May 1664. Witnesses, Ezekiell Jewit, Thomas Tenny.
1 CONT The name was signed "Hasin." Edward acknowledged 16 June 1673. [Ipswich Deeds, 4:41.]
1 CONT John Pearly and wife Mary of Newbury convey to Edward Hazen of Rowley 7 acres of upland in Rowley Village-land of Thomas Pearly, Daniel Wood, and Ezekiel Northen mentioned, (date not given in copy). Witnesses, Benjamin Rolfe, Thom
1 CONC as Hale. John and Mary acknowledged on 19 Feb. 1684 and she resigned her right of dower to Edward Hazen's administrators. [Ibid., 5:371.]
1 CONT "Hanah ye Relict and late wife of said Edward Hason deceased & Edward Hason son of ye said Edward Hason Joynt administrators" confirm to Thomas Hason land in Rowley Village where he now lives and which his father Edward before his
1 CONC  death settled upon him as his portion-land bought of John Pearly of Rowley Village and on 19 Feb. 1684 acknowledged by him, dated 14 May 1685. No witnesses. Signed "Hannah Hazzen alias Browne." Hannah acknowledged 19 May 1685; Ed
1 CONC ward, 4 Nov. 1685. [Ibid., 5:371.]
1 CONT Hannah Browne of Haverhill, widow and relict of Capt. George Browne, "for & in consideration of natural & christian love & afection which I beare unto my loving & beloved sonns Thomas & Edward wch I had by my former Husband Edward
1 CONC  Hazen long time deceased at Rowley," conveys to them all the interest in the estate of her brother John Grant of Rowley, dec'd; 1 Mar. 1699 or 1700. Witnesses, Thomas Eaton, Senr., Richard Saltonstall. [Essex Co. Deeds, 14:37.]
1 CONT Capt. George Brown and wife Hannah of Haverhill convey to Thomas Carlton of Bradford 76 acres laid out to her former husband Edward Hazzen of Rowley, dec'd, and her part of the estate of Edward Hazzen in Bradford-widow Smith, wido
1 CONC w Hobson mentionsed, 3 Apr. 1697. Witnesses, Abraham Perkins, Solomon Reves, Senr., Jacob Perkins, 3d. Acknowledged 3 Dec. 1698. [Ibid.,30:44,45.]
1 CONT Richard Hazzen, Thomas Hazzen, Edward Hazzen, Daniel Wicom, Junr., Nathaniel Storey, children of Edward Hazzen and Hannah, now wife of Capt. George Browne, quitclaim to Thomas Carlton, 3 Apr. 1697. Witnesses, Edward Carleton, Nath
1 CONC . Walker, Solomon Keyes, Senr., Jacob Perkins, tertius. [Ibid., 30:45.]
1 CONT An agreement concerning some land that was their father Edward Hazen's and that after his death was laid out to his wife for her thirds, was made by Thomas Hazen of Norwich, John Wood of Bradford, Timothy Perkins of Topsfield, Edw
1 CONC ard Hazzen of Boxford, Richard Hazzen of Haverhill; first, that Edward Hazzen is satisfied with 16 pounds already received, one ox gate in east end of ox pasture, and 5 pounds which his brothers promise to pay; secondly, that the 
1 CONC others are satisfied with a lpiece of march, call cowbridge marsh, and a piece of land called cowbridge lot, and another piece of land in Symond's new field from their mother's thirds, taking in brother Jeremy Person, brother Harr
1 CONC is' children, brother Gibson's children, and brother Wicom's children, they having their share with the others; dated 20 June 1716. Witnesses, Thomas Perley, Junr., Nathaniel Perley.d [Ibid., 34:189]
1 CONT On 4 July a "Hanah Hazen" was a witness to the will of Ann Swan, relict of Richard Swan of Rowley. [Essex Co. probate files, Docket 268976.] It is highly probable that she was the wife of Edward Hazen, as his daughter Hannah was p
1 CONC resumably married before that date.
1 CONT From Hazen family in America by Tracy Elliott Hazen
