0 @N6000000003949543487@ NOTE Thomas crossed the sound from Connecticut and took up residence at East Hampton on Long Island. He there married Clemons Scudder by whom he had one son named Constant and a daughter named Clemons.  He then moved to New England Tow
1 CONC n, now Fairton near Bridgeton, Cumberland County, New Jersey, where he became a heavy property holder and respected citizen.
1 CONT On April 4th, 1717 he and Henry Joyce received from Jeremiah Bacon "A deed for one acre of land in trust for the people called Presbyterians on the North side of the Cohansey River, to build and establish a  meeting house for the 
1 CONC public worship of God."  The substantial brick building was erected in 1735 and established the Presbyterian Church of Greenwick, New Jersey.  Thomas and his son, Clemons, as well as Thomas Ewing, Mercy Maskell, Thomas Padgett and
1 CONC  James Caruthers were members of this organization and liberal contributors.
1 CONT Sometime after his settlement in New Jersey, Thomas Maskell's wife Clemons died.  Afterward, in 1700, he married Mercy Stathem and had three daughters.
